KANT_TR['refl/kemp'] = {"refl:1619":["Common understanding is that which, in all its thoughts and judgments, has regard at the same time to the universal judgment of others, in order that, through agreement with it, it may become intelligible and convincing to itself. He who pays no heed whatever to this indulges in enthusiasm [*schwärmt*]. The universal |\nexternal judgment is a [mark] touchstone of the illusion which befalls us when we judge merely from the private situation in which we happen to be; and in that [universal judgment] we always have a guiding thread by which to test our own judgment. I hold up against the procedure of common understanding not the result of my judgments but my reflections, [to see] whether I am intelligible to everyone.",[]],"refl:1620":["L Ia:\nYou have hitherto thought a great deal, but presumably you have not reflected upon your thinking.\nIn the same way you have spoken for many years, but have not reflected upon language.* (g Speaking. Thinking aloud.)\n*(s We learn many things, for instance walking, without having been taught them.\nThinking cannot be learned from others, any more than walking; but one can learn to bring one's capacity for thinking under rules.)\nNevertheless, in learning a dead language you have found [haben] that it is bound (g to) certain constant rules, without which it could not be language, that is, the communication of one's thoughts. (g You have therefore followed the rules of a language without being able to name these rules themselves. (s Molière — *Le Bourgeois gentilhomme* — speaking prose — therefore speaking grammatically.))\nNow language is the communication of thoughts. Thinking, therefore, will also in all men be bound to certain rules. (g Rules which a man likewise cannot name, although he has long followed them.)\n[Some of these rules of thinking are universal, without distinction of objects, that is, rules without which we cannot think at all; and these rules are necessary.]\n(g It is, however, necessary to know these rules if we wish to advance to artificial thinking in [de] the sciences; in common life practice alone is needed. Walking, dancing, speaking, discoursing.)\nA universal doctrine of thought [makes] is [it therefore also] therefore possible*, and from it there follows also a universal doctrine of language, *grammatica universalis*. Thus, following the main arrangement of Latin grammar, French and German are also taught.\n*(s Whether it is necessary is another question. For the common use of the understanding it is presumably not, any more than learning to walk, for which practice suffices.)\nThis universal doctrine of thinking is logic. All thinking takes place through the understanding; logic is therefore the universal doctrine of the understanding. (s Science of the rules of thinking in general.)\nIf, then, you wish to reflect upon your thinking, you must make a beginning with a logic [as a]. (s Natural logic is that which is acquired through the use of the understanding. Logic as science.)\n*The understanding is the faculty of rules. A universal doctrine of the understanding therefore expounds only the necessary rules of thinking, without distinction of the objects, that is, of the matter** about which we think; it expounds, therefore, only the form of thinking in general and the rules without which there can be no thinking whatsoever.\n*(s [The necessary rules of the understanding are universal rules, and the rules of the understanding that are universal in every respect are merely [rules] formal rules, that is, they refer to no determinate object.] The universal rules |\nare necessary either only with a particular aim, namely for the knowledge of certain objects, or with every aim, so that without them thinking in general is impossible.)\n**(g Not that [form] according to which we know objects, but according to which we think at all; and thinking is only a part of knowing.)\nTo choose objects of thought for oneself is contingent; but the rules of thinking in general are necessary.\nThere are therefore rules of thinking which are necessary 1. only with a certain aim (g in relation to a certain object), or 2. with every aim (g form of thinking — rules which consider in knowledge not its content but the mere form of thinking (not of intuiting)). (Only the latter belong to logic.) [These are the rules] without which no understanding can be employed at all.\nNecessary rules must be capable of being discerned independently of all experience, that is, *a priori*, although experience may first supply us with examples of the use of these rules. We have the form of the understanding beforehand (s *a priori*); the objects with which it is occupied, that is, the matter of thinking, are given us by experience.\n(s Logic abstracts from all content of knowledge.)\n(g Rules) must be capable of demonstration; not from empirical psychology.*\n*(g Thereby the limits of logic are determined (*1mo* no doctrine of idealism, *2do* not of the origin of ideas, *3tio* not of prejudices). In this respect philosophy has to follow the lead of almost all the other sciences (natural right, religion, as consisting of theology and morals, each of which has its limits).)\nLogic: science of the universal rules of the use of the understanding in general; therefore not of the pure understanding, nor of its use in particular sciences, |\nL II: |\n[also] not of its learned use, nor of its common use, but of all its use. The examples, however, are |\ntaken from the common use of the understanding, which is itself afterwards corrected by these rules.",[]],"refl:1621":["Philosophy is an absolute unity. We cannot say that someone understands one part of it well and is weak in the rest. The spirit of philosophy must show itself in every part.",[]],"refl:1622":["The grammarians were the first logicians.",[]],"refl:1623":["*Logica est ars cogitandi generalis* [Logic is the general art of thinking].",[]],"refl:1624":["Logic, defined as the universal doctrine of the understanding, might still seem to contain the (g pure) concepts of the understanding of objects (categories) as well; it would then not abstract from all content of thinking. The definition of it [as] as a science which contains merely the formal rules of thinking is therefore the better.",[]],"refl:1625":["We are beginning a science which promises that it will teach [us] to think. You have hitherto (g of your own accord about) thought [about] many (g things), [without] but presumably have not reflected upon your thinking.",[]],"refl:1626":["What I am to discern [*einsehen*] I must know as necessary, and consequently *a priori*. If I am to comprehend [*begreifen*] it, I must also breaks off.",[]],"refl:1627":["General logic concerns merely the form of thinking, not the matter (the object). It abstracts from all content of knowledge. — For this very reason it is no *organon*, but it is nevertheless demonstrated doctrine, and therefore a canon. [It teaches] not according to what rules we think, but according to what rules we ought to think. [We ca] Not psychology. Yet we do derive these rules from the form of thinking.",[]],"refl:1628":["I have said that everything which happens in the world happens according to rules; the understanding, therefore, also carries on its business according to rules.\n(s Following rules without knowing them *in abstracto*. In walking. *Logica naturalis*.)\nThinking is the business of the understanding.\nBut as the objects are different, there must also be different rules of thinking: for instance, other rules in an object of experience than in an object of mere reason (virtue), other rules of the understanding for outer experience than for inner. Each science has its particular rules.\nBut there must also be one [science] which precedes all the sciences and contains the rules of thinking in general. Here we must abstract from every difference of objects,\nfrom all matter of knowledge.\nSuch a science will therefore bring (g merely) the form of thinking under rules: for instance, what is a concept? what is a distinct concept? what is a judgment, a determination? These rules are necessary (s without which there can be no thinking at all; they therefore abstract from the difference of objects) and essential [rules] of thinking in general. Logic. It is the propaedeutic of all the sciences and the critique of sound understanding. It is not, however, grounded on empirical (s psychological) principles; otherwise it could not contain necessary rules for every understanding. It requires no investigation of the origin of concepts; it treats of concepts in general, not of how they are produced in us but of what they are. Empirical psychology does not lie at its basis.\nIts use is the critique* of the understanding in general (s *ars cogitandi*) (s but not merely critique). It differs, however, from the critique of taste in that it [has a canon at its basis] is at the same time doctrine, that is, contains principles *a priori* which admit of demonstration, because they are necessary (g taste not). For the understanding proceeds according to rules; but it also itself determines in general what is required in order that something be a rule.\n*(s Since the (s act of) judging precedes the rules. Doctrine: since the rules must precede the (s act of) judging.)\n(s Critique (g art of judging according to subjective principles or objective ones) or doctrine; the latter is canon or organon.)\n(s Critique is opposed to doctrine. It is either *ars* or [*canon*] *doctrina diiudicationis* (g the former according to a norm, the latter according to a canon). The understanding requires principles, taste requires models, whose archetype is in us.\nCritical art or science; the latter is doctrine, not art.)\n(s *Ars critica*, not *cogitandi*. Doctrine admits of *scientia*.)\n(s The canon of doctrine is opposed to the *exemplar* (Polyclitus' rule in art).)\n*It is no organon. Through it we are not in a position to invent modes of knowledge as regards their content, because it abstracts from all matter, that is, from the objects. By mere grammar we cannot learn a language.\n*(g A canon is a demonstrated [principle of critique] doctrine. (s which serves for critique.))\nIt is only a canon of judging [of a criteri], not an instrument of invention. It teaches us to make knowledge agree not with the object but with the universal laws of thinking in general; it teaches only that the understanding in thinking agree with itself and with its universal rules.\nIt does not investigate how the understanding thinks and what takes place, but (g teaches) what ought to take place, that is, how it ought to think. The former is done by psychology or anthropology, and their observations are contingent. The latter is necessary and *a priori*.\n(s The former, for instance the doctrine of prejudices and errors, must go into the scholia.)\n*Logica est scientia regularum generalium vsus intellectus* [Logic is the science of the universal rules of the use of the understanding].",[]],"refl:1629":["Denomination. *Logica*. Science of reason. *Scientia rationalis*, not (s merely) as regards its form, but as regards its object: reason itself. (g *Empiricarum regularum non datur scientia* [Of empirical rules there is no science].) As regards content:\n1. Logic of universal (g human) reason: *logica* [*sensus communis*] *universalis*.\n2. Logic of the sciences. General logic concerns both.\nAs regards method:\n1. Scholastic. This comes first.\n2. Popular (s — the former first).\n(s not psychology: how we think, but how we ought to think.)\n(s As regards origin [and the]: *logica naturalis* as a natural disposition, or *artificialis* as science. The former is wrongly entitled logic; for it is no knowing, but merely routine and practice.)\n[*Logica*. 1. *pura*. 2. *applicata*. The former] General logic is merely canon and so far (s doctrine) analytic. When it is employed as organon it is entitled dialectic (s the art of propounding something in conformity with the form of truth). Logic of truth and of illusion. Analytic, as general logic, abstracts from every object and considers only the form of thinking. [But truth consists only] Agreement with the laws of the understanding is the formal element in truth.\nBut when this canon is employed as organon, and the formal principles are employed without the material [element] (of the object) [gebraucht] in judging objects, the outcome is mere illusion. For the material element in truth consists in agreement with the object, just as the formal element consists in the agreement of the understanding with itself.\n(s a part of philosophy)\nLogic of (s common) sound / Logic of speculative\n}\nunderstanding.\n(s Faculty of rules *in abstracto*.)\n(s To orient oneself when one has become too abstract; otherwise the concept is empty.)\n*Logica naturalis*, *Logica artificialis*.\n(s Doctrine of elements)\n*theoretica*\n*vel*\n*practica*: (g general) doctrine of method.\n*diiudicatio*\n*exsecutio*.\n(g art of connecting the manifold in a system.)\n[*Logica sceptica*] *Historia Logices*. Aristotle: merely objective laws, form of reason. Epicurus: logic of the investigation of nature. Locke: origin of concepts. Wolff and Leibniz: demonstrative and distinct. Moderns: not pure but mixed logic, with psychology. Crusius: fanatical logic. Lambert: an organon of mathematics and physics.\n(s dialecticians — sceptics. Verulam. *Organon*. Descartes: *de Methodo*. Malebranche.)\nUtility of logic. Not as an art of invention. Not as preparation for the sciences as regards their content, but for criticism according to principles in respect of form.\nWhen we have already abstracted certain rules from practice, we can easily express them universally; but without acquaintance [with the] *in concreto* they are unintelligible and [not to be proved] useless.\nA universal heuristic is impossible. The organon of the sciences comes after them.\nUtility of logic. Men philosophised before logic, and through logic matters have not become much better. Mathematics.\n(g It is a doctrine and not merely a critique of knowledge or of the faculty of knowledge. Yet it is not the organon of the sciences, but that of the critique of knowledge.)\n(s It is doctrine, not critique, but serves more for critique than for doctrine in sciences which have their object; for then it would be organon.)\n(s *Logica sensus communis* — *Logica scientifica*.)\n(s Mysticism, inner magic, theurgy, theosophy. Plato: the father of all mystics. Aristotle: the scholastic. Sceptics. Neoplatonists. Carneades and mysticism. Eclectics. Alchemy.)\n(s Pure logic is wholly analytic, that is, logic of truth. For it contains merely the rules *a priori* of the form of thinking, and these can be known only by analysing the acts of our understanding and leaving out whatever concerns the object. Agreement with these rules is the merely logical criterion of truth.)\n(s To represent *in abstracto* the rules which the understanding knows *in concreto* without consciousness (g faculty of rules). Metaphysics is abstract and speculative in the highest degree.)\n(s A mode of knowledge, so far as its acquisition is empirical, is subjectively historical; it is objectively historical when its acquisition can only be empirical.)\n(s Logic is the propaedeutic of all the sciences, but a part of philosophy.)\n(s Artist of reason (*physicus logicus*) or philosopher.)\n(s The ultimate end of human reason does, however, also presuppose the means to it.\nThese consist also in the theoretical use.\nPhilosophy, represented in terms of its end, and not merely as a system of the knowledge of reason from concepts, is the science of the ultimate end of reason.)\n(s Philosophical knowledge. — 1) rational, not historical. 2) philosophical, from concepts, not through construction.)\n(s Philosophy as doctrine of wisdom cannot be learned: for who is wise?\nLearning philosophy yields historical knowledge; philosophising, rational [knowledge], subjectively.\nNot to learn philosophy, but to learn to philosophise.)\n(s Doctrine of wisdom — teacher of wisdom or sage.\nPhilosophy: 1. as doctrine of skill; 2. as doctrine of wisdom. (g The ancients required that one should not merely speak prudently but also act so.)\nThe philosopher is the one versed in the laws of reason: either as art or as wisdom.)\n(s *Pharus intellectus*. *Medicina mentis et corporis*.)\n(s General logic has no practical part; that would be dialectic.)\n(s Division: doctrine of elements and doctrine of method. The latter is the doctrine of the form of a science in general. It does not show where [the science] is to be employed.)\n(s *Bivium* and *quadrivium*. *Septem artes*. Rabanus Maurus.\nJodocus. *octo partes*: *Nomen*, *Pronomen*, *Verbum*, *Participium*, [*Praepos.*] *adverbium*, *Praepositio*, *Conjunctio*, *Interjectio*.)\n(s Strange that logic can give no universally sufficient criterion of truth.)",[]],"refl:1630":["/\n*in abstracto* from concepts.",[]],"refl:1631":["/\nMathematical [truths] can to some extent be accepted on faith.",[]],"refl:1632":["§ 5. This definition places the specific mark of philosophy in its knowing its truths without faith. We must, however, know by faith much historical knowledge that lies at the basis of philosophy. For instance, we must believe that what Römer, and other philosophers after him, say about the phenomena of Jupiter's satellites is true, in order to infer from it something about the nature of light. One philosopher makes *observationes* and experiments; another believes them and draws a variety of consequences from them.\nWe shall therefore prefer to retain the definition which runs: *Philosophia est scientia, qvae circa rationes rerum versatur* [Philosophy is the science which is concerned with the grounds of things]. We must, however, distinguish philosophical knowledge from acquaintance with philosophy. The former is a *habitus rationes rerum cognoscendi* [a disposition to know the grounds of things] |\nand is acquired *meditando*, *examinando* and *perscrutando*; it consists more in the method of using one's reason than in filling the memory with many propositions already worked out. The *cognitio Philosophiae*, on the other hand, is acquaintance with what has been propounded in the writings of philosophers concerning the grounds of things. This kind of philosophy has its seat in the memory. One reads definitions, learns to understand them by means of the appended examples and scholia, impresses them on the memory, and in the same way makes oneself familiar with the connection of propositions in the demonstration of a theorem, in order to give it back faithfully. When such people have forgotten the words of the definition, they have also lost the ability to frame it anew. They are faithful parrots of great men, and say: we ponder, we inquire, etc. etc. etc.",[]],"refl:1633":["*Denominatio*. Various definitions. The author has needlessly departed from the customary definition. Faith is not opposed to philosophy. Philosophy rests, just as much as faith does, upon incontrovertible grounds, namely upon incontrovertible historical knowledge and upon inner experience.\nFor the division of philosophy see p. Mscr. 1.",[]],"refl:1634":["The first principal division of philosophy is 1. that in which the grounds are known through a consideration abstracted from the senses and from the distinct images of the |\nimagination, and so through the *intellectus purus*; 2. that which derives the grounds immediately by means of the comparison of sensible representations. The first is philosophy in the proper sense; the second is mathematics. These definitions determine the specific mark of both sciences. Now we can compare sensible representations with one another for various purposes. But as regards grounds, we can institute no comparison that would lead [darauf führen solte] immediately through the senses to the knowledge of the ground, save in so far as it concerns magnitude. Consequently mathematics is the only science in which a distinct insight into grounds depends immediately upon the senses, or upon their deputy, the imagination. [e.g. When I feel that fire burns.] For the knowledge of magnitude is nothing other than breaks off.\n(g Mathematics is not treated as a particular science [here] because it is too large.)\nIt might perhaps be said that philosophy is not sufficiently distinguished from mathematics by its definition, since mathematics also considers the grounds of [grounds] things, namely in so far as their magnitude is considered. Ought we not, then, to say that the mathematician has a philosophical knowledge of the relations of magnitudes? Does he not prove? Here we must observe that in mathematics the ground, and [its] the dependence of the relations upon this ground, are derived and known through a series of sensible comparisons, or of representations which can all be made sensible. For instance, what relation all three angles in a triangle bear to [the angles] two right angles, which can be contained in a semicircle. This, together with its ground, can be discerned by drawing on the board, and so making sensible, all the concepts leading to it. Philosophy, on the other hand, requires an insight into grounds such that their connection with what is derived from them is comprehended not through an immediate sensible series of representations, and consequently *per intellectum purum*. If, for instance, I ask why [according to the] the moon is eclipsed at certain times, I can discern this only if I have presupposed that a body which has no light of its own, but is exposed to another light, can be darkened only if something either covers it or prevents the light from falling upon it, etc. etc. These ideas cannot be made wholly sensible. But if I ask about magnitude, namely how long the eclipse lasts, I can exhibit everything on the board from the given relations of magnitude, which I express by numbers. Mathematical knowledge is therefore to be distinguished from philosophical. A philosophical knowledge of magnitudes and their relations is something quite different. [For instance, if I ask why why a figure cannot have [more] (g fewer) than 3 sides] I know for certain, for instance, that the circle is a figure which with its circumference encloses the greatest space that can be enclosed with this circumference. (g Geometry gives the proof.) But suppose I ask why the figure which encloses the greatest space etc. etc. must be so constituted as to be similar to itself throughout. That would be a philosophical question; and the same holds of perpendicular lines. A philosophical knowledge of geometrical and arithmetical problems would be excellent; it would pave the way to the art of invention. But it is very difficult. For instance, geometry proves that if from a point I draw to a line another line such that it makes equal angles throughout, this is the shortest of all possible [lines]; but why must one hit upon just this determination in order to draw the shortest? Only a sublime philosophy can show that.\nFrom this we can also see why mathematical knowledge is more certain than philosophical.\n*Mathesis pura* requires only the principle of contradiction; *applicata* sometimes requires that of sufficient ground.\n*Divisio Philosophiae in Theoreticam et practicam*.",[]],"refl:1635":["3. /\nL 3':\nHistory of philosophy in general.\n(s Science begins in the civilised condition, either from legislation or from the priestly order.)\n(s Beginning from common understanding; the boundary between it and science is hard to draw.)\n(s Need gives the occasion for the beginning of [scie] inquiry. In the crude condition, the universal need of the spirit in respect of the higher powers.)\n( Ancient division into logic, physics and ethics.)\nWe have no remains of the philosophy of the ancient [Greek] peoples prior to the Greeks. Of the Jews it is believed that they began to philosophise after the Babylonian captivity, although we have no remains of them, except Maimonides, who learned among the Saracens, *seculo XIII*. Their secret philosophy was the Cabbala.\nEgyptians: their philosophy is unknown.\nChaldeans. Zoroaster. *incertus*. *inde* to the\nPersians. Zerdusht. Oromazes and Arimanius. Magi.\n(s Astrology produced astronomy, demonology produced theology (g theogony). Superstition. Priests.)\nIndians. Brachmans, (g Gymnosophists, *hodie*: Brahmins,) Calanus. Metempsychosis. Stoic principles.\nChinese. Confucius. 550 years before Christ.\nGreeks*. The time before the sects. The Seven Sages. The foremost: Solon; the most learned:\nThales: *Sectae Jonicae autor*.**\nPythagoras: *secta Italica*. *Acusmatici*. *Acroamatici*. Metempsychosis.\nDemocritus. Atoms.\nEpicurus: *Ea voluptas, qvae nullam habet annexam molestiam, amplectenda* [That pleasure is to be embraced which has no discomfort attached].\nPyrrho: *Sophistas aggressus est*.\n[Thales] Socrates***:\nPlato: *secta Academica*.\nAristotle, *Peripatetica*. Best books: Logic. Rhetoric. Natural history.\nAntisthenes: *Cynica*.\nZeno: *Stoica*.\nRomans.\nLucretius.\nCicero. Pliny the Younger. Epictetus. Plotinus. Boethius.\nArabs: Avicenna and Averroes.\nScholasticism, Reformation.\nReformers: Bacon. Descartes. Leibniz. Those of the present must be divided into classes.\nHistory of logic.\nAristotle (s Dialectic, Canonic).\nAcademics, the new sect: Arcesilaus, Xenocrates doubted, Sextus Empiricus. Huet. Vayer. Bayle.\n(s Censors. Sceptics. Misology. Pyrrho.)\n(s *philodoxi vel misologi*.)\nScholastics. Ramus, his 2 chapters: *Inventio*. *Judicium*: *secunda Petri*. Malebranche. Locke. Tschirnhaus. [*Logica est vel Critica vel technica vel heuristica*]\n(s The edifice of the common use of reason: real grammar.)\n(s the technical, the dialectical (g *eristica*), the heuristic (*canonica*).)\nL 3:\n*(s Difference between the common use of reason and the speculative, that is, science.\nWhere does knowledge by reason begin to become philosophy? The universal through concepts *in abstracto*. Among the Greeks mathematics was older.)\nL 3':\n**(s As regards content: *Physici* and *theologi*.\n1)\nIonian school. Thales and Anaxagoras. Pythagoras.\nAnaximander. Anaximenes. Pherecydes.\n2)\n*Eleatici*. Xenophanes. Parmenides. Zeno the Eleatic.\nHeraclitus. Empedocles, Archytas.\nFirst in prose; Orpheus in verse.\n*Mathematici*: all the Italians.\n*Physici*: Democritus.\n*Theologi*: Anaxagoras.\n*Ethici*: Socrates.\n*Politicus*: Pythagoras.)\n***(s Anaxagoras. Socrates.\nAcademy. Plato.\nPeripatetics. Lyceum. Aristotle.\n(s Stoa Poecile.) Porch. Stoics (s Stoa.)\n(s Cynics. Cynosarges.) Gardens. Epicurus. (g Leucippus. Democritus.) (s Alexandrian academy. Museum.)\nAs regards form: Dogmatists, Sceptics: (g Sextus Empiricus,) the second Academy.\n1.\nAcademics:\nSpeusippus,\nArcesilaus,\nCarneades.\n2.\nAristotelians: Theophrastus,\nDemetrius of Phalerum.\n3.\nStoics: Zeno,\nCleanthes,\nChrysippus.)",[]],"refl:1636":["3. /\nL 3':\nDivision of the [merely practical] philosophical schools. (s Crusius, Wolff: dogmatic.) (s Critical: Locke.)\n1.\nmerely practical: Cynics, Socrates, Stoics.\n2.\ntheoretical:\na. dogmatic (s they treated either the matter or the dialectical form),\nb. sceptical.\nThe dogmatists:\nα. *ex principiis sensitivis*: Epicurus;\nβ. *ex principiis rationalibus vel*\n( *puris vel*\n*mixtis* )\n*ortus singularis vel*\n*e communi cum sensitivis principiis empiricis*.\n(s *Aristoteles principia intellectualia discursiva, non intuitiva statuit* [Aristotle laid down intellectual principles that are discursive, not intuitive].)\n(s One would suppose that Aristotle, who appeals to the senses, grounds himself on experiments.)\n*Ex principiis pure rationalibus post scholam Ionicam sub Anaxagora, Eleaticam sub Melisso et parmenide, Italicam sub Pythagora. Magnus defensor: Plato. Intelligibile oppositum sensibili. Illud: Numerus, Idea, Forma. Illud: omnium principium optimum, et eius intuitus: sapientia.* [From purely rational principles, after the Ionian school under Anaxagoras, the Eleatic under Melissus and Parmenides, the Italian under Pythagoras. Great defender: Plato. The intelligible opposed to the sensible. The former: number, idea, form. The former: the best principle of all things, and the intuition of it: wisdom.]\n*Qvi intellectualia cum sensitivis e communi principio duxit* [He who derived the intellectual together with the sensitive from a common principle]: Aristotle.\nSceptics. Zetetics. Academics.\nRomans. The Scholastics, dogmatists to a man.\nL 3:\nThe ancients lacked universities. (g A single teacher kept a school.) The religion of the ancients gave occasion to the fine arts (g to law and eloquence), because it was sensuous. The religion of the |\nmoderns gave occasion to subtle, abstract speculations, because it was spiritual, and to erudition in history and languages.\nIn the universities alone theologians and subtleties held sway. Scholastics. Aristotle. (g End of all taste and sound reason. Literature.) The philosophers who philosophised free from the yoke of the schools introduced the eclectic manner. Until at last, when outside the schools and learned societies the public sets the tone, a universal shallowness gains the upper hand.",[]],"refl:1637":["Philosophy [arose from need] first appeared in the civilised condition, among those who have leisure, among priests. It was theology, pneumatology and morals; cosmogony, magic.\nAmong the Egyptians. Among the Jews it was not needed. Among the Greeks and Chinese [it was] separate; among the latter it was a service of the government, and therefore morals.",[]],"refl:1638":["Sophists. Dialecticians.\nSceptics.\nDogmatists.\nPractical: Socrates (g and the three sects of the moralists: Cynics, Epicureans and Stoics.)\nTheoretical: Epicurus, Plato, Aristotle.",[]],"refl:1639":["/\nThe ancient Greeks (g small states) (g Chinese) philosophised for legislation and the state; likewise for eloquence, the arts, and for the court of law in lawsuits. Morals. Mathematics. The Jews, Egyptians and Persians [philosophised] for religion, following either their sacred books or traditions. Mythology (theogony). Rich in images. Figurative.\nThe Greeks [had] no universities. The Greeks propounded philosophy not as instruction in the schools but as a mere cultivation of reason. Hence, since they had no universities, their philosophy lacked disciplinary exactness and method. Dialectic, to the advantage of advocates and orators. No observations.\n(g No care on the part of the ancients in observing.)",[]],"refl:1640":["Lack of observations of outer and inner sense. They all philosophised from [hypot] ideas of reason, or hypotheses of reason, in that they took things of nature as their basis.",[]],"refl:1641":["Modern physics: natural right, experiments.\nWolff: order in thinking. Distinctness. Definitions.\nLocke: origin of human concepts.\nCrusius: the law of human reason immediate, instinct.\nIn logic, metaphysics and morals not very much further than the ancients. Physics.",[]],"refl:1642":["/\nThe best philosophy of the ancients was law.\nMathematical\nempirical.*\nMystical. (g Scholastic.)\nWolff\nLocke\nCrusius\n*(g Critical. Analytic.)",[]],"refl:1643":["Epicurus maintained the essential difference between the sensitive and the intellectual, but taught that the sensitive alone can contain truth and distinctness and become a science.\nPlato maintained the essential difference between the sensitive and the intellectual, but taught that only the intellectual yields science.\nAristotle maintained that there is no essential difference between the two, that both arise from the senses, and that reason is only a particular use of the senses, whereby the sensitive [representations] might become science.\nSceptics. Romans.\nBarbarians. Modern times.",[]],"refl:1644":["All philosophy is either speculative or merely practical. Of the latter kind were Antisthenes and Socrates: the former [a philosophy] of sensuous simplicity, the latter of wise simplicity. The former sought the man of nature; the latter sought the cultivated man in accordance with his essential determinations. Diogenes erred in the execution, in that he sought nature in those objects to which it has bound him most essentially, so as to permit nothing beyond this measure; his [philosophy] was therefore negative. Rousseau seeks out the natural beneath the artificial, and seeks the greatest |\nperfection in the civilised condition, without contradicting nature, and the means of attaining it; but he errs in holding this to be possible. Speculative [philosophy] can be pursued dogmatically or sceptically; the former, sensitively or intellectually.",[]],"refl:1645":["Philodoxy distinguished from philosophy.",[]],"refl:1646":["The Greeks had no universities, that is, societies of scholars who divided the sciences among themselves. They had no special theologians, but priests and philosophers. These exercised an influence on the commonwealth, on the mode of thought of the people and on government. As teachers they at the same time set an example.",[]],"refl:1647":["*Septem artes*: grammar, rhetoric, dialectic (s *Trivium*); arithmetic, geometry, astronomy, music (s *Qvadrivium*). (s Rabanus Maurus. Charlemagne.)",[]],"refl:1648":["Pyrrhonists: those who accept all manner of things because they seem so to them. The Academics reject with certainty. To Arcesilaus suspensions [of judgment] are |\ngoods, assertions evils; the equality of two opposed proofs is the chief argument. The wise man may [the assen] not opine: *ataraxia*. — Carneades made of them the new Academy. The Academics did not dispute the reality of our knowledge, but its universal validity.",[]],"refl:1649":["Historical knowledge is opposed to rational. Historical knowledge is either empirical, through experience, or, while objectively rational, subjectively accepted through imitation.",[]],"refl:1650":["All [modes of knowledge] disciplines belong either to art or to science. The former are those [where the rules of skill only from single actions] in which the single actions precede the instruction; the latter, those in which the instruction contains the ground of the single actions [constitute of knowledge].\nAll sciences are (g as regards form) either historical or rational. The latter are either (g mathematical or philosophical), of magnitude or of quality.\nFor arts and imitative sciences docility is required; for philosophy, genius.",[]],"refl:1651":["'To learn a philosophy' signifies [learning] subjective philosophy, that is, what has actually been thought; but in that case one cannot judge it. |\n'To learn to philosophise' is objective: how one ought to think, that is, the rules of the correct use of reason. The philosophical spirit is therefore distinct from the spirit of a philosophy, and consists in the method of reason. The philosophical spirit is accordingly original and therefore genius (for the original is not what is merely unique, but what is no *ectypon*).\nMathematics can be learned. It invents its knowledge from distinct ideas. (g devises its data for itself.) Philosophy [obtains its knowledge] through the analysis of confused [ideas]. (g Must seek them.)\nThe method of the former is synthetic, that of the latter analytic.",[]],"refl:1652":["2'.\nL 2:\nWe might distinguish world-science [*Weltwissenschaft*] and world-wisdom [*Weltweisheit*]. The first is erudition; the second is knowledge of the vocation [*Bestimmung*] of man* as regards understanding and will. Metaphysics and morals. On this view Hume is the greatest philosopher. (g In other words, philosophical science (g erudition) and philosophical wisdom.) No one asks after wisdom, because it greatly narrows science, which is an instrument of vanity.**\n*(g Wisdom is the relation [of a mode of knowledge] to the essential ends of humanity. Hence the vocation of man as regards understanding and will. The student of nature is no philosopher.)\nL 2'.\n**(s We cannot learn philosophy, but we can learn to philosophise. He is not a philosopher who understands some philosophy or other (|\nanother's attempt at philosophising). The philosopher is always original. Just as little [can one learn] the fine sciences.)\n(s The philosopher is either an artist of reason or the one versed in the laws of human reason. There is one way of expounding philosophy whose effect is felt at once in the school, but all its influence ceases in the mature use of one's reason. The other has no distinctive effect in the school, but all the more in life. The former requires mechanism; the latter, cultivation of genius.)",[]],"refl:1653":["The object of philosophical knowledge: quality. [Its] form is rational, not historical. [Its] degree is scientific.\n(s Rational form (g not historical) [vom gemeinen Verstande unterschieden]. Not matter.\nMatter: all objects.\nDegree: universal concepts *in abstracto*, not the common understanding.)\nPhilosophy is the rational science of the qualities of things.\n1. Science; 2. qualities; 3. reason.\na) [It is] distinguished from common knowledge, whose rules are confirmed in use by experience and go no further. b) bricht ab.",[]],"refl:1654":["By original genius [*Originalgeist*] we understand [nicht allein die Fähigkeit] the skill which cannot be acquired through imitation. Not every |\noriginal genius is characteristic. Genius [*Geist*] belongs to the invention of that which has an ideal as its ground; this holds also in the case of reason.",[]],"refl:1655":["Philosophical knowledge is distinguished: 1. from historical knowledge, as being knowledge through reason; 2. from common rational knowledge, as being speculative, that is, derived from universal concepts and rules; 3. from mathematics, as being derived from concepts, not from construction. In all three the objects are undetermined.",[]],"refl:1656":["Philosophy is the *organon* of wisdom. It is therefore concerned with the quality and the measure, or the external relation, of knowledge, in so far as knowledge accords with our true ends. It determines the use of the understanding *in abstracto* in respect of nature, etc.; but therein it seeks only the means to its own end. One is not a philosopher because one is an investigator of nature (an artist of reason [*Vernunftkünstler*]), but because one learns the right use of one's reason in respect of our present and future ends.",[]],"refl:1657":["Philosophical is: universal knowledge from concepts.\nMathematical is: universal knowledge from the construction of concepts.\nPhilosophical knowledge is empirical or rational according as the concepts are or are not abstracted from experience and their use is or is not a law of experience. The former [i.e. the empirical modes of knowledge] furnish no truly universal rules, but only rules of induction.",[]],"refl:1658":["/\nPhilosophy [*Weltweisheit*] which is known through belief. Citation. Authority.\nSkill. Philosophical knowledge.\nPhilosophy: *quid* [what]. Wit.\nMathematics: *quoties* [how many times]. Imagination.",[]],"refl:1659":["Parts of philosophy [*Weltweisheit*]: understanding. Feeling. Desire.",[]],"refl:1660":["2'.\nL 2:\nPhilosophy is the science of the maxims of reason, speculative as well as practical; |\nL 2': |\nit extends over everything. Modes of knowledge are philosophical in so far as they contain the means to the maxims of reason.",[]],"refl:1661":["Mathematics is a science of the determination of magnitudes *a priori*; it is rightly entitled *doctrina* or *mathesis*.",[]],"refl:1662":["Of philosophy one part belongs to learning [*Gelehrsamkeit*], the other to wisdom. To the former belongs physics. A man learned in philosophy is not, on that account, a philosopher.",[]],"refl:1663":["Philosophy is the legislation of reason; the philosopher often casts aside the subtlest speculation. Laws and rules of practice: genius stands under laws, and so likewise does skill. Skill |\nand wisdom. Subjective laws (g a maxim is a subjective law), in so far as they agree with the objective [laws], are maxims. Laws command categorically, rules hypothetically (under a problematic condition).",[]],"refl:1664":["Mathematics is the highest art of reason [*Vernunftkunst*], philosophy the science of reason [*Vernunftwissenschaft*].",[]],"refl:1665":["One can bring into a nation either a spirit [*Geist*] or a mechanism. The latter in the end produces a barbarous kind of learning.",[]],"refl:1666":["The modes of knowledge which belong to philosophy are distinct from those which belong to mathematics. The former are modes of knowledge from concepts, the latter from their arbitrary construction. Or: philosophical are the modes of knowledge from concepts *in abstracto*, mathematical those from construction *in concreto*.",[]],"refl:1667":["Philosophy, the path to wisdom.\nOf the probabilism of pure reason; e.g. in [regard to] beings of a wholly different kind from those of the [W] world of sense.\nExplanation of such an illusory plausibility [*Scheinbarkeit*].\nThe impossibility of the objects of pure reason cannot be proved synthetically; hence things are posited *probabiliter* [as probable]. This false probability is to be restricted by *retorsion*. So long as there is thinking in the world, reason will always transgress the limits of experience with its conjectures, [aber die Philosophie muß dagegen wohl gewafne] and there will be no possibility of abolishing this presumption, which lies in nature itself. But philosophy must be on its guard against it, in order to prevent reason from confusing the highest ends, to the detriment of sound reason.\nThe art of reason and the legislation of reason are very different in worth. In the first, mathematics is foremost; in the second there is much that is philosophical which belongs to the skill of the understanding.",[]],"refl:1668":["Philosophy is the science of the final end of human reason, and therewith of all ends, in so far as they constitute a system in our faculty of knowledge.",[]],"refl:1669":["/\nNamely, how one can attain to a certain mode of knowledge and know its marks. [Man muß aus behutsamkeit in der Ausübung immer |\nlieber voraussetzen, daß ein satz bloß warscheinlich sey.] A certain [proposition] convinces all men. The *logica probabilium* is a still more necessary part of practical logic [*Vernunftlehre*], e.g. in the estimation of historical credibility. Meanwhile one makes shift by proceeding as if one could know the matter with certainty, that is, by *approximation*.",[]],"refl:1670":["§. 7. In all sciences the theoretical part must precede the practical. One must first know how one ought to speak before one speaks; one must learn to think correctly before one occupies oneself with fruitless thinking. In mathematics alone can applied [*ausübende*] logic precede doctrinal [*lehrende*] logic. For there one has a sure guide to reflection: our senses [kön] or their *vicaria*, the *imaginatio*, can readily convict us of errors in our way of thinking. We can afterwards abstract a logic from it, as Tschirnhaus did. [verte]",[]],"refl:1671":["Zum Schluss von L §. 7: »logica — docens«:\ncontemplative.",[]],"refl:1672":["/\n§. 8. A science is always defined in its greatest perfection, because this is the point of view towards which all directions of our endeavours ought to converge, like the radii of a circle. So it is with logic. Yet one must almost concede that the first commendation does not hold good. One must already have thought before one can properly grasp the rules of correct thinking and use them with profit. One must at once know how to apply them to examples. That is why it is useless in schools. One must be able to walk well before one learns to dance, and he will learn orthography badly who has never yet written. Mathematics ought therefore rightly to come first. In it one cannot err, because one is well guided. Thereafter one always has a pattern for the rules of the correct way of thinking.\n2. *intellectum et rationem*: the higher and the lower faculty of the soul. It teaches [us] to ascend from sensible impressions to universal concepts, to distinguish the distinct representations of reason from the obscure concepts of the imagination, [and] not to take the sensuous sensation [der] (g of individual) things for the sensation of their causes. [Nicht] These are the improvements which it makes in respect of the boundaries of the lower and the higher powers. What it accomplishes in respect of the higher [powers] themselves will be seen from practice.",[]],"refl:1673":["/\nLogic treats either of a science which can be certain, or of one which can be probable, or of one which can know [only] problematically.\nThe *logica theoretica* shows the conditions under which a perfect knowledge is possible, and the *practica* how it is to be attained.",[]],"refl:1674":["It is not the lack of logical rules that occasions common errors, but the lack of attention.",[]],"refl:1675":["Analytic (g belongs to doctrine; dialectic to critique). Moments of understanding and reason (undetermined in respect of objects).\nAs critique, logic serves through formulae and terminologies. Grammar. Analysis. (g theoretical.)\nAs an *organon* it must be obtained through the *analysis* of the sciences. (g practical.) These precede logic.\nOne can make a mode of knowledge practical, but one cannot teach the *praxis*, because this would in turn require rules, the application of which would after all have to be expected from sound understanding.",[]],"refl:1676":["*repraesentatio est determinatio mentis* (g *interna*), *quatenus ad res quasdam ab ipsa* (g *nempe repraesentatione*) *diversas refertur*. It is that|\ndetermination of the soul which refers to other things. I call it referring when its constitution conforms to the constitution of the external things, *sive si rebus externis conformis est*.\nThe author [stellet sich vor] claims that the representation of a thing which is to be found in the soul has just such a likeness to the thing represented as a painting has to the object depicted. I maintain, however, that this is false, and prove it thus. When I see a house, there is, on this [vorst] view, a picture of the house in my soul which is like the house represented. Now since like things differ only in magnitude, there is depicted in my soul an entirely tiny little house, which, however small it may be, must yet occupy a space; and this is impossible. Similarly, when I feel the trembling of the air, the sensation of which I entitle sound: can I well say that in the [Substanz] interior of my soul there likewise occurs such a trembling? What is there in it to tremble? We can prove the same through experiences. When someone tastes something sour, can he well say that his representation depicts to him pointed and boat-shaped particles of salt which irritate his nerves of taste? And yet one sees through a microscope that they really are so constituted. etc.\nWhat, then, is there in the representation that agrees with the things represented? The representation, because it borrows its ground from the thing represented, agrees with it in this, that it is composed of its partial concepts in the same manner as the whole thing represented is composed of its parts. For example, one can say [eine] that the notes of a piece of music are a representation of the harmonious combination of the tones; not as if a note were like a tone, but because the notes stand in the same combination with one another as the tones themselves. When, however, the soul attends to itself, it does seem to observe that a representation within it appears to it just as when it sees a painting with its eyes. Nor can this be otherwise. A painting is made after the likeness of the object; as the object affects us, so too does the painting affect us, and the same idea, or determination of the soul, that was produced by the painting we can reproduce through the imagination; hence it must [uns allemal] just so [wohl als da] [gegen das object sich verhalten] bricht ab.",[]],"refl:1677":["§. 11.\n*cognitio est complexus repraesentationum clararum*. The notion of representation is more general; for it comprehends under it also the *obscuras*, of which we do not even know that we have them.",[]],"refl:1678":["§. 12. *Objectum repraesentationis est vel externum vel internum*. *Externum*: when one represents to oneself something distinct from the soul, [internum w] and under this even the body falls; or *internum*: when the soul represents itself to itself. (g We can also represent to ourselves our representation itself.) *Repraesentationes distinguuntur vel materialiter vel formaliter*. The *differentia materialis* is [ob sie ob s] that which is taken from the difference of the object represented; thus [repraes] the idea of a *universale est idea abstracti* or *notio*, the idea of an *ens singulare* is an *idea singularis*. *Formaliter* they are distinguished into *obscuras et claras*, *confusas et distinctas*, *completas et incompletas*, *adaequatas et inadaequatas*.\nConsciousness is a [idee] (g clear) representation of one's own idea. This is what makes it possible for the thinking power of the soul to come properly into exercise in its whole magnitude. For since the soul knows what it represents to itself and how it represents it, it can deal with its representations in various ways: compare, separate and combine them, and thereby perform what is entitled re|flection.\nThis the animals cannot do, because they are not conscious of their [representations].",[]],"refl:1679":["To be conscious of a [sache] representation is to know that one has this representation; that is, to distinguish this representation from the others.",[]],"refl:1680":["Consciousness is *sensus internus*. *Conscientia logica distinguitur a conscientia morali*. The heathens called the latter religion.\n*animalia habent sensum externum, non internum*.",[]],"refl:1681":["[Der Autor vermischt d] The author treats of knowledge; we shall treat of the idea in general. *divisio idearum ratione formalitatis*.\nAn obscure idea is one of which we are not conscious, or which we distinguish neither from ourselves nor from other things. We therefore do not even know that we have it; we must infer it. Either a total idea is obscure, or the partial idea is obscure. The total idea [is obscure] in sleep, in want of *attention*, when the *organum sensus* is impaired, e.g. in taste. The partial idea: when the total idea is clear. I hear a confused clamour; this is clear, but not the idea of any particular voice. I taste a mixture of spice, sugar, vinegar, salt, oil.\n*idea* [vel clara] *vel confusa vel distincta*. The former: when I have in the object represented no clear *ideam partialem*, e.g. the Milky Way; when I name sympathy. *distincta*: I come nearer to a fish-market.\n*completa, incompleta, adaequata, inadaequata* come later.\nI point to and see from afar something dark.\nThe obscure concept which men have of duty and virtue.",[]],"refl:1682":["To represent, to know, to think.",[]],"refl:1683":["(s Sensation) (s *relata ad obiectum*) (s *obiectivum*) (g *intuitus*)\n*repraesentatio*, *perceptio*, *cognitio*, *conceptus*,\n(s to represent something) (s to represent something to oneself) (s *conscius*) (s *cogitatio*,)\n(s *conceptus* (g *non*) *empiricus*)\n*notio*,\n(s *idea**)\n(s Archetypal idea [*Musteridee*], *dessein*\n*cognitio exemplaris* is directed to a purpose.)\n*(s we have no idea of plants and of the whole of created things.)",[]],"refl:1684":["The single representation which contains the ground of rules in respect of the manifold (friendship).\nThe idea of a book can be good, even though the book is full of faults. Hobbes. To search out the idea. Lost books: when no one finds out the idea. It cannot be found by patching together. It is a unity, and the manifold is its consequences. The |\nparts must have their explanation from the idea. Our *conceptus* refer to ideas. The images which serve as the original, e.g. the plan of a fortress, are not, properly speaking, ideas.\nThe objective idea is distinct from the subjective; the former is the archetype in God.",[]],"refl:1685":["Zum Anfang von L §. 11 »Die Erkenntnis«:\nwhereby we distinguish what it is.\nis an objective representation; the subjective is sensation. Knowledge contains concept, judgment and inference.",[]],"refl:1686":["(g that of which I am conscious: *Cognitio*. [repraesentatio referirt auf obiect])\n*perceptio* is [noch] subjective.",[]],"refl:1687":["The matter of knowledge we obtain through the senses, the form of appearance through understanding, the form of the concept through reason.",[]],"refl:1688":["Am Schluss von L §. 13:\nClearness.",[]],"refl:1689":["All clearness is either absolute (inner) or relative (outer) clearness. The former is the consciousness of the representation in itself; the latter, the consciousness of its relation (of identity and diversity) in comparison with others. The first, as well as the second, clearness becomes distinctness through consciousness of the marks of the absolute or relative representations.",[]],"refl:1690":["(s Distinctness requires that the manifold, in so far as it belongs to my whole representation, be clearly known.\nConfusion is often the cause of indistinctness, but not indistinctness itself.)\nClearness is presupposed in logic.\nFrom it, distinctness; [this] is opposed to indistinctness (s not to confusion; cause of both:)\norder and confusion.\nSensible distinctness in intuition (g distinguishing),\ndistinctness of the understanding in concepts (g distinctions).\nIndistinct intuition,\nindistinct concept.\nOrder in space and time (g *perspicuitas*),\norder through positions under concepts.\n(s 1. Subject and predicate; 2. ground and consequence; 3. whole and division. These are determinations of concepts.)",[]],"refl:1691":["Logical and [sinnliche] aesthetic distinctness.",[]],"refl:1692":["Distinctness is the clearness of the manifold in the representation of a thing, either of what is actually contained in the representation, or of what can be contained in it.\nAnalytic distinctness is *perspicuity*: knowledge that sees through [its object].",[]],"refl:1693":["[In] All knowledge has, first, [eine Beziehung] as knowledge, a relation to the object; secondly, to the knowledge of the object. In the first case it is representation; in the second [Bewustseyn] the universal condition is consciousness. Representation is intuition or concept; knowledge through concepts is thinking.",[]],"refl:1694":["Philosophy, in so far as it considers objects, belongs in the series of all other sciences and differs from them only in form: namely, its knowledge is knowledge through reason from concepts.\nPhilosophy, in so far as it considers the knowledge of objects in general, is the highest of all knowledge and the legislation of understanding and reason. Logic.\nAt the beginning. We begin the analytic of the understanding and dissect its acts. First, the distinction between matter (g object) and form (our object is solely the form). Knowledge as regards |\nmatter in general is entitled representation. The [Bedingung] knowledge of form presupposes consciousness (hence in logic we presuppose consciousness). As regards (g its) form [knowledge] is either intuition or concept. Knowledge through concepts is thinking. Logic considers thinking. Clearness and obscurity. The former [divides into] distinctness and indistinctness.\n(s Philosophy [betrachtet hat mit] differs from all other modes of knowledge only in form.)\n(s *Formaliter cognitio est vel intuitus*. (*sensitivi*.)\n— — — — *conceptus*: *discursiva* (*intellectualis*). *Logica forma*. *Cogitatio*.)",[]],"refl:1695":["Representation is either knowledge or sensation. The former [is] intuition or concept.",[]],"refl:1696":["1. Simple modes of knowledge are neither distinct nor confused.\n2. All philosophy is concerned with form, that is, with distinctness through *analysis*, above all in moral matters. Hence it does not, properly speaking, teach us to know something we did not know, but to know better what we already knew. Socrates was the midwife of the thoughts of his hearers.",[]],"refl:1697":["Logic begins with concepts and treats of their use. |\nTheir origin, whether from sensible intuitions or from the understanding, belongs to psychology and transcendental philosophy.",[]],"refl:1698":["*materia*: objects; *forma*: *modus cognoscendi*. Philosophical and common knowledge differ not in matter but in form. From this logic abstracts.",[]],"refl:1699":["Division of knowledge: 1. in respect of consciousness;\n2. in respect of the faculty of knowledge in general: aesthetic or logical;\n3. in respect of the connection of its [modes] with one another.",[]],"refl:1700":["Since logic is a science of the rules of the understanding, it does not begin with intuition, for intuition belongs to the senses; it begins with the concept, for this, as discursive representation, is the first element of thinking.",[]],"refl:1701":["*Conceptus*: representation of an object in general.",[]],"refl:1702":["Knowledge through concepts is thinking. Logic treats of thinking.",[]],"refl:1703":["1. Logical doctrine of elements.\n2. Logical doctrine of method, concerning the rules of a science.",[]],"refl:1704":["Every relation of representation is either solely to the subject, that is, belonging (g to [Gefühl]) to feeling, or also to the object, belonging to knowledge. In the latter case it is intuition or concept; [and intuition is either] empirical intuition, [i.e.] sensation, or pure intuition.",[]],"refl:1705":["4.\nL 4':\nOld division: *Apprehensio simplex*, *Iudicium et ratiocinatio*. Representations are divided into [Empfindung Ansch] subjective or objective. The objective are intuition or concept [die subiective Empfindung]; or also thus: all representations are sensation or knowledge. The former represents merely an alteration in us, the latter an object. Examples from taste and from sight. Knowledge is either intuition or concept. Singular or universal. To apprehend or to think. [Begrif] Both obscure, clear, distinct. Of mere sensations no concepts can be given to one who has not had them. To the contemplative concept intuition must correspond, to the practical, sensation. Sensation must not be taken for knowledge.",[]],"refl:1706":["The science of the perfection of intuitions: aesthetic; of concepts: logic.",[]],"refl:1707":["Propaedeutic concept of logic.\nPropaedeutic concept of aesthetic.",[]],"refl:1708":["Indistinctness [arises] from confusion or from the weakness of the partial representations. Even if order were brought among the latter, the whole would not on that account be distinct. Metaphysics. Logic speaks only of concepts, not of intuitions, and therefore only of marks.",[]],"refl:1709":["1. Distinctness and indistinctness, in so far as they concern thinking (concept) as well as intuition. Distinguishing of the parts in the whole representation. Distinct intuitions, distinct concept.",[]],"refl:1710":["One cannot confuse marks which one does not yet have. Hence modes of knowledge can be indistinct either through lack of marks or through confusion of marks. The former become distinct through addition, the latter through analysis.",[]],"refl:1711":["Knowledge contains intuition, through which objects are given to us, and thinking. Senses, and the faculty of thinking: the understanding. Concepts, judgments, inferences. Understanding, judgment [*Urtheilskraft*] and reason: in each case a different way of bringing intuitions under rules.",[]],"refl:1712":["With what should logic begin? With modes of knowledge in general, not with sensations as subjective representations.",[]],"refl:1713":["Logic: merely the formal part of philosophy. Logic treats of knowledge, not, however, as intuition, but of thinking; not as regards matter, but as regards form. *apprehensio simplex*, *iudicium* — bricht ab?",[]],"refl:1714":["We are indeed conscious of the representation, but not of what is contained in it —.",[]],"refl:1715":["Indistinctness [arises] from confusion or from weakness of consciousness.",[]],"refl:1716":["The ground is either the ground of knowledge or the [existential] ground of determination.",[]],"refl:1717":["Nothing is connected except where the one is *ratio* and the other *rationatum*. If I can know everything in one thing without calling in the aid of another, then they are not connected at all; or: in so far as nothing in my knowledge [of a thing] is determined through another, it is not connected with that other.",[]],"refl:1718":["Fortune and misfortune stand in natural connection with moral qualities.",[]],"refl:1719":["For a theft, original sin is not the sufficient ground.\nA good wit is not the sufficient ground of learning.",[]],"refl:1720":["The ground of possibility: the human imperfection of nature in respect of sin.\nThe ground of truth: that we are actually convinced, from knowledge of our duties, that the actions of men are sin.\nThe ground of existence: the corruption of man through an inherited evil disposition.",[]],"refl:1721":["Does not belong to logic; for logic treats not of modes of knowledge as regards their content (object), but solely of the form of the understanding, that is, of the thinking in them.",[]],"refl:1722":["The schools bring into us [die Ordnung und] the store (matter); the academy, through logic, the order of the modes of knowledge.",[]],"refl:1723":["We must speak here not of the ground of things but of [the ground] of knowledge.",[]],"refl:1724":["Knowledge of the connection of the *rationatum* with the *ratio* is what matters most. E.g. when someone says: sulphur and saltpetre are the ground of thunder; or that a man cannot sink if he is fastened to bladders, the ground of which is the air.",[]],"refl:1725":["Man ought to live virtuously.",[]],"refl:1726":["Reasonable knowledge [*vernünftige Erkentniß*] differs from philosophical in this, that the former cannot carry the grounds back to the most universal concepts of knowledge.\nReason is that capacity of the understanding whereby one is in a position to have distinct insight into the connection of things with their grounds.",[]],"refl:1727":["is opposed to learned knowledge.",[]],"refl:1728":["One knows the constitution of the whole system of the world *historice*. *Mathesis experimentalis*.\nThis knowledge, even if it is merely historical, is not on that account unreasonable. For unreason is not merely a lack of the application of reason, but an [Mangel] application of one's powers contrary to reason; e.g. if I learn history merely from pictures. [Ein]",[]],"refl:1729":["oberer Rand:\nIn knowledge of the understanding we know what things are; in knowledge from reason, what they must be. Our modes of knowledge are, in the former, descriptions or explanations (understanding [*Verstehen*]), in the latter, insights. The former arise from the particular, the latter from the universal. In the former bricht ab.\n(s The form of reason consists in knowledge from grounds, not in knowledge of the grounds (g causes). The knowledge itself must be *a priori*. Insight.)",[]],"refl:1730":["Knowledge of the understanding is opposed to sensible knowledge: 1. as regards origin; 2. as regards form: the form of the understanding is discursive, the sensible form is intuitive.\n(s The perfection of knowledge as regards understanding; 2. perfection as regards reason (both logical). Thereafter aesthetic perfection.)",[]],"refl:1731":["Rational knowledge is opposed to historical: 1. as regards |\n[Ursprunge nach, da iene] form, inasmuch as the latter knows only what something is, the former [knows it] from its grounds; as regards matter, however, it is opposed to empirical [knowledge], since the former is drawn from grounds *a priori*, the latter from experiences.",[]],"refl:1732":["Knowledge through reason, which has for its object the universal constitution of things.\nopposed to historical knowledge.\nReasonable knowledge: [knowledge] whose form is reasonable, whatever the object may be.\nopposed to historical knowledge.\nHere only the historical and sensible form is opposed to the rational. [a poster: a priori] *coordinatio*, *subordinatio*.",[]],"refl:1733":["One can distinguish modes of knowledge that are in conformity with reason, that are reasonable, and that are of reason.\nModes of knowledge of the understanding judge; reasonable ones infer.",[]],"refl:1734":["Historical modes of knowledge as regards matter [entha] are judgments of the understanding [dem Gegenstand d] bricht ab.",[]],"refl:1735":["Knowledge of the understanding is opposed to sensible knowledge. The former is universal (g objective), the latter particular and subjective.\nReasonable knowledge is [opposed] to unreasonable,\nrational knowledge to [sinnlichen] [empirischen] historical; the former knows the particular in the universal.",[]],"refl:1736":["Historical knowledge is that which (g is) given from an object.\nRational knowledge: in so far as the object is not given.",[]],"refl:1737":["Historical knowledge *objective*: history and description; *formaliter*: narration, insight.",[]],"refl:1738":["*cognitio formaliter rationalis est, quae depromitur ex methodi rationis regulis generalibus sive consistit in methodis vel habitu rationis*.",[]],"refl:1739":["6'.\nL 6:\nRational knowledge: knowledge of the (g particular from the) universal, [a priori] necessary.\nNot in so far as the knowledge is given through others or through the object: *formaliter*, *materialiter* historical.\nL 6':\nHe who does not know a particular philosophy from the universal [principles] of the use of reason knows it historically, as it is given. Knowledge of the universal is the understanding: (at most from the particular,) empirical.",[]],"refl:1740":["Faculty of thinking: understanding.\nOf thinking *a priori*: reason.\n(g Thinking —) *a posteriori* — yields historical knowledge.",[]],"refl:1741":["Knowledge (g through thinking) according to rules *a priori*: rational knowledge.\nKnowledge according to *data a posteriori* is historical knowledge.",[]],"refl:1742":["The first peculiar requisite of knowledge of the understanding is universality, and of rational knowledge, the particular [known] from the universal.",[]],"refl:1743":["Historical *subjective* or *objective*.",[]],"refl:1744":["All knowledge, subjectively, as it is actually acquired, is either historical or rational; objectively, the former may very well be rational.",[]],"refl:1745":["Reason is the faculty of knowing necessity. Understanding is knowledge of the universal, judgment [*Urtheilskraft*] of the universal in the particular, reason of the particular from the universal.",[]],"refl:1746":["A universal proposition can be empirical, e.g. all men are mortal.\nA subsumption likewise; but an inference always contains necessity.",[]],"refl:1747":["This definition is indeterminate; something may be perfect as regards magnitude or as regards exactness.\nBeautiful is what pleases. But it pleases through agreement with bricht ab.",[]],"refl:1748":["A sensible [vorstellung] judging of perfection is entitled taste. A mode of knowledge which is known as perfect by the sensible power of judgment is entitled aesthetic. Gothic taste.\n[Wenn et] The agreement of the manifold in a thing towards a common purpose is entitled perfection. When everything agrees in accordance with the rules of the lower faculties of knowledge, it is aesthetically perfect; that is, when the agreement is known solely through the sensible powers, and the pleasure is therefore aroused from the lower powers; e.g. the knowledge of epic poems, of the picturesque in description. If it is discovered through the higher powers, it also gives pleasure, but a distinct pleasure, which is sometimes not so charming.\nWhat taste is. Doctrine of taste.",[]],"refl:1749":["Historical knowledge can be combined with rational without losing anything of beauty (g and philosophical with aesthetic without [losing anything] of thoroughness). *Historia telluris*.\n*Historica praestruatur Philosophicae*. One must read books, gain experience. All knowledge begins with the senses.",[]],"refl:1750":["The definition is indeterminate; see the preceding.\nOne can say that someone has reasonable knowledge of duties without having philosophical knowledge of them.",[]],"refl:1751":["which has been carried back to the first principles.",[]],"refl:1752":["Perfection [is distinct] from pleasure, beauty too; but differently.",[]],"refl:1753":["In every perfection there is to be found, first, a rule or purpose, secondly, an agreement with it. In knowledge we have chiefly two purposes: to instruct ourselves, or to please ourselves, or both together. The first is attained solely through distinct insights\n|\nthe second [durch] in two ways: either through the beauty of the object or through the agreeableness of the exposition. The latter, because it cannot be attained through perfectly distinct representations, is the aesthetic perfection of knowledge.\nDistinct knowledge, without aesthetic aid, solely through the charm of the object [and] through logical perfection, that is, through the correctness [der Abhandlung] and order in which it is considered (g is), has within it a source of pleasure which surpasses everything aesthetic both in magnitude and in duration. Archimedes' pleasure in the bath. Kepler's on discovering a proposition.\nAesthetic is only a means of accustoming people of all too great delicacy to the rigour of proofs and explanations; just as one smears the rim of the vessel with honey for children.\nExample of a thorough science which arouses pleasure: the natural history of the earth. Who invented the science of aesthetic.",[]],"refl:1754":["Certainty, [vol] completeness, correct demonstration.\nSkeleton. Algebra.",[]],"refl:1755":["*perfectio concernit vel objectum cognitionis vel modum cognoscendi*.",[]],"refl:1756":["A mode of knowledge which is logically perfect can become ugly if it is clothed or expounded in such a manner that [dunkele] concepts which arouse the [dunkele] indistinct representation of a displeasure are mixed in with it; e.g. when the considerations in physics concerning generation, the structure of man and his animal economy are described in a filthy manner.\nBut since one ought never to charge anyone with deviation from a rule which he neither set himself nor was obliged to set himself, one cannot ascribe imperfection to a mode of knowledge because it does not observe rules which belong to another kind of knowledge.\nOne ought not to say: that knowledge which has no aesthetic perfections is [nicht] ugly. Geometry, algebra: are they ugly? Whose understanding do they disfigure? Neither that of Leibniz, nor of Maupertuis, nor of d'Alembert. Ugly is that which [wieder regeln verstößt die] rightly arouses displeasure.",[]],"refl:1757":["(g is) or rather ought to be.\nZuL §. 23 »Hässlichkeiten« (Z. 16.):\ncoarse expressions",[]],"refl:1758":["One can say: all perfection is beauty. Hence all knowledge arouses pleasure. The pleasure which is sensible, or which through the images of the imagination becomes almost sensible, is a particular kind of pleasure; this latter passes away. Sometimes the rules of the two kinds of perfection conflict with one another.",[]],"refl:1759":["barbarous words.",[]],"refl:1760":["Zuden letzten beiden Sätzen von L §. 24:\n*quaeritur*: when the rules conflict with one another, that is, the one weakens the other; e.g. metaphysics.",[]],"refl:1761":["Polyhistor. Leibniz. Morhof. Now: Kästner.\nSuch learning is like a garden which contains several hides of land.",[]],"refl:1762":["Objective magnitude.\nextensive: extent.\nintensive: importance.",[]],"refl:1763":["Subjective magnitude.\n(g Extensive.) Distinct. Correct and certain.",[]],"refl:1764":["Knowledge, because it admits of a more and a less, has a magnitude.\nThe magnitude of learned knowledge is distinct from its importance.\nThe magnitude is measured [1.] by the number of objects known, 2. by the degree of their distinctness and completeness.\nThe importance depends on the worth of the object known, e.g. knowledge of the world, of God.",[]],"refl:1765":["Any aesthetic knowledge can be false, provided only that it amuses; e.g. (g Milton:) It is said that God also commanded his angels that they should twice ten and — —\nThe truth or correctness of knowledge is a logical property of it.\n*Veritas est convenientia ideae vel cognitionis cum objecto*.",[]],"refl:1766":["Distinctness concerns what is formal in knowledge; in aesthetic [knowledge] it need only be clear.",[]],"refl:1767":["Certainty is known truth; it has degrees, and these rest on the distinctness of the knowledge.\nThere can be truth in someone's judgments and thoughts, and yet no certainty; e.g. in the opinion of Socrates concerning the immortality of the soul; Seneca on comets.\nSomething is said in company: one who is called as a witness knows it with certainty; another does not know it with certainty.",[]],"refl:1768":["a kind of importance; the consequences lie either in the understanding or in the will.",[]],"refl:1769":["[Practisch kan sie seyn in Ans]\nE.g. the doctrine of the Schoolmen concerning *universalia* is not practical. Practical and useful are distinct. [practisch auch dasjenige was]",[]],"refl:1770":["One must begin with the logical perfections, which can be treated even without aesthetic perfections. The others can follow upon them.\nNo one decorates a house before it has the requisite solidity.",[]],"refl:1771":["We do not eat merely to be satisfied.\nWe do not clothe ourselves merely for warmth.",[]],"refl:1772":["Mathematics has its logical perfections, but also imperfections.\nVery correct, but not distinct.\nNo [mode of knowledge] that is distinct and correct is without use.\nOpposite L § 33 »dass — mehr unvollkommen« (ll. 18f.):\nVery distinct, but trifles.\nOpposite L § 33 »als vollkommen — oder« (ll. 19f.):\n*e.g.* barbarous.\nOpposite L § 33 »hässlich zu — und zu« (ll. 20-22):\nfrigid, *e.g.* common logic.",[]],"refl:1773":["When the rules of beauty and of [aesthetic] logical perfection conflict with one another, the latter keeps its place, though [only] according to the purpose that one has in view.",[]],"refl:1774":["Whoever would put ornaments everywhere resembles the Gothic |\ntaste, or the finery of a coquette. In common discourse. A good rational and sensuous faculty of judging [Beurtheilungskraft].",[]],"refl:1775":["not to the detriment of truth, though to the detriment of method.",[]],"refl:1776":["Historical knowledge can be perfect without beauty, when it is dry.",[]],"refl:1777":["A learned man [Gelehrter]: one who has been taught [gelehret].",[]],"refl:1778":["Learning [Gelehrsamkeit] is distinguished from common knowledge either *materialiter* or *formaliter*. In the first sense it is |\nhistorical learning, in the second rational learning; the latter is either mathematical or philosophical.\n(s Knowledge of reason springs from rules.)",[]],"refl:1779":["Logical perfection: truth, distinctness, universality.",[]],"refl:1780":["Logical perfections relate to the object, beauties to the subject.*\n*(s The matter of knowledge, in its relation to the subject, is sensation. The form of knowledge, in its relation to the subject, is entitled appearance. All satisfaction [Wohlgefallen] is agreement. It is either subjective, when it rests on agreement with the subject: feeling or taste; or objective, when it rests on agreement with the object, and [in that case] it is universally valid.)",[]],"refl:1781":["The critique and the history of the beautiful belong to learning, but not the art [itself]. In general the skills of genius, for instance philosophy and the fine arts, do not belong to learning.",[]],"refl:1782":["Agreement with the object is logical perfection; consequently this is what is essential in all knowledge. The affecting of feeling is what is contingent; all beauty therefore requires logical perfection. This [logical perfection] must be present in historical knowledge also.\nWhen attention is directed to logical perfection alone, the knowledge is dry. When it is directed to beauty as well, only a determinate measure of logical perfection is possible.",[]],"refl:1783":["To the most perfect subject only those modes of knowledge would be pleasing which [please] in respect of their objective perfection. (g The conflict of taste with the form of reason is to be located in this, that synthesis is opposed to analysis.)\nThe right taste is that which agrees with the perfection of reason, even in the *synthesis*: *e.g.* examples, analogies.\nFeeling conflicts most of all with the form of reason.\nLittle sensation and much taste.",[]],"refl:1784":["The perfection of reason is directed to subordination, the aesthetic [perfection] to coordination. The former considers the *concretum in abstracto*, the latter the *abstractum in concreto*.",[]],"refl:1785":["[in respect of certainty, not of distinctness]",[]],"refl:1786":["Logical perfection is partly historical, partly rational; the former is speculative.\nThe aesthetic perfection of sensation or of taste (in itself).\nThe practical [perfection] of utility or of morality.",[]],"refl:1787":["Taste admits of no rules *a priori*, because it is meant to be a sensuous judgment, which cannot be passed according to such rules, but only in sensuous intuition.\nThe rules serve to explain and to criticise taste, not as *praecepta*.\nThe norms of taste are models, not for imitation (s precepts), but for judging.",[]],"refl:1788":["(On the doctrine of taste.) All [work] skill is either that of genius or that of a profession. A learned man by genius or by profession. A workman, etc. etc. The latter [are] workmen belong to art or to handicraft. Of the man of genius nothing can be promised; he can pledge himself to nothing. Nothing here depends on choice save the favouring of natural talent. It is like the pool of Bethesda. |\n(Against [this] John Streel:) Taste does not require genius. It is a long habituation in a manner.",[]],"refl:1789":["The pleasure which can be obtained only through [the feeling about] the sensation of the object (consequently through its matter) constitutes charm and emotion, and is entitled feeling.\nThe pleasure which can be obtained only [through the feeling] about the form of the object is entitled taste. Now the [form] knowledge of the form cannot be obtained through the effect of the object on the senses, but springs from the laws of the activity of the subject (chiefly of the lower [faculty of] knowledge, which coordinates). Therefore the |\nground of the satisfaction is subjective as regards the matter, and objective as regards its formal ground.",[]],"refl:1790":["(g A shady wood is agreeable in sensation.)\nAppearance differs from sensation in this: sensation is that element in sensibility which constitutes a part of the subject's own state, whereas appearance is an actual act of the faculty of knowledge, directed to the object as the cause of this sensation.",[]],"refl:1791":["Taste [Satisf] is directed [not so much] to the universality of satisfaction, and for that very reason to that form of the object which conforms to the universal laws of sensibility. What agrees (g with) the laws of the powers of our mind is agreeable. Even if [however] this agreeableness is small in [our] private relation, so far as concerns our own state, it is on the other hand represented as being universally valid for sociability. In solitude, indifference in regard to the beautiful. Young people have much feeling and little taste. The conditions of the beautiful form of objects are representations in accordance with relations of space and time. Those of beautiful knowledge: novelty, contrast, manifoldness.",[]],"refl:1792":["7.\nL 7':\nOne and the same thing can appear in many ways (g differently), but the concept of it must be one and the same. In the case of the good, therefore, that which produces satisfaction is universally valid.\nAll knowledge has either perfection of understanding [or]\nor perfection of taste. If it lacks the latter, it is dry; if it lacks the former, shallow.\nL 7:\nThe good pleases constantly; the beautiful pleases everyone, though not always, and taste demands variety.",[]],"refl:1793":["Beauty consists in the agreement (g of the form — appearance) with the [universal] laws of sensibility. Order. Unity.\nCharm [consists] in the agreement of the object, in so far as it alters our state, with the laws of sensibility: novelty.\nTaste is the faculty of discerning what pleases universally in sensibility.\nBeautiful objects,\nbeautiful representations of [sensation or of] imitation, or of the knowledge of objects.\nGood is what pleases in the concept, and so [completely] universally, and agrees with the object.\nKnowledge is logically perfect through agreement with the object, |\nand so according to rules of the understanding; aesthetically [perfect]: what pleases in sensibility, and so subjectively",[]],"refl:1794":["Logical perfection as regards form consists in truth (g in the concept) and its means; aesthetic [perfection] as regards form, in comprehensibility in intuition.\nThat form of sensibility which facilitates the perfection of the understanding is the self-subsistent beautiful [das selbständig Schöne]. It can serve to make universal concepts intuitive, and prepares appearances for distinctness through universal concepts.",[]],"refl:1795":["The judging of the matter takes place through sensation (s relation of the representation to the subject).\nThe feeling of form is taste. But since form can be known only *reflectendo* breaks off.",[]],"refl:1796":["The judging of the object through sensation is not universally valid; through true taste it is valid for all men; both are subjective. Through reason, however, it is valid for all [rational beings without restriction]. If the object accords with the feeling of the subject as regards matter, it is agreeable and charms or moves; if it accords as regards form with the |\nfeeling, it is beautiful; but if there is an agreement with feeling *in abstracto*, it is good.",[]],"refl:1797":["The form of beauty consists of two elements. First, in comprehensibility (g clearness) in intuition [*in concreto*] (agreement with rules of the understanding). Secondly, in the breaks off.",[]],"refl:1798":["The essential form of the beautiful consists in the agreement of [appear] intuition with the rules of the understanding. Music. Proportion. Maxims in an example.",[]],"refl:1799":["8'.\nL 8:\nPerfect sensibility is beauty. (s All beauty consists in presentation.) Sensibility, however, consists in agreement with the subjective laws of execution |\nL 8': |\nand the form is coordination in the case of *obiecta sensuum*, subordination in the case of *obiecta rationis*.\nAbstraction aids logical perfection, association aesthetic perfection. The abstract is isolated, dry, difficult; the concrete is hidden, lively and easy. From the particular there is no inference to the universal, and the knowledge of the particular [from th] in the universal is without intuition.",[]],"refl:1800":["8.\nL 8':\n1. Perfection of knowledge in general (g α, inner): truth, or agreement with itself. (s Manifoldness and unity. Magnitude.) β. Magnitude and manifoldness (g in one): *non quot, sed quantum*.\n2.\na. Logical perfection. (s Truth.) Distinctness through universal concepts, and certainty. (s Universality, precision.)\nb. Aesthetic perfection: distinctness in intuition.\n(g Form\nMatter)\nL 8:\n(s Rational. The universal *in concreto*. Shallow or dry. Logical, aesthetic loss.\n1. Perfection of knowledge in general as regards kind: logical and aesthetic.\n2. Logical, see α, β. Among modes of knowledge of one kind, as regards quantity or quality.\nAs regards logical perfection etc. etc.)\nL 8':\n( s (g 1mo see: NB 2. As regards form. Manifoldness and unity.) Perfection as regards quality and quantity. The former either logical or aesthetic. The latter either *extensiva*: as regards extent, multitude, or *intensiva*: as regards degree. (g NB 2. Theoretical or practical perfection. Only the former belongs here.) )",[]],"refl:1801":["Of aesthetic perfection [in so far as it] agrees with the logical. [Manif] Order (g *coordinatio*), unity (g *subordinatio*) and sensibility.\n(s The aesthetic perfection of knowledge, in agreement with the understanding, consists in this, that through it [of] much is thought of one thing, whereas in the logical, little of many things. It is art and not science.)",[]],"refl:1802":["Truth, manifoldness and unity are the perfections of every [mode of] knowledge.\nTruth either of knowledge in respect of the object in itself, or in relation to the intuition of the subject; the former is breaks off.",[]],"refl:1803":["By reading the ancients one may well sharpen sensation, but not taste.\nVirgil has more taste than Homer.",[]],"refl:1804":["In the left margin below no. 1763: \nPerfection concerns either quantity or quality. (g The latter is either material or formal perfection.) The latter is either (s theoretical or practical) according to rules of |\nunderstanding and reason, or according to rules of taste (s sensibility), or according to rules of the will [feeling]. (s Knowledge, feeling and desire.)\nLogical perfection concerns either the matter: truth, or the form: distinctness. (s Universality.)\n(s Logical (g according to concepts), aesthetic (g according to intuitions) and practical perfection.)\nDistinctness is opposed to beauty: *e.g.* sententious and enigmatic expressions.",[]],"refl:1805":["Connection with grounds according to the rule of reason or according to the rule of taste. According to the latter the following *canones* hold: what other people say is true, and what they do is good. According to the same [rule], that which bears a resemblance to something ridiculous is itself ridiculous.",[]],"refl:1806":["8'.\nL 8:\nThe teachers of taste have greatly erred in commending, in aesthetic perfection, sensation (charm and emotion) more than intuition. For intuition is what presents concepts *in concreto*, |\nL 8': |\ngiving back to them what the understanding has withdrawn from them by abstraction. Essentially beautiful.\nThere are creative intuitions, which are to be distinguished from [borrowed] empirical ones. There are deceptive or [dreaming] visionary intuitions, because they are not in fact depicted, but merely added in thought.",[]],"refl:1807":["Whether logical or aesthetic perfection must come first?\n[Whether the aest] The aesthetic must bear the mark of contingency upon it, and so must not be sought after.\nIn respect of the logical it must not be superfluous, but must carry (s intuition) unity with it.\nIt must [only serve] never be without simplicity.",[]],"refl:1808":["What is learning, learned knowledge?",[]],"refl:1809":["In the upper margin:\nLogical perfection relates to the object (g and is directed to quality or quantity), and is either truth (perfection of the matter, or as regards quality), or distinctness (perfection of the form), or magnitude (perfection as regards quantity). Subjective perfection is in relation to feeling (aesthetic) or to the will (practical).\nPractical: either in respect of the modes of knowledge themselves, or of things and their actuality.",[]],"refl:1810":["9. 10'.\nL 9':\n( s There is no principle of taste, (s (doctrine). Hence also no doctrine of taste (critique) for the imagination.) )\nL 9:\nAesthetic perfection [is either] in relation to knowledge or in relation to the mere feeling of pleasure. The former is business, the latter play.\nIt pleases greatly when business looks like play. It displeases when play looks like business. Now to bring sensibility and understanding into agreement for the furtherance of knowledge is a business; whereas to bring sensibility into accord with the feeling of pleasure according to universal laws (of distribution) is mere play, because it contributes nothing to knowledge, as with music and studied verbal sound or noise of sensation. Thus [dis] there can |\nL 10':\nbe no aesthetic perfection of knowledge [here], but merely perfection of taste. It displeases greatly when, in a [know] discourse designed for knowledge, we come upon what has been studiously sought for the sake of taste.",[]],"refl:1811":["The usefulness of knowledge is not beauty.",[]],"refl:1812":["The pleasing manner (not inclination) of choosing sensuously is ta|ste.\nTaste and its authors give culture without instruction, that is, polish.",[]],"refl:1813":["That object is beautiful the intuition of which pleases sensuously.",[]],"refl:1814":["Beauty is self-subsistent [selbständig] [which] where sensibility accords with perfection according to [the laws] of reason, according to universal laws. Self-subsistent beauty thus lies in the understanding, in so far as the understanding is suited to the subject. Original beauty cannot be drawn from experience either; it is rather the means of judging beauty. But from experience the understanding takes, as it were, the purpose of nature.",[]],"refl:1815":["To devise beautiful objects requires a creative mind; yet reason or experience must have given the thing itself, and the architectonic head merely orders and forms according to taste.",[]],"refl:1816":["The formative arts [bildende Künste] are distinguished from the imitative [abbildende]: the former are directed to beautiful objects, the latter to beautiful representations of objects. 1. That which represents the thing. 2. breaks off.",[]],"refl:1817":["It does seem that everything judged from private sensation, that is, from the part as *principium*, is subject to the criticism of what is judged from the universal, that is, from the whole; and hence that the variable is subject to the constant and unchangeable. From this latter comes the 'ought'.",[]],"refl:1818":["There are beautiful objects, (g and) beautiful [arts, and beautiful] presentations of objects: either making beautiful images of objects of every kind [or beautiful kn], and so in the representation of the senses; or bringing beauty into the representation of reason also.",[]],"refl:1819":["The ceremony at the changing of the hours is beautiful in contemplation, and good for the owner even without contemplation.",[]],"refl:1820":["Universal validity is either outer or inner. The first is directed to all persons, and is subjective and contingent; the second is directed to the object in general, and is objective and necessary.",[]],"refl:1821":["5'.\nL 5:\nAll knowledge is (g contains) intuition or concept (both can be distinct or indistinct). Knowledge through concepts is thinking. The faculty of intuitions is sensibility, the faculty of |\nthinking understanding (of thinking *a priori*: reason). (s Both together are required for knowing.) In the former case it [the understanding] is opposed, as a higher (disposing) faculty, to the lower faculty of sensibility, which supplies the matter. (s Thus intuition belongs to sensibility, and concept to understanding.)\nThe perfection of knowledge according to laws of sensibility is aesthetic; that according to laws of the understanding is logical. The [per] former is an agreement with the subject (s pleasure or displeasure), the second with the object; both according to universal laws*, universal logically or empirically.\nL 5':\n*(g [For] In the case of the first: novelty, ease, liveliness, scope. (g how we are affected in intuition or sensation.) In the case of the last: truth, universality, purity. (g To both belong distinctness [and] universality, but of different kinds.) The faculty of originally producing both kinds of (products of) knowledge is genius. (It is the source of the rule; to judge it is entitled either power of judgment or taste.) What is objective in sensibility is intuition; consequently this agrees with the understanding and is by its nature universally valid, since otherwise men would not understand one another. Feeling has private validity.)\n( g There is no doctrine (*a priori*) of taste, but there is indeed a critique of it. Rules, not rational ones. )",[]],"refl:1822":["Sensuous pleasure [sensibility] has two purposes: first, immediate gratification; secondly, the furtherance of perfection, and so the relation to intellectual satisfaction. To the latter there belongs, in respect of logical perfection, making knowledge sensible, that is, presenting it *in concreto* (intuition); secondly [to] in practical matters, to move and stir. It is a play which makes serious occupation welcome.",[]],"refl:1823":["We pass the judgment of taste not from a rule but from intuition; for the rules are not *a priori*.",[]],"refl:1824":["The power of judgment curbs and orders sensation; taste [curbs and orders] spirit [and] dryness.",[]],"refl:1825":["Sensation, power of judgment, spirit and taste. Fine art.\nAgreement either with the (g universal) laws of sensibility or with those of the understanding.\nAgreement with the subjective laws: sensation, intuition, ease.\nThat with objective laws, logical: truth, distinctness, with the rules of knowledge in general.",[]],"refl:1826":["Since what is essential in every representation is the idea of the thing, all aesthetic perfection is a union of the subjective with the objective.",[]],"refl:1827":["Taste: what pleases in intuition.",[]],"refl:1828":["The character of the thing and its worth.",[]],"refl:1829":["Two kinds of distinction: what the thing is (g relates to the object, the other to the subject), and how it pleases. What pleases pleases either (s mediately or immediately. What pleases immediately pleases either) in a private relation or universally; the former gratifies. What pleases universally pleases either according to universal laws of human sensibility* or according to laws of reason; the former is beautiful, the latter good.\n*(g in the appearance. These judgments are universally valid; those concerning charm have only private validity. In [judging] whether something pleases in the appearance, one must set aside all share of the subject and its well-being. The charm of a beautiful object [goes] passes away |\nwith time, although the judgment about the beauty not only remains, but also indicates the very same degree of it.)",[]],"refl:1830":["Beautiful presentation of objects. *Hypotyposis*. Beautiful objects. Beautiful modes of knowledge. Beautiful sciences. Critique.",[]],"refl:1831":["Beauty is the harmony of sensations.",[]],"refl:1832":["The understanding is entitled the higher faculty, and sensibility the lower, not because the former is pre-eminent and the latter of lesser worth, but because that which treats [the material] stands *a priori* to that which is to be treated. Otherwise sensibility without understanding is something, but understanding without sensibility is nothing at all.\nThis is to be rightly understood: the understanding is here regarded as a human understanding, which is only the faculty of [conc] discursive knowledge, and intuition is taken [in the] only as sensible. But since intuition in general can be partly sensible, partly pure, the understanding [partly of the sens] is also regarded as a faculty of intuitions, though not in man.",[]],"refl:1833":["There must be something which makes a perfection out of agreement with laws of sensibility or of understanding. The good.",[]],"refl:1834":["Understanding and intuitions constitute [the] what is objective in knowledge.\nWhat is subjective has to do with sensation, power of judgment, spirit and taste.\nSensation and spirit belong to the content, power of judgment and taste to the form.\nThe final aim of aesthetic knowledge is truth and [mo] goodness of disposition. Hence they [i.e. its subjective elements] accord with understanding and will through subjective means.",[]],"refl:1835":["To expound something historically or dogmatically.",[]],"refl:1836":["[Beauty, dryness\nthoroughness]\nBeauties are opposed not to distinctness, but to the depth of knowledge. Everything must begin from the crude. Beauties make thoroughness unrecognisable.\n(s Charm, that is, effect on feeling, is not beauty, but *parergon*.)",[]],"refl:1837":["Knowledge through an idea is architectonic. Knowledge through aggregates is technical. City.",[]],"refl:1838":["The logical perfection of knowledge as regards matter is truth (agreement with the objects); as regards form, distinctness, as condition of truth according to laws of the understanding.\nAesthetic perfection as regards matter is sensation (feeling); as regards form, intuition* (liveliness). For, considered as knowledge, sensation is directed to the object, and intuition is only the form of representing it. [This holds] although [as alteration] intuition [sub] is properly [only subject] objective, whereas sensation is subjective.\n*(g accords with the perfection of the understanding.)\n(s Aesthetic perfection: intuition and feeling.)",[]],"refl:1839":["The purpose of all logical perfection is to bring everything to concepts (g to universality), and these to distinctness.\nThe purpose of all aesthetic perfection is to bring everything to intuition, and this to liveliness. In both, however, order and harmony in one idea are also needed.\n(s Agreement with the object — truth.\nAgreement with the subject — agreeableness.)",[]],"refl:1840":["The sciences for the culture of taste, acquaintance with the ancient poets and the Romans, are so called because they further *humanitas*. They further the sociable side of knowledge, relieving the dryness of the sciences through what is universally pleasing in sensibility; they further likewise the concord of men in instruction, and the connection of the teachings of the ancients with our own. Through these breaks off.\n(s Aesthetic perfection for the (g furtherance) communication of knowledge is business.)",[]],"refl:1841":["Aesthetic perfection is (s merely, for the purposes of knowledge) play (s *tanquam per otium* (s — easy)); logical perfection is business (s *tanquam per negotium*). When business (s is as free as)* seems to be play, while yet retaining its perfection, it pleases taste, in that it is useful. When play is carried on as business** (s pedant), it displeases, in that it is of no use and yet is laborious. Studied beauties displease.\n(s The chief logical perfection, in so far as it is to be merely logical, is truth.\nThe chief aesthetic perfection, in so far as it is to be merely aesthetic, is sensation.)\n*(s is carried on as play: shallow head.)\n**(s Purposive (g conforming to law) [is] turns out — the first is eloquence, the second poetry.)",[]],"refl:1842":["The power of judgment is directed to application; understanding to the form of perfection. 1. Sensuous modes of knowledge among themselves; 2. the harmony of sensibility with understanding.",[]],"refl:1843":["In matters of taste everyone always has the model or archetype in his head.",[]],"refl:1844":["Sensation and spirit move; power of judgment and taste direct and moderate. The former is the wind in the flutes, the latter the fingers.",[]],"refl:1845":["Perfection consists in general in agreement with universal laws (what pleases universally and necessarily): either with universal objective laws, whereby knowledge acquires agreement with the object (distinctness, thoroughness), or with subjective laws, whereby it accords with the nature of the subject. The latter is aesthetic. What sets the powers of our mind [kn] in easy and vigorous play is agreeable. What sets them in harmonious play is beautiful. What at the same time sets the higher powers in a play accordant with sensibility is good.",[]],"refl:1846":["What arouses and animates the understanding belongs to spirit (s what animates the whole faculty of knowledge); what arouses sensation, to sense.\nWhat accords as regards form with the understanding belongs to the power of judgment; what [accords] with sensibility, to taste.",[]],"refl:1847":["Sensation and spirit belong to genius. Power of judgment and taste produce nothing, but only administer, in relation to the object or among themselves.\nWhat agrees with the subjective laws of the understanding has spirit.\nWhat animates belongs to sensation and spirit; what preserves and protects, to power of judgment and taste.\nGenius is architectonic; [it] creates. Power of judgment and taste administer.",[]],"refl:1848":["One chooses something from deliberation (because it is cheap, because it is healthy), or from appetite (inclination), or from taste. Food and drink. In the same way a house betrays choice by deliberation or by inclination, comfort, etc. etc. Taste makes no choice, but only directs it.",[]],"refl:1849":["To sensation belongs the inner [aspect] of sense (pleasure).\n(s The greatest perfection of knowledge lies in intuition; sensibility is to restore what the understanding takes away.)",[]],"refl:1850":["The faculty of choosing in a universally valid manner is taste. The use of universal rules *in concreto* is the power of judgment. Feeling discriminates only for oneself; its judgment is not valid for others. Taste [is] social.",[]],"refl:1851":["Whether taste has constant and universal rules. [It has,] but they are to be known not *a priori* (g *in abstracto*), but *in concreto*. [Taste] is a sensuous power of judgment, valid for all. Sensibility gives no judgment except *in concreto*; otherwise it would be understanding. All rules leave out much of the *concretum*. The rules are not laws, that is, [laws] which make aesthetic perfection and are universal.",[]],"refl:1852":["Whoever does not choose what pleases universally has no taste.",[]],"refl:1853":["The egoism of taste. One's own taste changes. Corrupted taste. Necessity of a model, to form and preserve taste. The models [are] not for imitation, but for judging; not patterns and objective, but serviceable on the average, though not sufficient in themselves.",[]],"refl:1854":["The faculty of choosing the sensuous [element] that pleases universally is taste",[]],"refl:1855":["The means of the beautiful is art, the rule nature. Nature is not the model (s but an example) of the beautiful, for the beautiful lies in ideas; yet nature is its *substratum*. In the beautiful, nature signifies the unconstrained; art, the purposive and orderly; the artificial [gekünstelt], however, the laboured. Everything is natural that seems to have arisen according to a universal law of efficient causes.\n(g When art resembles chance and chance resembles art, this is the unexpected.)\n(s Beauty is that character of an object or of a mode of knowledge whereby the faculties of knowledge are set in harmonious attunement.)",[]],"refl:1856":["Taste is the polish (*politesse*) of the power of judgment.* One is polished only through intercourse. The sensuous power of judgment has its ideal, but taste does not; taste has examples, but not models. An archetype, not an example. The Venus of Praxiteles not a pattern.\n(g Idea, which is drawn from many examples.)\n*(g Taste shows itself in the moderation both of the strong and of the ornate. Taste costs nothing; it is obliging, courteous as it were, and playful; it puts no one to trouble; it is virtue from inclination.)",[]],"refl:1857":["The sketch belongs to the power of judgment (g image) (drawing), the |\nrough working-out to genius, and the refinement to taste.\nThe idea breaks off.",[]],"refl:1858":["Of the idea of the beautiful. Beautiful objects and modes of knowledge.",[]],"refl:1859":["[*Regulae*] *Norma gustus non est lex generalis, sed exemplar* [The norm of taste is not a universal law, but an exemplar].",[]],"refl:1860":["*Diiudicatio aesthetica* (g *secundum sensum communem*) *est gustus. Secundum sensum privatum est appetitus. Gustus est iudicium societatis sive sociale* [Aesthetic judging according to common sense is taste; according to private sense it is appetite. Taste is the judgment of society, or social judgment].\n(s Barbarous beauty, oriental.)",[]],"refl:1861":["Power of judgment, *jugement*, sound understanding are one and the same, namely the capacity of the understanding in application.",[]],"refl:1862":["Simplicity furthers universal validity; for what can easily be represented or procured is suited to the understanding of all. |\nTaste seeks to avoid roughness, and also offence against usage. It presupposes beauty or greatness, yet, like smoothness, it produces an *analogon* of beauty.",[]],"refl:1863":["Of the models of taste. Of genius.",[]],"refl:1864":["Sensation and power of judgment together make up sensible intuition. Sensation is distinct from feeling: the former is the matter of sensuous knowledge, the latter what is subjective in satisfaction, and is either emotion or charm. Feelings corrupt the judgment of the understanding through the interest they arouse. What is truly beautiful must please in intuition. Whoever is interested in it is corrupted and cannot judge impartially. Whoever judges according as something charms or moves him no longer judges about the object; that is *metabasis eis allo genos*. Such a judgment is not advantageous to the understanding and does not concern what is essentially beautiful. This is the good in intuition (all intuition, however, is sensible). To the power of judgment belong design, order, precision, naturalness. It is not the gratification arising from sensation that produces the satisfaction; rather it is the satisfaction, in so far as it arises universally from all four elements, that produces the gratification.",[]],"refl:1865":["8'.\nL 8:\n[Philos] Historical knowledge is opposed as regards matter [to] the dogmatic, because the object of the |\nL 8': |\nformer is an object |\nof the senses, that of the latter an object of the understanding. *Formaliter* a mode of knowledge is rational when it is grounded on the universal rules of method according to which it can arise *a priori*. It is entitled historical, however, when it is grounded on [the universal laws of] a particular example of the use of reason. In the latter case philosophy sits in the memory. In the second case a pupil puts to shame one of the first kind. The latter seems ignorant, the former learned; afterwards the latter [proves] rational, the former a blockhead.\nL 8:\n(g In the didactic method, historical or rational form.)\nL 8':\nCommon knowledge (s aggregate) and science (s system) (s [in respect of connection] not as regards quality or degree, but as regards order and unity) differ in this: science holds modes of knowledge as contained in a whole of knowledge of a certain kind, whereas common knowledge holds them only as pieces belonging to no whole. Science therefore contains, besides the partial modes of knowledge, an outline of the whole and the place of each particular mode of knowledge in this whole. That knowledge is historical through which something is known in so far as it is given. Everything historical is either *materialiter* (s what can be known only in so far as it (object) is given) or *formaliter* historical: the former only in so far as the objects are given, the latter in so far as a knowledge of the objects is given. Philosophy is *materialiter* rational, but can be *formaliter* historical, when one merely learns the philosophy of another, just as it is given. That does not yet make a philosopher who can philosophise, but [only one] who knows a philosophy.\n(s Science of reason [is] either mathematics or philosophy; the sum of historical knowledge is learning.)\n(s Logical perfection of historical knowledge and of knowledge of reason.)\n(s Historical sciences [distinguished] from sciences of reason.)\nSome rational sciences can be learned; others can be acquired only through genius and its cultivation. The former belongs to learning. The instruments of learning: philology.\nMathematics can prove its universal rules *a priori*, and the application *in concreto* is intuitive.\nIn philosophy — — — — — — — not intuitive.",[]],"refl:1866":["Common knowledge and science; [both either] the latter: knowledge in a system.\nScience\nof learning* is the sum of historical sciences. (g depends on how one knows something.) of insight is the sum of the sciences of reason.\nArt\nart of teaching; mechanical (handicraft), depends on how and by what means one is able to do something; of genius.\nKnowing and** being able.\nIn all [of these] regard may be had to logical and to aesthetic perfection. It depends on which is the principal concern. Aesthetic perfection can have a private relation and accommodation, or a relation to perfect sensibility in general. In the latter case it is beauty.\n*(g which is grounded merely on what is taught; hence mathematics does not belong to learning.)\n**(g as science [is related] to common knowledge, so art [is related] to handicraft (mechanical), namely in that one knows and is able according to rules (instruction *a priori*).",[]],"refl:1867":["Modes of knowledge are practical as precepts and as incentives: the former in so far as they subject [us], the latter in that they move [us]. Neither belongs to logical perfection.\nThe aesthetic [perfection] stands in closer connection with it.",[]],"refl:1868":["The perfection of knowledge is 1. theoretical (g in relation to knowledge), 2. practical (g in relation to the will); the latter does not belong to logic.\nThe former is either logical (g objective) or aesthetic (g subjective).\nAesthetic [perfection] must have a relation to the logical. A certain idea lies at its basis.\n(s Ease, because it is contingent (s play). A sociable manner of presenting the *objects*.)\nUnderstanding in intuition *in concreto*.\nLiveliness, examples, sensible distinctness; not emotions or charms.\nUniversal concepts. The natural.\nWide-ranging influence.\nA studied striving after the beautiful does not look well. An easy manner. Logical and aesthetic [perfection] detract from one another* (g dry or shallow. Contrived beauties also appear to aim at persuading.), but they also support each other.\n*(s distinct for the sensible faculty of knowledge and obscure or con|fused\nfor the understanding; the former in order to recognise it among ordinary appearances, the latter in order to arrange it under concepts. Beauties of knowledge are *parerga* and must be represented as such.)",[]],"refl:1869":["To the beautiful (g to producing it) there belong not precepts (rules) but models (g rules need examples (illustration)); and these models are not for imitation, since imitation would itself in turn be the beauty, but for analogical conformity. The models of style are the ancients. Just because they are our models, they cannot be surpassed. Boldness is the wish to surpass the first models (those of nature) themselves; and this is feasible, because nature has made everything not for our sensible satisfaction alone, but also for use. No one of our own time can become a true model. The archetype [Urbild] is the model that is in no way borrowed. It lies solely in the idea. Morality requires [nothing] rule, permits no model, but does permit example.",[]],"refl:1870":["Taste* is the final culture; preparatory exercise through the reading of good models.\n*(g Method of teaching and delivery.)",[]],"refl:1871":["The understanding is in itself already common (g judgment: commonly valid; sense has private validity); hence we do not say *intellectus communis*, but *vulgaris*, that is, the empirically competent understanding. This understanding, however, is also entitled *sensus communis*, because what is judged by the understanding in immediate connection with the senses is reckoned as belonging to sense.\nTaste can arise only through the comparison of many judgments of the senses.",[]],"refl:1872":["The faculty of choosing what pleases the sense of everyone. *Facultas diiudicandi per sensum communem* [the faculty of judging by common sense]. The faculty of choosing sensibly and with universal validity is taste. It concerns the form rather than the matter of sensibility.\nUniversally valid laws are not universal; rather, the question is how they are valid. The understanding judges according to universal laws, that is, according to concepts.",[]],"refl:1873":["Imagination and understanding are the two active sources of representations. [Imagination] Sense is passive.",[]],"refl:1874":["Aesthetic perfection is either [according to private laws or universal laws of sensibility. Taste] (s agreement with the necessary or with the contingent laws of sensibility; the former in agreement with the logical.) Agreeable or beautiful; it charms or it moves.\n(g not according to rules *a priori* or *praecepta*, but norms.)",[]],"refl:1875":["in the presentation.\nBelow it, as elucidation:\nAgreement with the *subject*. Beauty concerns presentation and delivery.",[]],"refl:1876":["Aesthetic perfection: the subjective agreement of the faculties of knowledge in the representation of a thing.*\n(g Hence not concept, but feeling of pleasure, yet universally valid. Understanding and sensibility.)\nLogical perfection: concept (universal). Distinctness.\nUnderstanding: empirical also; the rule objective — Reason: *a priori* — Judgment [Urtheilskraft]: understanding and sensibility in relation; reflective.",[]],"refl:1877":["Philodoxy, not philosophy.\n(s Historical or rational knowledge. Common, learned.)",[]],"refl:1878":["The sum of all historical modes of knowledge is entitled erudition [Gelehrsamkeit]. Erudition is, properly, the historical knowledge of the sciences.",[]],"refl:1879":["Historical knowledge as science is erudition.",[]],"refl:1880":["Spirit [Geist] consists in the stirring of the powers of the mind to a more extended use; it is something other than dry knowledge. Art gives no sign of spirit.",[]],"refl:1881":["Novelty and ease are merely subjective perfections.",[]],"refl:1882":["The sum of formal historical modes of knowledge is entitled erudition, *erudition*; to it belong [phil] literature and philology. The philosophical mind and the fine mind [schöne Geist] are neither of them learned men. Sciences of insight, or erudition. Art, either of utility or of beauty. Agreement with the *object* (matter): truth. Distinctness (g in concepts): form.\nThoroughness, dryness. Beauty, ease.\nCogency [Bündigkeit] is lively thoroughness.\nOf the good meaning of »making something sensible«.\nTruth: in agreement with the *object* — with the *subject*: distinctness in concepts and in intuition.",[]],"refl:1883":["Historical science is erudition.",[]],"refl:1884":["Logical perfection aims at knowing the particular in the universal; aesthetic perfection, at knowing the universal in the particular. The former brings everything to concepts, the latter to intuitions. They further one another in form. The former abstracts; the latter unites much in one concept.",[]],"refl:1885":["Manifoldness and unity  Magnitude — quality\n)*\n[Distinctness of order and form] [Truth of the m]\n*(g As regards matter it is truth, as regards form (g unity) order.)",[]],"refl:1886":["Universality, truth (and distinctness), manifoldness and unity in all knowledge.\nDistinctness, and therefore order and unity, are only means.",[]],"refl:1887":["Subjective truth in appearance; subjective distinctness in intuition (s liveliness); subjective universality is popularity (g *sensus communis*).",[]],"refl:1888":["On the union of logical and aesthetic (g difficulty) perfections.\n1. Universality, the universal in the particular: popularity, and making speculative modes of knowledge (s *in abstracto* or *in concreto*) comprehensible to the common understanding.\n2. Distinctness through few marks: abstraction; and liveliness through many, without abstraction.\n3. Truth with the *object*; subjective [truth]: with the subject's way of thinking, truth in appearance.\n4. Necessity. (s from concepts.) (s custom.) Empirical [necessity]: through experience, custom (g inner as well as usage), for example in morals, feeling.\n(s Classics. Humanists.)\n(s Conflict of aesthetic with logical perfection. Hence beauty is a product of art that looks like nature.)",[]],"refl:1889":["Feelings are least of all to be united with taste.",[]],"refl:1890":["The truth of semblance [Schein] requires only slight analogies, for example in the fable; namely, as the imagination lets things speak through |\nthe inner speech which, in judging them, we let them address to us: echo.",[]],"refl:1891":["Immediate satisfaction merely in what is formal in the mode of representation is taste; in what is material (for sense), it is the feeling of the agreeable and the disagreeable. Mediate satisfaction, through reason, in the good is the way of thinking [Denkungsart].",[]],"refl:1892":["In logic [there is no] no account whatever is taken of practical perfection.\nOf the beautiful there is no science, only art.\nArt is a capacity for execution which cannot be [taught] learned by rules. Knowing and being able are here different. For (g skilful) presentation for knowledge is not knowledge. The rules of presentation are subjectively universally valid and yet *a priori*.\nTo choose the beautiful presupposes science. The culture of judgment in respect of the beautiful through modes of knowledge is the *humaniora*.",[]],"refl:1893":["Excitement of feeling without a preceding logical-aesthetic form is contrary to taste.",[]],"refl:1894":["Essential aesthetic perfection is that which contributes to knowledge in general; thus [the] not feelings, but what, as intuition, makes the concepts of understanding concrete.\n(g Ideas of intuition (g represented.) are the essential and the *substratum*.)\nSensation is merely subjective, but intuition objective.\nSensation; judgment is concerned with how the sensation accords with the concept.\nSpirit (genius): animation through an idea. Taste compares it with the universal sense.\nAnimation through associated sensations is different from animation through a universal *principium* of the association of many sensations.\nInstrument of animation, or *principium*.\nField for fancy, and its unity.\nWe treat only of the beauty of knowledge, not of objects.",[]],"refl:1895":["What agrees with the laws of a power of representation pleases. Now what pleases immediately either pleases universally or yields only a private satisfaction. The former [pleases] according to laws either of sensibility or of the understanding: beautiful and good. Although *a priori* grounds of what pleases sensibly can be given, they are not demonstrative; the *criterium* is experience.",[]],"refl:1896":["What pleases only me is agreeable.\nWhat is to be entitled beautiful must please universally.",[]],"refl:1897":["The universality of satisfaction is known and proved either *a priori* or only empirically.",[]],"refl:1898":["Emotion and charm belong not to the objective but merely to the subjective, and so do not belong to the beautiful except contingently. But intuition that corresponds to the concept —",[]],"refl:1899":["Perfection (aesthetic) in general, or what contributes to it.",[]],"refl:1900":["Everyone who has an inner *principium vitale* [vital principle] of knowledge has a *genius* (genie). *Sed genio suo indulgere non licet nisi [sub] hac lege, ut sensui communi, h.e. genio aliorum, etiam indulgeamus* [But one may not indulge one's own genius except on this condition, that we also indulge common sense, that is, the genius of others].",[]],"refl:1901":["Logic is doctrine, aesthetic is critique (because its principles are not *a priori* but empirical, and yet contain universal subjective laws (of form).) Taste is complaisant, the understanding commanding.",[]],"refl:1902":["Sensation is, properly, only subjective and for [understanding] sense; intuition is objective and for the understanding. Hence spirit is required for sensation, judgment for intuition; and judgment, in respect of both together, is taste.",[]],"refl:1903":["Of knowledge: 1. agreement with the universal laws (g conditions) of intuition (sensible) or of thought.\n(s The form of sensibility is intuition. Sensation.)\nAesthetic and logical perfection. Essential. Unity of intuition and of thought. Ends.",[]],"refl:1904":["Logical perfection*, formal: distinctness. Material: truth (through concepts).\nAesthetic perfection:**\nDistinctness and truth of intuition.\nHarmony with the subjective conditions of [all] the [whole] entire faculty of knowledge is aesthetic; with the objective conditions, logical. (The conditions of appearance are subjective.)\n(g subjective harmony, objective harmony.)\nUniversal validity in sensation: aesthetic; in thought: logical. (s Sensation weakens logical perfection.)\n*\n(g[Magnitude] Universality as concept. Rules are empirically universal.)\n**(g Singular, as intuition and sensation. Sensation constitutes what is contingent in aesthetic perfection.)",[]],"refl:1905":["Objective perfection concerns knowledge of the *object*. Intuition also concerns the *object*, but still in relation to the *subject*, to the whole of human nature.",[]],"refl:1906":["The aesthetic perfection that furthers the logical is interest and not affect (in respect of feeling); the preacher must not excite tears.",[]],"refl:1907":["That in the representation of which sensibility and understanding accord to form one [mode of] knowledge is beautiful.",[]],"refl:1908":["The ground of a universal satisfaction according to laws of sensibility is beauty; (g according to laws) of a particular [satisfaction], charm. Understanding belongs to beauty. Charm. Sensation.",[]],"refl:1909":["The united interest of imagination and understanding.",[]],"refl:1910":["Essential aesthetic perfection is the agreement of intuition with concepts; contingent [aesthetic perfection], with sensations.\nSelf-subsistent beauty.",[]],"refl:1911":["Beautiful knowledge. Presentation of concepts. Elegance of speech [Wohlredenheit], eloquence and poetry.",[]],"refl:1912":["6'.\nL 5:\nThe subjective agreement of the faculties of knowledge* in the presentation of concepts.\nL 6':\n(s The beautiful presentation of what is thought, through words, is beautiful delivery (not knowledge).)\n(s to communicate oneself universally: *humaniora*.)",[]],"refl:1913":["Essential aesthetic perfection, that is, accordance with the understanding, is sensation; and this is a hindrance to concepts.",[]],"refl:1914":["The universality in the beautiful is empirical (understanding). There is, however, a universality of reason which is necessary.",[]],"refl:1915":["Agreement of knowledge with the subjective conditions of its possibility.",[]],"refl:1916":["Beautiful knowledge can be explained through its subjective perfection, but beautiful objects cannot.",[]],"refl:1917":["On  and \n1. Perfection\nof knowledge as mere knowledge.\n2.\nin relation to the will.",[]],"refl:1918":["Quality: distinctness (g subjective) (lively, singular). (g aesthetic: clearness of intuition.)\nRelation: truth. (g 2. subjective truth in appearance.) (s 2. Relation, aesthetically, is to the *subject*. Charm and emotion.)\nQuantity: universality. (g subjective.) (g 3. The universal in the particular.) (s subjectively for all.)\nModality: certainty, necessity (s of knowledge in general. The customary, usage. It is *coustume*.) (g 4. empirical necessity. Approval. Universal. The customary. Taste.)",[]],"refl:1919":["Magnitude of content.\nOn L § 26 »importance«:\nof the consequences.",[]],"refl:1920":["The aesthetic [perfection], as regards matter, in sensation; as regards form, in (g unity in) intuition and [determinateness] manifoldness.",[]],"refl:1921":["Sensations do nothing but animate; they do not enable us to know the object better.",[]],"refl:1922":["That displeases which our mental power resists as a hindrance to its free play. Thus what conflicts with sensibility according to universal laws (empirical ones, it may be) displeases aesthetically.",[]],"refl:1923":["Beauty of knowledge is the agreement of the freedom of the imagination with the conformity to law of the understanding in the presentation of concepts. Poetry has the former as its end, eloquence the latter, elegance of speech both.",[]],"refl:1924":["Faculty of knowledge, feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and faculty of desire.\nIf the faculty of knowledge, according to principles, is a ground of pleasure, it is at the same time a faculty of desire; that is, the knowledge is practical.",[]],"refl:1925":["All logical perfections are objective. When they are made subjective without conflicting with the former [their objective character], though with its limitation, they are then aesthetic.",[]],"refl:1926":["Taste permits no precepts and rules,* because the imagination gives the law, namely the universal judgment, and the imagination is free.\n*(g For taste is subjective judgment, which determines the judgment not [through] according to concepts, but according to pleasure and displeasure.)",[]],"refl:1927":["Necessity of the sciences for taste. Logical perfection lies at the basis, as in a meal, but only that of the common sense.",[]],"refl:1928":["Beauty and virtue agree in this, that they are judged according to the satisfaction of the commonwealth [des gemeinen Wesens], and not according to private gratification. They require a spectator who is interested in the gratification of all. There is only this difference: in the former the grounds of satisfaction are merely empirical and have no necessity whatever *a priori*; in the latter they are intellectual, and must necessarily arouse the approval of everyone, because they concern the harmony of the ends of rational beings in general. Taste and sentiment (g character) are both disinterested; both do one honour. The former can be confirmed only by the concurrence of the judgment of many men over long periods; the latter, through reason, by each. In nature, beauty and utility are, for sense and for reason, enjoyment and ends; in freedom, manners and virtue. The one prepares for the other.",[]],"refl:1929":["1) Faculty of knowledge; 2) feeling of pleasure and displeasure; 3) faculty of desire.\nLogical, aesthetic, practical.\nUnderstanding, judgment, reason.",[]],"refl:1930":["*Sensus communis*: the commonly valid sense. The popularity [of the sense] of a cultivated and finer sense is taste, *gustus*. Taste requires cultivated sensible judgment. Inability to choose in a popular manner is tastelessness. The universal pleasingness of sensible choice is taste. The propensity to choose perversely is false, inverted taste.",[]],"refl:1931":["Since the beautiful carries no interest with it on its own account (that is to say, its existence is indifferent to us), and yet a satisfaction in it takes place, this satisfaction must consist in the arousing of the feeling of that in which we do take an interest, namely the harmony of understanding and sensibility for a knowledge in general.\nThus it is not the beautiful that gratifies, but the harmony of the powers of knowledge. For the union of the two is good in every respect, whereas the furthering of the understanding, for example mathematics, is good only in one. The agreement of representations with [universal] laws of sensibility can be universal only if it is directed to knowledge.",[]],"refl:1932":["The agreement of sensibility with the understanding in a knowledge is beauty. Beauty does not rest on sensation, charm and emotion, for these contribute nothing to knowledge.\n(s Sensation does not belong to knowledge at all.)\nThe feeling of this harmony of the two powers of knowledge constitutes the satisfaction in the beautiful.",[]],"refl:1933":["Aesthetic perfection is either that of taste, that is, of the form of sensibility, that is, of taste; or that of the matter of sensation, that is, of feeling. The former grounds a universal satisfaction.",[]],"refl:1934":["Pleasing in the concept: good; in sensation: agreeable; in reflection: beautiful.",[]],"refl:1935":["Beauty concerns only one part of knowledge, namely the sensible presentation of the concept, not logically *in abstracto*, but aesthetically *in concreto*.\n»Beautiful knowledge« is an expression without sense; for the more I attend to what is subjective, the less is the objective element in the mode of representation, that is, the knowledge, furthered. But the beautiful mode of representing an object, as presentation of a concept, consists in the (g subjective) agreement with one another of the two [knowledge] faculties of representation which belong to knowledge [toge], each following its own laws independently. Hence freedom of the imagination together with the conformity to law of the understanding. Taste is sensible, subjective (aesthetic) judgment (g in so far as it of itself agrees with logical judgment). It is either [the judgment] of subsumption under a given concept, and so of the presentation of that concept, that is, the judgment of delivery for a knowledge; or [the judgment] of the object of the senses as a beautiful object (of the agreement of the imagination with the understanding for a knowledge in general [without c] prior to any concept). — Freedom of the imagination |\nin accordance with the conformity to law of the understanding. The aesthetic perfection of the mode of representation has no rule that determines [it] (g that precedes [it]), because the imagination is to accord with the understanding precisely in its freedom. Aesthetic perfection in the (g immediate) relation [to the] of a representation, through the imagination, to feeling (not by means of knowledge to that feeling) is not beauty, but charm or emotion.\n(g NB. Here we treat not of beautiful objects, but of the beautiful presentation of concepts through the imagination; the objects may even be ugly.)\n(g Agreement for knowledge is logical perfection.)\n(s Beautiful is a mode of representation in which [the fr] the agreement of the free play of the imagination with the conformity to law of the understanding is felt.)\n(s Taste is aesthetic judgment.\nOf the beautiful as such there is no science. A universal satisfaction is demanded, which is therefore not mere feeling; for that (the agreeable) cannot serve others as a rule of satisfaction. And yet the satisfaction cannot be grounded on universal rules.)",[]],"refl:1936":["Sin of omission. When one does not arrange the order properly. [Wide]\nWhen one does not conform to rules, e.g. of distinctness, of certainty.\nImperfection of lack.\nprivative (g ignorance). He who\ndoes not give to the poor man.\n*contrarie dictum*: he who oppresses him.\nOpposite L § 36 »perfection — become« (l. 25f.):\nWhen one acts according to contrary [rules].\nOn L § 36 »fault« (l. 28):\nErrors.\n[Either against logical or against aesthetic rules.]",[]],"refl:1937":["It is superfluous to bring intuitions into knowledge by art where common sensibility supplies greater ones, e.g. paintings of natural things: flowers.\nSo it is also with charm — with voluptuousness.\nThis is harmful, however, when the charm is by nature disproportionate and detracts from the other charms.*\nOne must [seek] give charm or intuition to rational knowledge and to rational motives, and combine with it a charm that leads one to despise sensibility.\nFaults of aesthetic are the superfluous, the long-winded. Measuredness and ease are pleasing to sensibility.\n*(g To flatter a despot is no art; but to contradict him in a polite manner, to put the rein of reason on him without his noticing it: that is art, for which he thanks us afterwards. In this way one will not, indeed, eradicate the voluptuous impulse; nor ought one even to weaken it much; but one should only let it borrow the right charms from virtue.)",[]],"refl:1938":["The ignorant man does not know how to ask; the man of insight does not know how to answer (s the questions of the ignorant man, who does not even know his own ignorance. *Quaerit delirus*). Since the former does not even know the questions, it is no wonder that he does not know his own ignorance in respect of the answers. He alone is to be called an ignoramus (g idiot) who knows everything without [knowing] (g this) that he knows nothing; who therefore, though he does not even know (g rightly) how to ask, nevertheless answers all questions.",[]],"refl:1939":["Nothing is more contemptible than what is imitated (there are, however, imitations according to universally valid laws, customs). Nothing is more [harmful] repugnant than the contrived (laboriousness in what belongs merely to agreeableness) (g it gives itself airs and boasts), [nothing] because beauties\n|\nmust come of themselves and are accidental grounds of satisfaction. Hence simplicity (what is not contrived), *naïveté*, in so far as it cannot have been learned: natural, yet not common.\nNegligence, the *air degagèe*, a master in trifles, who gives laws and does not prescribe, who is impolite not from boorish ignorance but according to a new taste.",[]],"refl:1940":["A lack that is taken for a knowledge is a formal error, e.g. empty formulae, tautological definitions.",[]],"refl:1941":["Beauty is dryness — its contrary: ugliness.\nLack of practical perfection is uselessness; its contrary: harmfulness.\nOn L § 36 »fault« (16328):\nTransgressions of the law: *reatus*.\nIgnorance and error are opposites — the latter diametrically.",[]],"refl:1942":["True beauty must not be contrived and coquettish, affected and laboriously adorned.",[]],"refl:1943":["The good could be still better. Negatively evil.",[]],"refl:1944":["What is good could yet be better, namely complete.",[]],"refl:1945":["Not to explain is a lack; to explain falsely is a fault.\nA lack, e.g. of a syllable in a verse, can at the same time be a fault. The latter is any transgression of a law.",[]],"refl:1946":["Beautiful +; not beautiful (g dry) 0; ugly –.",[]],"refl:1947":["*Oppositum* is entitled the opposite. [a×0 = oppositum]\n*Contrarium*, the contrary. a–a = contrarium.\nDiametrically opposed, as pleasure and pain. Thus [knowledge] true knowledge is negatively opposed to ignorance, contrarily opposed to error.",[]],"refl:1948":["When one makes comparisons, one must first have brought the things under a common concept, and in respect of it investigate their agreements or their lack of agreement. E.g. is a peasant's house or a dovecote more perfect? Is an elephant or a bird of paradise more perfect?\nOpposite L § 37 sentence 2:\nIf the aim is to please, the author is right; if the aim is to know and to be useful, it is false.\nOpposite L § 37, beginning of sentence 3:\nThe practical knowledge of a general can be greater than that of a bookworm.\nPedantry.",[]],"refl:1949":["E.g. the geography of a traveller.\nOn L § 37 sentence 2:\n[Homer and] Pope and Locke.",[]],"refl:1950":["A merchant, a statesman, an estate manager [Oeconom], who have studied. Likewise surveyors.\nMother wit, school wit.",[]],"refl:1951":["Experience.\nOn L § 39 »indeed, although — many learned men«:\nE.g. theoretical and practical economy.",[]],"refl:1952":["[Learned knowledge] Philosophy knows little of much; common [knowledge], much of little.",[]],"refl:1953":["Every distinct insight into the ground draws after it an uncommonly long series of consequences.",[]],"refl:1954":["Learned knowledge in general.\nHarms in respect of the moral,\nof the aesthetic.",[]],"refl:1955":["To study [is som]: to learn a science, that is, knowledge [of the from] (g learning with) insight. Insight, however, is the judging of an object from [its] grounds. Now positive (national) law is not an object of judging, for it is not left free [to judgment]. Roman law must be judged. Hence the learning of positive law is as little a studying as the learning of the catechism. Such learning is entitled memorising and routine. Studying is required for [all inst] all knowledge, where it is not to be a mere craft.\nOne even learns a language without studying it.",[]],"refl:1956":["This poverty arises in two ways. 1. When one has learned knowledge of nothing. 2. When one has learned knowledge of a very small *object*, e.g. the criticism of the Latin language. 3. When one has it in an incomplete manner.",[]],"refl:1957":["Thus the common man knows nothing at all of the course of the stars. A mere maker of almanacs knows it without the grounds.\nA historically poor knowledge can [sometimes] only seldom be philosophically rich [but only seldom]. A philosophically poor knowledge can often be historically rich. Scaliger, Salmasius.\n(s The ignorant man who has the arrogance to decide is an idiot. The layman has no knowledge of law.)\n(s Learned and ignorant: historical. *Sophista* and *idiotes*. *Clericus* and *Laicus*. (g *initiati* and *profani*.) *Patronus* and client. *Demagogos* (*patricius*) and *plebeii*. Some always set themselves up as leaders of others.)",[]],"refl:1958":["It would be better to divide [ignorance] into absolutely or unconditionally necessary, conditionally necessary, and voluntary ignorance. (s contingent. E.g. ignorance of the circulation of the blood, of the pressure of the air.)\nIn certain matters ignorance is unavoidable for us, e.g. in the mysteries; the nature of the first elements of bodies. Hypothetically necessary ignorance is that which we have owing to lack of aids to knowledge: e.g. [the ignorance], in the ancient astronomers, of the moons and ring of Saturn, or of the animalcules in pepper-water, or, in the heathen, of the right way to serve God. [Voluntary] Wilful [ignorance] arises either from negligence or from incorrect application of the powers of the mind. The former is the commonest source; hence much is also decried as impossible which is not so, e.g. the nature of the bond between soul and body, etc. *Philosophia pigrorum*. The latter occurs when one cultivates the historical part more than the philosophical, e.g. philologists; experimental physics; theologians who treat only of revelation. (g Deliberately, voluntarily.)",[]],"refl:1959":["Absolutely and conditionally.\nThis condition is external, e.g. microscope, *tubus*; internal: in the will.\nWilful: when the ignorance is contrary to reason.\nRational: e.g. Socrates.",[]],"refl:1960":["The limits of learned knowledge, which are determined either by the nature of the *object* and its relation to us, or by our duties.",[]],"refl:1961":["We cannot know everything in a learned manner. We need not know everything in a learned manner. We ought not to know everything in a learned manner. 1. The state of our soul before birth. 2. What has no consequences [for] in practical insights, e.g. the slippers of the ancients; whether there were philosophers before the Flood. 3. We [can] ought not, e.g. the [state] kind of happiness of the future life. [The mysteries] The commonest practical arts.",[]],"refl:1962":["Incorrectness of the expression »above the horizon«.\n2nd rule: we must set nothing above the horizon without ground, e.g. [by saying] that it is impossible to have insight into the manner of generation of men and animals.\n1st rule: we must believe that much is raised [beyond] above our horizon. Folly of the free-thinkers. That human knowledge is not connected with all possible objects. Comparison with someone who sent *tabulae logarithmorum*, or a book on the pure *calculus algebraicus*, among the Hottentots.\n3rd rule: what is above my horizon is not on that account above the horizon of others. For the humbling of vanity.\nE.g. to measure the mountains in the moon.\n»I do not comprehend the thing: it is impossible.«",[]],"refl:1963":["If a matter is in all probability set above the horizon |\nand the striving after it cannot be kept up without detriment, one must give it up. Gold-making, quadrature of the circle.",[]],"refl:1964":["Nothing is below the horizon (g of learned knowledge) except that of which one knows that learned knowledge cannot and need not carry it further than common knowledge does. (g 2. where learned knowledge is merely historical and has no influence on insights and rational knowledge: heraldry.)\nKnowledge of insects seems contemptible to some; likewise that of filth; for the physician, the inspection of urine.",[]],"refl:1965":["E.g. the philosophical cobbler. Mathematical method in those parts of knowledge which in turn are connected with no learned knowledge. *De gestu oratoris*.",[]],"refl:1966":["Socrates': *qvae supra nos, nihil ad nos* [what is above us is nothing to us].",[]],"refl:1967":["What, in respect of the (g chief) aim of men, is useless and (g yet) too [laborious] extensive to be known in a learned manner without detriment to that aim, is outside the horizon, etc. etc. E.g. metaphysics for a mathematician; syllogistic for a soldier; womenfolk.\nOne must neither extend nor restrict the horizon too much. He who wants to know too much knows nothing. He who believes of certain things that they do not concern him often deceives himself, e.g. history in the case of the philosopher. But of poetry he may believe it.",[]],"refl:1968":["One must have a horizon of learned knowledge. (s Not wishing to learn everything.)\nOne must not change the horizon of one's knowledge too much. (s Not falling from one thing to another.)\nOne must not be so rash as to determine the horizon of others: first, because one does not know their capacities well enough; secondly, because one does not know their aims well enough.",[]],"refl:1969":["[Since] The extent of knowledge depends: 1. on the magnitude of one's horizon, that is, on the number of *objects* known; 2. on their importance.",[]],"refl:1970":["To this belongs: 1. that he examine for which part of knowledge he has the greatest capacity and liking; 2. what is more or less necessary to him in (g respect) of certain duties; [That] 3. what cannot coexist with the necessary duties; 4. that he seek rather to extend his horizon too much than to narrow it.",[]],"refl:1971":["The imperfection of knowledge is, 1st, either of defect or of privation; the former:\nof ignorance; the latter: of error.\n2nd\nIgnorance either concerns the material, and is a defect of historical knowledge, or the formal, and is a defect of rational knowledge.\nEvery error is a formal imperfection.\n[Ignorance] [The imperfection of knowledge concerns either the material (ignorance) or the formal]\nIgnorance is either material: defect of historical knowledge;\nor formal: defect of rational knowledge.",[]],"refl:1972":["The horizon of the human race (s in general, or of one man in particular.)",[]],"refl:1973":["The horizon is determined logically, practically and aesthetically. (s objectively, or subjectively. Popular. Womenfolk. Courtier.)\nThe logical horizon is either historical or rational. The former is much wider than the latter; but it [lim] is limited by its relation to reason, or to utility, or to taste. (s Private horizon.)\nOn the horizon of what men can know in all the sciences in general.",[]],"refl:1974":["The horizon of modes of knowledge according to age, sex, rank or way of life. Logically determined (above or within); practically (below or outside).\nTo find the horizon of my knowledge. Not to change it lightly. To compare it with that of others and of the whole human race.\n(s 1. Objectively: what we cannot or need not know.)",[]],"refl:1975":["(g Adequacy) Congruence (g of the limits) of knowledge [and] with the limits of human perfection (s of the ends of men: the actual or the necessary) [is] obtains in so far as knowledge, objectively considered, is adequate both to the faculty of knowledge and to utility. Accordingly the horizon is determined both logically and practically.\nIf the knowledge is less than the logically determined horizon, [of less is what imperfection is] it is nevertheless in itself possible for man; if it is greater, it is impossible; if it surpasses (g exceeds) use, it is practically impossible (g since it does harm); if it is not sufficient for use, or if it conduces little, it is practically possible, but is not adequate to practical necessity. The radius of the horizon is determined by the greatest utility according to one's powers.\n(s The horizon is determined practically through choice [*arbitrium*].)",[]],"refl:1976":["On the heading \nThe quantity of knowledge as regards degree is universality (reason);\nas regards multitude, extent (historical).\n(s The magnitude of knowledge can be understood materially or formally; in the first case it is extensive or intensive. In the second it is the logical magnitude of universality *in abstracto*, or the aesthetic magnitude of fullness of thought *in concreto*.)",[]],"refl:1977":["Something goes above my horizon when it requires a greater radius, a greater height of my point of view, than lies within my capacity.\nSomething is, or lies, outside my horizon when it requires a different standpoint from the one in which I am placed.\nHorizon is the adequacy of the magnitude of the whole of one's modes of knowledge to the capacities and ends of the *subject*.\nThe horizon will differ either in magnitude or in position, that is, according to the standpoint.",[]],"refl:1978":["2. Ignorance of the grounds of knowledge.\n3. Ignorance of the grounds of things. The common man asks what something means, or what use it is; the philosopher, whence it comes, its cause; the rational man, on what grounds this knowledge is accepted as true.",[]],"refl:1979":["The universal horizon; the particular horizon in respect of certain kinds of men and ends; and the individual horizon for a single head.\nMy horizon is not the measure of that of others.",[]],"refl:1980":["The outer extension of knowledge: when the public also takes part. The inner: that of the learned man.\nIntensive magnitude loses in the former case. But that [magnitude] which accords at the same time with quality, enlightenment, can increase thereby.\nAdequacy to the (g necessary) ends of humanity: both for oneself and as a citizen.",[]],"refl:1981":["On the heading \nMagnitude of extent: multitude.\nMagnitude of content — manifold validity.\nMultitude of knowledge: extensive magnitude.\nDegree of knowledge: intensive magnitude;\n*non multa, sed multum* [not many things, but much].\n( s (g Quantity) 1. manifoldness. 2. content. )",[]],"refl:1982":["I cannot, I need not, I ought not to know certain things. The last I cannot\n|\nsay on account of uselessness — below the horizon — but in order not to be a hindrance to other ends.",[]],"refl:1983":["Does not belong to logic, but to the architectonic of philosophy [Weltweisheit].",[]],"refl:1984":["Imperfection in respect of magnitude.\nOf quality. Form. Either of lack or of error, potential or actual.\nDifference in magnitude. Lack or\ncompleteness\nDifference in quality. Error or truth\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\nrelatively to the *object*.\nDifference [in form] in *modus*: distinct, certain (relatively to other modes of knowledge).",[]],"refl:1985":["On the heading \nExtension and degree. E.g. boiling water in a kettle [has] more heat, but not greater [heat].\nMany arguments, each of which proves nothing at all.\n(s Objective) universality is logical magnitude,\n(s subjective) [manifoldness] aesthetic.\nDistinctness is the ground of logical perfection.\nLiveliness is the ground of aesthetic perfection.",[]],"refl:1986":["The quantity of knowledge is either objective or subjective.\nEach is either material or formal.",[]],"refl:1987":["*Amplitudo cognitionis* [extent of knowledge].\n*Intensitas cognitionis* [intensity of knowledge].\nThe perfection [of knowledge] in respect of quantity is, materially, its extent [Weitläuftigkeit] and, formally, its universality; in respect of quality it is, materially, truth and, formally, distinctness and certainty.",[]],"refl:1988":["The mathematician has a large field of knowledge (for him it is infinite). The philosopher has a larger field of objects, namely, including the supersensible, of which he has no knowledge, or at least no theoretical knowledge. For the supersensible of morality is capable only of practical knowledge.",[]],"refl:1989":["On the heading:\n[It] concerns the matter of knowledge, and therefore does not belong to logic.\nOn L § 41, beginning:\n(g in poetry) *nugaeque canorae* [and melodious trifles].\n*Splendida miseria* [splendid poverty], in contrast with wealth, the *opulentia* [richness] of knowledge.",[]],"refl:1990":["It is of great importance to determine the limits of human knowledge, and therewith its *territorium* [territory], its *sphaeram* [sphere].",[]],"refl:1991":["We must first ask, not about utility, but about perfection (g of knowledge).\nThe horizon of humanity in general (g whether [it is to be determined] by utility — division of labour in study) and the private horizon, e.g. that of a social station, an age, a sex.",[]],"refl:1992":["Acquaintance with the horizon is needed in order to determine the limits of one's knowledge, and to do so according to principles. 1. What one cannot know*, in order to know how little it is that men can know. 2. What, in view of our purpose, we need not know.** 3. That the knowledge of which is dispensable and worthless for everyone. (g The various chimeras in religion.) (g What we ought not to know. Harmful to us.) (g What we ought not to brood over, e.g. the impure intentions of men or divine judgments.) Demarcation |\nof our knowledge. Ignorance is not disgraceful. A considered renunciation of various kinds of knowledge, leaving them to others, is praiseworthy; [compare] the distribution of the land among different cultivators. [There should be] no contempt for what one does not need. *Ars non habet osorem* [art has no enemy].\n*(g One must not assume this too hastily. And yet [one must] proceed according to method.)\n**(g In religion man as man (g esoteric, exoteric) needs but little; but the purpose here is practical.)\n(g III. Whether the horizon of sound reason restricts that of science?\nThe horizon determined by others: men need not know everything.)\n(g 1) What can I know or not? 2. What may I [know] or not? 3. What ought I [to know] or not? — The last is no breaks off.)\n(s In youth we amass knowledge; in old age we seek to judge its worth.)\n(s In youth we cannot ourselves determine our horizon.)\n(s The horizon of knowledge through reason needs to be drawn according to principles, above all where experience cannot serve as a guide. *Ars non habet osorem nisi ignorantem* [art has no enemy save the ignorant].)",[]],"refl:1993":["The horizon (g determined theoretically or practically) is either the universal horizon of humanity or a particular one; the latter is either that of popular knowledge in general or that of science.\n[Pr] [It is] subjectively determined by sex, age, vocation [Bestimmung].",[]],"refl:1994":["Private horizon and universal horizon.",[]],"refl:1995":["Speculative self-knowledge. It [the horizon] is determined: 1. logically, according to the faculty; 2. aesthetically, according to taste; 3. practically, according to utility — the interest of the understanding, of feeling and of the will.\nThe logically determined horizon: A) in respect of the objects, 1. of historical knowledge, 2. of rational knowledge, and the latter either a. pure or b. physical. B) In respect of the subject: 1. the private horizon, 2. the universal horizon of human knowledge (g of the species) in general.\n(s The (theoretically) logically determined horizon: what we can know; the practically [determined]: what we ought [to know].)",[]],"refl:1996":["12'.\nL 12:\nThe horizon is aesthetically determined (g popularity) by its |\nconformity to the good tone of society. |\nL 12': |\nThe good tone is either that of gravity, or the tone of the court (g or of politeness, complaisance), or that of flattery; [the former makes the] the latter makes the *galant* man; the same combined with the last: the *petit maître*. French omniscience. It is, as it were, a drawing in bird's-eye perspective: surface.",[]],"refl:1997":["The limits of the empirical knowledge of the whole human race cannot, indeed, be determined *a priori*; but naturally it has limits. Historians a thousand years hence cannot know as much as we know now, and in addition what will have happened in those thousand years. The same perhaps holds of the observer of nature. The mathematician will perhaps be unable to hold all the discoveries already made in one head, and so will be unable to discover anything of which he could be sure that it had not been discovered before. But then everything will rest on the discovery of methods (in natural history) and principles, by which we can, without burdening the memory, find everything for ourselves at will. Hence he who brings history under ideas that can endure deserves well of it, like a genius. Otherwise, if as much must be forgotten as is added, knowledge will no longer grow, or will even decline for want of encouragement to new modes of knowledge.",[]],"refl:1998":["(s Horizon of the sound understanding or of speculative reason.)\nOne must determine one's horizon early, and not change it lightly (g [but] always extend it). [Skill. Practical horizon.] [One must] not measure the horizon of others by one's own (g division of labour), nor hold that to be useless which is of no use to us (g which does not please us; *ars non habet osorem*). [One must] seek to determine in advance the horizon of the whole human race (in respect of past and future time), e.g. history. [One must] seek to determine the place of one's science within the horizon of knowledge as a whole (g and to unite it with others). *Encyclopaedia universalis*. Universal chart. (g *Mappemonde* of the sciences.)\nThere is no need to fear what d'Alembert fears. With the extension of mathematics new methods will be discovered which abridge the old and make the multitude of books dispensable. Uns[beschw] What oppresses us is not the burden; rather, the volume narrows the space for our modes of knowledge. Critique of reason, of history and of historical writings, [and] a universal spirit which [auf de] is directed to human knowledge *en gros* and not merely in detail, will continually make the extent smaller without diminishing anything in the content; only the dross falls away from the metal, or the baser vehicle or husk which was necessary for so long.",[]],"refl:1999":["On the heading:\nQuantity — extensive (knowledge spread either internally within one man or externally among many men);\nintensive: the degree, i.e. magnitude as the ground of many and great consequences.\nThe sphere of a man's knowledge: whether something lies outside or within it, and in general whether a man keeps within his |\nsphere (of knowledge or of business) or goes beyond it. This is distinct from the horizon of a man, [namely] what he (g ought,) can and may know. The latter concerns the faculty; the former the end. — Logically, aesthetically and practically determined horizon. — Only the first belongs here.",[]],"refl:2000":["On the heading:\nWe here speak of the [perfection] quantity of a knowledge in general, in so far as we abstract from its [quality] specific difference, [namely] whether it is empirical or rational.",[]],"refl:2001":["The horizon of opinions must not extend to pure truths of reason, but only to empirically grounded propositions. The horizon of knowing [Wissen], in respect of speculative knowledge, is unnecessary for the practical; but the practical one must know. The horizon of belief extends only to the defence of the practical, which is attacked by speculation.",[]],"refl:2002":["Owing to the limitations of the understanding there always remains ignorance.\nFormally, this is of two kinds: learned and common ignorance.\n[The latter: when one] The first: when one clearly discerns the limitations of knowledge, and so the field of ignorance, [and] where it begins (g and thus knows the magnitude and the causes of the ignorance). E.g. a surveyor can determine by how many feet he may err in taking a certain distance. A philosopher knows where ignorance begins owing to lack of the given data [und des Man]. E.g. a philosopher proves, according to the rules of art, how little we can know regarding the structure of gold.\nThe second is unconcernedly ignorant, without [grent] discerning the grounds and limits of the ignorance.\nMaterially: ignorance, contemptuous [ignorance] of objects too exalted (s Enthusiasts extol ignorance, theological enthusiasts as well as enthusiasts of genius), or of those that are necessary, worthy and capable of being known.\nA woman who has studied is not ashamed not to know the logical figures.",[]],"refl:2003":["Blameless (s *inculpabilis*, not *meritoria*): [ignorance of things] that are above our horizon.",[]],"refl:2004":["Misology.\nOn L § 50, ‘praiseworthy ignorance’:\nin respect of the secret malice of men. Misanthropy.",[]],"refl:2005":["Voluntary.\nFurther, at the end of L § 50, from β1−ε2:\npraiseworthy.",[]],"refl:2006":["Learned ignorance: when one discerns in a learned manner why we do not know something; e.g. that it is not certain that bodies exist.",[]],"refl:2007":["Not ignorance, but knowledge of one's ignorance, the |\nmodesty of opinion regarding one's knowing, which does not puff one up. What Socrates knew is praiseworthy; this knowledge presupposes science.",[]],"refl:2008":["In respect of the speculative employment of our cognitive faculties no ignorance is praiseworthy; at most it is permissible, because it [concerns what], though not above our horizon, is yet outside it.",[]],"refl:2009":["There is a difference between not knowing and ignoring something, i.e. taking no notice of it. It is good to ignore much which it is not good for us to know. (s Much hidden evil (g impurity of heart) in men, or evil gossip.) (s What others say to our disadvantage (Socrates: let us so act that no one believes that man).) From both, again, is distinguished abstracting, i.e. when one ignores the application (g still suspends the knowledge of something), whereby one obtains the knowledge *in abstracto* and can better consider it in its universality as a principle.\n(g It is (g for a prince) a duty to ignore what belongs to the private life of his subjects, private opinions, etc.)",[]],"refl:2010":["At the end of L § 51:\nA shameful ignorance: 1. of such things as are very necessary to him and also easy to know.",[]],"refl:2011":["From the theoretical point of view much ignorance is *inculpabilis* [blameless], but none *laudabilis* [praiseworthy]. From the practical, it is often praiseworthy, on account of the wrong that we might do to others by inquiring, or of inner corruption.",[]],"refl:2012":["Teachers of reason [Vernunftlehrer] [speculatively learned] are commonly historically ignorant.\n*Idiota* and *sophista*.\n(s Ignoramus and philosopher. The former can be a sophist; idiot and philologist. The former can be a humanist; *laicus* and *clericus*.) s In both, skill is distinguished from critical science; handicraft from art. )\n(s Limitations and limits.\nA scholar without science.)",[]],"refl:2013":["One can never represent one's ignorance except through science, just as a blind man cannot represent darkness to himself until he has gained his sight.\n(s Socrates. The ignorant man does not even know that he knows nothing.)",[]],"refl:2014":["What we have said of the extent (g sphere) of human knowing, and within it of the determination of the sphere of each [individual], properly belongs in the scholia.",[]],"refl:2015":["Such a man is like a nobleman who seeks only to acquire many estates, but is in debt on all of them, or like a traveller who travels through the whole earth in a few years.",[]],"refl:2016":["Excess in this is entitled learned windbaggery, charlatanry, an inordinate craving for wide reading.\nFurther, from µ−ψ:\nPhilology: the science of the instruments of learning.\nFurther, from ψ−θ:\nCritique of the sciences diminishes pride.\nFurther, L 15', from β1:\n*Pansophia*.\nFurther, from ρ−ψ:\nPolymathy is concerned with knowledge through reason.\nFurther, from φ (? µ−ξ?):\nPolyhistory puffs up.",[]],"refl:2017":["A shallow age: when science becomes general, and the public judges in Latin.",[]],"refl:2018":["(s *Humaniora* — barbarous learning.)\n(s What is learning, and what is a learned man? Not a philosopher, but yet, in the sense of the faculties, a *litteratus*.)\nAll learning is either barbarous or rational, without the superintendence of philosophy.\nThe barbarism of learning rests on tradition, superstition, etc., and in our time is still to be met with chiefly in history, either because history does not offer philosophy a helping hand, or because it is bound by theology.\nBarbarous learning may contain much diligence, but without an end, without an idea, above all [without one directed] to the good of the human race.\n[Another fault is the]\nBarbarism is not removed by elegance; for elegance does not belong to learning, but to taste and culture. It is rather a decline of all learning when, in place of knowing, playing gains ground, in fables and poetry. It concerns only the manner of things.\n( s *Humaniora**. Refinement. Politeness. Urbanity. Rusticity. *\ng (s Acquaintance with the ancients.) Which promote the union of\nscience with taste, wear away roughness,\nand promote the communicability of men,\nin which humanity consists.\n)",[]],"refl:2019":["Historical learning — science of reason.\nThe philologist is the connoisseur of the instruments of learning: languages and books.\nCritical philologists.",[]],"refl:2020":["A Cyclops is a mathematician, historian, describer of nature, philologist and linguist who is great in all these things and holds all philosophy about them to be dispensable.\nPolyhistory: Cyclopean learning.\n(s *Belletrist* — *bel esprit* affectation [Schöngeisterey].)",[]],"refl:2021":["The Cyclopean scholar: one who lacks an eye, namely philosophy, while possessing great science in other respects, and who nevertheless pronounces on everything.",[]],"refl:2022":["The humanist (*belletrist*), *bel esprit*.\nThe linguist.\nThe *litterator*.\nThe philologist. (g *pertinet vel ad eruditionem vel ad humanitatem vel ad cognitionem rationalem* [belongs either to erudition, or to humanity, or to rational knowledge].)\nThe polyhistor. Scaliger. Cyclopean learning.\nThe *pansophus*.\n[The philosopher] The universal spirit. Philosopher. Mathematician. Historian. Poet. Orator. Properly, the architect of the sciences.\nLearning and taste [drawn] from works in dead languages.",[]],"refl:2023":["Architectonic of the sciences, which considers their affinity and their systematic connection in a whole of the knowledge that is of interest to humanity. It is philosophical and therefore not polyhistory. A system according to ideas. Encyclopaedia is distinct from it. Compendium.",[]],"refl:2024":["Seven *artes*: logic, arithmetic, dialectic, astronomy, [orator] poetry, rhetoric and music.\nNine Muses and three Graces.\nWork and\nplay.\nThe sciences serve either immediately for agreeableness or for gain. The former: liberal arts; the latter: bread-and-butter art [Brodkunst].",[]],"refl:2025":["1. All [sciences] instruction belongs either to humanity or to science (s skill). The former contains the liberal arts (s *ars ingenua, liberalis*), which merely civilise (s please for their own sake) and [eine auf Geschmak sittl nebst ein] establish the agreeableness [der in der wechs.] of the mutual communication of men. They are distinguished from the bread-and-butter arts (s *ars mercenaria — servilis*), which [sich] are only means [zum s] to our self-interested ends.\n2. To science (s *vel habitus vel scientia*) belong historical science and science of reason. The sum of the historical is learning (s polyhistory — polymathy). [Brodkunst und freye Kunst] [Das Werkz] The sum of the instruments of learning is philology. Consequently [diese] the philologist comprises the linguist [den humanisten] (s and) the *litterator*. (g His opposite is the *belletrist*.) (s And philology belongs to science; humanistic study to liberal art.) Learned languages and books in all sciences. Historical [Kennt] knowledge of all sciences is polyhistory.\n3. The sum of knowledge through reason is (s not learning, i.e. a science that must be taught to one, but one which each can discover for himself) philosophy and mathematics. Polymathy.\n(s Cultivation through the latter is that of the mode of thought.)\nA universal mind [mit] driven by vanity makes the pansophist.\n(g Ancient models of good style (g and knowledge of the world), or merely the [Kentnis] skill of becoming acquainted with the ancients. The English. [Verfei] Culture of sound understanding and of taste.\nPainstakingness (punctiliousness) in respect of order, with neglect of the end.\nCyclopean learning without philosophy.)",[]],"refl:2026":["All instruction is either in art or in science. The former is either bread-and-butter art or liberal art. The first is handicraft. Liberal art is that which no one can himself produce when moved by payment, in which, therefore, the spirit is wholly free, i.e. the art of genius. Such, properly, are only the arts of the spirit and not those of bodily use.",[]],"refl:2027":["The polyhistor\nas philologist [ist humani] cultivates;\nas humanist\ncivilises.\nAs philologist he is linguist and *litterator*;\nas humanist he is [a student of the] classics and their interpreter.\nThe *belletrist* is the ape of the humanist.\nThe humanist [who works] according to (g contemporary) models [zu se] in living languages is no scholar. Only dead languages are now learned languages.\n(g To culture belongs that of *habitus* or *scientia*.)\nThe philologist differs from the humanist in that the former seeks among the ancients the instruments of learning, the latter the instruments for the formation of taste.\nThe *belletrist** is no scholar (g connoisseur), but a dilettante in the knowledge of matters of taste, according to fashion. He does not need the ancients. He |\nis a humanist of the contemporary writings of taste (s or rather the ape of the humanist).\n*(g *Institutio* (g methodical culture) *pertinet vel ad humanitatem vel ad* (g *scientiam*) *eruditionem latius dictam* [instruction belongs either to humanity or to erudition in the wider sense].)",[]],"refl:2028":["It turns on sufficiency in respect of quantity and of degree. *Extensionis et intensionis* [of extension and of intension].",[]],"refl:2029":["Order in study.\nWhat you can do today, do not put off till tomorrow.",[]],"refl:2030":["*Ars longa, vita brevis*. Hippocrates.\nMotive.\n1.\nTo let useless trifles go;\n2.\nto let no time [fahren] pass [that might serve] for the acquisition of an important mode of knowledge.",[]],"refl:2031":["As, in conversation, to appear learned; to earn one's bread.",[]],"refl:2032":["Of people who forget little. Likewise of those who forget too much.\nThe extent of knowledge can become a cause of forgetfulness when it has been acquired without the order of reason, without reflection, and without those characteristics which are discerned through close attention. In that case [etwas] much may be forgotten and yet much remains.",[]],"refl:2033":["On the concluding sentence of L § 61:\nOne who learns music forgets the pieces he learnt at first, but retains the facility in playing. So, after we have lost the particular items of knowledge from memory, there remains a facility for thinking further in the manner of the knowledge once acquired.\nYet one must guard as far as possible against negligence in attention and in the faculty of recollection.",[]],"refl:2034":["E.g. the science of philosophy and mathematics in its whole extent.\nOn L § 62, no. 2:\nThis is either the natural or the acquired capacity.",[]],"refl:2035":["If this principal science has but slight influence either upon knowledge or upon use, one must combine it with other sciences, in which one must likewise seek to become proficient.\nFurther, from µ−φ:\nNot a bread-and-butter art, but the principal business.\nThe principal science from an economic or a learned point of view.\nFurther, from µ−φ:\nCyclopean learning. The one eye must always be philosophy. Subsidiary science belonging to humanity and to the connection among scholars.\nFurther, from φ (ω?):\nTheology (g as *disciplina principalis*) has the most auxiliary sciences (g *subsidiarias*).",[]],"refl:2036":["If the principal science is not large enough to serve as a point of reference, he must employ it as far as possible in aid of others.",[]],"refl:2037":["(s The misuse of a small knowledge: when one holds it [vor] to be complete in all things.)\nExample of Faustus.\nPedantry: when one always brings in what one knows (s from lack of knowledge), without regard to where it is fitting or useful.\nThere are many kinds of pedants: military pedants, political pedants, pedants of finery and gallantry. Of the *Rep. Sabinensi*. School pedants. E.g. He said: O finery, etc. A strict connoisseur, etc.: the wine which, etc.\nIn medicine: when he cures only according to Hippocrates; in jurisprudence: when he judges according to the *placitis clarorum Jurisconsultorum* [opinions of famous jurists]. Theological pedant: when he is always citing the Fathers.\nCharlatanry: when this use of one's limited knowing arises from arrogance.\nPedantry is, moreover, also taken objectively, when it signifies useless hair-splitting.\nOne must not call that pedantry whose use one does not know; e.g. many logical principles. Example: the drilling of soldiers.\nCorruption of the present time: that we rear no learned pedants, but yet aesthetic ones.\nOne must apply oneself to sciences of which one can make use in conversation; e.g. modern history, geography, *jus naturae*, morals.\n(s Learned *petits maîtres*, who pass off a common knowledge as a learned one.)\n(s Popularity in writing books, both in content and in language (lack of Latin; Parliament, on account of the Code).)",[]],"refl:2038":["Exactness and assiduity in respect of knowledge which is only a means, without the skill to make it conform to its ends.\nWhen one makes, not the use of one's skill conform to the occasions, but these to the display of one's skill.\nA defect of sound [und pr] understanding [und der] (s in respect of the use of learning). Skill, as it were, which is not directed by prudence in its application.\n(s Expert and amateur.)",[]],"refl:2039":["18'.\nL 18:\nPunctiliousness about rules [or precepts] is pedantry. Practical rules are not always [laws, i.e. *a priori* sufficient] precepts or norms. An action is not good or right because it conforms to them; rather they are general directions as to the [Mitt] way in which what is right and good is to be attained. But their execution requires sound understanding and therefore judgment, (*in concreto*) the rules, e.g. for making out the braid for a hat. School knowledge. Pedant [Schulfuchs]. Calligraphy.\nL 18':\nHe who takes an empirical rule, or rule of necessity, for a law is a pedant.",[]],"refl:2040":["Knowledge as it can be communicated universally (g dilettante) or [auch d] only to connoisseurs.\nPopularity in respect of content or of manner.\n(s The great world. School or toilette.)\nPopularity, when it becomes universal, becomes the cause of shallowness.\n*Malim convivis quam placuisse coquis* [I would rather please the guests than the cooks].\nPedantry affects only the passage of knowledge out of the school into the world.\nGallantry is the courting of the approval of taste: affectation.\nScholars by profession or by taste.\nGuild scholars\n|\nScholars outside the guild\n(g The French are the great masters of popularity, the Germans the least, and they err by an affected gallantry.)\n(g Popularity consists in this, that participation in a science is not business but play.)",[]],"refl:2041":["Of the rules (g and conditions) of the popularity of a learned knowledge, e.g. that of philosophers. [This arises] when one treats nothing [and] philosophically from any other point of view than because it interests the amateur, or even merely social intercourse; e.g. prejudices, knowledge of men, the weather, ghosts, birthmarks; *aer fixus* in champagne, and pyromancy. Popularity in matters or in methods. The latter is a work of genius. The scholastic philosopher must have much understanding; but the popular one [must have] much practical judgment and knowledge of men through intercourse.\nPopularity in respect of the understanding or of taste.",[]],"refl:2042":["In society one must not repeat and recite one's school lesson, but make use of the skill acquired through that lesson. A certain form belongs merely to learning and falls away in practice; if it is retained, it is entitled pe|dantry.\nThe constraint of rules, in so far as it shows through, is pedantry.\n(s Punctiliousness of scholastic perfection in the free use of the understanding.)\n(s Popularity is the general usefulness of knowledge in respect of the community.\nThe professional or the amateur (g dilettante, connoisseur of art) of science. Making general.)",[]],"refl:2043":["The pedant is the slave of rules.\nThe *galant homme* is the freethinker in regard to rules.\n(s The craving for genius is free-thinking in regard to rules.)\n(s *Magister pedaneus*.)",[]],"refl:2044":["The manner of teaching or learning a science is either (s correct by the standard of the school) expert or (s competent in the matter) popular (g esoteric or exoteric); the first is scholastically correct, the second (g generally useful) condescending. The restriction (g narrowings) of knowledge through the constraint of the school is pedantry (when one does not make it generally intelligible or pleasing); the shallowness of knowledge through popularity is the gallantry* of style. Knowledge of the world, in so far as it moderates scholastic constraint without loss of thoroughness, is the courtly [manner].\n*(s Or rather [arising] from taste and the refinement of the world. To spoil knowledge out of gallantry. Punctiliousness of method — thorough and popular. For school and world.)",[]],"refl:2045":["Scholars, though not those by trade, are the *dépositaires* of all the knowledge and science on which the great world prides itself [and outside the guild]. It would soon run wild into bears, as indeed it was before, if scholastic diligence were to slacken. Scholars outside the guild can be formed only by members of the guild, and every science receives only in the hands of the latter its exact determination or *praecision*, its order and distinctness, its auxiliary means, its extent and limits. Knowledge of the world furthers only the application of science suited to circumstances, which is either courtly or *galant*. The condescension of learning merely to the simplicity of common sound reason, without flattering either pride or the [ease] refinement of taste, is the greatest achievement of judgment. Use of polyhistory: to know how a science borrows auxiliary means from others. Gesner's *Isagoge*.",[]],"refl:2046":["Pedantry is opposed to knowledge of the world, which [eine] is merely an extended knowledge of the fitting use of science, and so an enlarged judgment.",[]],"refl:2047":["18'.\nor 2. Gallantry. (s *Petit-maître* manner.) Thoroughness and politeness. (s *Ostentatio erudita*, |\nL 18': |\n*fastus* [learned ostentation, pride].)",[]],"refl:2048":["Lack of popularity arising from habituation to the scholastically correct is pedantry. Lack of the natural (*bon sens*) arising from striving to be artful is affectation. The surprising manner of the natural, which comes close to art, is naïveté.\n(s He who cannot communicate otherwise than in a scholastically correct manner is a pedant.)",[]],"refl:2049":["Pedantry: the universal in abstract formulae.\nPopularity: the universal in experience.\nGallantry: [directed] to universal taste.\nCharlatanry: empty learning and pomp of wide reading.",[]],"refl:2050":["There are methods which are necessary only for learning, but fall away in application and in life. These are scholastically correct.",[]],"refl:2051":["Knowledge of the world and school wisdom. Sound understanding.",[]],"refl:2052":["The conformity of knowledge to the object is scholastic perfection (s thoroughness).\nConformity for all subjects is popularity. The striving after scholastic perfection to the detriment of a possible popularity is pedantry.",[]],"refl:2053":["Pedantry is learning without any popularity.",[]],"refl:2054":["The *bel esprit* apes the tone of the learned and becomes a chatterer; the scholar [apes] the tone of gallantry and becomes a pedant.",[]],"refl:2055":["The go-cart of rules, or the compass.",[]],"refl:2056":["Besides the objective perfection of knowledge, it must also have the property of being communicated as easily (g and universally) as possible. If science is restricted only to the use of learners and yet comes out into the world before amateurs, it is pedantic.",[]],"refl:2057":["Conscendent, [rising] to learning. Condescendent, [descending] to common concepts.",[]],"refl:2058":["*Lenocinia orationis* [meretricious ornaments of speech]. Coquettish beauty. *Veneres*.",[]],"refl:2059":["Popularity is the skill (also the mode of thought) of accommodating oneself to the interest and capacity of others. Popularity in exposition is the talent of accommodating one's knowing to the interest and capacity of common reason (or of mere amateurs). Such a philosophy presupposes knowledge of man and clear insight, in order, even without one's tools of scholastic expressions and methods, to communicate thorough insight [to obtain], and as it were to play, so that science costs no effort, and to bring science into the tone of society.\n(s Boasting and playing. Charlatanry and *petit maître*.)",[]],"refl:2060":["The master of formalities is a pedant. Punctiliousness in formalities: pedantry.\nInflated learning without the manners of social intercourse: pedantry.\nThe pedant is most conspicuous in intercourse with women. |\nHe is full of formality and scholastic method. Pedantry in the military estate.",[]],"refl:2061":["17.\nL 17':\nA man is a head (*ingenium*) who thinks for himself.\nA man is a [mere] brush who merely imitates, a mere instrument, as it were, which another uses to copy his thoughts.\nIn the latter [Pünctlik] exactitude degenerates into punctiliousness (s micrology).\n(s Pedantry and gallantry in arts and sciences. Mode of representation.)\n(s Micrology in formalities. Pedantism. Affected thoroughness, and affected popularity (s gallantry). Scholastic or popular.)\nThoroughness requires a methodical head; brooding punctiliousness makes a hair-splitter of formalities. Brooding punctiliousness (s useless exactness) in formalities (s school formulae and terminologies) is pedantry ((g as critic) sniffer-out of formalities).\nPurposive exactitude in formalities is thoroughness.\n(s Freedom of the mode of thought from the constraint of formalities.)\n(s Guild scholars — scholars outside the guild.)\n(s *Magister pedaneus*: the formal element of scholastic method outside the school.)\n(s Muses and Graces. Scholastic and popular.)\nThe pedant is a hair-splitter of formalities (like a hair-splitter of words), a brooder over formalities (g a huckster), (g a whittler of formalities (*Logodaedalus*)). He is restricted in respect of the [spirit] kernel of things, and looks [er] only at |\nthe garment and the shell. He is the miscarried imitation or caricature of the methodical head.\nCeremonial at courts, and also in social intercourse: what is it but a hunting and picking after formalities? In the military it is not quite so, though it seems to be. But in conversation [ist], in dress, in diet, in religion there is often much pedantry; |\nL 17: |\nthe old form and cut cannot easily be changed.",[]],"refl:2062":["The pedant is either, as scholar, opposed to the man of the world, and is the inflated scholar without knowledge of the world, i.e. knowledge of how to bring his science home to people; — or [der K] he is, as the man of skill in general, but only in [Anseh] formalities, not in essence and end, opposed to the useful. Matter is always useful.",[]],"refl:2063":["Two degenerations of the prevailing taste in regard to the sciences:\n1. when they are pursued merely for the school: *doctor umbraticus* (s *graeculus*);\n2. [when they are pursued] merely for social intercourse or the world.\nPedantry and gallantry. The first: restrictedness in respect of use; the second: in respect of content.\nScholastic thoroughness and at the same time popularity.\nWhen the fashionable world is the supreme judge, everything becomes shallow and fickle.",[]],"refl:2064":["Purposive exactness in formalities is thoroughness (scholastic perfection, scholastically correct). (g Non-)purposive [G] (contrary to purpose) exactness [is] pedantry. This is taken only relatively.",[]],"refl:2065":["Magnitude is distinct from extent. The latter consists in manifoldness, the former in the quantity of consequences. E.g. a pension of a thousand @ French is much smaller than one of some hundred @ English.",[]],"refl:2066":["The object of the knowledge of the knowledge of antiquities is indeed great in respect of extent, but not in respect of fruitfulness.\nBut mathematics is both.",[]],"refl:2067":["The magnitude of the consequences need not always be measured by bodily utility.",[]],"refl:2068":["When a harvest has yielded much grain and weighty grain, it is great.",[]],"refl:2069":["Arithmetic.",[]],"refl:2070":["Knowledge can be great in respect of what it comprehends under itself, or in respect of what it contains in itself. The former, universal knowledge, is often (g in)determinate for use in particular cases. Knowledge directed to the great, i.e. the whole, in the use of the understanding, is to be distinguished from subtlety in the small, micrology. (s Latin books.)\nA deficiency which does [blos] not conflict with the end is no fault. Hence one must avoid [etwa] professing a great purpose, lest the deficiency afterwards become a fault.\nOne must not, without need, profess [to give] a complete concept or a demonstration.",[]],"refl:2071":["Great in respect of matter or of form. *Non multa, sed multum* [not many things, but much].\nMany kinds.\nHe who goes from the great to the small.",[]],"refl:2072":["Absolute magnitude, or that in respect of consequences (importance).\nAbsolute magnitude, either of content (g mathematical) or of extent (logical magnitude).\nTechnical or architectonic magnitude.",[]],"refl:2073":["18'.\nL 18:\nOf the degree of knowledge and of logical importance, not of practical.\nL 18':\nEvery knowledge is logically important which furthers logical perfection as regards form; e.g. every mathematical proposition, every newly and distinctly discerned law of nature, or philosophical definition of anything. Practical importance cannot be foreseen; it must be waited for.",[]],"refl:2074":["A discovery can be great in respect of the power of mind [required] or of its consequences: difficulty or importance.\nImportant objects do not make important modes of knowledge.",[]],"refl:2075":["What the author calls learned knowledge should be scientific [knowledge], opposed to common [knowledge]; *dignitas* and *foecunditas* — mathematics cultivates in itself, but is nevertheless also a good instrument.",[]],"refl:2076":["1) [In the subject] In respect of the objects, extensively or intensively greater knowledge.\n2. In respect of the subjects: extensively, to many men (through living languages); or intensively, through insight, greater [knowledge] en|lightenment,\nknowledge of what belongs to one's true and necessary ends.",[]],"refl:2077":["It is a great fault to want to know the consequences at once in every part of knowledge. One must do as the miners do.\nA knowledge without important consequences is entitled hair-splitting [Grübeley], e.g. scholastic philosophy.",[]],"refl:2078":["This magnitude is entitled degree; the previous one was entitled extent.\nFurther, L 19':\nThis is the first thing to which one must apply oneself in practice.",[]],"refl:2079":["On the concluding sentence of L § 70:\nBecause otherwise knowledge is kept back from what is important.",[]],"refl:2080":["Of the native land of Homer.",[]],"refl:2081":["At times it contains much, but our knowledge is not proportioned to it. E.g. [the question] of the intermediate state between death and resurrection.",[]],"refl:2082":["Many trifles, when they are combined, must not be regarded as trifles.",[]],"refl:2083":["The knowledge of Celtic antiquities. Of the *marmora* [inscribed marbles]. Runes.",[]],"refl:2084":["Casuistical morality.\nSwift's devout meditations upon a broomstick. The dedication of his treatise on making gold out of dung, to Woodward.",[]],"refl:2085":["He who reflects as zealously upon the seat of the human soul as upon immortality does not think with due proportion. *Ars longa, vita brevis*.\nGreat in small things, and small in great. Cited from Haller.",[]],"refl:2086":["In which he is capable of the knowledge of the greatest scholars.",[]],"refl:2087":["But the power of judgment [Beurteilungskraft] can be guided and formed.",[]],"refl:2088":["or upon one another.",[]],"refl:2089":["A mind [Geist]: 1. a great one,\n2. a strong one,\n3. a fine one,\n4. a small one.\nThat this very fact indicates that we ought to regard as small what a great mind regards as small is deceptive.",[]],"refl:2090":["A small mind: one whose knowledge is not proportioned to things.",[]],"refl:2091":["Example in metaphysics.",[]],"refl:2092":["Electricity. Spinning top. Magnet.",[]],"refl:2093":["The perverse application of the principle [of contradiction] of sufficient reason, of the best world, of the *principium indiscernibilium*.\nO ornament of the best world, etc., etc.\nFurther, L 21' from β1−κ3:\nE.g. mathematical method.",[]],"refl:2094":["E.g. the philosophical cobbler.\nThe venerable and highly estimable *metaphysica*.\nBeside L §. 82 »werden — verachtungs«:\nFundamental crotchets [Grundgrillen].",[]],"refl:2095":["One must not be subtle in trifles.",[]],"refl:2096":["The invention of the air-pump, algebra, Kepler's harmonic ratios\n}\n}\n}\nTo these belong those who collect *observationes*, e.g. meteorological ones.",[]],"refl:2097":["That imperfection which arises from a voluntary neglect of duty is entitled a disgrace. It is our duty to employ our powers of understanding.",[]],"refl:2098":["Important for science, but not for the dilettante.",[]],"refl:2099":["In the doctrine of the human soul, the generation of men and animals is an intermediate truth.",[]],"refl:2100":["Toil is commonly proper to the investigation of sublime truths.",[]],"refl:2101":["E.g. those verses which can be read forwards and backwards; *anagrammata*.\nE.g. the fallacies of the sceptics. Of the ball which is to be forced through a hole, etc. The Liar. The Dreamer.\nFurther, L 22 from ψ−θ:\n*nugae difficiles* [laborious trifles].",[]],"refl:2102":["Many trifles make something great, e.g. in history.\nNo universal truths are trifles.",[]],"refl:2103":["1. According to difference of purpose. In respect of the law of nations, what ceremonial the envoys of each nation have is no great inquiry; but in respect of statecraft it is important.\n2. According to difference of consequences.\nOn the concluding sentence of L §. 89:\nIt may have no great consequences, but yet many consequences, e.g. the rules of propriety.\nWhat constantly recurs, even if it were of little use in each case, is yet of much use.",[]],"refl:2104":["Something may not be great in respect of bodily utility, and yet be so in respect of the understanding.\nLanguage.",[]],"refl:2105":["In respect of theologians it is an indifferent inquiry whether the souls of children take their origin from the souls of their parents, but not in respect of philosophy.",[]],"refl:2106":["When a mind is of pre-eminent greatness, it comprehends trifles also within the range of its knowledge.\nE.g. Leibniz.\nThis is not to be recommended to everyone.",[]],"refl:2107":["On the beginning of L §. 92:\nIt is a mode of knowledge *subjective*, but not *objective*.\nE.g. Ptolemy's system of the world.\nOn the second sentence of L §. 92:\nThere are learned and common falsities, and [things] *formaliter* or *materialiter* false. E.g. thus [*Harmonia praestabilita*] the restoration of all things is a learned falsity, but ghosts a common one.\nFurther, L 23':\nIt is *formaliter* false when something seems to be learned and is yet common, e.g. the *causae aequivocae* of the Scholastics, the *species visibiles*.\nThe plastic force, entelechy, *lusus naturae*.",[]],"refl:2108":["On the beginning of L §. 93:\n*Convenientia cognitionis cum subjecto* [agreement of knowledge with the subject]. E.g. the immortality of the soul.\nOn sentence 2 of L §. 93:\nThe knowledge of the simple parts of bodies.\nOf the cause of the winds.",[]],"refl:2109":["The internal marks [Kenntnisse] of incorrectness rest on the contradiction of the concepts with one another, or on the want of connec|tion;\nthe external: when it contradicts other things, or has been determined by external grounds which are a mark of incorrectness.",[]],"refl:2110":["*Criterium*: means of separation.",[]],"refl:2111":["Of the truth that there is a God we have internal and external marks. *Item*: of the Christian religion.\nBut of the incorrectness of the proposition, the highest good consists in pleasure, we [likewise] have internal and external marks.",[]],"refl:2112":["Ariosto tells the tale that everything which men have lost here on earth is to be found again yonder in the moon; this lacks internal possibility.\nThe eagle which flew about in the air as a machine lacked internal possibility.\nBut the stories of ghosts, and likewise of exorcists, have an internal possibility.\nThe story of the swallows and the sparrow, but not the story of the [nestlings] that were walled up [verstopften Jungen].",[]],"refl:2113":["The possibility of the opposite is a mark of what is arbitrary in a judgment.",[]],"refl:2114":["The possibility or impossibility of knowledge is either *objectiva* or *subjectiva*. The first is determined by the impossibility of the object, the second by the impossibility of the representation of it. Certain knowledge of the inhabitants of the moon is impossible *subjective*; the opinion of purgatory, however, *objective*.\nAbsolute: to know future things beforehand. Hypothetical: to know the figure of the first particles of matter.\nWhat is false *subjective* may be true *objective*, e.g. a dream.",[]],"refl:2115":["If the *objectum* is possible, then the knowledge also is possible *in sensu absoluto*.",[]],"refl:2116":["The practice of the rules of Christianity, says Bayle, is possible *in abstracto*, but not in a prince. The proposal of the Abbé [de Saint-]Pierre was impossible in connection [with other things].\n(s E.g. the proposition, There is a God, is a consequence of correct grounds and a ground of correct consequences.)\nIt is possible in connection [with other things] to see the mountains in the moon. If, therefore, someone knows their existence, the knowledge is possible; but in former times it was impossible.",[]],"refl:2117":["This is an external mark *a posteriori*.",[]],"refl:2118":["(g *Canones*)\n[Something] a mode of knowledge may be possible taken in and by itself, and yet not be possible in connection [with other things]. E.g. a republic of women.",[]],"refl:2119":["If something has no consequences, it is not on that account impossible.",[]],"refl:2120":["Something may be possible in itself and yet not be possible in connection [with other things]. 1. It is groundless, e.g. ghosts; that there are inhabitants within the earth. 2. False consequences, e.g.: if the world is a necessary being, it cannot be altered.",[]],"refl:2121":["Where we perceive no internal impossibility, there the possibility is probable.\n(s If we discover no internal possibility, it is improbable.)\nIf we see nothing which externally contradicts it, the external possibility is probable.\nWhere we assume no ground, either *a priori* or *a posteriori*, there the knowledge is arbitrary.\n(s Subjective holding-to-be-true.)",[]],"refl:2122":["It is not impossible, e.g., that the earth should perish by a comet.\nOn L §. 98 no. 2:\nWe do not have insight into the possibility of the attraction of the magnet.\nOn L §. 98 no. 3:\nE.g. because it is possible, it is not on that account at once true.\nFor a long time the groundlessness of sympathy was not perceived.\nL 24'. On L §. 98 no. 4:\nE.g. immortality of the soul.",[]],"refl:2123":["On the heading\nThe marks of truth are objective or subjective. [The latter eith] the former rational or empirical.",[]],"refl:2124":["On the beginning of L §. 92:\nTruth and falsity are only in judgments.\nIt agrees with the object when it agrees with itself.",[]],"refl:2125":["When one is given a confused or unlearned mode of knowledge and is to make it learned, but gives it back just as it was, this deception is a falsity *quoad formale*; as e.g. in the case of *qualitates occultae*. Circles in definitions.",[]],"refl:2126":["What is truth? [Because] This question can be answered only by such rules as already presuppose that I can distinguish the true from the false.\nWhen I seek the means of preventing incorrectness, I do not yet demand the means of attaining to truth, but first of all [only] to ignorance; just as with the winnowing-shovel I do not demand that the wheat be increased, but that the chaff [Trespen, brome] be removed.\nUntruth is a hindrance to a mode of knowledge, but not igno|rance;\nto remove it, so that there may first be 0, requires an [even] act of a true mode of knowledge.\nIn philosophy, might not error and truth, reckoned together and subtracted from one another, give the same 0 as the grossest ignorance?",[]],"refl:2127":["What is universally valid is valid also for me (ground of moral judgments). What is to be valid for my reason must be universally valid (ground of the impulse to communicate one's knowledge to others [ih], a logical experiment as it were, and of the injustice of the prohibition against writing what one thinks in matters of religion).\nFalsity arises when a judgment according to subjective laws of reason or of other cognitive powers is taken for an objective one.\nA judgment is logical through which I express a property of the object;\naesthetic, when it contains a sensation.\nTruth is the agreement of knowledge with the object (through the knowledge of it), and therefore with itself.\nSensation is not a judgment and is never false.\nThe true judgment must indicate not my own sensation, that is, my state, but the property of the object, and must therefore be universally valid.",[]],"refl:2128":["Truth.\nThe first question in respect of it is: whether the representation be a *sensation* (whether its object be actual).\nThe 2nd question in respect of it is: whether the representation be a mode of knowledge (whether its object be possible).\nThe 3rd question in respect of it is: whether the representation be a knowledge of a mode of knowledge already given (truth of judgments).\nThere is thus, 1, the question about the object and its relation to the representation, without comparing the representation with itself. For if the cause of the representation is subjective, it lies either in the imagination of an external object or in the form of thinking every [external] object under a certain shape.\nThe objective *criterium* of truth is the agreement of the [modes of knowledge] representations in a judgment with one another according to [the] universal laws of understanding and reason, that is, [immediately or by means of] through intuitions or concepts.\nThe subjective *criterium* of truth is the agreement of one judgment with others, both in the same subject and in different subjects.",[]],"refl:2129":["Possibility of the [mode of knowledge] judgment is not yet truth.",[]],"refl:2130":["Truth is known either from grounds *a priori* or from consequences *a posteriori*. The truth of the consequences proves the truth of the knowledge thus: from a [true] false mode of knowledge nothing true can follow; [anything true] flows rather from that part of the knowledge which is true. But where many consequences are true, one believes that all the rest may be true (hypotheses) (s *inductio*); for any incorrectness lying in the proposition would have had to come to light.",[]],"refl:2131":["In logic we cannot set forth the marks of knowledge of the understanding in general, but only the acts of the understanding in general in judgments [vo]. That is, it is merely analytic. A mode of knowledge may be logically correct and yet not on that account true. Where, therefore, there is nothing further to warrant it than logical form, the knowledge is merely dialectical.",[]],"refl:2132":["Agreement with an object cannot be specified without something being determined in respect of this object, that is, [without] determining something in respect of the object. Logic is therefore merely analytic in respect of knowledge in general.",[]],"refl:2133":["(ν−ξ?) L 23.\nMarks of truth in general cannot be specified, because these must always be related to *objecta*; [what can be specified are] only the conditions of a mode of knowledge (g of the understanding) in general, that is, of judgments in general: that they do not contradict themselves. (That is, the understanding cannot itself determine universally whether judgment has been made in conformity with its laws; that must be [the task of] the power of judgment [Urteilskraft] *in casu*.)",[]],"refl:2134":["In judging, illusion [Schein] is opposed to truth *negative*. |\nError [is opposed to it] *contrarie*. Through the first nothing is determined, through the second the opposite.",[]],"refl:2135":["That a mode of knowledge is false we know from its contradicting itself, but only through reason. If it contradicts others, it is false only if it contradicts true modes of knowledge.\nIf falsity cannot be known from an internal contradiction, it can be known from the relation to other modes of knowledge only when all marks of truth thereby cease.",[]],"refl:2136":["Whence the possibility of errors in discursive knowledge, historical as well as rational? And, on the other hand, the freedom from errors in mathematics? Because the latter gives its concepts in intuition *a priori*, and comes down to nothing but congruence.",[]],"refl:2137":["24'.\nL 24:\n1. Possibility of a mode of knowledge in itself. *Categoria subiecti*.\nL 24':\nPrinciples, indemonstrable.\nL 24:\n2, [in relation] its agreement with grounds and consequences.\nL 24':\nproved propositions.\nL 24:\n3. [The correctness of the consequences] With others in combination in a whole, system.\nL 24':\nconfirmed [propositions].",[]],"refl:2138":["Analytic or synthetic mark of truth.",[]],"refl:2139":["Above and to the right\nWe can more easily infer from the consequences to the grounds apagogically than directly, because one false consequence already makes a mode of knowledge false.",[]],"refl:2140":["(g Immediate knowledge of truth.)\napagogical [Er] means of truth,\nanalytic, from consequences,\nsynthetic, from grounds.\nAll mediate.",[]],"refl:2141":["Subjective truth [is truthfulness; this is found] (s is found) in sensations (s and the consciousness of them) alone, [which] (s when they) |\ndo not express the property of the object at all. (g Internal and external *criterium* of truth; objective and subjective.) The limits of the understanding are not the source of errors. An error does, after all, arise in part from reason. It is therefore always in part true.",[]],"refl:2142":["Error and truth are only in judgments (s and indeed when these are thought as propositions. The geometer says: suppose there were in a ® more than one *angulus rectus*).\nJudgments are acts of the understanding and of reason.\nTruth is agreement of the understanding and of reason.\nThe understanding alone does not err* (s because it cannot conflict with its own laws). Nor do the senses (s because they do not judge at all). The grounds of error must lie in other powers [also not]. Pure reason does not err. But the human [understanding] can have no knowledge without intuition, which is sensible. In logic, sensibility is merely the subjective element in knowledge, so far as it does not agree with the objective, as precipitancy, confusion, habit, miscalculation. Logic has to do only with the agreement of knowledge with itself, according to the *principium contradictionis* as well as the *principium rationis*.\n*(g Nor does it judge alone, that is, independently; for there enter into [its judging] all those conditions which supply it with one mode of knowledge or another. In man it is in part passive. Some things are supplied to it, others withheld. Subjective conditions. One must become conscious of what one thinks in the con|cepts; and this depends upon the state of inner sense, whether its horizon is clear or clouded. Sensuous stimulus.\nSensibility is thus a power which is always required for judging, the understanding the other; together they make the oblique motion along the diagonal, in which there is something true and also something false.)\n(g As in reckoning: good rules, but slips in the execution.)",[]],"refl:2143":["My judgment is to agree with the object. Now I can compare the object with my knowledge only by knowing it. Diallelon.",[]],"refl:2144":["Truth of the judgment, or truth of the intuition of an object.",[]],"refl:2145":["Logical truth consists in the agreement of the predicates with the representation of the object that has been assumed. Real [truth]: [with] the [agreem] relation of the representation to an actual object. The latter is the truth of intuitions. In sensations there is subjective truth: they are actual, e.g. [what is] agreeable.",[]],"refl:2146":["Representations, be they what they may, determine the object. If they do not agree with one [object], they represent another. There can therefore be no falsity in them. If they represent what is impossible, they represent something impossible.",[]],"refl:2147":["24'.\nL 24:\nNon-essential marks of truth consist not in the agreement of reason with its own laws, whether according to internal or external marks, but in agreement with the reason of others. This gives no mark (s proof), but [only] a presumption of truth. Others therefore [the opinion] have |\nL 24': |\na *votum consultativum*, not *decisivum*. Hence the impulse to communicate oneself is bound up with the desire for knowledge, because our own judgment must be rectified from the standpoint of another. Self-conceited reason and participating reason. Egoist and pluralist, in the logical sense. Timid reason, on the other hand, is that which entrusts to others the decision about truth; it is often also servile.",[]],"refl:2148":["*Conditio sine qua non*. The understanding must be in agreement with itself in judgments, propositions and certain assertions *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2149":["We have hitherto spoken of the subjective *quantitas* of knowledge.",[]],"refl:2150":["We have spoken: 1) of the quality of knowledge: whether clear or obscure. 2) of the quantity: whether extensive or intensive. 3. Relation: [Tru] whether true or false — [knowledge] as related to the object.",[]],"refl:2151":["What is truth? Diallelon.",[]],"refl:2152":["The question whether a mode of knowledge agrees with the object must be preceded by the question whether it agrees with itself (in respect of form). This is the business of logic.",[]],"refl:2153":["The *criterium* of a mode of knowledge which suits all objects (g must be indifferent to all objects) is one which properly bears upon no determinate object (distinguished from others). It is therefore the *criterium* of the knowledge which bears upon an object in general (g or rather abstracts from all (g difference of) objects), and consequently [the *criterium*] of a judgment that agrees with itself.",[]],"refl:2154":["1. Quantity: logical universality. 2. Quality: distinctness. 3. Relation: truth.*\nOnly a formal, not a material *criterium* of truth.\n*(s Truth may be brought under relation, but also under modality; according to the latter, the relation is possible, actual or necessary.)",[]],"refl:2155":["The matter of knowledge is the object. Agreement with it is truth. Logic abstracts from the matter; consequently it gives no *criterium* of truth save that without which knowledge would not be knowledge in general at all, that is, [the *criterium*] of the agreement of knowledge with itself. This is the formal and propaedeutic *criterium* of truth (negative). The material [*criterium*] is agreement of judgments with intuitions, and therefore not formal tautology and identity.\nThe material *criterium* of truth must bear upon the difference of objects, and consequently not abstract from it. There can therefore be universal formal, but not a material *criterium* (g universal, either sufficient or necessary) of truth.\nIt is absurd to demand a *criterium* of truth which would suffice for the [distinguishing] determination of it in all judgments; for if it is to be universal, it can determine nothing in respect of truth as regards content.",[]],"refl:2156":["Truth in the principles: rule.\nTruth in the [appl] use: correctness.",[]],"refl:2157":["The conflict of subjective causes of judgments with the objective [grounds] is the ground of error.",[]],"refl:2158":["That which makes error possible is illusion [Schein], that is, the subjective which is taken for objective.",[]],"refl:2159":["External *criteria* of truth: the assent of others; hence the *allegata* of jurists and philologists. If one wishes to be an inventor, one demands to be the first; if one wishes only truth, one desires predecessors. Citations of the mathematicians.",[]],"refl:2160":["In our knowledge, everything is entitled sensible which is valid merely subjectively.",[]],"refl:2161":["The material *criteria* of truth consist in the agreement of knowledge with those representations which relate immediately to the object, and therefore in agreement with intuitions and perceptions.\nMight we not, even without the experiment with the reason of others, be able to distinguish illusion, that is, the subjective in judgment, from the objective, e.g. in religious faith?",[]],"refl:2162":["In and\nIn logic we can specify only formal *criteria* of truth, that is, the conditions of the agreement of knowledge, as knowledge in general, without relation to the object (as matter). These *criteria* are negative, namely, that no incorrectnesses are found in the form. Even when one speaks of knowledge in general, there can be question of nothing more than the form. (In transcendental logic the matter is universally determined and distinguished; hence [there are] *criteria* of truth, but no *organon*.)",[]],"refl:2163":["Objective *criteria* of truth are logical (g agreement of the understanding with itself).\nSubjective *criteria*: assent of others.",[]],"refl:2164":["Beside the conclusion of L §. 96:\nKnowledge of truth immediately, without inferences. Indemonstrable.",[]],"refl:2165":["The supreme *criterium* of truth (it is always only in judgments) is possibility (problematic), or that it is grounded (categorical) [or], and indeed through the impossibility of the opposite (apodictic).",[]],"refl:2166":["Possibility. Truth. Necessity.",[]],"refl:2167":["*Principium contradictionis*: possibility of the judgment.\n*Rationis*: truth; assertoric.\n*Disjunctionis*: necessity; apodictic, because the opposite is impossible.",[]],"refl:2168":["If all the consequences are true, then the assumed ground [are] is true; hypothesis.",[]],"refl:2169":["That from which a thoroughgoing connection with what is given follows is true.",[]],"refl:2170":["The internal *criteria* consist in the agreement of the understanding with its own laws.",[]],"refl:2171":["The external mark of truth is the assent of others: *citata*, the appeal to celebrated teachers of law, and the general agreements of peoples. [It] is not logical, but psychological.",[]],"refl:2172":["The (g logical) *criterium* of truth in logic is, first, that of possibility according to the principle of contradiction; secondly, that of logical actuality according to the principle of sufficient reason. Possible and grounded.\nThe historical *criterium* of truth [the text] breaks off?",[]],"refl:2173":["Logic can indeed give us universal *criteria* of the correct use of the understanding, but not of the power of judgment; for it gives only rules, and not at the same time [a means of deciding] how one is to distinguish what falls under them (the failing of legislators in making laws fit particular cases. *Jus certum*, nothing but *casus in terminis*). The mark of the correct use of the power of judgment is external, and consists in the assent of others, who verify or reform our [judgment]. All the conditions must, as with the mathematician, be drawn into one rule in order to determine the power of judgment. In *jus* this is not feasible (g because the smallest circumstances give rise to rights and obligations); hence the jurists cite the *sententiae* of other *jurisconsulti*.",[]],"refl:2174":["Logical estimation of truth and falsity (g in itself, in relation to the understanding in general). 1. From the knowledge in itself; 2. [from its] connection with other knowledge: a. with the grounds, b. with the consequences. Logic here considers only the formal rules. The external mark of truth is the understanding of others; [what is so judged is] therefore true not internally, but relatively.",[]],"refl:2175":["The [internally] objectively valid *criterium*; the [externally valid] subjective: assent of others* (s for the avoidance of illusion). [Citations.]\n*(s In judgments of taste we have no other.)",[]],"refl:2176":["The internal (logical) *criteria* of truth: 1. Possibility, for problematic judgments; 2. that they are grounded, for assertoric; 3. that [they] one must necessarily judge thus and not otherwise, that is, that the opposite is false, for apodictic judgments. The external mark of truth (after one has first judged for oneself) is, for the avoidance of deception by illusion, the concurring judgment of others.",[]],"refl:2177":["A universal material *criterium* of truth is impossible, because it would have to contain the agreement of knowledge with the object without distinction of objects. But it is precisely in the distinguishing of the object that objective truth consists. A universal *criterium* of truth can therefore only be formal, that is, it can consist only in the logical marks of the agreement of knowledge with the universal laws of understanding and reason, that is, of the agreement of knowledge with itself. This, however, is not sufficient for objective truth. *Conditio sine qua non*. Mere agreement with these [laws], [taken] as a sufficient *criterium*, makes dialectic.",[]],"refl:2178":["From the consequence we can indeed infer to a ground, but without determining it; only from the sum of all the consequences can we infer to a determinate ground, [namely] that this is the true ground. This totality cannot be known apodictically.*\nThrough the principle of contradiction I can infer positively the possibility of knowledge (not of things), negatively the impossibility of knowledge and of things, indirectly the necessity of knowledge and of things. As a negative principle it is universal; as positive, whether direct or indirect, it is not sufficient as a *criterium* of truth.\n(g The third principle is that *exclusi medii*, since truth is proved through the falsity of the opposite.)\n*(s The consequence can be a negative sufficient *criterium*: namely, if a false consequence flows from a mode of knowledge, it is false; but not: if no false consequence flows, it is true. Hypothesis.)\n*(s There are thus three logical (g formal) ways of finding truth: 1) *principium contradictionis et identitatis*; 2. *principium rationis*; 3. *principium exclusi medii inter 2 contradictoria*.)",[]],"refl:2179":["*Cognitio sit possibilis*.\n*Cognitio sit rationabilis*.",[]],"refl:2180":["Above and\nThe connection of a mode of knowledge with truths given elsewhere, as consequences, also belongs to the internal logical *criteria*. For there the mark of truth [is] is, after all, internal to a mode of knowledge in general.",[]],"refl:2181":["Logical possibility: agreement of the knowledge with itself; actuality: connection with other [modes of knowledge] that are given.",[]],"refl:2182":["3) *Criteria*.\n1. That it does not contradict itself (for judgments);\n2. that it has a ground (for propositions);\n3. that it has no false consequences.",[]],"refl:2183":["To the objective *criteria* belong the internal (principle of contradiction) or the external: grounds and consequences.",[]],"refl:2184":["The logical *criterium* of possibility is the principle of contradiction: that the thought is possible.",[]],"refl:2185":["The internal, that is, logical *criteria* of truth are: 1) internal possibility; 2) sufficiency for given consequences, but not apodictically; 3) the false consequence from the opposite.",[]],"refl:2186":["Nothing is true because it is possible; besides absence of contradiction, a sufficient ground is therefore also required.\nIf this ground is not completely sufficient, the knowledge is merely probable.\nWhat is inferred from grounds of the best and of the wisdom of God is merely probable, e.g. inhabitants of the planets. For there are no *sufficientes rationes* for this.",[]],"refl:2187":["Conviction is an insufficient ground (g *ratio cognoscendi*) of objective certainty.\nTo this belongs not only my own conviction, but also that of others.\nIt therefore makes truth probable, namely when it has arisen from consideration of the objective grounds.\nBut if it has arisen merely from trust in others, it makes it suspect. (g E.g. the opinion that the sun stands still.)\nWhere an impartial examination is certain, there the presumption of at least some truth is probable. For:\n1. We must presuppose that every understanding holds something to be true only on account of the connection of a proposition with its grounds.\n2. That if there were no grounds at all, or no connection at all, no conviction would arise either.\nIn a conflict of opinions, therefore, where love of truth rules on both sides, one must seek just as much wherein the other is right as wherein he is wrong.\nE.g. of monads. Example of Maimonides.\nWhere a multiplicity of opinion from different grounds is met with, there is a presumption that not all is true.",[]],"refl:2188":["E.g. There are many gods. Man still has the image of God.\nFurther, beside L §. 100 »in derselben wahr — derselben« (l. 26f.):\nHe who found another reading of a passage.",[]],"refl:2189":["Correctness is *perfectio formalis*; consequently it does not rest upon multiplicity — —\n(s Correctness is agreement with the norm.)",[]],"refl:2190":["Gross and subtle are, properly, those higher degrees either of incorrectness or of correctness *formalis*. Exact is the mean.\nE.g. the knowledge of the constitution of the earth among the Indians is gross, because [it contains] an incorrectness which could have been avoided by an easy application of distinct concepts. Mahomet's paradise.\nSubtle is a correctness which is obtained by a great abstraction from confused concepts, e.g. Newton's science of colours.\nExact is that correctness which is obtained [by not] through concepts that correspond to the constitution of the object, e.g. Socrates on prayer.",[]],"refl:2191":["Gross knowledge of the thing in the *Orbis pictus*.\n(s Knowledge gross in respect of matter, as regards truth; in respect of form, as regards thoroughness.)",[]],"refl:2192":["101-102:\nExact and rough estimate. [*Cognitio*] *veritas late vel stricte spectata*. *Veritas approximando exacta*. *Respectu definitae*.\nη−φ. In L §. 102, beginning:\n*Late vel stricte determinata veritas*.\nOn L §. 102 »cognitio exacta«:\n*acu tangere*. Further: *praecise* — *circa*.\nOn L §. 102 »welche aber — crassa« (265 31f.):\n*praeter propter*, *circa* in a builder's estimate. Further: room for error. *plus minus*. Further: *generaliter*. On the whole it holds. Further: superficially, on the average, indeterminately. Further: raw, *rudis*.\nAbove and below »Alle grobe — und« (265 32f.):\nTo work something out from the rough, and afterwards to add the refinement.\nWhat suffices for the purpose, although much refinement is lacking, is yet not gross, but simple and natural.",[]],"refl:2193":["Something is exact when one carefully seeks to avoid small incorrectnesses.\nLack of exactness, accordingly, is when small incorrectnesses creep in. E.g. instruments: compasses, clock, telescope.\nFurther, on the conclusion of L §. 102 from »je grösser« (266 27) onwards:\nThe moon is as large as the Peloponnese.\nOne must know [the length] (g length) of the year exactly in order to determine [it]; but it is not necessary to determine the course of Jupiter just as exactly.\nFurther, L 26 beside the conclusion of L §. 102 (from µ−ψ):\nPartial truth in errors.",[]],"refl:2194":["1. In that case the connection must be incorrect, e.g. one who infers from the [wisdo] unity of God against the Trinity.\n2. From false grounds true things can correctly follow, because falsities nevertheless have something correct on one side. [One who infers from the goodness of God that he will damn no man.]\n3. In that case the incorrectness is only in respect of the mode of knowledge, that is, subjective.\nOne who infers from the goodness of God that he will damn no man.",[]],"refl:2195":["When I infer the immortality of the soul from its simple nature.",[]],"refl:2196":["Complete correctness is exactness [Genauigkeit]. Precision is punctiliousness. If one does not wish to attend to the parts, [knowledge] is not on that account gross.\nGross knowledge is below the common form of the understanding; subtle is above it. With gross and subtle, it is a matter of the degree of the form of the understanding, and not of correctness.\nSubtle is what requires a greater attention in order to be clear, either because the object is small or because its difference from other things is small. Universal abstract concepts are subtle; distinctions.\nWhy is the subtle held to be unnecessary? Because sound understanding is sufficient for what is useful. Teachers must be subtle, but not in their exposition, except where they also wish to make teachers.",[]],"refl:2197":["When it is a question of the ground of truth, it is\neither true according to logical rules\n— — —\naesthetic —\n— — —\nmoral: practical assent.\nObjectively, every mode of knowledge is either true or false.\nSubjectively — — — — — — or ungrounded.",[]],"refl:2198":["*Tolerabiliter vera sunt, in quibus est approximatio ad veritatem* [Those things are tolerably true in which there is an approximation to truth].",[]],"refl:2199":["»It is to be [tru] assumed« and »it is true« are different.\nThe former relates to (g universal) grounds of the judgment in the subject (g use of the knowledge), the latter to [grounds] in the object (g knowledge in itself). Logical, aesthetic, practical presupposition. The latter shows that, without any regard to its use, the knowledge contains in itself grounds of judgment.\nHolding-to-be-true from objective grounds (g judgment of truth) is either certainty or probability.\nHolding-to-be-true from grounds of application is either [the text] breaks off.",[]],"refl:2200":["*Regula late vel stricte determinans*. A room for arbitrary determinations always remains (in ethical laws), but not in juridical ones. *Determinatio practica* is also for the most part *lata*. [The task is] to determine this *latitudo*, the latitude [Spielraum] of [error] truth and error, in respect of magnitude. *Latitudinarius* in respect of morals.\nThe object may be determined *late* or *stricte*, but the ground of knowledge must be stringent.\nFor exactness subtlety is often required. Hence that is entitled subtle which is superfluously exact, inasmuch as many a closer determination can be left to choice.",[]],"refl:2201":["All rules restrict, but the *latae* leave a room for free choice. [The determination] of the mean [betw] within the latitude of the contradictories, whereby the truth is determined *stricte*, [is made] not *a priori* but *a posteriori*.",[]],"refl:2202":["The want of exactness which gives rise to an error contrary to the end [in view] is the coarseness of the determination. To the coarse the correct is opposed *contradictorie* [as its contradictory], and the fine *contrarie* [as its contrary].",[]],"refl:2203":["What *subtilitaet* [subtlety] is in respect of knowledge, *delicatesse* is in respect of taste; so also is correctness in feeling, and likewise punctiliousness of conscience (s *scrupulositaet*).",[]],"refl:2204":["*Cognitio est vel vaga** [knowledge is either vague] (that which has no determinate object) (g e.g. perfection, happiness) *vel determinata* [or determinate]. *determinata vel stricte vel late determinata* [the determinate is determined either strictly or broadly] (g goodness and right); *prior* (g objectively) *exacta, posterior rudis* [the former exact, the latter crude]; subjectively: *subtilis vel crassa* [subtle or gross].\n*(s Not too much, not too little. *aurea mediocritas* [the golden mean]. Hair-splitting [Spitzfindigkeit] is a mere play of the faculty of judgment.)",[]],"refl:2205":["Aesthetically true: what is described as it actually appears, or what everyone says.",[]],"refl:2206":["Grounds of holding-to-be-true [Vorwarhalten]: aesthetic and practical. Or [else] something is true according to logical laws.",[]],"refl:2207":["The degree of truth (g as regards content, not as regards ground) is distinct from the degree of holding-to-be-true. The former is partial in respect of the object, the latter in respect of the knowledge of the object.",[]],"refl:2208":["*tolerabiliter* [tolerably] true. (s That whose incorrectness does not hinder the end.) (s Partial truth is distinct from degree of truth; the latter is [leicht] approximation.)",[]],"refl:2209":["Either partial truth: e.g. what is said universally may yet hold of a great deal.\nOr a partial degree of truth. *Probabilitas* [probability] is distinct from semblance [Schein], *verisimilitudo*.\n(s True on the whole (as regards the intent).)",[]],"refl:2210":["In and \nTruth has no degrees. More than, all too true.\nThe distributive [and] the collective universality of truth.\n1. There is something false in a mode of knowledge otherwise true. 2. Or: not all truth, [not] the whole truth.",[]],"refl:2211":["True, or false, or a logical *adiaphoron* [something indifferent]: *non liquet* [it is not clear] (g a problematic judgment, not a proposition), when nothing is determined from the *data*.",[]],"refl:2212":["In historical (g empirical) assertions a man may be wholly wrong, as, for instance, one who places before the birth of Christ an event which in fact took place after it. In rational [Sätz] judgments, on the other hand, he must always be right when regarded from one side. It is therefore fair to grant him this right first, and only afterwards to add the incorrectness as a mere limitation.",[]],"refl:2213":["All our dispute about truth [ist] has a common interest, as [it has] between friends (husband and wife). It ought therefore to be sympathetic, not exclusive, self-seeking and egoistic. I must begin by noting wherein the other is right.",[]],"refl:2214":["Probability properly concerns only things, not modes of knowledge. The knowledge of the probable must be true.",[]],"refl:2215":["Totality is either collective, that is, everything in the knowledge is true; or determining, that is, it is congruent with the object. True as regards degree.\nEither the whole of the knowledge is true, or one thereby knows the whole given determination of the object.\nWhat this man says is wholly true — he speaks the whole truth.\nIn L §. 101:\nEither the truth concerns the whole of the knowledge that is present, or the whole object. The former is necessary, otherwise the knowledge is coarse; the latter is *circa* bricht ab.\n(s *Cognitio vaga* [vague knowledge] or *exacta* [exact].)\n(s True on the whole, but not exact.)",[]],"refl:2216":["On the whole, that is, in the main, this man is right, but not wholly right.",[]],"refl:2217":["Everything I say is true; but I do not say the whole truth",[]],"refl:2218":["The (g *praecise*) exact bears on truth (objective).\nThe subtle on attention (subjective).",[]],"refl:2219":["Historical determination to within half a century. Astronomical.",[]],"refl:2220":["Knowledge is entitled exact (g when it is adequate to the end) in respect of the object, and subtle in respect of the cognitive powers.\nUnter genau steht als conträrer Gegensatz: *rudis*, unter subtil: *crassa*. Vor *rudis* der s-Zusatz: (ω): *exacta — vel*, hinter *rudis* der s-Zusatz (ω): crude, those that are not sufficiently — Gross [Crasse], those that are too much determined for the senses, to which they do not belong.Inω ist *crassa*(Z. 15) in crasse verwandelt und links davon concepts — hinzugesetzt, rechts davon: subtle — coarse. The mean is precision, not micrology.",[]],"refl:2221":["*Cognitio vel vaga vel determinata* [knowledge is either vague or determinate]. *determinatio stricta vel lata* [determination strict or broad] for demarcation, but not logical.\nWe determine first *late* [broadly], then *stricte* [strictly]. e.g. Philosophy is a science of reason.\n*repraesentatio vaga plane non est determinatio* [a vague representation is no determination at all]. e.g. *Mediocritas in absumendis opibus* [moderation in spending one's wealth].\n*perfice te* [perfect thyself]: *regula vaga* [a vague rule]. It is of no use.",[]],"refl:2222":["The approximate, the exact and the precise determination, as opposed to the undetermined. Common, coarse. Pardonable error.\nGross concepts.",[]],"refl:2223":["Dogmatic truths are, properly, all universal truths; for these are not known immediately through experience and the senses.\nInferred and observed truths.\n*veritas Historica* [historical truth]: it thunders most in the mountains.\n*veritas dogmatica* [dogmatic truth]: the matter of thunderstorms is located in the clouds.\nHistorical modes of knowledge are here considered as regards their matter (the object), and, as [knowledge of] things that have happened, are [von] distinguished from dogmatic [modes of knowledge], whose object is a property of possible things.\nZuL §. 104 »Lehrgebäude«:\nWhen a manifold of knowledge flows from one single ground. e.g. The *propagatio per traducem* [propagation by transmission].\nHistory is no system.\nL 26 zu demselben Wort aus υ−θ:\nsystematic or aphoristic exposition.",[]],"refl:2224":["Of the harmfulness\nutility\n)\nof systems.",[]],"refl:2225":["The form of the system must first be sketched out.\nHistorical form has no ground in reason.",[]],"refl:2226":["All doctrinal systems [Lehrgebäude] are learned projects. Sketches, however, are necessary for methodical inquiry. Without a doctrinal system all inquiry is haphazard; but one must always regard it only as a project until it has been proved.",[]],"refl:2227":["All systems are either historical or rational. In the former there is empirical |\n(g understanding-), in the latter rational unity. In the former there is synthetic unity, in so far as many [things] are the ground of one (whole, which is one and the same with these taken together); in the latter analytic [unity], in so far as the manifold is connected through a ground *a posteriori* or *a priori*.\n(s Synthetic unity is the unity of taking-together, analytic unity the unity of derivation. The former is empirical, the latter rational. In the former the manifold [sind] is parts, in the latter consequences.)\n(s For example, derivation of history from the principle of the union of men in a commonwealth. Second principle: of the union of states in one whole — either by fusion through war or by lawful combination through law.\nMen have not gone to work systematically according to their concepts, but nature proceeds with them systematically.)",[]],"refl:2228":["Universal rules from concepts. Modes of knowledge of the understanding from concepts — ideas.\nApodeictic knowledge from concepts is dogmatic.\n(s Apodeictic knowledge from the construction of concepts is mathematical.)",[]],"refl:2229":["That [knowledge be] in agreement with reason is thoroughness (g shallowness, *superficialitas*). The systematic [is] cogency [Bündigkeit].\nHistorical modes of knowledge can be acquired without a system; mathematical ones also [gl] to a certain degree; but philosophical ones (g of pure |\nreason) not without a system. [Das Gant] The outline of the whole must precede the parts.",[]],"refl:2230":["Many imagine that they have written systems, and have only aggregates. These require only a manner, but systems a method.",[]],"refl:2231":["A system is distinct from a rhapsody. The former can be expounded in various ways: aphoristically, in dialogue, etc etc.",[]],"refl:2232":["Many imagine that system belongs only to exposition; but it belongs to the object of knowledge and to thinking.",[]],"refl:2233":["Historical sciences and sciences of reason are both systems, and are distinct from common knowledge; the former constitute erudition. A system cannot be made by juxtaposition, but only by derivation.",[]],"refl:2234":["Connection of modes of knowledge in one principle, that is, in the idea of the whole, wherein all the parts are members — system. *infelix operis summa — quia totum ponere nescit* [unhappy in the sum of his work, because he knows not how to set forth a whole].",[]],"refl:2235":["Truth *formaliter* [formally regarded] has only the singular — —\nFerner:\nThere are truths in respect of matter, but only one truth in respect of form.",[]],"refl:2236":["The holding-to-be-true of universal modes of knowledge is either dogmatic or problematic.\n[Allgemeine] Truths of reason: either *dogmata* or *mathemata*.",[]],"refl:2237":["All certainty is either empirical (certain) — [or] apodeictic.\nAll truths: historical or truths of reason.",[]],"refl:2238":["Fables. Plays of wit. Poetry. Examples — —. Butler.\nWriters of comedy.\n(s Significance of dreams,\ncomets,\nghosts and sorcery.)\nGegenüber von L §. 106 »eine — sie« (Z. 29-31):\nThis is false. e.g. Kepler. Newton.\nZuL §. 106 Schluss:\nAccording to the intent, with Maupertuis it is necessary, with Euler not.",[]],"refl:2239":["If the aesthetic (g or logical) is merely in connection with truth, the truths are distinguished only *accidentaliter* [accidentally]. But if [eines] each is a ground of a different degree of truth, they are distinguished *essentialiter* [essentially]. Proverbs are aesthetically true.",[]],"refl:2240":["There are unnecessary truths, trifles.\nThere are hair-splitting and very abstract truths.",[]],"refl:2241":["*in sensu absoluto* [in the absolute sense]: yes; but [in the] *respectivo* [relative sense]: no.",[]],"refl:2242":["Error arises 1. from the desire for knowledge. |\n2. From the want of the requisite fundamental concepts. 3. From [Nachlaßigkeit] the omission of attention.",[]],"refl:2243":["The first endeavour (s NB.) of logic is negative, namely [das Ge]: to prevent errors or to remove them (to undo them). *catharcticon* [a cathartic]. The second is positive: to increase and to further modes of knowledge. *organon*./\nWhen it is more likely that certain modes of knowledge will bring about errors, ignorance is the more advisable.",[]],"refl:2244":["We can become conscious of error only through our understanding; and we can therefore err only in so far as the understanding acts contrary to its own rules. This, however, is impossible. No power of nature can act contrary to its own law, [und] when it acts alone. But just as bodies in empty space fall according to |\nthe laws of gravity, or describe perfect parabolas, and yet [im] deviate from this rule through the resistance of the air, so other activities of the soul, such as stimulus, imagination, etc., combine with the judgments of the understanding; and we err in being conscious of this mixed effect as of a judgment of the understanding. For example, we have an impulse to compare concepts *qva identitatem et diversitatem* [as to identity and difference], [aber s] which is wit; but also an impulse to combine them, positively or negatively, which is understanding; and the one act mingles with the other. Imagination combines concepts previously connected; hence also imitation.\nThe case is the same with the morality of actions. We do what we disapprove. Only there is [hiebey] this difference here: that the disapproval can in the one case be simultaneous with the action, whereas in the other [mit] it can only follow upon the perverse judgment.",[]],"refl:2245":["We err either sensitively or intellectually. [Der] Optical illusion: in which the understanding nevertheless does not err; we are deceived and yet not [deceived]. Fear [ohne allen gewissen deutlichen] together with the greatest certainty of the opposite; timidity in a large assembly; fear of death in a wretched man.\nApprehension of erring is not yet doubt. A wavering state.",[]],"refl:2246":["If we had a pure reason and a pure understanding, we should never err; and if we had a pure will [würden] (without inclination), we should not sin.",[]],"refl:2247":["*apparentia* (*phaenomenon*), appearance, (* which is taken for the thing itself, is)\n*species* (g *veritatis*), semblance [der Schein]. (g Seeming [Scheinbar].) *probabilitas*: probability.\n*speciem evolvere et exponere* [to unfold and expound the semblance].\n( s Appearance, a (g empirical) intuition, of which I do not know [ob] how much of it is to be ascribed to the subject, how much to the object. )( s The subjective grounds of judgments which are taken for objective are semblance. )",[]],"refl:2248":["*non ex ignorantia sola, sed ex specie* [not from ignorance alone, but from semblance].",[]],"refl:2249":["We should not be able to see *a priori* that errors are possible, that is, that an understanding could act contrary to its own laws. Experience teaches it us, and was bound to teach it us. Errors are therefore *phaenomena*, which NB. must be explained, not only in general, but each particular error [also], as to how it was possible; for otherwise it cannot be thoroughly removed or pre|vented.\nTo distinguish the subjective [was auf] which (g in being taken for objective) has influence on our judgment, that is, the sensible [und die Bestimmung derse] in us, which leads to error, we can attempt even without comparison with the judgments of others, merely by putting ourselves in thought in the place of others.",[]],"refl:2250":["The understanding deviates from its rules. The grounds of this lie in what is not understanding, that is, in the sensible.\nThe limits of the understanding are not the causes of errors.\nAn error is always *partialiter* [partially] true. Its subjective cause is a *phaenomenon* and must be explained. Error in rules and in application.\nSensibility consists in subjective grounds of knowledge,\nerror in confusing the subjective with the objective.\n(s A criterion of truth (negative), [namely] that one has not erred, is therefore [present] when no combination according to sensible (g subjective) laws has come in place of [combination] according to logical [laws]. Error must be explained as a *phaenomenon*.)",[]],"refl:2251":["For error there is required, besides falsity, semblance also, and indeed here logical semblance.\n(s What is true regarded merely subjectively can, with consciousness [thereof], be logically, that is, objectively, false.)",[]],"refl:2252":["In the judgment is truth or falsity. In the [Vorwarhalten] proposition, error or truth subjectively.",[]],"refl:2253":["Error is opposed to holding-to-be-true, not to truth. The mean is the problematic judgment.",[]],"refl:2254":["27.\nL 27':\nThe (g logical) opposite of truth is falsity. The counterpart [Wiederspiel], or diametrical opposite, is error. The holding-to-be-true of what is false rests on semblance. |\nL 27: |\nTruth is agreement with the object, semblance, on the other hand, [agreement] with the subject. Every error presupposes a semblance.",[]],"refl:2255":["he who does not judge does not err.",[]],"refl:2256":["We speak only of the formal cause of error: deviation from the logical laws.",[]],"refl:2257":["formal truth *iuridice* [in the juridical sense].",[]],"refl:2258":["In judging, sensibility exerts influence not on the understanding but on the judgment.",[]],"refl:2259":["Merely subjective propositions (of the immediate consciousness of sensation) are always true, provided only one is truthful. Truth and falsity lie not in concepts but in judgments, and indeed [in judgments] as propositions. — (g Error is a false judgment in so far as it is held to be true.)\nHence an easy way of preventing errors — either not to judge at all or to judge problematically. Truth is (as regards form) agreement [mit Gesetzen des des Urthei] of the proposition with laws of the understanding. All effects, however, always agree with the laws of the powers from which they arise. The understanding by itself, therefore, does not err, because it always judges correctly; — nor do the senses, because they do not judge at all. Besides these, however, there are no other [Erk] sources of judgments. Error therefore arises only when both together exert influence on a judgment, e.g. diagonal motion.",[]],"refl:2260":["There is, properly, no unavoidable error; but there is an un|avoidable\nignorance (s e.g. where hell may be). Except where one is compelled to hold one of two opposed modes of knowledge to be true, e.g. in religion.\nAn error is either [theoretisch] practical, e.g. that there are presentiments and significant dreams; [Oder praktisch] (g that the soul dies); [or theoretical:] that bodies are infinitely divisible.\n(s That the earth stands still.)",[]],"refl:2261":["It might be asked whether there are small errors. Comparatively.\n[The error] concerning the materiality of the human soul is a great error; that concerning the sleep of the soul a lesser one. Errors are great either practically or theoretically. The error that [Ehrb] ambition must be the end of actions is practically smaller than [the error] that it is sensual pleasure.\nAn error can be theoretically great but practically small.\nGegenüber von L §. 112 »und desto — Folglich muss« (Z. 30f.):\nThat not every thing has its ground.",[]],"refl:2262":["When we meet with [Irrthum nicht] contradiction in persons of insight and impartiality, or have historical grounds for confirmation, we must suppose that it is an apparent contradiction.",[]],"refl:2263":["In this way nothing is false when it is held to be true. But [one must] make the falsity manifest.",[]],"refl:2264":["The doctrine of the restoration of all things is covertly false.\nThat matter can think.",[]],"refl:2265":["That when someone has the hiccups, another is thinking of him.\nFerner L 28':\nThat snow is black.\nHow it nevertheless comes about that clever people have absurd errors.\nPrejudices which hinder inquiry.\nFerner L 28 aus ρ−φ:\nmocking, bitter, biting censure. For this, guilt is required, and an evil or obstinate intention.\nFernerL 28' aus θ (? ψ−χ?):\nhe who denies universal logical criteria of truth is absurd.",[]],"refl:2266":["To him who errs the falsity is not manifest.\nWe compare the error with the ideal of the *sensus communis* [common sense]: average magnitude of understanding. An error which can coexist with this is pardonable. The man abuses the licence which men have to be ugly.\nWe cannot prove to anyone that he is absurd, because reasoning would be in vain. When we prove the absurdity, we are no longer speaking with the one who errs, but with [one who is] rational. But to the latter it is not necessary to lay bare the absurdity. *deductio ad absurdum*.",[]],"refl:2267":["Unavoidable semblance.\n*ignorantia invincibilis* [invincible ignorance]: from it, *error invincibilis* [invincible error]. *juridice* [juridically]. e.g. A law has not been promulgated in a certain place. Error in the balance [of accounts]: when I do not know of the claims of others, or [the case of one] who buys another's property after public proclamation.\nHere the judgment cannot be suspended.\nPersonal censure, or censure of opinions.",[]],"refl:2268":["28'.\nL 28:\nHe who errs is a putative possessor (g *bonae fidei* [in good faith]) of truth. If he is instructed and still persists, he is then an unjust possessor, and can be brought *ad absurdum*.\nL 28':\n(g Persistence in a manifest error which has no grounds [vor] in its favour is absurdity.)",[]],"refl:2269":["From error one does not return directly to truth, but first to the consciousness of one's ignorance and to suspension of judgment. One is made wiser by experience, but not on that account more insightful.\nGross error: that which shows ignorance in common knowledge (g of the *sensus communis* or of science), or an offence against common attention. Absurd error: that for which nothing serves |\nas excuse, not even semblance. Dangerous error: that which draws after it many other errors, especially practical ones. (s consequence in *ius* [law].)\nSympathy with the infirmities (g *homo sum* etc etc. Chremes.) of universal human reason. Leniency in judging them.\nTolerable error. [Error] hard to avoid. Way of thinking: to judge problematically, not decisively. (s to assert irrevocably.)\n(s To orient oneself in thinking (for popular writing).)\nTouchstone\nThe external mark of error is* the incompatibility of the judgments of others with our own. This is a hint to examine our procedure [Man], not to reject it. (s Even in the case of one's own experience, still to take counsel of that of others; but not in the case of mere truths of reason, except as regards their application.) One may be right in the matter and wrong in the manner, that is, in the exposition. Universal semblance is the greatest hindrance to truth; and he who errs with the great multitude appeals to common human understanding. This is also in itself a touchstone for discovering the faults of artificial use. Test, counter-test. (s Orienting oneself through the common understanding.)\n*(s the internal [mark]: that [Vorstell] judgments do not follow from the same principles. Logical danger.)",[]],"refl:2270":["Error in principles is greater than in their application. False rules. An offence against grammar is worse than [one against] vocabulary. (g In logic worst of all.) *Priscian vapulat* [Priscian is beaten].",[]],"refl:2271":["To distinguish the subjective from the objective determining ground of the judgment, there serves, besides the judgment of others, the comparison of our judgment with other truths as grounds or consequences.",[]],"refl:2272":["An external (g not logical) mark of truth is comparison with the judgments of others; for the subjective will not be present in all others in the same way, and the semblance can therefore be discovered by this means. *citatio autorum* [citation of authors]. Jurists.",[]],"refl:2273":["1. Possibility of error; 2. of partial truth.\nUniversal conditions for avoiding error: a) to think for oneself; b) to think oneself in the place of another; c) always to think in agreement with oneself. 1. The enlightened, 2. the enlarged, 3. the consistent (cogent) (g consequential) mode of thought.",[]],"refl:2274":["What, taken by itself, cannot be other than true; or what is true conditionally.\n}\n}\n}\nthe soul is a spirit. man shall rise again.\n(s That in 3 weeks we shall\nhave the shortest day.\nThat it is cold in winter.)",[]],"refl:2275":["*id, ex qvo diversitas rerum cognosci potest, est nota* [that from which the difference of things can be known is a mark].\nWhere there is consciousness there are marks; but where marks [se] are represented there is not always consciousness. e.g. When I do something absent-mindedly. (s Cut something in two.)\nThe marks must be clearly represented.",[]],"refl:2276":["Grounds of knowledge are either internal or external, in relation to others.\nThis relation is either one of identity or one of opposition and difference.\n[Erkentnis] External grounds of knowledge are marks.\nTherefore *notae vel identitatis vel diuersitatis* [marks either of identity or of difference].\nA mark is that of which I am conscious in a thing.",[]],"refl:2277":["What I [am conscious of] in a thing which I represent to myself through a concept is its mark. The mark of comparison is a characteristic sign [Kennzeichen] (s of derivation).",[]],"refl:2278":["Every concept always represents a universal mark of certain things.",[]],"refl:2279":["That in a thing which constitutes a part of the knowledge of |\nit, *cognitio partialis* [partial knowledge], is the mark. We know things only through marks.",[]],"refl:2280":["What is regarded as belonging, as a part, to the whole (g possible) representation of a thing is entitled its mark.",[]],"refl:2281":["We know things only through marks; that is just what is meant by 'knowing' [erkennen], which derives from 'being acquainted with' [kennen]. For the understanding is a faculty of thinking, that is, of knowing discursively through concepts; and concepts are marks of universal use.\nIntuition comes from the senses. Through marks the understanding resolves intuitions and puts them together. Reason then proceeds from what the marks contain to what the whole concept contains.",[]],"refl:2282":["A mark is not always a concept of the thing, but often only of a part of the thing. e.g. The hand is a mark of man; but only having hands is this mark as a concept of man. The partial concept thus serves, through its universality, to bring the thing under a ground of distinction, even without comparison.\nA partial representation as ground of knowledge of the whole representation is a mark.",[]],"refl:2283":["The partial concept as ground of knowledge of the whole representation is the mark. The ground of knowledge is of twofold use: either internal, [that] of derivation, or external, [that] of comparison. The latter is [comparison] either of identity or of difference.",[]],"refl:2284":["Marks as grounds of knowledge for internal use, [namely] in order thereby to know the thing; or for external [use], in order to compare it.",[]],"refl:2285":["The mark is regarded, first, as a representation in itself; secondly, as belonging, as a partial concept, [oder] to another representation, and thereby as ground of knowledge of the thing.",[]],"refl:2286":["29.\nL 29':\nA mark is a partial representation, (g which) as such (g is a ground of knowledge). It is either intuitive (g a synthetic part), a part of the |\nintuition; or discursive, a part of the concept, which is an analytic ground of knowledge. |\nL 29: |\n*vel intuitus vel conceptus partialis* [either a partial intuition or a partial concept].",[]],"refl:2287":["All our concepts are marks, and all thinking is representation through them.\nWe are here speaking only of marks as concepts.",[]],"refl:2288":["Human knowledge is, on the side of the understanding, discursive; that is, it takes place through representations which make what is common to several [things] the ground of knowledge, and so through marks as such.",[]],"refl:2289":["An analytic mark is the partial concept of a concept regarded as an actual whole. A synthetic mark (g is the *compars* [co-part] of a concept [als Gehor], in so far as the latter belongs together with the former, as a part, to another |\nwhole) [als]: the part of a concept regarded as a [wirkli] possible whole, which is first to come about through several parts. All concepts of reason are of the former kind; the latter can be concepts of experience.",[]],"refl:2290":["The partial concepts of my actual concept (those which I think in it) are analytic; those of the merely possible whole concept are synthetic marks.",[]],"refl:2291":["All marks are analytic or synthetic. (Place.)",[]],"refl:2292":["The mark of an animal is that it has no reason.\nThe mark of reason is that one can consider universal truths in abstraction.\nThe mark of a clock is that it indicates the time. The mark of the indication of time is that it divides time into equal parts, in accordance with the course of the sun.\n[Das Merkmal einer Stad ist: daß eine Menge Häuser]\nThe mark of godliness is the love of God.\nThe mark of the love of God is a living knowledge of his perfections.\nThe mark of this living knowledge is [Ergebung] a constant occupation with divine things.\n( g The mark of a philosopher is that he can state the ground of things. The mark that he can state the ground is that one can understand from it why something is. )",[]],"refl:2293":["(s *Complexus vel series* [complex or series].)\n(s *divisio* and *subdivisio*.)\n(s Extensive distinctness (g through concepts or intuitions) — deep distinctness.)\n(s Clearness of the marks is distinctness.)\n(s Completeness.) The series (s aggregate) of co-ordinated marks (s synthetic, of empirical concepts) is a straight line without limits. (s Definition.)\n(s Thoroughness.) The series (s *Series*) of subordinated [marks] *a parte ante* [on the side of the antecedents] comes up against irresolvable concepts and is terminated. (s Deduction.)\n(s [Faßlichkeit] (s systematic.) Cogent.) The series of subordinated [marks] *a parte post* [on the side of the consequents], in respect of the consequences, is infinite.\n(s We have a highest *genus*, but no lowest *species*.)\n(s Through connection with grounds *a parte ante* knowledge is thorough; through connection with the ultimate consequences *in concreto* it is cogent.)\n( s *Notae tanqvam compartes (aggregatum). Notae tanqvam subpartes (seriem)* [marks as co-parts (an aggregate); marks as sub-parts (a series)]. )\nBehold, there are three dimensions.\nIn the practical: feeling, desire and power.\nTime has three dimensions: the simultaneous, the before, the after.\nThe complex of (s immediate) marks:\nas aggregate\nor\nas series\nof the immediate\nof the mediate\nmarks.",[]],"refl:2294":["(g In respect of all knowledge) Extensive, intensive and protensive (g completeness in the connection of the grounds of knowledge) perfection |\nof knowledge (s distinctness) in the connection of its grounds: 1. [in] of the composition of the whole; 2. of the [ableitung] derivation from principles; 3. of the application to (g consequences) other things. (g Complete, deep and fruitful distinctness.)\nThe totality of an aggregate has its limit in the adequate (definition).\nThe totality of a series of marks has its limit in the simple.",[]],"refl:2295":["29.\nL 29':\nThe magnitude of a ditch*, which is determined in respect of the quality of the purpose, rests on breadth and depth. The length can afterwards be continued as far as one wishes, in proportion to the magnitude of the purpose and of one's means. In the same way knowledge, in respect of the whole of the marks as aggregate or as series, [is] extensive and intensive — the former for the completeness, the latter for the thoroughness of the system — [and] can further be continued protensively, in respect of the consequences, to infinity.\nL 29:\n*(g To drain a piece of land (indeterminate in size) only breadth and depth are required, and indeed the proportion [between them] according to the nature of the soil, as one — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — —)",[]],"refl:2296":["Simple beings are those which are not composite.\nFerner aus γ−φ:\nAlgebra has no negative equations.\nFerner aus φ−θ:\nAll [Me] marks can become negative.\nFerner aus φ−θ:\nAffirmative marks are necessary, because negative ones are possible only through them.",[]],"refl:2297":["*Negantia non declarant praeterqvam in contradictorie sive immediate oppositis* [negative marks do not make clear, except in what is contradictorily or immediately opposed] (s not composite, not imperfect). (s [Wenn ich sage: es ist keine dreyekigte, auch keine vielekigte Figur.])\n*sunt steriles. Sterilitas earum minuitur per positiones, sub qvibus continentur et qvae negationibus aeqvivalent* [they are sterile. Their sterility is diminished by positings under which they are contained and which are equivalent to the negations].",[]],"refl:2298":["(g Limiting and extending marks.)\nNegative marks serve only to ward off errors; they are therefore unnecessary where it is impossible to err. Of a being such as God the negations are very necessary.\nNot knowing, abstracting, denying.",[]],"refl:2299":["Negative concepts (g in respect of the object) are freedom, simplicity, etc etc. But negative marks (g of knowledge) for concepts serve only to ward off errors, as: the soul is not corporeal. Through the former I seek to understand something; through the latter only not to misunderstand or err, even should I know nothing of it.",[]],"refl:2300":["The negative use of all knowledge is that of limiting our judgment to truth.",[]],"refl:2301":["Because negation is to be understood only through affirmation.",[]],"refl:2302":["A mark is more important which contains the lesser under it. e.g. That man has reason, or that he laughs.\nIt is a [Wicht] fruitful mark that[er] God contains in himself the ground of all possible and actual things, as compared with my saying: he is [der vollkommenste unter allen Wesen] the holiest being.\nFerner L 30 zu L §. 118 »leviores« etc. (Z. 24):\nThe circle is the figure which rolls most easily.\nFerner L 30' zu L §. 118 »diese aber nicht« (Z. 27.):\nThe mark of a [thiers] fish, that it is dumb, is a small mark.",[]],"refl:2303":["Fruitful marks [are those serving] for much use.\n[Groß] Important ones for great use.\nEmpty ones for no use: tautological.\n(s Logical importance, practical importance.)",[]],"refl:2304":["for distinguishing.\nZuL §. 118 »fruchtbare«:\nfor derivation.",[]],"refl:2305":["Neben der ersten Zeile von L §. 118:\nFor use.\nFerner \nor numerous, complete in number.\nFerner L 30' aus φ−θ:\nin respect of internal use (g derivation) (s for derivation from them as ground) or external [use]: for identity or distinction (g comparison). The former are the more important. (g Distinguishing tokens [Warzeichen].)",[]],"refl:2306":["Logical (s or grammatical) importance is distinct from practical (g utility). Logical fruitfulness [is distinct] from practical serviceableness.",[]],"refl:2307":["relative importance. (g e.g. of the starry heavens.)\nAbsolute importance can never be imparted to another (g who himself has no standard), e.g. [that] of the knowledge of God, of the future [life] and of morality, and of the contemplation of them; only relative importance [can be so imparted].",[]],"refl:2308":["All marks are grounds of knowledge.\nZuL §. 119 Satz 2:\nA sufficient mark is [one sufficient] always to distinguish the thing from all [other] things. (s e.g. Of the animal, the want of reason.)\nOf the dog, barking is insufficient. (s A bird that barks.)",[]],"refl:2309":["relative.",[]],"refl:2310":["Necessary:\nthose which must always be met with in the thing represented.\nOf a triangle: that it has 3 angles.\nZuL §. 120 »zufällige« etc.:\nOf a triangle: that it has an obtuse angle.\nIn a man, erudition.",[]],"refl:2311":["31.\nL 31'. β1. Zu »äusserliche Merkmale« (Z. 24):\nGenealogy.\nPlace.\nFerner L 31 aus β1−ρ:\nExternal [marks] are never internally necessary.\nL 31'. β1. Zu »Diese — Menschen« (Z. 27f.):\nhis capacity. Virtue.\nL 31'. β1. Zu »Gründe aller übrigen Bestimmungen« (Z. 28):\nIn the triangle, the 3 sides.\nL 31'. β1. Zu »Eigenschaften« (Z. 29):\nIn man, soul and body.\nFerner L 31 aus µ−ψ:\nThinking. From it mortality cannot be understood.\nFerner L 30 aus µ−θ:\nunsuitable word.\nL 31'. β1? ε2−κ3?? Zu »essentia«:\nIt [logic] can find the logical essence of all concepts, but not the real essence. But the former need not be found where the concept is empirical, e.g. water, since not everything in the thing can be derived from it in any case.",[]],"refl:2312":["Zu»Die letztern« etc. (31126ff.):\n(g What belongs necessarily to the concept is for logic.)\nThe first fundamental concept of all the necessary marks of a thing is the essence. [Marks] belonging to the essence* are either *constitutiva* [sondern] or *rationata*. *Extraessentialia* are either *interna* or *externa*.\n*(s *ad essentiam pertinentia vel extraessentialia* [pertaining to the essence or extra-essential]; (s *priora*) *ut constitutiva vel ut rationata (originarie vel derivative)* [as constitutive or as consequences (originally or derivatively)].)",[]],"refl:2313":["Necessary or contingent marks.\n*ad essentiam pertinentia vel extraessentialia* [pertaining to the essence or extra-essential].\n*ad essentiam pertinentia ut rationes vel ut rationata* [pertaining to the essence as grounds or as consequences]. *essentialia et attributa* [essentials and attributes].\n*extraessentialia vel modi vel relationes* [extra-essentials either modes or relations].",[]],"refl:2314":["*essentia est conceptus rei primitivus, completus, determinatus* [essence is the primitive, complete, determinate concept of a thing].\n*ad completudinem reqviritur: coordinatio; ut sit primitiva notio: defectus subordinationis* [for completeness there is required co-ordination; for it to be a primitive notion, absence of subordination].",[]],"refl:2315":["[Der nothwendige Gr]\nThe [noth] fundamental concept: everything that belongs necessarily to a thing.",[]],"refl:2316":["Essential constituents [Wesentliche Stücke] belong to one another as co-ordinated marks in an aggregate, that is, the essence; attributes [belong] to the [Wes] *essentialia* in a series.",[]],"refl:2317":["We are here concerned solely with the completeness of the aggregate of co-ordinated marks.",[]],"refl:2318":["Natural essence — logical [essence]. (g The sufficiency of the marks, as regards co-ordination or as regards subordination.)\nNature and essence of a thing.",[]],"refl:2319":["*complexus omnium notarum conceptum aliqvem primitive constituentium* [the complex of all the marks primitively constituting some concept].\nThe sum of all the primitive marks which together make up the whole concept of a thing.",[]],"refl:2320":["The essence is *complexus notarum* (g *primitivarum*) *interne* (g *conceptui [qvodam] dato*) *sufficientium* [the complex of (primitive) marks internally sufficient (to a given concept)].",[]],"refl:2321":["All predicates in respect of which an object is determined through its concept belong to its logical essence. Those [durch] on which, as determining grounds, everything that belongs to its existence depends, [belong] to the real essence.",[]],"refl:2322":["We are not here treating of the essence of things (g *essentia* or *habitus*). Rather, everything that belongs to a concept belongs to it either (g necessarily) essentially (that is, the concept cannot subsist without it) or extra-essentially. The concept contains *constitutiva* [constituents] as parts, through which alone it is first possible, and *rationata*, consequences. Taken together, the former are entitled the essence; each of the remainder is entitled an *attributum*. The extra-essential elements [Stücke] attach to the *obiectum* either internally or externally: for example, distinctness is internal, and popularity or influence in the sciences is external.",[]],"refl:2323":["The *esse rei* [being of the thing] or *conceptus* [of the concept]. What pertains *ad essentiam* (*esse conceptus*) is the inseparable, necessary mark.",[]],"refl:2324":["The logical or the real essence. Logic abstracts from all content, and therefore also from the thing itself.",[]],"refl:2325":["The measure of the three angles does not belong to the logical essence of a triangle; for this is contained not in the concept but in the construction.",[]],"refl:2326":["On the interchanging of the attributes with the essentials [Essentialien], e.g. the definition of the square. Only in mathematics.",[]],"refl:2327":["*Repraesentatio cum conscientia conjuncta est cogitatio* [A representation joined with consciousness is thought].",[]],"refl:2328":["*Clara cognitio: qua res a se invicem distinguimus* [Clear knowledge: that by which we distinguish things from one another].\nThe representation which everyone has of an animal is clear.",[]],"refl:2329":["The peasant's knowledge of a precious stone.\nIn every [Erke] clear mode of knowledge there must be marks.\nOn the closing sentence of L § 124:\nWhoever believes that he is right, but can adduce no ground, has an obscure knowledge of this ground.",[]],"refl:2330":["32.\nL 32'. On »cognitio absolute obscura«:\nThe word of the Schoolmen, *entelechy*.\nOn »cognitio relative obscura«:\nThe concepts of the enthusiasts: *el gibbor*, little worm [Würmelein].\nOpposite L § 125 »Dunkelheit der Sachen«:\nRelative obscurity prevails in the consideration of the origin of the human soul.\nOpposite L § 125 »Dunkelheit in dem Kopfe« etc. (31738f.):\ne.g. the man who could not read with spectacles.\nFurther, L 32 beside the same words:\nWhoever calls a profound work obscure.\nL 32'. On the penultimate sentence of L § 125:\nThe concept of how the body causes ideas in the human soul is wholly obscure; but the concept of the manner in which the mechanism [triebwerk] of the human body operates is *partialiter* [partially] obscure.\nOn the last sentence of L § 125:\nAt the least we must stop at some marks, of which we have no further marks, e.g. colours. Indeed, in the sciences too.",[]],"refl:2331":["Beside the last three sentences of L § 125 and the first of L § 126:\nAn example is not a mark, and does not belong to the concept as a part; it belongs, as intuition, to the use of the concept.\nIntelligibility [Verständlichkeit] is clearness through examples.\nObjective distinctness often causes subjective obscurity.",[]],"refl:2332":["(g Distinctness in respect of degree which rests on the subjective clearness of the marks, that is, on their vividness, is aesthetic.)\nLogical distinctness [ge] is often possible only to the detriment of the aesthetic. Conversely, distinctness through examples (g similes) which do not fit exactly, but must be taken (g only) by way of an analogy, is harmful to the logical.",[]],"refl:2333":["(g Clearness through concepts or intuition.)\nThe subjective clearness, and even distinctness, (g of the knowledge* which one has) can very often (s not) be combined with the objective, namely, [the clearness] of the knowledge which one seeks. E.g. [Der] what duty is: one may there speak of laws of happiness which are very intelligible, but which do not in the least explain what one demands to know, namely, duty. To extricate oneself from this latter obscurity, one must first be able to see, and at the outset support the still unpractised judgment of the eyes only by groping about. Whoever lacks this second sense, namely, that of judging knowledge from principles, and so *a priori* and *in abstracto*, not merely *in concreto* [im Gegebenen], so far as the examples palpably lead. [sentence breaks off]** Comprehensibility [Faßlichkeit] and distinctness differ as ease and thoroughness, as making easy and drawing out easily [leicht vormahlen].\n*(s [empirical] aesthetic distinctness through examples is not logical [distinctness] through concepts.)\n**(g Intelligible is what I know through examples *in concreto* (without marks). Distinct: what I know through clear marks [er|kenne].\nThus much can be intelligible which is yet not distinct, and much distinct which is hard to understand, because it extends to remote marks, whose connection with intuition is possible only through a long series.)",[]],"refl:2334":["I seek not to bring distinctness [zu brin durch] into the modes of knowledge which one has, but to produce distinct modes of knowledge [in das] of that of which one had as yet no knowledge, but merely representations.",[]],"refl:2335":["We perceive obscurity only in a distinct knowledge, when through it we perceive distinction of whose grounds we are not conscious.",[]],"refl:2336":["Faults in instruction.\nOn L § 126 No. 2:\nSaying of Socrates.\nOn L § 126 No. 3:\nI can forget.\nOn L § 126 No. 4:\nCapacity and fundamental concepts accumulate.",[]],"refl:2337":["There are degrees of obscurity. One night is darker than another. E.g. the man whose concept of vanilla is that it is a fruit is not so obscure as the man whose concept knows it only as a created thing.\nFurther, L 33':\nWhen one reads a book, it is more obscure the first time than the second.",[]],"refl:2338":["Obscure knowledge is knowledge, just as a picture remains a picture that has been hung in a dark room.\nWithout obscure knowledge there would be no clear knowledge either.\nFurther, L 33:\nWe are often in bad humour without knowing why.\nThe concept of the sour, the sweet, is obscure.",[]],"refl:2339":["A necessary obscurity [in our knowledge] of the connection of the soul with the brain.\nMan has a want of powers from neglected cultivation.\nFurther, L 33 from β1−ε2, beside »wenn — Dieser« (Z. 26f.):\nObscure knowledge of essence.\nOf the nature of spirit.",[]],"refl:2340":["Whoever listens to a sermon without attention.\nFurther, L 33 from β1−ε2, beside »aus — gewisse« (Z 29f.):\nAn obscure knowledge of the origin of dreams.\nWhen we hear that bodies have a force of attraction.",[]],"refl:2341":["From the want of concepts.\nNo ignorance is, properly speaking, praiseworthy, except in so far as [the knowledge] could have bad effects, e.g. not knowing all the vices of Suetonius' emperors.\nI make it a point of honour not to know it.",[]],"refl:2342":["All knowledge is either true or false.\nObscure true knowledge is the material for clear true concepts, e.g. that of the Christian in religion. Sense [Der Sinn]. False, on the other hand: [ente] sympathy.",[]],"refl:2343":["The making clear of a knowledge. But the production of a clear knowledge is not *evolutio*. Synthetic origin of clearness.\nFurther, L 34' (from ε2−κ3? ρ2?):\nWhy it is entitled unfolding [auswikelung].",[]],"refl:2344":["2. Whether one should and may make it clear?\nOn L § 131a No. 3:\n3. The doctrine of monads presupposes a presupposition of metaphysics.\nOn L § 131b:\nRules of attention.\n1. To direct one's eye to the thing alone. To abstract.\n2. Long — — —. Ghosts in the night.\n3. With as many senses or cognitive faculties as is possible, e.g. in botany, seeing, tasting, smelling. Water.\n4. Comparison with others, e.g. green colour is of middling strength; one can make it from yellow and blue.\nTo decipher old runic writings.\nFurther β1−ε2:\nI hear a tone; I have obscure knowledge of its nature.\n1. Attention to the thing in all sorts of circumstances, in the bell.\n2. I take the tone of a string, of a glass, of a pipe.\n3. I abstract from the matter.\nVibration of the air.",[]],"refl:2345":["[Marks] which are to be found in no other thing than this,\nand without which the thing cannot be.\n*essentialia reciproca*.\nOn L § 132 »ausführlich klar« (Z. 23):\ncompletely clear.\nFurther, from γ−φ:\nSufficient for comparison or derivation.\nFurther, L 35' from ψ−θ:\nThe author here distinguishes completely clear from completely distinct.",[]],"refl:2346":["Everyone has a clear knowledge of a star; but if at night he takes for one a light burning in a distant village, or a shooting star, then his knowledge is not exhaustively clear.\nThis is the [extensive] objective magnitude of clearness.\nTo the complete clearness of knowledge there belongs [a knowledge] a representation of such marks as are *essentialia reciproca*, and which therefore both belong to the thing necessarily and unalterably and are shared by it with no other. E.g. I distinguish the triangle from all other figures by its three angles; but I cannot distinguish man from other beings by thinking, for God and all other spirits also think.",[]],"refl:2347":["The knowledge of colours is exhaustively clear, but not wholly clear; for in colours I distinguish nothing from one another [within them], and this belongs to distinctness.\nThe intensive magnitude of clearness belongs to distinctness.\nIf I distinguish a man from all others at all times and in all circumstances, I have the greatest (g objective) clearness that I can have. But if I distinguish the distinguishing marks in him himself, I have a distinct knowledge.",[]],"refl:2348":["not when I distinguish it from all things.",[]],"refl:2349":["e.g. wisdom from cunning, devotion and piety.\nOn »leichter« (Z. 23):\nFacility can be obtained through more frequent comparison, e.g. in tasting.",[]],"refl:2350":["*Completudo externa* cannot be attained without *interna*. The latter can be attained only of pure concepts of reason and arbitrary concepts, but not of empirical ones; hence these last are inexplicable.",[]],"refl:2351":["*Completudo* [*profunditas*] and *praecisio* constitute *soliditas*, thoroughness.",[]],"refl:2352":["this is not always necessary, indeed is seldom even possible, because I neither wish nor am able to compare a thing with all others.",[]],"refl:2353":["Aesthetically complete knowledge there is none at all. Completeness [Vollständigkeit] ought not to be entitled *adaequatio*.",[]],"refl:2354":["The totality of the (g whole) clear marks, either as an aggregate or as a series. Exhaustiveness or thorough distinctness.",[]],"refl:2355":["One can make two kinds of effort: 1. to know clearly (s something) (s through that) which one does not (s previously) know, or 2. to know clearly (s through that) what one already knows. The first can perhaps become wholly clear, the other not. A merchant who wants not to gain but to know how he stands. The first act is synthetic, the other analytic.",[]],"refl:2356":["The intensive magnitude of distinctness: depth.",[]],"refl:2357":["Degrees of distinctness through co-ordinated or subordinated marks*. Sufficient distinctness in co-ordination is *completudo* (g and together with precision *adaequatio*); that in subordination: completeness [Vollständigkeit].**\nThere are degrees of co-ordinated distinctness, as of subordinated.\n*(g The whole is equal to all its *compartes* taken together; hence also the [whole] distinctness of the whole representation is *completudo* (internal or external). The distinctness of all *partes subordinatae*, on the other hand, is profundity.)\n** (g Distinctness through co-ordination comes about *per synthesin*, through subordination *per analysin*. The former in mathematics and in nature, the latter in the philosophy of pure reason.)",[]],"refl:2358":["»To make a distinct knowledge«: synthetic [marks] distinctness.\nOr »to make a knowledge distinct«: analytic.",[]],"refl:2359":["Total distinctness through co-ordinated marks in respect of the concept, not knowing the thing exhaustively.",[]],"refl:2360":["A mode of knowledge, as ground of distinction [und], is relatively or absolutely sufficient.",[]],"refl:2361":["Synthetic as well as analytic distinctness can be complete. 1. Exhaustive, 2. thorough distinctness.",[]],"refl:2362":["*Cognitio est vel extensive vel profunde distincta* [Knowledge is distinct either extensively or in depth]. Here only the first is still treated.",[]],"refl:2363":["Distinctness through analytic marks, and so *a priori* [through concepts], of what lies in the concept.\nDistinctness through synthetic [marks], through what is added beyond the concept, and so is given as a mark in intuition (pure or empirical).\nNot all distinctness rests on the analysis of a given concept. That [durch] of mathematical and [physical] empirical knowledge rests on the extension, and so the synthesis, of marks.",[]],"refl:2364":["When one distinguishes the thing from others in many respects.\nVividness of knowledge consists chiefly in making it like the impression of the senses and expressing all its various marks through sensible representations.\nOn »der Stärke nach klärer« (Z. 27):\nThus the representation of red colour is *intensive* clearer than that of celadon.\nA picture in which the colours are very bright.\nFurther, L 36:\n[The knowledge] of the soul is *intensive* clearer.\nThat of the body *extensive*.",[]],"refl:2365":["Subjectively greater clearness (s through sensation: for that is not objective) is vividness; objectively greater [clearness] is [Deut] intelligibility [ver|ständlichkeit].\n(s evident. cogent. logical: distinctness of analysis.)\nClearness through externally sufficient marks.\nOn L § 135 sentence 2:\nClearness through internal [marks].",[]],"refl:2366":["Aesthetic clearness\nLogical clearness.\n(g rests on the magnitude of the\n(g determination of the object.)\nalteration in the subject.)",[]],"refl:2367":["On »lebhafte Erkenntniss« (33324):\nHence vividness arises from sensibility, from interest, from extensive magnitude.\nFurther, from ψ−θ:\nco-ordinated marks.\nFurther, from ψ−θ:\n*vivacitas* (s brightness [Helligkeit]) *et perspicuitas*.",[]],"refl:2368":["Extensive clearness through external marks, intensive through internal. The former through co-ordinated, the latter through subordinated [marks]. The former: broad, the latter: deep clearness. Want of the first: dryness; of the last: shallowness [Seichtig|keit].\nThe former excellence is aesthetic, the latter logical. It is impossible to make knowledge perfect on one side without losing on the other.",[]],"refl:2369":["Broad (understanding —) distinctness hinders the deep (g distinctness of reason), and the latter the former. The deep brings dryness, and vividness shallowness. Deep and high are the same. Shallow, deep, thorough [gründlich] are all analogies from divers.",[]],"refl:2370":["Vividness concerns intuition; strength, sensation.",[]],"refl:2371":["Clearness perfect in a logical respect is that which fits knowledge for distinctness through concepts; in an aesthetic respect, through intuitions. Thus the first rests on representation *in abstracto*, the second on representation *in concreto*.\nClear concepts can be very serviceable for distinguishing.",[]],"refl:2372":["Vividness is the greater relative clearness, strength the greater absolute clearness. The former in comparison of it with others; *per notas proprias*: for distinguishing; *per communes*: for identity.\nThe vividness 1. of merely clear, 2. of distinct knowledge. The |\nlatter *per notas immediatas plures*. Internal distinctness is either broad, and this is also vivid; or deep, and this is dry. One must begin with the former. When I know something immediately in examples (*concreto*), I know it vividly; the more mediately, the fainter and drier. Extensive distinctness does not have its limits as the intensive does. —",[]],"refl:2373":["(κ3?) L 36.\nVividness often hinders and furthers distinctness.",[]],"refl:2374":["Vividness for distinguishing, distinctness for seeing through [Durchschauung], that is, for internal knowledge.",[]],"refl:2375":["Sensible distinctness and distinctness of the understanding (g clearness); the former through (g in) intuition, the latter through concepts.\nDistinctness of reason through [vern] modes of knowledge *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2376":["On »der Stärke nach klärer« (33327):\nExternal clearness. Distinctness is internal clearness.\nFurther:\non sensation, having force, not interest.\nFurther:\nwhereby others are obscured.",[]],"refl:2377":["Distinctness is a logically greater clearness, vividness an aesthetically greater clearness.\nVividness of the impression (interest) (g strength), or of intuition and idea (extensive magnitude).",[]],"refl:2378":["Evident, cogent, thorough.\nAesthetic distinctness of knowledge through examples, logical distinctness through concepts.",[]],"refl:2379":["Magnitude of clearness in intuition or in the concept.",[]],"refl:2380":["Logical sharpness in separation, aesthetic strength in the extension of the imagination.",[]],"refl:2381":["Luminous — bright concepts.",[]],"refl:2382":["Vividness is representation which arouses the feeling of life.",[]],"refl:2383":["Sensible representation has vividness in intuition and strength in sensation. The first alone is advantageous to the understanding; the second makes [one] partial. The aesthetic art is to make the former outweigh the latter through the harmony of intuition, by means of imagination, with ideas of reason.",[]],"refl:2384":["Whoever has not often compared a thing with others, his knowledge [of it] becomes obscure.\nWhoever has not represented it in separation must know it obscurely.\nWhoever has not turned his attention to it.",[]],"refl:2385":["Second degree of clearness.\nWhen the marks are clearly represented, [e.g. I have a clear] that is, when one can distinguish them from others; thus when not only the total representation but also the partial is clear. E.g. I know that gunpowder has an expansive force when it is ignited; I know that it consists of sulphur, saltpetre and charcoal. — —\nWhen I distinguish the marks from one another.\nIn distinctness I can state the marks.\nFurther, L 36. On »In jener — klar« (33824, 33918):\nI can state the marks.",[]],"refl:2386":["On the beginning of L § 138:\nA knowledge that is indistinct can still be clear; but if the marks are not also clearly known, I cannot state them, and so cannot make my clear knowledge comprehensible to others.",[]],"refl:2387":["On the 2nd sentence of L § 138:\nThe fine sciences [Schönen Wißenschaften] are those which furnish the rules for guiding the |\nlower cognitive faculties, that is, confused knowledge. *Aesthetica*.\nFurther, L 36 on the beginning of this sentence, from β1−κ3:\nTo seek to increase extensive clearness.",[]],"refl:2388":["Opposite the end of the second and the beginning of the third sentence of L § 138:\nWhen someone treats the metaphysical question of the origin of evil as Leibniz does, it is beautiful and thorough at the same time.",[]],"refl:2389":["When I attend to the parts of a knowledge, I analyse it. This is entitled *reflexio*. Now since I cannot have total distinctness without partial clearness, nor this without separate consideration, |\nI must analyse and consider each part separately, e.g. the body, space, the motions, the figure.",[]],"refl:2390":["e.g. *notio irresolubilis* [unanalysable concept]: being and not-being.\nBeside L § 139 »oder — werden« (34026-34117):\nTo such [Begrif] things there corresponds only a clear concept.\n(s First, analytically, to arrive at partial concepts; then, synthetically, from these back to the whole.)",[]],"refl:2391":["We must distinguish understanding [verstehen] and comprehending [begreiffen]. When I know something clearly or distinctly, I understand [it].\nBut fully comprehending [breaks off]",[]],"refl:2392":["Not all distinctness arises analytically, namely through marks which already lay in the concept of a thing; much arises synthe|tically\nthrough marks which one does not include [in de] in the concept, but connects with it as belonging to it. Composite concepts can be made distinct analytically, simple ones synthetically. In the first act there arises merely a new form of the concept, in the second a new matter. The subject is distinct from the concept through which it is thought. This concept contains marks of it; hence the concept which in one case serves to designate the (g logical) subject is in another used in place of the predicate, e.g. a body. This does not hold of the *conceptus singularis*, e.g. the earth, Julius Caesar, etc.; but even through such a concept we still represent many marks, whereby we think an individual subject.",[]],"refl:2393":["Distinct modes of knowledge are made *per synthesin* in mathematics.\nObjects are made distinct *per synthesin* in physiology,\nand modes of knowledge are made distinct *per analysin* in the *scientiae rationales*.\n(s *per synthesin vel arbitrariam vel ab obiecto datam vel a cognitione rationali datam* [through synthesis, either arbitrary, or given by the object, or given by rational knowledge].)",[]],"refl:2394":["The following degrees are to be distinguished:\n1. To represent something to oneself.\n2. To know [wissen] something: to represent it with consciousness. (s to represent to oneself with consciousness.) (s [perceive] *percipere*.) (s *apprehendere* [to grasp]: the beginning of *percipere*.)\n3. To be acquainted with [kennen] something: thereby to distinguish it from others in comparison.\n4. To understand [verstehen] something. (s What I am acquainted with and understand, that I know [erkenne].) (s to be able to expound and communicate it to others.) To know something through the understanding. (s *concipere*: to know through a concept. *intelligere*: through a judgment.)\n(s knowing — opining.)\n5. To have insight into [einsehen] something. (s *perspicere*.) To know something through reason. (s to understand something *a priori*.) (s through grounds, either possible or also actual.)\n6.* To comprehend [begreifen] something: to have insight into it sufficiently for a purpose. (s to be able to make it oneself.) (s to have complete insight into it through reason.)\n*(s I comprehend that which I can (relatively) determine *a priori*, and so should also have known from *data* (g or been able to know) if it were not given, e.g. an eclipse of the moon, but not heat from friction. The rest is entitled insight in explanations.\nOne can explain something which experience gives, that is, form a concept of its possibility, without yet having insight into it, that is, without knowing *a priori* its necessity, and so that the object will be so. For example, one explains the dissolution of salts in water by the same attraction whereby water makes things wet; but one would not have been able to predict it, had experience not shown that a piece of salt can in this way be wholly dispersed in a liquid. We have insight into nothing but what we can make.\nFor explanation a hypothesis suffices (principle of possibility),\n—\nInsight\n—\ncertainty of the ground.)",[]],"refl:2395":["Either neither the object nor its concept is given: arbitrary; mathematics.\nOr the object is given, but not its concept: observational; physics.\nOr the concept is given, but not the object: analytic; metaphysics, morals.",[]],"refl:2396":["Synthesis is wherever the concept is not given, but made or extracted.\nAnalysis is wherever the concept is given, but confused.\nWhere the thing is not given me (through the senses) (g and no concept either), there I think it *per synthesin arbitrariam*.\nWhere the thing is given me (through the senses) (g but no concept), there I think it *per synthesin empiricam*.\nWhere the concept of the thing is given me: *per analysin*.\nNow the thing itself can also be given through the concept, as *in moralibus*.",[]],"refl:2397":["1. All distinctness is intuitive or discursive (through concepts); the former aesthetic, the latter logical.\n2. a. Analytic, explicative; b. synthetic, ampliative. Both through the clearness of the marks which constitute either the actual concept that we have of the object or its possible complete concept. Empirical distinctness. [All] clearness of the partial representations in a whole given representation, or of the whole representation as a part of another given whole representation.\n(g Explicative marks. *nota explicans vel augens* [explicative or ampliative mark].)",[]],"refl:2398":["We comprehend only what we can make ourselves.",[]],"refl:2399":["L 37\nDistinct modes of knowledge arise through *analysis vel empirica vel rationalis* or through *synthesis*.",[]],"refl:2400":["In the analytic origin of distinctness I proceed from the |\nwhole to the partial representation; in the synthetic, from the parts to the whole.",[]],"refl:2401":["On the first »inconceptibile«\nUnthinkable; inexplicable (what it is); e.g. genius, or the self-help of nature in illness.",[]],"refl:2402":["The method of inversion is that whereby I can conversely produce again *a priori* the concept of what has been explained from the grounds of explanation; e.g. chemistry, which decomposes mineral waters and produces them again, but not wine.\nPhysical astronomy and optics can do this.\nWhether I can synthetically reproduce an analysed concept must be shown from the properties that flow from it.",[]],"refl:2403":["I do not comprehend the thing; it is impossible. La Motte.\nFolly of the freethinkers; likewise of the common man in respect of astronomy.\nOpposite »eine Erkenntniss — schliessen« (34625-34733):\nWe must, however, hold the [in]comprehensibility of a thing suspect when we are assured that we have the fundamental concepts needed for it, e.g. the talk of the enthusiasts.\nOn »was wir begreifen, das begreifen auch andere«:\nHence we must explain ourselves distinctly.\nOn »was ich jetzo begreife, werde ich auch künftig begreifen«:\nHence we should impress the marks on ourselves by attention and repeat them often.\nOn »was ich jetzo nicht begreife« etc.:\nHence we should not become faint-hearted.",[]],"refl:2404":["[abstrahere] *evolvere*.\nOn L § 142 No. 2:\n*reflectere*.\nOn L § 142 No. 3:\n*comparare*. E.g. indivisibility, understanding, will. Government of the body by the soul.\nOn L § 142 No. 4:\n*coordinare*.\nOn L § 142 No. 5:\n*abstrahere*.",[]],"refl:2405":["The distinct knowledge of the opinions of certain enthusiasts.",[]],"refl:2406":["Nothing more can be added to a complete concept; for if it contains all the marks, there are no more. But the very same thing |\nthat was already thought in it can be added tautologically. The [abs] prevention of this tautology is precision. *Conceptus completus et praecisus rem adaequat* [A complete and precise concept is adequate to the thing].",[]],"refl:2407":["(s Extensive distinctness through co-ordinated marks, intensive through subordinated. Congruence.)\n*Completudo*: broad. *profunditas*: thoroughness. *praecisio*: exact measure [Abgemessenheit]. (g sume —) adequate. Commensurate [Angemessen].\n(s Perfection of the understanding — of reason, and both together.\nPrecision is the negative perfection of the extensive magnitude of distinctness.)",[]],"refl:2408":["First, of extensive and intensive distinctness; secondly, of the former as abundant, defective, or adequate.",[]],"refl:2409":["Distinctness of the understanding (g through co-ordinated marks) (g through immediate marks), when it is complete, is entitled *completudo* — extensive.\nDistinctness of reason (g through subordinated marks) (g through mediate [marks]) is depth: intensive, *totalitas*.\nDistinctness of judgment* (g through subordination under the marks) is that in which the mark is the intuition corresponding to the concept.\n(g Through examples; the intuition is here also [regarded] as a mark, that is, a partial concept, not indeed of the given concept, but of a knowledge through it.)",[]],"refl:2410":["Analysis is carried further or less far according to the diversity of purposes.\n1. When one wishes to comprehend the thing from its ground and to exercise oneself.\n2. When one wishes to make it comprehensible to others. — — Until the marks are clear.\n3. In order to prove it. — — — — — —\n4. So that I may distinguish things which agree in many respects.",[]],"refl:2411":["[Thoroughly sufficient] *sufficientia extensiva* and *intensiva*. The former: exhaustiveness; the latter: thoroughness.\n[A high degree of knowledge *a priori*, very universal, is required for thoroughness.]",[]],"refl:2412":["This rests more on the kind than on the multitude of the marks, e.g. what flame is.",[]],"refl:2413":["*Completudo* (s extensively (g complete) (sufficient) distinctness) is [suff] total distinctness through co-ordinated marks (s exhaustiveness).\n[ad] [Completeness] Depth is total distinctness through subordinated marks.\n(s Intensively complete distinctness is *profunditas*.\nBoth together: thoroughness.\nThe extensive magnitude of distinctness, so far as it is not abundant, is entitled *praecisio*.\nPrecision together with *completudo* constitutes *cognitio, quae rem adaequat*.)",[]],"refl:2414":["The intensively adequate knowledge in *profunditas*, together with the extensively adequate in *completudo* and precision, [ist] constitutes the *consummata cognitionis perfectio* [consummate perfection of knowledge]: exhaustiveness and thoroughness together.\nExhaustiveness and exact measure [abgemessenheit] together constitute commensurateness [Angemessenheit].",[]],"refl:2415":["One must seek out the *essentialia reciproca*, or compare things with all possible [others]. How the classes help in this.\nFurther, L 40 from β1−ψ:\nconstant and proper marks.",[]],"refl:2416":["Exact measure [Abgemessenheit].\nAn exactly measured exhaustiveness. Commensurateness [Angemessenheit]. (s adequate.)",[]],"refl:2417":["When one mark is determined by another.\nBeside »Erkenntniss — ist« (Z 20f.):\nNot in the beautiful.\nOn L § 151 end:\nSo that too many marks do not confuse him.",[]],"refl:2418":["On the first sentence of L § 152:\ne.g. in mathematical problems.",[]],"refl:2419":["On the first sentence of L § 153:\nWhoever is exhaustive on the mathematical point and brief on other matters.",[]],"refl:2420":["Logical, that is, scholastic, [distinctness] combined with the aesthetic or popular. The universal *in concreto* for the common understanding.",[]],"refl:2421":["A dark mind is one that is clear in obscure idols and obscure in those which can be known most clearly.",[]],"refl:2422":["Truth is *convenientia cognitionis cum objecto* [agreement of knowledge with the object]. Certainty therefore requires not only *clara repraesentatio objecti*, but also *clara repraesentatio cognitionis de objecto et convenientiae ipsius cum illo* [clear representation of the knowledge of the object and of its agreement with it]. E.g. I represent something gleaming behind the clouds as the moon. This knowledge is clear and true. But it becomes certain when I clearly represent to myself this agreement of my representation with the object, when I distinguish the representation through which I see the moon from that through which I see an atmospheric sign or the sun.\nWe are not certain of the general conversion of the Jews; we do not clearly have insight into the [Merkmal d] truth of our knowledge.\nSocrates was not certain of the immortality of the soul. He represented immortality to himself, but he did not clearly have insight into the agreement of his knowledge with the object.",[]],"refl:2423":["On the 2nd sentence of L § 156:\nNo thing is uncertain in and for itself.\nOn »gewiss falsch« (Z. 34):\nWhen someone drives on a wrong road and represents it to himself |\nas wrong, but in such a way that he does not have insight into the agreement of his representation with the object.",[]],"refl:2424":["It is sensibly certain that the light of the sun warms.\nBut it would be learnedly certain if I had insight into the marks whereby the representation of heat agrees with the representation of light.\nIf I have insight into the manner in which the marks determine certainty, it is logically certain.",[]],"refl:2425":["e.g. that there is a God.",[]],"refl:2426":["We have an exhaustive certainty that the sun is the cause of summer.\nWhen I am thereby in a position to distinguish it from all the marks of the opposite.\nOpposite the end of L § 159:\nNon-exhaustive certainty: whether the moon is inhabited. We lack marks, or those we have are not the right ones.",[]],"refl:2427":["Beside the closing sentence of L § 159:\nThe man who becomes faint-hearted in misfortune has no exhaustive certainty of the temptation.",[]],"refl:2428":["Certainty removes *formido oppositi* [fear of the opposite]; but this is no sure mark of objective certainty.\nOn L § 160 »die unausführliche — vertreiben« (36427f.):\ne.g. Two who travel a road.",[]],"refl:2429":["Certainty of a believer,\nof a freethinker.\nOn the end of L § 160:\ne.g. a freethinker, because he does not have insight into those opposing truths which conflict with his marks.",[]],"refl:2430":["It resolves itself finally into merely sensibly clear concepts, and has nothing superfluous.\nOn L § 161 end:\nOne must seek to know mathematically everything that can be known with mathematical certainty.\n(s We have a mathematical certainty of the immaterial nature of the human soul.)",[]],"refl:2431":["On the expression: it is as certain as 2×2=4.",[]],"refl:2432":["The ultimate concepts are either wholly simple or concepts of experience.",[]],"refl:2433":["e.g. It is certain that Troy was destroyed, but not whether Achilles existed.\nIt is not wholly certain that after death men are to behold distant heavenly bodies. In part, namely that they are to know the universe more nearly, it is certain.\n[It is certain that the blessed are to come to heaven. But whether the same]",[]],"refl:2434":["That of which my sense can perceive the truth in a vivid manner (g and [sie gen] [alle] distinguish enough marks of it), of that one is convinced. Many have indeed a certainty of the truth of religion, but no conviction. Exhaustive certainty is conviction.\nTo produce conviction there are required enough (g and the right) marks, and a clear representation of them, e.g. that there is a God.",[]],"refl:2435":["Living knowledge of truth.\n(s In relation to others.)",[]],"refl:2436":["2. is taken with the absurdity of the opposite. Knowledge which is not undeniable is merely probable.",[]],"refl:2437":["Accepted,\nÜ The opposite:\nSettled,\nÜ Uncertain,\nIndisputable.\nÜ Groundless,\nEvident,\nÜ indemonstrable,\nIntuitive, thorough,\nÜ disputed.\ncogent, consequent.\nÜ",[]],"refl:2438":["When a knowledge is certain through a clearly discerned connection with its grounds.",[]],"refl:2439":["In the sciences.\n[It is credible\nplausible\nAccepted\nFrom]\nFurther, from ψ−θ:\nFrom secured principles; shallow without them.",[]],"refl:2440":["4. cogent in connection and system, in proofs.",[]],"refl:2441":["Whoever has insight into something in a connection with many grounds, to him it is very certain.",[]],"refl:2442":["according to all its determinations.",[]],"refl:2443":["45.\nL 45'.\nIt is certain to us that the blood circulates. Was it therefore certain to our forefathers?\nOpposite L § 165 Nos. 2-4:\nThere is a difference between the two propositions, »This I do not know certainly« and »This I know as uncertain«. I know the theory of inhabited heavenly bodies as uncertain, and I know the Un [breaks off].\n(g Because one has no certainty of the immortality of the soul, one must not on that account be held to be denying it.)\nDo not read the books of the doubters.\nL 45. On L § 165 No. 2:\nIt is only a fault of modes of knowledge.\nOn L § 165 No. 3:\ne.g. the prediction of comets.\nOn L § 165 No. 5:\n*posterior dies docet priorem* [the later day teaches the earlier].\nOn L § 165 No. 6:\ne.g. the *objectiones* of the scoffers at religion.",[]],"refl:2444":["A traveller takes his time when he packs.\nFurther, L 45' on L § 166 end, from γ−ρ1:\nTo give assent in a provisional judgment, but not in a determining one.",[]],"refl:2445":["45'.\nL 45:\nA theologian has a shallow knowledge when he proves more from the Fathers than from Holy Scripture.\nL 45':\nNot everything which cannot be demonstrated in the highest degree is shallow.",[]],"refl:2446":["(g On the unchangeableness of rational belief.)\nIt is indeed entitled moral certainty; but it is not knowing [Wissen], only believing, as with the jury. When one absolutely must pass a judgment, and that from moral grounds, and may not remain *in suspenso*, then this judgment is necessary.\nThe principle of the self-preservation of reason is the foundation of rational belief. In such belief the holding to be true has the same degree as in knowing, but is of another kind. It is derived not from knowledge of the grounds in the object, but from the genuine need of the subject in respect of the theoretical as well as the practical employment |\nof reason. It always remains believing; it will never become knowing; and as the former it is also the most purposive for creatures. — Knowing puffs up (if it is illusion [Wahn]), but knowing up to its limits (Socrates) makes humble.",[]],"refl:2447":["What is grounded on rational belief is morally certain.",[]],"refl:2448":["Historical belief cannot properly be opposed to knowing, for it can itself be a knowing. It is entitled merely a belief when it is practically sufficient, though logically insufficient. — For [it to be] morally sufficient much is required: [namely,] to take something upon one's conscience.\nRational belief alone can be distinguished from knowing. It rests, however, on a practical ground. Yet it is also a hypothesis against which nothing can be said, and which is nevertheless very fruitful as a presupposition.",[]],"refl:2449":["In opinion one is still free (g problematic); in belief, [one judges] assertorically (s one declares oneself). (s In knowing, apodeictically, |\nirrevocably.) In the former one need only bring forward a better opinion against it; in belief, however, I am already bound as regards the subject.\n(s *A priori* belief.)\nThat holding to be true is objective which is valid for everyone without distinction of subject; through it an object is determined for it.\n(s Of what I merely believe, I modestly refrain from giving it out as a judgment valid for others also (objective), e.g. the probability of narratives. These are subjective grounds, not all of which can be made logical, that is, objective.)",[]],"refl:2450":["43.\nL 43':\n(s to conjecture)\n(s to expect)\nOpining,\nBelieving,\nKnowing\nHolding to be true\nWagering\nSwearing\n(s to have more grounds on one side than on the other)\n(s to conjecture)\n(s to assume)\n(s to assert)\nKnowing and believing are decided, opining undecided.\n(g Certainty is the subjective *completudo* of holding to be true.)\n(s Belief: subjective necessity of holding to be true. That holding to be true is (s logically) objectively necessary (s sufficient) |\nwhich determines the truth from grounds (which are communicable) that are independent of the constitution of the subject.)\n(s To belief there belongs not only subjective *sufficientia* but also subjective necessity.)\n(s He who opines reserves to himself the retraction of his judgment. (g God knows better.) *Salvis melioribus* [saving better views]. He who believes: not so, but unchangeableness, yet with freedom for everyone else.)\n(s A holding to be true that is consciously insufficient is opining. A sufficient holding to be true [is] conviction. If it is (g practically) subjectively necessary: believing (g wagering); if it is (g logically) objectively necessary: knowing (g swearing).)\nL 43:\n(s In speculative (g philosophical) knowledge one can indeed opine, but not believe. In mathematical [knowledge] one can neither opine nor believe, but only know. In empirical (g or historical) [knowledge], all three. In practical [knowledge], believing alone.)",[]],"refl:2451":["Practical belief is decided and completely certain, so that its holding to be true is complete *in sensu practico*, and can receive no addition even [nach der] through the greatest grounds of speculation.\nOpining is contradistinguished not from believing, but from knowing.\nBelieving is opposed to knowing more properly than merely, as in the historical [case], to knowledge of reason; for* we say also of historical knowledge, e.g. where Madrid lies, that we know it, although we have it from the narratives of others.\nIn actions according to belief everything proceeds honestly, but not |\nin presumptuous asseverations and oaths, except when one swears that one sincerely believes this.\nThe distinction in holding to be true [scheint] seems really (g to) be merely practical, namely, how much one could wager on it.\n*(s The subjectively sufficient holding to be true which [nicht] is at the same time objective is conviction; if it is not, persuasion. (s Lottery winnings.))",[]],"refl:2452":["When the grounds of holding to be true are only part of the sufficient ground, it is entitled probability.\nWhen holding to be true is regarded only subjectively, and so is not universally valid, it is entitled plausibility [Scheinbarkeit].\nWhen knowledge is apparently certain, it is entitled persuasion.\nWhen objective certainty is intuitive, it is entitled evidence.",[]],"refl:2453":["The modes of knowledge of reason contain not merely what is, but, since they are *a priori*, what [things] must be or necessarily are (apodeictic).",[]],"refl:2454":["(s All conviction is either logical (the object is certain) or (g practical: belief as against knowing) moral (I am certain).)\nCertainty is either empirical* or rational (s of the common understanding or of speculative reason).\nThe rational is either apodeictic (s knowing) (speculative) or (s rational belief) moral (practical).**\nThe apodeictic is mathematical or philosophical; the former intuitive, the latter discursive.\n*(s One can also know historically, not merely believe; e.g. geography. Yet some of it goes no further than belief.)\n**(s Consequently that is morally certain the denial of which abolishes morality.* Something can be morally certain only for the jury.)\n*(g Moral certainty is subjectively the greatest of all. If (g something according to the prescription of the laws) a judge can be juridically certain that someone is the debtor, he is not on that account yet morally certain of it, that is, he cannot assert it with the most scrupulous conscientiousness. Moral certainty, however, holds only for him who is to do something; he can and must presuppose it with all conscientiousness. But he cannot on that account assert it objectively for others with all conscientiousness. The *argumentum a tuto* is not conscientious.\nMoral certainty is not objective, that is, [it is not that] a thing is certain, but subjective: I am etc etc.)",[]],"refl:2455":["Certainty empirical or rational. The first intuitive or discursive. The second likewise.\n(s The empirical either through our own experience or through the report of others.)",[]],"refl:2456":["Certainty is either demonstrative or *probationis* [of proof].",[]],"refl:2457":["Certainty is either (s practical or) empirical or apodeictic (g with consciousness of necessity); the latter either discursive or demonstrative.",[]],"refl:2458":["Believing rests on a thoroughgoing agreement of what cannot be proved. What, then, is the *criterium* of truth in it?\nSubjectively sufficient holding to be true is not illusion [Schein]; [illusion arises only] when this is taken for truth. I say that I quite certainly believe this, but not that it is quite certain. Both lie in the understanding: the one in concepts [a priori] *in abstracto*, the other *in concreto*.\nTo assume (s possible, problematic), to concede (s to agree negatively, to let pass), to presuppose (s objective).",[]],"refl:2459":["Opinion is a holding to be true that is consciously insufficient; it is a provisional (s or determining judgment) holding to be true. (g *sub conditione suspensiva ad interim*. Interim assent.)\nHolding to be true: the subjective validity of the judgment (s *valor subiectivus coram intellectu*).\nMode [der Urtheile] of holding to be true:\nOpining (s it may be), believing (s is contingent), knowing (s is necessary).\n(s Not so much degrees as kinds of holding to be true.)\nOpining*: a [unvollstandig] subjectively (s a consciously) incomplete holding to be true.**\nBelief is a conviction (g[hinreichend] complete holding to be true) which is subjectively sufficient, but not from objective logical grounds, and which therefore cannot be universally communicated. (g e.g. A pious man believes.)\nCertainty is the [subjective] necessity [in der qvalitaet der Urtheile] of holding to be true, namely, that it is impossible for us to posit the opposite in the judgment.\nEverything true is objectively certain. Complete holding to be true is that which relates to sufficient grounds.\n*(s a holding to be true that is consciously insufficient objectively as well as subjectively (s opinion).)\n(** s Persuasion: when it is subjectively sufficient*; conviction: when it is objective[ly so]. Certainty (g empirical or) not apodeictic; the apodeictic is not always demonstrative. *(s Persuasion is subjective holding to be true (s through illusion), in so far as its ground is not contained in the objective at all.) )",[]],"refl:2460":["43.\nOn the concluding sentence of L §. 157:\nL 43':\nAll certainty resolves itself finally into a sensible one.\nThe [pragmatische] practical certainty* is (g firm) belief.\n(s 1.) Apodeictic certainty (s logically sufficient). (s Belief: practical.) (s 2. empirical.)\n*(g either from grounds without which no *praxis* would take place, or [from grounds] whose removal would conflict with the universal practical laws.)\n(s Moral certainty does not concern knowledge objectively, but right and wrong; |\nL 43: |\nit is negative.)",[]],"refl:2461":["43.\nL 43':\nBelief is a holding to be true from grounds that are inwardly sufficient, though not outwardly sufficient.\nL 43:\n(s I believe [in] another world, although I do not know [it].)\n(s How do I recognise that my holding to be true is subjectively sufficient, that is, can never cease.)",[]],"refl:2462":["43.\nL 43':\nBelieving is an assumption of a [mode of] knowledge by design [aus Vorsatz].\nOne must assume some things even though one does not certainly know them, on account of the usefulness of presuppositions, which are in the end confirmed by their consequences. What one has first believed, one afterwards knows. Worthy of assumption.\nComplete holding to be true according to practical laws (not from practical grounds) is belief. I believe that there is an America (not: I know it according to logical grounds); for if, with such testimonies, I were still unwilling to assume it, what then would I assume [bey] in respect of my actions or resolutions? The grounds here are, indeed, merely theoretical; but [eine anal die Gesetze] when they are regarded on the analogy of practical [grounds], they are sufficient according to practical laws. This assuming is then a complete decidedness, when it must be assumed according to necessitating practical laws. E.g. I believe that there is another world. This belief, if it were grounded merely on reports, would not be legitimate; for then more grounds of credibility would be required. Moral belief is that which is necessary according to moral laws [Wo wenn ich] as a consequence or as a ground of morality. Here |\nthere is a ground which decides for me, in accordance with morals, what I am to assume.\nAccording to practical laws in general it runs thus: if I were unwilling to assume this, then with such a manner of judging I could do nothing prudent. I need not answer for my opinion (except when someone disputes the correctness of my grounds), for I have [merely] assumed it. But what I believe I must answer for.\nL 43:\nOn a mere opinion one may well institute an investigation of a *factum*, but an accusation requires a belief that the other is guilty. In chemistry, propositions that are believed.\n(s *qvod dubitas, ne feceris* [what you doubt, do not do]. Moral certainty.)",[]],"refl:2463":["In believing one assumes something; one is decided.\nIn opining, [one is] *in suspenso*. One is still free. In the former, under constraint.",[]],"refl:2464":["One must first opine before one [entscheidet oder] assumes or asserts. Must one not, in the decision, opine by way of trial?",[]],"refl:2465":["Persuasion: objectively insufficient holding to be true. Certainty: objectively necessary holding to be true, which, when it is at the same time subjective, is conviction. The subjectively sufficient [holding to be true], when it is not objectively so, is entitled [Glaube] persuasion; if it is at the same time objective, conviction. Certainty* *a priori* is apodeictic, either intuitive, [that is,] evidence, or discursive.\n*(g Certainty *a posteriori* is empirical. Sufficiency of holding to be true without necessity (g belief) takes place when its grounds are merely greater than all the grounds of the opposite, although they do not make the opposite impossible. These [grounds] can be subjective without being objectively sufficient, and then it is entitled persuasion; or both at once: [Uberzeugung] belief (without necessity).",[]],"refl:2466":["Holding to be true is objectively sufficient when it holds for everyone.",[]],"refl:2467":["All knowing is logical and merely objective; we can know even from testimonies. Believing, however, is always something aesthetic.",[]],"refl:2468":["In certainty it is altogether impossible to assume the opposite.",[]],"refl:2469":["Of what I believe I am morally certain; but the [Erk] object is not on that account certain (logically). Morally certain is not [logisch] juridical[ly certain].",[]],"refl:2470":["Historical [Vorwarhalten] believing is only opining; for otherwise we can also know historically. That is morally certain which I can assert with all conscientiousness. One can also believe mathematical propositions, but not philosophical ones. Practical belief rests on subjective grounds of willing. It can have practical sufficiency for all men; but the holding to be true is nevertheless logically insufficient, namely when I abstract from all subjective grounds. If I regard man merely according to his understanding and as practically indifferent, then the proof of the existence of God and of the other world is not speculatively adequate. He is, however, interested in it, and in respect of this [interest] he sees himself *in absurdo practico* if he does not assume it. The conviction is not less, but rather more secure in the practical sense, though of another kind.\nNarratives are not the proper objects of belief, for they are a knowing. Nor are truths of reason (g in respect of them one cannot opine). But the presupposition* of the theoretical conditions for moral employment is [such an object].\n*(g what can only be made subjectively sufficient, but not objectively distinct. e.g. the perpetrator of a crime.)",[]],"refl:2471":["Mathematical truths of reason one can indeed believe on testimony, but not know; historical ones, however, [one can] know. Philosophical ones [one can] not even believe. Philosophy admits in itself of no mere persuasion.\nWhat are the objects of opining, believing and knowing?",[]],"refl:2472":["A practically indifferent reason could not always be convinced. A practically legislative reason must necessarily presuppose it, [ob es gle] but in practical relation, although logically it can only be persuaded.",[]],"refl:2473":["Truth [als] is an objective property of knowledge.\nThe judgment through which something is represented as true (g the re|lation\nto my understanding, and so to a particular subject) is subjective: holding to be true.\nHolding to be true is either combined with consciousness of subjective necessity, and is then entitled certainty, or with [consciousness] of contingency and of the possibility of the opposite. The latter is [s] either insufficient both subjectively and objectively, and is opinion; or objectively insufficient but subjectively sufficient: believing.",[]],"refl:2474":["42.\nL 42':\nWhat I opine, (g there) I judge (g my holding to be true) consciously (g only as) problematic. (s The consciousness of the changeableness of my holding to be true.)\n*What I believe: assertoric, but not as necessary (holds for me). (s For belief, argument is enough; it need not be proof.)\nWhat I know: as apodeictic according to laws of the understanding. Even if the truth is merely empirical**, the holding to be true (g relation to the ground of knowledge) is nevertheless apodeictic, that is, universally necessary (holds for all).\n*(g This distinction concerns only the faculty of judgment in respect of the subjective criteria of the subsumption of a judgment under objective rules. I ask only whether with me it is an opining [sey], that is, contingent, not whether it is objectively contingent; just as, when I do not venture to say that it is true (objectively), I express only my holding to be true. As, for example, to swear *de credulitate*.)\nL 42:\n**(g it is impossible that I could be deceived in my judgment of experience; there the holding to be true is apodeictic, although the proposition is objectively contingent.)",[]],"refl:2475":["(s My holding to be true is solely either:)\nOpining (problematic),*\nKnowing (assertoric),**\n*a priori* (apodeictic).***\n*(s [Glauben] Action as if I were immortal (g not acting against it).)\n**(s because I believe that I am immortal (and direct actions to it also. Sacrifices).)\n***(s because we all know that there is another world (not to heed the present personal well-being of this life); the latter would be harmful.)",[]],"refl:2476":["Understanding and reason are free: subjective causes do indeed affect the understanding, but do not determine it. Hence there is here a kind of *culpa* (though no *dolus*) in error (except in the case of an inwardly con|trived\nbelief resting on a voluntary abandonment of all inquiry).\nOutwardly there can even be *dolus* in the affectation of inner conviction.",[]],"refl:2477":["Holding to be true from a ground of knowledge which is neither subjectively nor objectively sufficient is opinion.*\nHolding to be true from a ground of knowledge which is subjectively, but not objectively, sufficient is belief. (s is not communicable or capable of proof.) (s Private judgment.)\nHolding to be true from a ground of knowledge which is both subjectively and objectively sufficient is knowing.\n*(s A holding to be true that is consciously insufficient both (g neither) objectively and subjectively is opining. One that is not objectively, yet subjectively, sufficient: [Meynen] believing. One [sufficient] both subjectively and objectively: knowing.)",[]],"refl:2478":["On the heading »On the certainty of learned knowledge« (35918):\nModality of [Erkenntnis] holding to be true.",[]],"refl:2479":["Holding to be true can be apodeictic without the knowledge being objectively apodeictic. The former is only the consciousness that one cannot possibly have erred in the application of indubitably certain rules, e.g. in experience. It is certain that it is experience.",[]],"refl:2480":["Of matters of opinion*, of belief** and of knowledge. Matters of belief are those in which holding to be true is necessarily free.\n*(s preponderance of insufficient grounds (g with which I am acquainted) over those of the opposite — *verosimile*, opinion.)\n**(s preponderance of subjectively sufficient grounds over those of the opposite.)",[]],"refl:2481":["Objectively there is no distinction between truth and certainty.",[]],"refl:2482":["The creditor does not know, but is practically certain of the payment. Narrative requires belief. Yet there is a knowing [here], that is, believing necessarily.",[]],"refl:2483":["Without taking an interest in something, there is no practical belief.",[]],"refl:2484":["Certainty in belief can only be practical, under the presupposition of unchangeable maxims. I am morally certain, not: it is morally certain.",[]],"refl:2485":["Certainty of the senses is a metaphysical question. Empirical certainty concerns the mere relation of the sensible. Rational certainty [is] either of mere objects of reason or [of objects] of the senses.",[]],"refl:2486":["A holding to be true from merely subjective grounds is persuasion. From objective and subjective grounds, of which the former are not sufficient without the latter, it is believing; from merely objective [aber] insufficient [grounds]: opinion; from objective and sufficient [grounds]: knowing.",[]],"refl:2487":["44'.\nL 44:\n(s Here subjective sufficiency rests (g logically) on this, that one cannot count one's grounds, but only weigh them.)\nAll belief is either theoretical (s in God as hypothesis, [that there are] purposes in nature) or practical. In the former all grounds of conviction are objective (logical); in the latter, in part subjective (practical, or interest). The theoretical is [entweder] historical [auf Zeugniß gegründet oder rational auf]: [belief] either in empirical propositions or in rational ones (the latter takes place only in mathematics). The practical is a rational belief, [sowohl] partly in respect of modes of knowledge, partly [in respect of] the interest of every rational being.\nL 44':\nTheoretical (g empirical) belief (g in respect of what is merely for reason.) (g which, in respect of what lies beyond every theoretical faculty of knowledge, cannot be entitled an opining) is never (conviction) combined with consciousness of its* unchangeableness; for it is sufficiently or preponderantly grounded only so far as my present knowledge extends. Only the belief that rests on practical subjective conditions which are at the same time objective can be combined with consciousness of unchangeableness. I am morally certain.\nIn kinds of knowledge which concern no objects of possible experience one cannot opine and lay claim to probability; one must either know or believe.\nBelieving is the idea of a free holding to be true.\nOpinion is a holding to be true which can become complete.\n*(s That which I assume from moral grounds, and in such a way that I am certain the opposite can never be proved.\nOf opinion I see that the opposite can perhaps be proved.)",[]],"refl:2488":["The [blos] holding to be true from merely objective grounds is either opining or knowing (insufficient or sufficient).\nHolding to be true from merely subjective [abe] [grounds], which [gewiß] are always insufficient: persuasion; from objective and subjective [grounds], of which the former are not sufficient in themselves, but are sufficient together with the latter: believing.\nOf what one knows, one hears no counter-grounds.\nOf what one believes: likewise not.",[]],"refl:2489":["Believing yields a conviction that is not communicable. (on account of the subjective grounds.)\nKnowing must allow of being communicated, and commands assent. Postulates [it].\nIn that [was] in which each person need only be convinced for himself, believing suffices.\nLogically sufficient holding to be true — without interest.\nPractically sufficient holding to be true — [wenn d] with a view to interest, and indeed [the interest] of everyone.\n(s A holding to be true taken subjectively as sufficient, because I know that no one will refute me. e.g. The moon has inhabitants. Theoretical rational belief.)\n(s On being morally certain. It is to be understood merely subjectively. I can be certain of the unchangeableness of my practical belief. Belief in a practical respect (in respect of what I have to do, as means or absolutely) is not on that account always a practical belief.)",[]],"refl:2490":["If we consider our knowledge merely objectively, it is either conviction or persuasion.",[]],"refl:2491":["Practical belief is grounded on a knowing in respect of the laws. I know that he who conducts himself well is worthy of happiness, and I believe that he will become partaker of it. Rational belief. God and another world.",[]],"refl:2492":["A holding to be true that is consciously insufficient objectively as well as subjectively is opinion. One subjectively sufficient but objectively insufficient — — believing. One sufficient on both sides — — knowing. Subjectively sufficient holding to be true is so either absolutely or for the present (sufficient according to what I know). The latter applies to historical belief, the second to moral [belief]. Whence can I know |\nthat it will always suffice? Because it is the condition of an objectively necessary purpose. Why, however, can I not communicate this conviction? Because it presupposes a moral disposition. The reason is this. He who regards morality as in itself certain *a priori* must assume a divine being. He who believes that morality itself presupposes a divine existence even in the judging [of it] cannot be convinced of this by moral belief.",[]],"refl:2493":["The sufficiency of holding to be true (in belief) can be put to the test by wagering or by swearing. For the first [gehört] comparative, for the second absolute Zuläg] sufficiency of objective grounds is required. Where these are lacking, a holding to be true that is absolutely sufficient subjectively nevertheless holds good.\nOn moral certainty of the existence of God as hypothesis.",[]],"refl:2494":["If doubt about the existence of God and of another world were to impair morality, then the existence of both is morally certain.",[]],"refl:2495":["Believing is a holding to be true that is sufficient for practical reason (and therefore subjectively), in which, accordingly, an interest (a subjective ground) which nevertheless holds for everyone is laid at the basis, while |\nthe objective [grounds] (merely theoretical judgments) are nevertheless not sufficient (logically).",[]],"refl:2496":["*Sensus veri et falsi*.\nThe problematic judgment, which I assume.\n—\nassertoric and apodeict. holding to be true\na proposition",[]],"refl:2497":["To persuade oneself (g not valid for everyone) (*mihi persuasum est*); to profess to oneself something as true (*sic sentio*); to be convinced (*convictio*).",[]],"refl:2498":["Since opining has a merely subjective adequacy, even reasonable men may, from the same causes, differ greatly in belief: from habit, inclination, haste in judgment. One cannot communicate one's belief to another.",[]],"refl:2499":["Matters of belief are propositions which (g belong to knowledge of reason, but) do not belong to possible knowing, yet [zu] through interest do belong to\n|\nknowledge. Believing is therefore a merely subjectively grounded holding to be true.\n(g Faithful [gläubig]: *fidelis*.)",[]],"refl:2500":["A scholar may opine in respect of the advantages of trade; a merchant must believe; a judge [must] know.",[]],"refl:2501":["Mode of holding to be true. Problematic or assertoric judgment (holding to be true); provisional assertion is the cause of opinion.",[]],"refl:2502":["The frivolity of opinions. Man is an animal that opines etc etc.",[]],"refl:2503":["Belief — What I wish, I readily believe, if only I had ground for it. (s But not on that account lightly; rather, I seek to persuade myself that I may also hope for it, which is good with a practically commanded intent.) — If, however, it is a duty to wish it (for there is no duty to believe), then I have a right to believe it, if I can. — But if I cannot believe it (e.g. the future life), I have nevertheless ground enough to act as if such [a life] lay before me. — Thus |\nthere is a ground for believing that is sufficient with a practical intent, even though the theoretical [ground] is insufficient for me; and as regards the latter, I may still doubt.",[]],"refl:2504":["Moral certainty: *cautissimi cuiusqve praeceptum*: — *Qvod dubitas, ne feceris*. Pliny. One must be completely certain that something is permitted. In moral matters one may venture nothing on what is uncertain, that is, resolve at the risk of offending against the law.",[]],"refl:2505":["45.\nL 45':\nThis agrees with *certitudine subjectiva*.\ne.g. holding the inhabited planets to be true.\nFurther L 45' from ψ:\nTo assume something (s *posito, sed non concesso*) is not yet the same as to assent.\nFurther L 45 from γ−ξ:\nto concur\nto concede to admit\n)\nto judge provisionally",[]],"refl:2506":["*aeqvilibrium judicii*.\n*suspendere* (s not *differre*) *iudicium** *ad iudicandum*. (s *iudicium praeceps, anceps*.)\n*(s not *renunciare iudicio*)\nto leave something *in suspenso*.\nnot because we doubt, but because we do not know enough.\nTo (g hold back) postpone or give up one's judgment.\nproblematic (g assertoric).\nYouthful or mature reason.\nUndecided or dogmatic cast of mind. εποχη, *suspensio iudicii*.",[]],"refl:2507":["We cannot withhold our assent except in so far as we recognise the possibility of error. (s Constraint to assent through the understanding: mathematics.)\nWe can withhold our assent even in the case of the most evident proofs, if we know that people have before now been deceived in similar ones.\n(s Fear of the opposite from fear of the object. *argumentum ad crumenam* (s wagering), (s *verecundiam*.) *ab utili*. Swearing.)\nGrounds not of conviction but of inquiry, which give one something by which to direct one's investigation to the side where truth is conjectured to lie, are provisional judgments; sagacity.",[]],"refl:2508":["The will has no immediate influence on holding to be true; that would also be very absurd. When it is said that we readily believe what we wish, this refers only to our good-natured wishes, e.g. those of a father regarding his children.\n*suspensio iudicii est vel indagatoria vel sceptica*. There is, further, a withholding of judgment which belongs to the principles of lazy philosophy, merely so as not to need to decide anything, and to seem prudent in one's laziness; or disbelief in rules. Consciousness of the limitation of human insight. Particular direction.",[]],"refl:2509":["He gives assent easily who (s does not think for himself) has a peaceable and sociable mind. The propensity to resistance often comes from |\nthe fear of being the dupe of others. The cleverest can often be deceived more easily than the stupid.",[]],"refl:2510":["To judge problematically through provisional, not determining, judgments.",[]],"refl:2511":["We assume, give assent, (concur.) reject. We withhold assent in the case of mere problems of knowledge (problematic). We suspend the *iudicium* when we are *in aeqvilibrio*. Needle-point. It is difficult to maintain oneself in it. The mind must be unbiased, not preoccupied. In all these respects the will has a power to direct the understanding. This would be good only for inquiry, but it happens also for concurrence. We must assume something by way of trial, and because it merely has grounds in its favour; but we do not yet assert it.",[]],"refl:2512":["*Suspensio Iudicii* is either critical or sceptical (s *renunciatio*): the former for the sake of inquiry, the latter so as to judge nothing. (s The judge's *non liquet*.)\nThe demand to remain firm in a proposition once proved, whatever difficulties may arise, is unreasonable and impossible.",[]],"refl:2513":["Philosophical propositions (pure, synthetic) cannot be so unhesitatingly put forward as settled, on their own credit and in isolation, with proofs. One must look to the consequences: whether they hold good there, and whether they do not betray a lack of more exact determination, or even error. In this they differ from mathematical [propositions]. The philosopher must therefore survey the whole of his science, in order to judge each proposition in relation to all, and [nur] only then to determine its true value.",[]],"refl:2514":["On becoming uncertain from confusion.\nOn being uncertain from mistrust of oneself.",[]],"refl:2515":["When something is held to be true from an insufficient ground without investigation.\nBeside »praecaria cognitio« (Z. 40025):\n*in sensu deteriori*.",[]],"refl:2516":["Prudent prejudice. When one holds something to be true from an insufficient ground until (g the examination has been made or) the opposite has been proved.\n*In sensu saniori* it is the [Beyfall] ground for keeping our assent open, a ground derived not from the inner constitution of the object, but from other connected things. e.g. Prudent people hold this to be true. [Ich urtheile auf keine Weise ins blaue.] He who is acute in many matters will not be absurd in others.\nWe must have prejudices, so that we may be enticed to pursue the proofs carefully. e.g. prejudice regarding the Christian religion. It is the sources of prejudices that make them harmful.",[]],"refl:2517":["Holding to be true from insufficient grounds is not prejudice, but conjecture.\nTo be prepossessed beforehand for or against something, *praeoccupirt* [preoccupied].\nPrejudices resist instruction, in that 1. they do not want to be instructed, 2. they judge the instruction by the very prejudice that was to have been abolished. Hence they are more harmful than ignorance. It is not advisable to ground a prejudice in others even (g of) true propositions. (s and blameworthy to instil them deliberately. Prejudice of experience.) Formal falsity. *Praestigiae sensus interni*.\nNatural and introduced prejudices. (s The former e.g. of age, of parental love, of family, of laziness.)\nProverbs, *canones*, maxims [sententzen], aphorisms. (s *brocardicon*.)",[]],"refl:2518":["On L. §. 169 »to assume through a prejudice« (Z. 27):\ne.g. most men [so assume] religion.\nBut when I do not concede so much to prejudice, but only that it makes something probable to me. e.g. that this or that man is not the author of a book.",[]],"refl:2519":["All inquiry requires a provisional judgment as to the side on which we conjecture the truth to lie. No immediate truths require inquiry, except a subjective one, [namely] whether they spring from the understanding or from habit, that is, reflection. The provisional judgment does not make the inquiry dispensable.\n(s Prejudice of experience.)\nImitation, habit, inclination (s and fear), predilection.\nProverbs*. *Canones*. *sententiae***. Refutation succeeds only through deviation.\nTo reflect means to compare something with the laws of the understanding and of reason. A universal rule for judging something without reflection is entitled a prejudice.\n*(s are indeed valid for the common public, but habit has only private validity.)\n(** g s *Brocardicon*\n(s sayings) mottoes aphorisms didactic maxims\n(s *formula*)\n*proverb*:\n*sententia*\n*Canon*: *loci communes*: where one finds the precept without reflection. Reckoning-board. Multiplication table. )",[]],"refl:2520":["Precipitancy is a lack of suspension of *iudicium*.\n(s The subjective ground [einer Regel] of judging [der vor aller Überlegung vorhergeht], in so far as it becomes an objective rule, is prejudice.)",[]],"refl:2521":["We judge only through the understanding. In a prejudice, therefore, there must precede the judgment, that is, the comparison with the understanding, some|thing\nwhich contains the ground for directing the act of the understanding; just as partial judges know what verdict they are to give even before they have heard the grounds.",[]],"refl:2522":["Maxims of provisional judgments.\nMaxims of reason in general.",[]],"refl:2523":["Provisional judgment is not prejudice; it is rather a caution for avoiding prejudice.\nPrejudice of high esteem and of low esteem.",[]],"refl:2524":["[Das Vorwahrhalten aus (g blos) subiectiven Gründen.]\n(g A universal ground for judging prior to reflection.)\nA subjective [Gesetz] ground* of holding to be true prior to all reflection.\n*(g which is derived from the disposition of the mind to judgment. The disposition is a sensible *habitus*. The *habitus* of sensibility produce custom [Angewohnheit], which is a necessity of acting, just as habit [Gewohnheit] is a facility.)",[]],"refl:2525":["There are good maxims of provisional judgments, which, however, must not become principles of determining judgments (s that one does not understand a reasonable man), e.g. country folk always complain. Women's tales are not reliable. Rules that have many exceptions.\nThe martyrdom of philosophy, which writes in vain for the present age, and usefully only for the future.",[]],"refl:2526":["On passive and active reason.\n(s The *principium* of a passive employment of reason is prejudice.)",[]],"refl:2527":["(s Formula: a rule whose expression facilitates its application and serves it as (g pattern) example.)\nMaxim [Spruch]: a proposition that is bound to certain customary expressions. (s whose expression makes remembering easier for the common understanding. Mechanism in place of the understanding.)\nProverbs (s *proverbia*, vulgar formulas) are current (g received) formulas of prudence. *vehicula* [vehicles]. What is peculiar to them in different nations.\n( s aesthetic, classical formula. Sentences [Sententzen] of wisdom. *Canones*, *brocardica* of science. (s A pronouncement that is law.) *Dicta* (s sacred formulas) of faith. in the wisdom of others. Propensity to a mechanism of reason instead of its spontaneity under laws. They allow themselves to be led. )",[]],"refl:2528":["[Prejudice] is a principle [arising] from subjective causes (s of judgments) which are falsely taken for objective grounds. Causes [are here not Erk] here signify grounds of the origin of a judgment [and], which are distinct from the understanding. Prejudices must be principles.",[]],"refl:2529":["Prejudice is, properly speaking, not a judgment, but that which in sensibility is a preliminary steering towards judgment. No one will be willing to confess his prejudice in the form of a principle. I am not superstitious.",[]],"refl:2530":["A subjective *principium* of judging which is falsely taken for an objective one: prejudice.\nAbove the beginning of the Reflexion there is later added:\nPrejudice, regarded subjectively, is, |\nto which lines 406.19-20 (apart from the final word) are to be drawn as predicate.\nBelow the beginning of l. 406.19 there is later added:\nobjectively it is a principle (proposition).\nLater (θ? ψ−χ??) lines 406.19-20 are so altered that they read:\nA *principium* (propensity) of judging from subjective causes, which are falsely taken for objective [grounds] principles: prejudice.\nTo the right of the superscribed words 'from — causes' (l. 7) there is then finally (in ω) further added:\nto make [them] objective principles.",[]],"refl:2531":["The logic of provisional judgments, as distinguished from determining [judgments]. Illusion [Schein]. The sun moves. Prior to investigation.",[]],"refl:2532":["45'.\nL 46:\nPrejudices are judgments which anticipate the understanding, so that the understanding afterwards comes too late. The understanding is at first passive.\nL 45':\n(g The judgment of a passive reason, in so far as it is used as a principle, is prejudice.\nWhence the name pre-judice [Vor-Urtheil]?)\n(g An [judgment] objective principle of judging from subjective causes — illusion.)\nL 46:\nThe principle of a passive reason. The mechanism of reason in principles is prejudice. Mechanism is that which is possible according to laws through the impression of external causes. Outside reason there is inclination.",[]],"refl:2533":["A principle from subjective causes of sensibility, which are falsely taken for objective grounds of the understanding.\nA principle of passive reason. Without objective reflection.",[]],"refl:2534":["Prejudice is the *propension* (propensity) of the understanding to judge from subjective causes. These bring it about that one judges without reflection.",[]],"refl:2535":["A subjective *praesuppositum* [presupposition] which is falsely taken for a *principium*.",[]],"refl:2536":["A judgment which precedes reflection. Reflection is comparison with rules of the understanding.",[]],"refl:2537":["The propensity (g to a certain manner of judging) to judge prior to reflection; not merely *iudicium praeceps* [precipitate judgment].",[]],"refl:2538":["Provisional judgments taken as principles are prejudices. Most provisional judgments arise from subjective causes. When they are held valid as principles, this always arises from subjective causes. This too is a propensity of a passive reason arising from inner *illusion*, from which one can be freed by comparison with the judgments of others.",[]],"refl:2539":["Prejudice is the confusion of a universally operative subjective cause of judgment with the objective ground.",[]],"refl:2540":["Prejudice (g objectively) is a principle of correct provisional judgments which are falsely regarded as determining judgments. For example, the apple does not fall far from the tree.\nsubjectively: inclination, habit.",[]],"refl:2541":["Illusion which is held valid in place of a principle.\nSubjectively: the propensity to (g one's own) persuasion.",[]],"refl:2542":["Prejudice is illusion in so far as it is made into a principle.",[]],"refl:2543":["Prejudices obstruct the independent use of our talents and of our reason.",[]],"refl:2544":["To accept something as true through a prejudice is always wrong.",[]],"refl:2545":["What is subjective in prejudice is the propensity to pass universal judgments on particular grounds.",[]],"refl:2546":["Obstinacy of prejudices.",[]],"refl:2547":["Prejudice is the propensity to regard the subjective necessity of a judgment as a [ground] proof of its objective necessity.",[]],"refl:2548":["*Praejudicium* is a principle of judging which precedes reflection. Principle of a passive reason.",[]],"refl:2549":["*propensio causam iudicii in subiecto pro ratione iudicandi in obiecto habendi* [the propensity to take the cause of a judgment in the subject for the ground of judging in the object].",[]],"refl:2550":["Prejudice. The subjective ground (g A maxim) of (g universal) judgments which [as principle] is (g falsely) taken for an objective principle (of reason). It is therefore a maxim of passive reason. — Illusion is to be regarded either as an *illusion* of the understanding, that is, a ground of possible errors, or as an *illusion* of the faculty of judgment, or of reason. In the first case it is the illusion of the senses in the single judgment; in the second, that of the reflective faculty of judgment (g and prejudice), which ascribes to the object what is required merely by the act of the imagina|tion\nfor a [universal] common judgment; in the third, an illusion of wit and of the faculty of discrimination in universal judgments, which regards what belongs to some as a universal objective condition.\nPrejudice is a maxim [z] of judging objectively from subjective grounds. (g e.g. from examples of others' judgment, or from habit.) All mottoes [Denksprüche] (g as principles) are [Voru For] prejudices *in terminis* [in so many words]. (g Imitation,) inclination, habit.",[]],"refl:2551":["There are metaphysical, physical, moral [prejudices].\nFurther L 46:\nCommon prejudice about unlucky days. dreams.\nFurther L 46 γ−ρ:\ndistinguished from the aesthetic.",[]],"refl:2552":["*Si omnes patres sic, at ego non sic* [if all the fathers [think] thus, yet I do not]. *Nullius in verba* [on the word of no one].\nPrejudice, moral, in matters of experience. It should move us to examine, not to give assent.\nWith this, writings and also opinions are received. It should move us not to hold something at once to be absurd.\nFurther L 46' from ψ−χ:\nThe authority of testimonies above one's own reason | of the empirical above the rational.\nFurther L 46 from θ (? ψ−χ?):\nFrom imitation. (g as contemporaries) In order to despise others.",[]],"refl:2553":["Mark of self-love: when one holds something to be false because one cannot understand it.\nFurther L 46 from θ (? ψ−χ?):\nThe [prejudice] of self-love.",[]],"refl:2554":["Dreams have been believed in from ancient times.\nYet they are made use of to confirm new things. *Praejudicium* [*consensus*]. (g *testimonium*. *Citationes* [citations].) All peoples believe that there is a God, that the soul is immortal.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ), under the end of L § 170a no. 3, above 'praeiudicium — wenn' (ll. 28f.):\nThe prejudice of habit.",[]],"refl:2555":["The most recent philosophers maintain that the soul has a body after death.\nThe moderns do not believe in the influence of the moon.\nFurther L 47 at the end of L § 170a no. 4:\nor [one holds it] to be false because it is old.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ) on L § 170 'novelty' (l. 28):\nfrom the advantage which posterity has over earlier generations in its resources. Innovations are partly disagreeable, partly agreeable.\nProvisional judgment.",[]],"refl:2556":["The agreements of things with one another support each other's probability.\nFurther L 47 from γ−ξ:\n[The prejudice] of the (g lost) effort of having learnt it. Of unity of explanation. To prefer subjective grounds.\nFurther L 47 (from σ−φ? ω??) on 'system of doctrine' (l. 27):\nof the great man.\nFurther L 47' (from γ−η) opposite the end of L § 170a no. 5:\nThe prejudice in favour of learning.\nThe prejudice in favour of the common understanding.\nFurther L 47, left margin beside the end of L § 170a no. 5 and the beginning of no. 6 (from σ−φ):\nto accept something through a prejudice.",[]],"refl:2557":["is opposed: when one does not hold a truth to be true on account of the ease with which it is understood.\nFurther L 47':\nVerse.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ) above 'part of the lazy' (l. 29):\n[The prejudice] of ease.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ) above 'trust' etc. (ll. 29-31):\nOf sound understanding: when one has learnt nothing. Likewise of learning, and in general of what one has learnt with effort.",[]],"refl:2558":["With reading just as with meditating.\nFurther L 47' from γ−κ:\nThe prejudice of unity.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ) above 'of the' etc. (l. 17):\nof common knowledge *in concreto*.",[]],"refl:2559":["e.g. All the opinions of the ancients are useless. *In historicis* [in historical matters].",[]],"refl:2560":["Our dear forefathers did not judge thus.",[]],"refl:2561":["The French in respect of the Germans. In respect of individual persons.",[]],"refl:2562":["When, in opposition to great men, one imagines that one will not, after all, understand the matter better.\nFurther L 47' from µ−φ (? ω?):\nFavourable judgment in favour of the taste of the ancients.\nFurther L 47 from ψ−θ:\nNot everything accepted through prejudice is to be rejected, but the prejudice αυτοσ εφα [he himself said it] is.\nFurther L 47' from θ:\nJurists: *Citata*. *Praeiudicium est vitium cognitionis formale* [prejudice is a formal defect of knowledge] (not merely *defectus* [a lack]).",[]],"refl:2563":["Of the prejudice of taste, indeed even of feeling.\nOn L § 170a no. 1:\n[The authority] of the multitude in general or of individual men.\nThe former: either from logical grounds or from sensation.\nThe latter: because one is [wohl zum] indolent, or likes to belittle others (s and to exalt one alone. Envy has brought in *despectum* [contempt]).\nOn L § 170a no. 2:\nEgoism consists in this, that in respect of rational knowledge one finds the judgment of others unnecessary, and does not take their contradiction as a sign of a hidden untruth.\nThe egoism of indifference or of self-conceit. The former merely isolates itself.",[]],"refl:2564":["Since the universal validity of our judgments for every reason indicates objective truth, there follows (s that the judgment of others is an external criterion of truth) the [thei] necessity of a participating reason, which is opposed to egoism; likewise the right to make one's judgments known, and love of honour as the incentive of the sciences. A participating understanding and a participating will are always good; a correct and sound understanding is always bound up with uprightness, at least conversely.\nThe provisional judgment in favour of others consists in this, that when others (g evidently) appear to have erred, one prefers to believe that one does not understand them.\n(s The prejudice of the person: one's own or another's*. (s Only in historical matters does authority hold.) Truth must hold anonymously. One must not ask whether it was begotten of noble parents.)\n*(s egoism: the prejudice of indifference to the judgment of others as a *criterium* of the truth of our own.)\negoism was later (ω) altered to egoismi, and above it was added: obsequii vel, to which 'praeiudicium' from L § 170 is to be supplied. Finally, in ω, the words were further added:\n(s First, to think for oneself. To think in the place of every other. Hence to test one's judgment against others.)",[]],"refl:2565":["Since we find it necessary to instruct others of our judgments, we must be not merely communicative but participating; and the impulse to communication which guides our understanding is in us only in order that through others we should verify our own judgments.\nWhat has been able to survive antiquity must be good, just as with the Russians who are able to survive their hard upbringing. (s But what was faulty has also disappeared.)\n(s The ancients invented everything beforehand.)\n(s The harm that great men have done through their greatness. It is not good to make an idol for oneself and to think of a man as great.)\n(s Envy of others makes a man excessively great. The spirit of imitation lulls to sleep. Reform becomes difficult.)",[]],"refl:2566":["The communicative inclination of reason is fair only on condition that it be at the same time bound up with the participating inclination. Others are not apprentices, nor judges, but colleagues in the great council |\nof human reason, and have a *votum consultativum* [advisory vote]; and *vnanimitas votorum est pupilla libertatis* [unanimity of votes is the apple of the eye of liberty]. *liberum veto*.",[]],"refl:2567":["The authority of the multitude (s sound understanding, *sensus communis*). The authority of the person. (s or of the multitude. In historical matters — in judgments of reason — in taste.) It frequently arises from envy towards the rest. It is founded by extravagant praise. The prejudice of the mode of knowledge [Erkentnisart]: feeling, inspiration. The authority of learning. (s The unlearned. This the learned have in favour of the common understanding.) Of sound reason. Of antiquity. Of distance. Common prejudice and learned [prejudice].\n(g The multitude is either that of the common crowd or that of the learned (s citations).)",[]],"refl:2568":["People who grow old in a doctrine are much in favour of the old. The flighty, the lively, the fashionable, in favour of the new.",[]],"refl:2569":["47.\nL 47':\n(s A. 1,) The prejudice of* antiquity seems to come from this, |\nthat one believes the world is growing old. (s The prejudice of tradition [Herkommen] (s *absqve autore* [without an author]).)\n*(s [The prejudice] of authority arises, as does the supreme power of a single man, from envy towards the rest, so that they may be equal.)\n(s Propensity to prejudice in religion, because we do not ourselves have to answer for what has been handed down to us.)\n(s The subjective ground of esteem or rejection.)\nThe old also pleases us through an *illusion* of fancy. (s as does the distant, the rare. The knowledge of what is old or distant is itself learning.)\nThe prejudice [wiede] of novelty is either this: that one esteems the new highly because it contradicts the old; or: because it adds something. (s Gratitude.)\n3. (s On no. 1.) There is a [prejudice] well-founded provisional judgment in matters of taste in favour of antiquity, which arises from this, that time, as it were, sifts writings, and only the good ones have remained; just as one judges from some old and strong [men] that all were so.\n(s Aesthetic superiority.)\n(s Because knowledge of them and of their language is learning, and they, in a dead language, are the sole abiding models.)\nFurther, the judgment upon correctness of language: [über] whether the expression is flat and vulgar or too far-fetched and forced, the characteristic quality of the expressions, even the beauty of the phrases, are decided in their favour. In this respect the ancients do not stand under the law, but themselves give the law.\nThe need, amid the changing form of taste in the course of time, to have an enduring archetype, which must necessarily be composed in an unchanging language, demands at the same time that this be a dead language, regarded as the medium of learning. This occasions the inconvenience of having to study languages, instead of learning them through intercourse. Will one of our living languages ever in future become a dead and learned language? Which will it be, and will it supplant the ancient ones? The present age is the most inconvenient, and will become still more so as more nations cultivate themselves.\nIf one looks merely to works of the understanding and not of taste, a dead and learned language is not necessary.\n––––––––––\nFor a language to be preserved as a dead and learned one, what is required is [entweder] [daß] not that works of the understanding and of reason should shine forth in it, since this (g their) content can be preserved in any other language, but works of fine wit, especially poetry, whose beauty is peculiarly bound to the language. But since this is not yet a sufficiently weighty (g and general) incentive to learn a dead language, there is no other motive than religion. Its documents and the first [Vertheidig] state of the Church make necessary not only its own language but also kindred languages.\n(s 2.) The question is whether the prejudice of antiquity does not come rather from the [self-loving prejudices of novelty] difficulty which they [the ancients] had. One (s is first astonished, afterwards — —) greatly admires what surpasses expectation according to a general rule: thus we admire the reason of a woman or the science of a middling burgher, and it seems that the merit of people of this kind is greater (s because they have fewer resources than we), and consequently |\nthat it is also in keeping with magnanimity to extol it. Moreover, there is something ugly in speaking ill of others after their death*, above all of one's teachers.\nL 47:\n*(s one can judge them and not condemn them.)\nL 47':\n(s 6. We should not have thought them capable of it.)\n(s 6. Astonishment is taken for admiration.)\n(s We admire the ancients because we have a low concept of antiquity, according to the circumstances of their time.)\n( s (s On 2.) We admire a woman, but to the disadvantage of her sex, because we hold the circumstances in which she is placed to be disadvantageous and obstructive; and the ancients, because we regard the circumstances in which they lived as little favourable to their abilities. From this admiration of difficulty overcome there arises, through an *illusion*, the immediate high esteem of their propositions. It also seems fair to judge the ancients with respect, because we are their pupils, and coarse censure of them is uncivil. )\n(s On 3)) When one knows the ancients, one despises the moderns, just as he who has travelled far praises distant lands at home and his fatherland abroad.\n(s The learning of knowing the ancients is admired.)\n( s (s 3.) The knowledge of what lies in distant time or space is itself learning, and thereby the old and the foreign are admired. )( s (s 4)) We shall ourselves one day become ancients, and we promise ourselves the honour that we show to our forefathers. )\n(s 4. Gratitude. 5. Envy towards contemporaries (s *pascitur in vivis livor* [envy feeds upon the living]).)\n(s 6. From laziness: old people like to leave things as they were; *querulus* [querulous].)\n(s 7. Gratitude.)",[]],"refl:2570":["Mistrust of the whole of human reason.\n[Of the] heart.\nThe former befalls the most rational, the latter the most upright.\n(s When one expects more of reason and of the heart than either can perform, there arises hatred of reason and hatred of men: *misology* and *misanthropy*. This befalls only the experienced.)\n(s Misanthropy. Misology.)",[]],"refl:2571":["The prejudices of assent have their ground either in esteem for the person (s authority, antiquity, multitude) or in inclination (g imitation) (fellow-countrymanship, fellow-pupils). (s *Praeiudicium rationalismi, empirismi* [prejudice of rationalism, of empiricism]. Superstition. Trusts in *facta*.)\nOr [in] want of regard for the importance of the judgments of others. One isolates oneself: egoism, through indifference or self-conceit.\nOr from our relations to knowledge. Habit. Novelty. Systematic unity.",[]],"refl:2572":["Prejudices are either of pride or of humility (s timidity); the former concern oneself and one's party, the latter |\nothers. To the latter belongs the humble regard of the common man for the learned, especially in matters of religion.",[]],"refl:2573":["Against sciences which have so often erred: *scepticismus diffidentiae* [scepticism of distrust]. Weakness is mistrustful. Fear of uttering something foolish, which will be laughed at, is also the cause of an assumed mistrust.",[]],"refl:2574":["The judgment of the multitude is a *criterium* of truth. The servile [prejudice].",[]],"refl:2575":["The prejudice of trust rests on inexperience and boldness; that of mistrust on experience and diffidence: hesitant.",[]],"refl:2576":["*Praejudicium est vel hominis vel (s facultatis cognoscitivae) temporis et loci. Prius vel Personae vel multitudinis. Hoc vel eruditorum vel intellectus communis.* [Prejudice is either of man or of (s the cognitive faculty) of time and place. The former either of the person or of the multitude. The latter either of the learned or of the common understanding.]",[]],"refl:2577":["*Praeiudicium hominis est vel personae vel multitudinis* [The prejudice of man is either of the person or of the multitude] (s *vel temporis vel loci* [or of time or of place], of antiquity, of the new). (s *egoismus*, *pluralismus*. plu|ralism:\nwhen one believes that with many voices one can dispense with the inner criteria of truth; what all the world says is true.)",[]],"refl:2578":["Whether it may also be permissible to recommend prejudices, or at least to leave them untouched. That is to say: to make everyone immature [unmündig].",[]],"refl:2579":["The fear that, if old prejudices were to fall away, the long-standing edifice would then collapse, is groundless. A grown man has sound feet even when his go-cart is taken from him. (s Rich peasants are easier to govern than poor.)",[]],"refl:2580":["The prejudice of leaving men in immaturity [Unmündigkeit] under the guardianship of the ancients.\nThe path of erudition [Belesenheit] instead of that of reflection.",[]],"refl:2581":["Prejudice against an age, against a class [Stand].",[]],"refl:2582":["*Praeiudicium vel egoismi vel pluralismi. Hoc vel personae* (of another besides myself) *vel multitudinis. Posterius vel eruditorum vel rationis communis.* [Prejudice either of egoism or of pluralism. The latter either of the person (of another besides myself) or of the multitude. The last either of the learned or of common reason.]",[]],"refl:2583":["Probability is a knowledge of truth through insufficient grounds, which are nevertheless more sufficient than those which the opposite has. e.g. That the moon is inhabited.\nIf someone who is familiar in a house was present at the time when something disappeared, there is a slight ground breaks off.\nFurther L 47 from µ−φ (? ω??) above L § 171 'reject — our' (ll. 27f.):\nDistinction: persuasion — conviction.",[]],"refl:2584":["The knowledge of falsity through etc etc.\nFurther L 47:\nthat a rich man should steal.",[]],"refl:2585":["All merely probable knowledge is uncertain, but not on that account doubtful.\nDoubt is the equilibrium of judgment. For example, whether there are [über] still more planets than we know. Whether a comet will ever touch the earth.",[]],"refl:2586":["The immortality of the soul is to Christians certainly true, to the [freethinkers] Epicureans certainly false, to philosophers probable, to freethinkers doubtful.\nOpposite L § 172 'improbable — can' (ll. 19-21):\nThat there are ghosts is accepted through a prejudice.\nIt is probable that there is a [earth moves, not the sun] subterranean fire. It is also improbable.\nThe Christian religion is improbable in its mysteries, but probable in its historical credibility.",[]],"refl:2587":["A very great degree of probability is certainty.\nOn the end of L § 173:\nIt is probable that air (g which is) full of very coarse vapours is heavier than pure air; but it is false.",[]],"refl:2588":["One should not pass judgment without need [postpone] and should accordingly postpone it, unless the rules of prudence require [otherwise].\nOn the beginning of the second sentence of L § 174:\nin the degree that it deserves.\nOn the end of L § 174:\nThis is not an obligation, but a consequence.\nCapacity for trust and for doubt.",[]],"refl:2589":["A.\nHistorical probability (g Mostly decreases with time):\na.\nIt is presumed that untruths betray themselves.\nb.\nOne holds a narrative to be groundless when the way in which the narrator could have come to know it is hard to see. But one does not on that account hold it to be groundless when one knows no causes.\nc.\nOne infers to actions from moral marks.\nd.\nInner possibility, likewise connection, confirm it.\nHence from the constitution of the thing and the constitution of the witnesses (g plurality, no one of whom has it from the others). [One looks] to the attendant, preceding and following circumstances. Whether the witness was able to tell the truth; willing; and confirmation.\nRare things have an improbability. e.g. Great armies.\n    ( g Historical: 1. Constitution of the thing. Inner possibility. Example: rare things not. 2. Constitution of the witnesses: whether he was able, willing [to tell] the truth etc etc. Plurality of witnesses. 3. From the circumstances (g attendant circumstances): at what time he said this. Preceding, following; in confirmation, not against assent. )\nB. physical (g Does not decrease with time):\nWhen [two things] the opposite of a thing is possible in many ways.\nWhen two things are constantly found together.\nWhen a thing requires a combination of many circumstances, it is not — —.\n( g When two things are always found together. When the opposite of a thing is possible in many kinds of ways. When a thing requires a combination of very many contingent circumstances. )",[]],"refl:2590":["e.g. a man who is under suspicion is found at the scene of a murder.\nFurther L 49':\nThe probability of actions from their motives.\nFurther L 49' from ω (? φ?):\nShould properly read: in this judgment I am [certain] conscious of my conscientiousness.",[]],"refl:2591":["In probability one must compare the grounds that one knows for the thing not with the grounds of the opposite, but with the sufficient ground of certainty. The former constitutes only plausibility [Scheinbarkeit], *verisimilitudinem*. In philosophical matters probability is not to be measured, but felt.",[]],"refl:2592":["When an inclination represents something as of interest, one [fält] no longer acts according to probability alone, but multiplies [it] by the importance. Smallpox [inoculation] of children. Thunderstorms.",[]],"refl:2593":["Objective probability (g *probabilitaet*.) is merely the possibility of the object, and the ground of it is certain.\nSemblance of truth from subjective grounds.\n( s practically, personally acceptable something can be without its declaring itself as to probability. *Procax* [forward]. )",[]],"refl:2594":["In respect of the supersensible, if the knowledge is not certain, then we can in it not at all breaks off.",[]],"refl:2595":["*verisimilitudo* gives the ground of a provisional judgment;\n*probabilitas* (s the insufficient ground) of a (g [practically]) determining judgment, for determining the understanding;\n*certitudo* (s the sufficient [ground]) of a (s [logically] determining) necessary judgment.\n(s When it is a question not of the probability of things but of judgments, the only concern is that these should approach truth in conformity with the rule.)",[]],"refl:2596":["The necessity of holding-to-be-true is, when subjective, conviction; when objective, certainty.\nThe possibility of holding-to-be-true, subjectively: plausibility; objectively: probability.\nProbability of things, or of knowledge alone; mathematics considers the former through wholly certain grounds.",[]],"refl:2597":["No (g universal) rules of probability in general can be given, except that the error of different people will not fall on one and the same side, but [that] there must be a ground of agreement in the object. (g For error comes from subjective causes that are taken for objective grounds. But the former cannot be the same where subjects and circumstances differ.) Likewise: that, when |\nerror is committed from two opposed sides in equal quantity and degree, the truth lies in the mean.",[]],"refl:2598":["When the *momenta* of probability are homogeneous, they are counted (in mathematics); when they are heterogeneous, as in philosophy, they are weighed, that is, estimated by their effects, and these by the overcoming of obstacles in the mind; the latter, however, yield no relation to certainty, but only of one plausibility to another.\n(s The mathematician can determine the relation to the sufficient ground; the philosopher has only *verisimilitudo*: subjectively, practically sufficient.)",[]],"refl:2599":["The object is probable; but our knowledge, in so far as we know about the object, is true.",[]],"refl:2600":["Probability is truth from insufficient grounds, which nevertheless have a greater relation to the sufficient ground than those of the opposite. Practically sufficient is [that] where the motive is greater.",[]],"refl:2601":["*Verisimilitudo*: the relation of the grounds for the assertion to |\nthose of the opposite, without having the standard of certainty. For example, that the planets have inhabitants stands to certainty as this end stands to the sum of all possible ends.",[]],"refl:2602":["Probability is the relation of the insufficient ground of holding-to-be-true to certainty (more than half). Probable representation of things prior to investigation; probable knowledge is not to be accepted, but examined; yet knowledge of probability can be entirely well-founded.",[]],"refl:2603":["The subjective ground of holding-to-be-true which is greater than that of the opposite is plausibility (*verisimilitudo*); the objective etc etc. is *probabilitas*. This can be known only when one knows the relation of the grounds to the sufficient [ground].\nThe ground of holding-to-be-true is either objectively greater [als d] or subjectively greater than that of the opposite.\nThis can be found out only when one compares the grounds of holding-to-be-true with the sufficient ground; for then |\nthe grounds of holding-to-be-true are greater than the grounds of the opposite can be.",[]],"refl:2604":["*verisimilitudo* is magnitude of persuasion, *probabilitas* approximation to conviction.",[]],"refl:2605":["If there are still 10 tickets in the lottery urn, and among them 2 prizes: how much can I stake against the urn? [Not] 2 against 8 (grounds for against grounds against); [but] my probability is 2/10, the urn's 8/10, hence 1/5:4/5=1:4.\n(s With two dice 36 cases are possible; among these 5 by which 8 is thrown; hence *probabilitas* =5/36.)",[]],"refl:2606":["Everything future is true (or the actual), but our judgment about it is not always probable.",[]],"refl:2607":["In *a priori* knowledge only mathematics can judge probably, not metaphysics; the latter must know, because *a priori* knowledge is necessary; to opine in it is only a relation of subjective grounds on both sides, and can suffice for belief.",[]],"refl:2608":["The probable judgment is uncertain; the judgment about probability must be certain.",[]],"refl:2609":["It is not probable that someone will hit three times in succession (when the dice have a point on half [their faces] and nothing on the others). [aber es nicht un] From this one might conclude that it is also not probable that, when he has already hit twice, he will hit the third time too. But it is not probable to get this series of hits [zu haben] as a whole, though it is probable for each part taken separately. He who has had much good fortune has not on that account greater cause to fear misfortune, e.g. shipwreck or thunderstorm, or of dying in old age. For in this case it is as if I drew a ticket every year and the blanks remained, these always diminishing in proportion to the previous prizes. Yet one can say: he who comes often must at last come to grief.",[]],"refl:2610":["In the whole of the infinite series of causes every action is determined, although, when it is to take place, it is free. For it is still not determined by the grounds which man can know.",[]],"refl:2611":["That what is contingent in each single case should nevertheless be necessary in the whole: thus what a man does is in each single case naturally contingent; but in the whole he nevertheless does what God wills. Thus providence is intent on the preservation of the species and has |\nabandoned every individual. If men lived long, every rogue would come to the gallows.",[]],"refl:2612":["The past free throw has no influence on the following one; but every throw has *a priori* an influence on the whole, in the idea of the possibility of all throws together.",[]],"refl:2613":["48.\nL 48':\nWhen the throws have already taken place, one does not ask about the probability of the |\nL 48: |\npast ones; for these are actual; it must therefore not be brought into the reckoning, though it must be when this improbable case is not yet actual. One represents the matter as if [die Ausgezo] all possible cases (tickets and blanks) were in one urn; the more of the former I have drawn out, the fewer would remain* and the greater the ratio of blanks to prizes. [So wie] Whether we do not have a certain quantum of |\nL 48': |\nsleep, of digestive power, so that the less we allot to ourselves now, the longer we can live on it, is the question.\nL 48':\n*(g but I must put back those drawn.)\n(g The Turks' means to long life.)",[]],"refl:2614":["Whether a fortunate general can be called prudent?",[]],"refl:2615":["To draw correct rules from incorrect observations, provided only these are many.\nThe degree of possibility which the mathematician states must be certain, that is, must necessarily suffice in an infinite series of cases.",[]],"refl:2616":["Distinction of probable knowledge from knowledge of probability. Widows' funds. Whether a comet will strike the earth. Thunderstorms. Insurance.",[]],"refl:2617":["To draw what is true from a multitude of observations. How the inclination of the two sexes to a vice compares. Confessional. How many blanks there are in a lottery urn.",[]],"refl:2618":["Between the first two lines of L § 174:\nArt of deciphering.",[]],"refl:2619":["From the number of actual cases (white and black balls) to guess the number of each that is present.",[]],"refl:2620":["Properly one ought to say: the knowledge is plausible [scheinbar], the object |\nprobable. For the knowledge of the probability of the object must be true.",[]],"refl:2621":["When the grounds of holding-to-be-true are not objective at all, no step whatever is taken thereby nearer to the object; therefore one cannot posit probability there either, e.g. whether the soul thinks after death; rather, knowledge which rests on belief accepts what is not probable at all.",[]],"refl:2622":["'A thing is probable' is said only of events. Otherwise: 'our judgment is probable'.",[]],"refl:2623":["In respect of the supersensible there is no probability; we are not on the path of knowing at all.\nWhether many insufficient grounds of different kinds (many proofs) amount to a proof?",[]],"refl:2625":["Practical certainty is distinct from pragmatic and moral certainty.",[]],"refl:2626":["That is morally certain whose removal would remove all morality; the following two theoretical propositions are morally certain:\nThere is a God;\nThere is another world.",[]],"refl:2627":["What is certain on the presupposition of good moral dispositions is morally certain; hence it is only subjective and not sufficient for every intelligent being. I ought therefore to say: I am morally certain.",[]],"refl:2628":["Conscientiousness is the cast of mind [Denkungsart] that always [only according to] speaks and acts with moral certainty [judge and].",[]],"refl:2629":["Above and \nWhat is certain according to laws of the ends of the will is morally certain. Logical principle — and practical purpose.\nMoral certainty is only subjective, namely: to declare something certain conscientiously.",[]],"refl:2630":["Moral certainty (s is the probability which suffices for obligation.) is the necessity of presupposing a proposition that is logically uncertain, as the condition of an apodictically certain practical proposition (and indeed [the condition] under which alone this latter, as a law, can become a maxim). Pragmatically I can be certain [daß] through miracles that there is a God who commands us to do this or that which is in itself permitted (I am thus certain in respect of the contingent maxims of prudence). But I am morally certain that there is a God, because this is the presupposition under which alone apodictically certain practical propositions, e.g. thou shalt not deceive, can become maxims.",[]],"refl:2631":["One can only say: I am morally certain; for moral certainty expresses merely the conscientiousness of the judgment. For example, there is no such [certainty] in a court of heresy.",[]],"refl:2632":["What is certain according to the principle (of Pliny): *quod dubitas, ne feceris* [what you doubt, do not do], is morally certain (probabilism of the Jesuits).",[]],"refl:2633":["A doubt of the existence of God [drawn] from evils.\nFurther, L 49':\nNot all doubts must make a man doubtful or uncertain.",[]],"refl:2634":["Difficulties: what one cannot comprehend.",[]],"refl:2635":["Scruple concerning eternity, the Trinity.\nFurther, L 49':\nScruples remain when one cannot comprehend how a thing is possible, even granting that one also does not see that it is impossible.\nFurther, L 49, from µ−υ:\nsubjective doubt.\nFurther, L 49, from ψ−θ:\nTo resolve the doubt.\nFurther, L 49, from ω:\nThe [fürwahr] opposite of being certain is subjective doubt, e.g. fear; the opposite [der obiecti] [arising] from grounds of knowledge is objective [doubt]. This is either objection or scruple.",[]],"refl:2636":["A difficulty is not an objection.\nsubjective.\nobjective.",[]],"refl:2637":["Doubt is either objective or subjective. The first consists in the grounds for untruth, the second in the uncertainty itself.\n(s Mutual objections are not proofs.)",[]],"refl:2638":["Doubt is merely a (g subjective) hindrance to holding-true.\nObjection is a (g objective ground) ground for holding false a mode of knowledge that has been held true.\nThrough the former our knowledge [blos] is merely rendered ungrounded. He who doubts asserts nothing.\n(g Doubts arise about the grounds; objections bear upon the proposition.)",[]],"refl:2639":["It is modesty to present one's objections merely as doubts.",[]],"refl:2640":["Doubt is sometimes taken subjectively, as the state of an irresolute mind, but also objectively, as the knowledge [ein] of the insufficiency of the grounds for holding-true. Objection is the knowledge of the grounds for the opposite, though with the consciousness of their absolute insufficiency, yet also of their relative sufficiency to counterbalance the grounds for the truth.",[]],"refl:2641":["When someone has a project for something, e.g. to make a *perpetuum mobile*, and I bring forward the doubt [arising] from friction.",[]],"refl:2642":["The ground taken from the goodness of God against the infinity of the punishments of hell is a doubt which has great semblance.\nFurther, L 49, from ξ−φ:\nThe ground of a provisional judgment; objective semblance for the understanding, subjective for inclination.",[]],"refl:2643":["In two ways: 1. [daß der Zweifel die Sache nicht umstoße] by showing that it [zuglei] infers falsely. 2. By explaining what is specious in the doubt and showing that it does not conflict with the truth.\nFurther, L 49':\nfirst, by discovering what is true, 2. what is false in the doubt.",[]],"refl:2644":["One can raise doubts against the Trinity; but they are all answerable. Certain doubts, however, are unanswerable without a purified logic.",[]],"refl:2645":["*Ad resolutionem dubiorum pertinet* [to the resolution of doubts belongs]:\n*Nodum resecare, non solvere* [to cut the knot, not to untie it]. When one shows that it is not necessary to answer it. e.g. Against the objections drawn from evils against the goodness of God. One cannot engage in all difficulties. *Difficultates non auferre, sed differre vitium* [not to remove difficulties but to postpone them is a fault]. When one represents bodies as composed of little bodies. When one resolves a difficulty by means of another in which the same [difficulty] occurs. e.g. When one explains the origin of men from other men. (s Whence came chaos?)\n*Ad resolutionem difficultatis pertinet* [to the resolution of a difficulty belongs]. *Status quaestionis* [the state of the question];\n*quid pertineat, quid non* [what is relevant, what not].\n(s *Respondere obiectionibus, non refutare* [to answer objections, not to refute them].)\n*Quaestionem obnubilare, non diluere* [to cloud the question, not to clear it up]. Cuttlefish [Blakfisch].",[]],"refl:2646":["To cut off a doubt is: when one [zei] opposes to it the truth of a clearer proposition.\nTo resolve a doubt is: when one shows its falsity.\nIn the case of an objection the semblance must be explained.",[]],"refl:2647":["To cut the knot: to prove that a mode of knowledge is nevertheless true, or that its opposite has harmful consequences, object what one will.\nTo dispose of objections [abfertigen] is: to show that one does not oneself understand anything of what one objects, e.g. the evil in the world.",[]],"refl:2648":["dispose of.\nAbove «schlechterdings» etc. (447 24f.):\nOne can have an objective doubt which cannot be removed, and yet subjectively hold [something] true without doubt. morality.",[]],"refl:2649":["If the semblance [Schein] has not been resolved and explained (g its title examined and deduced), the error has not been cured at its root. For the semblance can still deceive in other cases, and one does not know whether the refutation itself does not rest upon semblance. The subjective causes must be developed which are easily taken for objective, notwithstanding a reason otherwise clear-sighted.\nRefutation must always be undertaken with satisfaction to the rights of common human reason. Not *argumentum ab odio* [argument from odium], lest minds be divided in respect of the truth.",[]],"refl:2650":["The sceptical method is that of making the matter ready for a verdict by summoning all the grounds for and against.",[]],"refl:2651":["Not against mathematical [truths], but scruples.\nOn L §. 177 «und — werden» (ll. 25f.):\n2. but when the doubts find acceptance with many great men and the answer is seen to be without effect, this should at least make one cautious.",[]],"refl:2652":["1. That truth which only shows that something is, without showing how it is possible, is more exposed to doubts than others.\n*item*. That which has no known example.\n2. To freethinkers the doubts of a Bayle, Toland, Marquis d'Argens are insoluble.\n3. This proposition is directed against Bayle. He maintains: truths of faith and truths of reason are so directly opposed to one another that |\nsomething must seem plainly false according to reason, and indeed cannot be seen as other than false, which is nevertheless in fact true.",[]],"refl:2653":["The incorporeal nature of the human soul can be counted among the established truths.\nFurther, from ξ−φ:\nwhich were long doubtful (g disputed) and afterwards become certain: lunar air. Southern continent.\nFurther, from ψ−θ:\n*exploratae* [ascertained].",[]],"refl:2654":["It is an (g un)established truth that the soul continues its thinking without interruption after death.",[]],"refl:2655":["In the lower margin, probably \nIt increases conviction if one oneself seeks out and resolves the doubts that can be raised against a truth.",[]],"refl:2656":["If someone [nicht] has something in mind which hinders him in [Einsicht] assenting to the truth, and cannot express it, this need not be [erklärt] answered; nor can it be answered.\nOn L §. 178 no. 3:\nMany doubts can be raised against the Christian religion which one need not answer.\nOn L §. 178 no. 4:\nThe doubts which [1.] attack the ground from which one is certain of a truth. e.g. the credibility of the witnesses of an historical event, the inner possibility. [2. Den Zweifel aus dem Grunde hebt, in dem man zeigt, was den Schein der Warheit ausmacht.]\nOn L §. 178 no. 5:\nTo this answering it belongs that one presents the doubts in their full strength, abstracts for so long from the truth of the opposite, and afterwards refutes them. One removes the doubt at its root by discovering the false semblance.\nIn return, doubts contribute indirectly to the certainty of knowledge. He who, in a matter against which doubts can be raised, has never from the beginning had any, has not reflected enough upon it. In a matter where the rules of prudence require us to act according to probability, one should least of all let oneself be held up by doubts.",[]],"refl:2657":["Uncertainty concerning the order of the weather.\nOn L §. 179 no. 2:\nThe ancients were uncertain [ob das Blut circulire oder nicht] whether the soul lives after death.\nOn L §. 179 no. 3:\nWe are uncertain whether the moon has inhabitants, because we cannot observe it at close quarters.\nFurther, L 50':\nIn a theologian it is not blameworthy to be uncertain whether a *perpetuum mobile* is possible, but in a mechanic it is.\nL 50'. On L §. 179 no. 4:\nIf one very often occupies oneself with doubts and turns more attention to them than to the truth, uncertainty is kept alive.",[]],"refl:2658":["What, then, are the marks of certainty? 1. objective: *convenientia cum principiis catholicis* [agreement with universal principles]; 2. subjective: the light of conviction. Malebranche has knowledge only of the latter; 3. external: *consensus hominum et antiquitas* [the agreement of men and antiquity].",[]],"refl:2659":["It is uncertain whether the world is finite or infinite.\n[e.g. daß wir nicht wißen, wo der Ort der Seeligen oder verdamten sey.]\nOn L §. 180 no. 2:\nIt is no disgrace to a jurist to be uncertain whether, in the dispute over monads, Leibniz or his opponents are right.\nFurther, L 50':\nIt is necessary at times to confess uncertainty. Harmfulness of the mathematical method.\nL 50' on L §. 180 no. 3:\nA divine must be in no uncertainty in respect of the origin of evils.",[]],"refl:2660":["Of the mania for doubt.\n(s Pyrrhonian and academic doubt.)\nIts remote origin is ascribed to Socrates and the Academics, because they contended against the Sophists.\n*Dogmatici, Sceptici*. Pyrrho. *non liquet* [it is not clear]. He denied the certainty of the *dogmata*, not of experience.\nScepticism. *Logicus*: the Liar. *Physicus*: Achilles. *Moralis*: that the rules of virtue are arbitrary. *Historicus*: Bayle.\nSome sceptics denied the certainty of sensations [Empfindungen] of the senses. Pherecydes had his larder full of food and drink. He noticed that every day something was missing. He sealed the room. His servant took the seal off and sealed it up again. He complained of it to his friend. The friend laughed at him. The servant did it too crudely — — —\n(s Pyrrhonian and academic doubts. Arcesilas and Carneades.)\nHarmfulness of scepticism in morals [and] theology.\nRefutations from their own propositions. Arcesilaus said: not even this is certain, that everything is uncertain.\nGrounds which give occasion to scepticism. Discord of opinions. Of rational Pyrrhonism. Fundamental rule according to it: where the rules of prudence do not require us to act according to certain rules, one must suspend one's decisive judgment so long as clear counter-grounds are still present.",[]],"refl:2661":["Lower margin:\nOne must recognise not merely the uncertainty, but the insufficiency, and consequently the inadmissibility, of a decision. To call something uncertain as regards knowledge in general is itself to pass an uncertain judgment, and would amount to saying that something is uncertain in itself.",[]],"refl:2662":["Absolute scepticism declares everything to be semblance. It therefore distinguishes semblance from truth, and so must have a mark of the distinction, and thus presuppose a knowledge of truth. [Denn]\n(s Objective uncertainty can coexist with subjective conviction, i.e. with belief [Glauben]. Holding-true can be objectively changeable but subjectively unchangeable.)",[]],"refl:2663":["Critique is the art of judging the relation of holding-true to certainty.\nDogmatism: the way of thinking [Denkungsart] that [keine andre] lays claim to apodictic certainty. Scepticism: [the way of thinking that] admits none.",[]],"refl:2664":["Dogmatists (g universal knowledge by reason from concepts) and sceptics. Asserting and doubting philosophers.\nA maxim is a subjectively practical principle. The maxim of doubting is scepticism.\nComplaints about the uncertainty of human knowledge and about its limits.\nThe sceptical method is: to set grounds together with counter-grounds at equal strength.",[]],"refl:2665":["The critical method suspends judgment in the hope of attaining to it.",[]],"refl:2666":["The sceptical, dogmatic and critical way of thinking. Dogmatism is the doctrinal way of thinking* without critique (s of reason itself).\n*(s in philosophical [Erkentnis] knowledge that extends itself *a priori*. The semblance here arises through success (g either) in [Fäll] mathematics or in such cases where experience confirms the propositions or where the concepts are elucidated by mere analysis.)",[]],"refl:2667":["Dogmatism is the [Grundsatz] prejudice*, in [Vernu Beurth] respect of modes of knowledge by reason, that one can dispense with the critique of this faculty of reason itself (g for the sake of success), e.g. mathematics and physics — but not in metaphysics or pure knowledge by reason from concepts. — Scepticism: the prejudice, for the sake of failure, of trusting no knowledge by reason.\n*(g of the sufficiency of reason in respect of all its employment, [auch] without needing a critique of its own faculty. — This has produced scepticism. — Criticism is the [Zut] confidence of reason in itself, restricted to the condition of its self-knowledge.)",[]],"refl:2668":["Lower margin, below no. 2655. Probably on the beginning of L §. 178:\nTo fear something, it is not necessary that it be probable, but merely that it be possible; but to hope [for something], it must be probable. [weil man nicht] Hence critique is more necessary than dogmatics.",[]],"refl:2669":["Dogmatism is blind trust in the faculty of reason to extend itself *a priori* (g without critique) through mere concepts, [ohne] merely for the sake of its [Gelin] apparent success.",[]],"refl:2670":["Dogmatism is the way of thinking (g maxim) of being exempt from all critique of the faculty of reason, even in judging of those things [die nicht durch Anschauung] whose concept is not capable of any intuition, merely for the sake of apparent success.",[]],"refl:2671":["The supposed success in the investigations of reason (g in mathematics indeed, but not for that reason in knowledge by reason according to concepts) has produced dogmatism, i.e. the maxim of being able to judge about objects *a priori*, whether they be those of the senses or supersensible, without any previous investigation of our faculties of knowledge. But in respect of the latter [the supersensible], the supposed success consisted only in this, that [man weder auf ein], so long as one met with no contradiction, no intuition (pure or empirical) refuted us.",[]],"refl:2672":["There is no probability in judgments of pure reason concerning ideas and their reality.\nScepticism as a principle renounces all assertoric judgment; belief [renounces] the apodictic.",[]],"refl:2673":["something.\nIn the right margin on L §. 181 «gemeine Meinung»:\ne.g. that there were pre-Adamites; that Paradise was in the moon.\nIn L §. 181, above «Meinung» (l. 461 27):\nPresupposition.\nIn the right margin on L §. 181 «gelehrte Meinung»:\nThat a comet caused the Flood.",[]],"refl:2674":["Either at the same time probable or arbitrary.\nOn L §. 182 no. 2:\nBecause they are without probability and without use. e.g. That Hell is in the sun.\nL 51'. On L §. 182 no. 3:\n3. To some it is an opinion that the earth rotates, to others a certainty.\nL 51. On L §. 182 no. 5:\nLimits of opinions.",[]],"refl:2675":["On L §. 182 no. 5?\nHypotheses are indispensable. 1. (g They are experiments of the understanding.) One must approach many a truth by the way of probability. 2. They are met with everywhere. e.g. in dividing. 3. They show the truth either through manifold agreement, e.g. the hypotheses of the motion of the earth; or by showing which grounds one is not to assume (g e.g. gravity). 4. They encourage the understanding through the prize of discovery.\n*Hypothesis subsidiaria* [subsidiary hypothesis]. When in (g the application of) a hypothesis (g or [in the attempt] to show its agreements with the *phaenomena*) yet another occurs. They weaken the probability.",[]],"refl:2676":["Hypothesis: existence of bodies.\nFurther, L 51:\nIt must be tested according to the rules of probability. It can attain to certainty.\nL 51. On L §. 183 no. 2:\nThey are the scaffolding which afterwards falls away. e.g. that of Tycho.\nOn L §. 183 no. 3:\ne.g. That the earth is an animal. Kepler.\nOn L §. 183 no. 4:\nLove for this child of the understanding. e.g. subterranean fire.\nL 52. On L §. 183 no. 5:\nWhen that which I assume for the explanation of something else is not known from other marks, but only from its agreement with the *phaenomena*, e.g. magnetic matter.\nThe hypothesis must [warscheinlicher seyn als d] be more distinctly known than what is explained, and so must not share the same obscurity with it. e.g. freezing [explained] from a frigorific matter.\nL 52'. On L §. 183 no. 6:\nBy showing that no other can take place, and that it satisfies all the *phaenomena*.\nOn L §. 183 no. 7:\nDescartes.",[]],"refl:2677":["L 51:\nAn uncertain mode of knowledge, when it is held to be uncertain, is entitled opinion; if it seems certain, it is persuasion. This is always *formaliter* false. An opinion can be *formaliter* true and [for] *materialiter* false, and conversely.",[]],"refl:2678":["The opinion of the truth of a ground [inferred] from its sufficiency for the consequence is hypothesis. If the impossibility of all other grounds is proved (g this happens when all the consequences of the assumed ground agree with the appearance), it becomes certainty. If the very same thing that is established through its consequences can also be proved *a priori* independently, the hypothesis is confirmed. (s i.e. if the matter of the ground is independently certain, or also the universal form. [nur nicht die zusamm])\n(s Unity from the unity of the ground)\nThe fruitfulness of a hypothesis: when still more true consequences flow from it. Simplicity. That it be not arbitrary *a priori*.\n(s An opinion as the ground of a system is hypothesis.)\nSome hypotheses are necessary, as: there is another world; some are contingent.\nThe absolutely necessary hypothesis is that of the necessary being. It cannot be proved *a priori*.\nFreedom is a necessary hypothesis, without which practical propositions would not be possible. There is a God, another world: a moral hypothesis.\nThere are subjectively necessary hypotheses of the possibility of knowledge through reason, e.g. that everything has a beginning.\nThe possibility of a hypothesis must be certain. Rational hypotheses are not permitted.",[]],"refl:2679":["(s From the consequences I cannot safely infer a determinate ground, except when the ground fits all the consequences.)\nThere are three kinds of hypotheses: 1. Those by whose assumption I can explain a manifold of mutually agreeing consequences. (s Belief is not a hypothesis, for it is not opining.)\n2. Those without which I can have through reason no concept of the possibility of certain *data*.\n3. Those without which the thing is impossible.\nThe supreme hypothesis would be that which [ich] is in itself a hypothesis and cannot be further comprehended through reason.\n*Hypothesis originaria** [original hypothesis] is not capable of any proof, [li] but is a *sumtion* [assumption] which lies at the basis of the employment of reason.\n*(g without which [eine andere] a universal employment of the understanding and of reason would be impossible.)",[]],"refl:2680":["51.\nL 51':\n1. The assumed ground must be certain as regards its possibility*.\n2. The consequence. 3. Unity of the ground.** No *hypothesis subsidiaria*.\n(s 4.*** Sufficiency for all consequences. (g This is always uncertain.) This is absolutely not possible. Hence it always remains hypothesis.)\n*(s Possibility cannot be assumed by hypothesis.)\n**(s because otherwise the contingent combination of several assumed grounds is a new hypothesis.)\n***(g In metaphysics there are no (s theoretical) hypotheses. Because (g the consequences are not given in experience) the actuality of the consequences themselves is not certain *a posteriori* in it; namely, it cannot be confirmed by experience. We cannot assume an ethereal body, because we do not even know for certain that we shall come into connection with the corporeal world. But *a priori* these consequences cannot be known; otherwise the ground would not be a hypothesis. Metaphysics is concerned with modes of knowledge which, being so universal, cannot be proved by experience. It goes beyond the limits of experience. The consequences, therefore, are not given through experience.)\n[The s-additions so far, except for ll. 6f. (ω), date from ρ−χ. In θ the two following s-additions were then added, the first of which is joined by a stroke to the 3 in 467 1, which was probably changed at the same time into a 4:]\n(s 3. It must not contradict any known truths (appearances).)\n(s 5. Were it sufficient for all consequences, it would be certain.)\n[Finally in ω a new no. 2. was inserted to the right of 1. Der — seyn (466 27); accordingly the 2 in 467 1 was changed into 3, and perhaps (though not probably) only now also the |\nfollowing 3 into 4 (cf. 467 23f.). Further, in three more s-additions (ω) nos. 5-7 were added:]\n(s 2. The consequent truth must be certain. e.g. — — ghosts.)\n(s 5. The sufficiency for given consequences must also be certain.)\n(s 6. The sufficiency for all consequences remains uncertain; hence a hypothesis is always only a hypothesis.)\n(s 7. Hypotheses cannot be forged wholly *a priori*, because the relation of cause to effect cannot be known wholly *a priori*.)\n(s NB. [H] As a hypothesis a proposition can be necessary in order to defend a mode of knowledge certain in itself; for this it is only necessary to show that it is not impossible. It is a right which one has against him who himself permits himself hypotheses.)\n[The two following remarks from ω also seem to relate to Rfl. 2680 (in the form deriving from ω):]\n(s E.g. the Copernican system. 1. That the earth rotates is possible. 2. That the stars seem to move from east to west is certain. 3. That this can follow from that is [breaks off].)\nL 51:\n(s 1) Not sorcery — not metempsychosis. 2.) To try to explain something false by hypothesis, e.g. that it has rained stones. 3) when the consequence is indeed truth, but [die Cons] in|consequence\nin the connection with the ground. e.g. cold with the north wind.)",[]],"refl:2681":["All hypotheses are relative [Vorausset] warrants to assume something arbitrarily, namely, in order to find, (g for) something that must have a cause, this cause. They are warrants to invent heuristically. Absolutely, one may invent nothing at all. Unity of the ground is the proper mark of truth, especially the greater the manifold in the effects. *Hypotheses subsidiariae* weaken assent. Natural events do not allow hypotheses to obtain apodictic certainty, because we never know the effect completely. Interpretation of Scripture. Ancient history.",[]],"refl:2682":["A hypothesis is an opinion that something is, because the consequences, which are [actual], can be derived from it. The possibility of the presupposition must be certain.",[]],"refl:2683":["There are *canones* of reason *a priori*, i.e. rules without which no employment of reason is possible. e.g. That certain laws, according to which alone we can bring appearances under rules, are true. Of these no opinion holds. e.g. Hydroscope. Ventriloquists. |\nMontgeron's Abbé Paris. Sympathy. This certainty is that of the rational employment of modes of knowledge.",[]],"refl:2684":["Necessary hypothesis of the theoretical (g moral) and practical employment of our reason in general.",[]],"refl:2685":["What cannot be proved by itself, e.g. human freedom, but must necessarily be presupposed in respect of another practically certain mode of knowledge, e.g. morals, is a practically necessary hypothesis of a theoretical mode of knowledge.",[]],"refl:2686":["If a hypothesis is necessary in order to prove something, I cannot assume it without having made its possibility certain. |\nBut if the proposition is certain even without it, and only the objection, which likewise rests upon hypothesis, is to be warded off, then I can assume it without showing its possibility. So it stands with the immortality of the soul, [als ein] in so far as it is necessary to the energy of morality.",[]],"refl:2687":["That hypotheses as such can never obtain complete certainty lies in the mode of inference, that of inferring determinately from consequences to grounds.",[]],"refl:2688":["The effects in experience are contingent and can have another cause; but morality is absolutely necessary and, since it is determined through concepts *a priori*, can have only a condition determined *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2689":["No opinion in mathematics, metaphysics, morals.\nThe hypothesis subjectively necessary to reason for the theoretical or practical employment of every reason, e.g. freedom: *hypothesis originaria*.",[]],"refl:2690":["Hypothesis is [die Warheit die] the holding-true which rests solely upon the sufficiency of an opinion for known consequences.",[]],"refl:2691":["If it is in itself necessary that something ought to happen which is not possible without a presupposition, then this [presupposition] is necessary. Now it is not in itself necessary to explain the existence of the world, but it is necessary to promote the highest good morally.",[]],"refl:2692":["A hypothesis is necessary when without a presupposition nothing at all can be done in respect of a problem. (s or an objection against a certain truth cannot be refuted, e.g. a future life, for refuting the objection on account of the evil of this life.) Theoretical hypotheses are optional. A practical one is necessary.",[]],"refl:2693":["Hypothesis is a way of opining through reason (thus a necessity of inferring from subjective principles of reason).",[]],"refl:2694":["Hypothesis is a judgment which infers from the sufficiency of a ground for given consequences to its actuality.",[]],"refl:2695":["Question: whether a conviction or subjective certainty always presupposes the objective [certainty]; whether, therefore, two persons of equal powers of insight cannot be convinced of opposite propositions.\nThe rules for the guidance of the understanding which arise from the comparison of subjective knowledge constitute *logica experimentalis*.\nBoth are right.\nCaution in regard to the presumption of prejudice.",[]],"refl:2696":["On the second sentence of L §. 184:\nsince certainty in the subjective sense does not agree with certainty in the objective [sense].\nFurther, above «Der Irrthum, durch» (l. 25), from ρ3−ψ (? κ3? µ?):\nnot merely error.\nFurther, in γ−ξ the words «Der — sind» (ll. 25f.) are replaced in the margin by the following remark:\nHolding-true from grounds of which one does not know whether they are [subiec] merely subjective or also objective",[]],"refl:2697":["Of the greatest extensive clearness and small intensive [clearness].\nFurther, L 52':\ne.g. to unmask dazzling inferences.\nL 52'. On L §. 184 no. 2:\nHence those who know no mathematics are easily persuaded.\nOn L §. 184 no. 3:\nFrom obscure insight into the grounds.\nOn L §. 184 no. 4:\nOne wants to reach one's end. Examination is laborious. Occasioning motives are necessary. Rational prejudices. One must remember one's false persuasion and be cautious.\n5. When one takes the grounds of the opposite into no consideration at all.",[]],"refl:2698":["[Ein subiectiver Grund des] Holding-true from a subjective (g [zureichenden Gründen]) ground* which is held to be objective (g [zureichend]).**\nIf we know that we are free from all subjective grounds, and yet the holding-true is complete, we are convinced. The influence of subjective grounds is very imperceptible.\n*(s without [zu untersuchen] being able to distinguish whether they [nicht] are objective or merely subjective, [ob] or [when we] take the preponderance of the grounds we know for the preponderance of the grounds in themselves.)\n**(s When we have grounds on one side and know of none on the opposite side, nor seek them. *verisimilitas* [verisimilitude].)",[]],"refl:2699":["Persuasion (g of honest people.) can be morally good, although logically bad.",[]],"refl:2700":["Complete holding-true from subjective grounds which in [Gewisser] practical relation are valid as much as objective ones is also conviction, but not logical, rather practical, conviction.",[]],"refl:2701":["objective. An extensive learned knowledge about a principal object.\nFurther:\nAstrology is not a science.",[]],"refl:2702":["The sum-total of a knowledge as*\naggregate is common knowledge; as system it is science.\nThere is historical science and science of reason. In a science we often know only the modes of knowledge, but not the things represented through them; **thus there can be a science of that of which our knowledge is not knowing [Wissen].\n*(g in the aggregate the unity is contingent, in the system necessary.)\n**(s Critique.)",[]],"refl:2703":["The manifold [ist] of knowledge is either an aggregate or a science. In the former it is connected (g fragmentarily) rhapsodically*, in the latter systematically; in the former according to one and the same concept, in the latter according to an idea. The former knowledge is that of the amateur, the latter that of the master. The condescension of the latter is popularity; [its] singularity is pedantry.\n*(g technically (s according to similarities); in science architectonically (s according to affinities under a common descent).)",[]],"refl:2704":["Art [Kunst], from *können* [to be able].\nThe capacity for carrying out a practical idea, which does not follow immediately from the knowledge of the rules. (s is skill. Systematic skill is art.)",[]],"refl:2705":["The unity of manifold skills, in so far as they hang together through an idea, is art. [Der Zusammenhang Der] Ability [Können] from imitation and not according to an idea is handicraft.\nColumbus's egg.",[]],"refl:2706":["Knowledge without science, skill without art.",[]],"refl:2707":["Art is [systematische] skill in the carrying out of an idea which is not yet determined through [das] the common knowledge of the [Gegenstandes selbst] rules.",[]],"refl:2708":["A system is a whole in so far as [es] (s the consciousness of its totality) is possible only according to principles.",[]],"refl:2709":["Surveying is only mechanical art, for if one knows the theory one can [do] the latter. Properly, art in the objective sense is [was] a skill which is not possible at all through instruction [und], under which imitation may also be counted, [also] and may be entitled art of genius; but in the subjective sense too many a thing is entitled art (g which) in comparison with another is merely mechanical skill, which is opposed to free skill, which cannot be learned.\nThe sum-total of skills, considered as a system, may also be entitled art, i.e. (g as) a whole which is possible only according to principles.\nArt in the objective sense is",[]],"refl:2710":["Morals. Logic.\nL 53'. On L §. 186, end:\nOf the gesture of an orator.",[]],"refl:2711":["Chiromancy.",[]],"refl:2712":["either of the unconditioned [certainty], from the concepts of the subject and predicate, or of the conditioned, from the connection.",[]],"refl:2713":["Through inferences grounded on experience.\nOn L §. 189, sentence 2:\nThe equilibrium of fluid matters.\nOn L §. 189, sentence 3:\nThat nothing can grow without air.",[]],"refl:2714":["(s all certainty is either theoretical or practical*; the former either [mathematisch gewiß] empirical or rational certainty [(diese apodictisch)].)\nempirical or apodictic, and the latter intuitive or discursive.**\n(s I am morally certain of that which, according to my conscience, it is necessary to accept as true. e.g. The deed of a criminal who confesses it.)\nan apodictic,\nmathematical proof, which has evidence.\nHypotheses cannot be proved mathematically.\n*(g The practical [certainty] is likewise grounded either on empirical or on rational laws of the will; the former: [those] of prudence, the latter: of morality and of duty. I am morally certain when I know that to accept something as true and to act accordingly agrees with my whole duty.)\n**(s Practical certainty is either pragmatic, according to rules of prudence (where the rule [und ihre Hyp Gewiß] is merely hypothetically necessary, as in games of chance), or moral, according to principles of morality (where the rules are absolutely necessary, and the hypothesis of their observance *in concreto* is thereby practically certain, an article of faith of reason). Of the supposed moral certainty of the existence of bodies.)",[]],"refl:2715":["intuitive or discursive certainty. Both historical or rational.",[]],"refl:2716":["That is morally certain whose holding-true (g rests on principles which) are inseparable from the maxims [uns] of duty.\nOf no mode of knowledge, in relation to the object, can one say that it is morally certain, but only of one's belief, in relation to ourselves, that it has moral certainty. I find myself obligated to think thus.\nMoral certainty is the greatest.",[]],"refl:2717":["theoretical, practical.\nFurther, L 53, on §. 190, sentence 1:\nI am not in a position always to know the truth. I cannot know whether there may not nevertheless be creatures in the moon, although it has no air.",[]],"refl:2718":["Decimal reckoning.\nFurther:\nTo divide a circle into 360 degrees.\nFurther, from γ−ρ (? ψ−χ??):\nare arbitrary signs of thoughts.\nFurther, from γ−χ:\nNo truth is arbitrary; rather it is necessitated by the object; they are *positiones* [positings].\nFurther, from ψ−χ:\n*Positiones voluntariae* [voluntary positings].",[]],"refl:2719":["Complete certainty from the distinct knowledge of the connection of a judgment with its grounds.",[]],"refl:2720":["The ground [of a] of [complete] [exhaustively distinct] certainty.\nL 54'. Opposite L §. 191, sentence 2:\nEither *demonstratio genetica* [genetic demonstration] or *per rationes cognoscendi* [through grounds of knowing].\nIn the first, the ground precedes the truth, that is, [the ground] without which the predicate does not belong to the subject; e.g. All men err. Or *per rationes cognoscendi*: here [the] truth is inferred from the consequence to [the ground]; e.g. Because errors are perceived.\nThe former demonstrations are the better.",[]],"refl:2721":["*Baculus stat in angulo* [The stick stands in the corner].\nOn L §. 191 »probatio insufficiens«:\nFor example, [the proof] of the existence of God from the agreement of the nations.\nFurther, L 54, beside the last two sentences of L §. 191, from µ−ψ:\nApodictic, intuitive certainty. Demonstrative.\nTo represent *a priori* in intuition.\nImmediately certain.",[]],"refl:2722":["Of subject and predicate: 1. of the analysis of both; 2. of the consideration of their connection.\nFurther, L 54', opposite the beginning of §. 192:\nEither not necessary or not possible.\nL 54'. On §. 192 »indemonstrable knowledge«:\nWhere nothing distinct from the proposition itself (g neither *materialiter* nor *formaliter*, neither other things nor (g a resolution into) more distinct concepts) can be assigned as its ground; e.g. *Quicquid est est* [Whatever is, is].\nWe must not, out of laziness or out of prejudice, give something out as certain and indemonstrable.\nFurther, L 54, from µ−ψ:\n1. Indemonstrable as a groundless assertion,\n2. [or] an immediate certainty.\nFurther, L 54, from ψ−θ:\nnot to be proved.",[]],"refl:2723":["There is no demonstration except where the universal can be proved *coram intuitu a priori* [before an *a priori* intuition], and so *a priori* and yet *in concreto*.",[]],"refl:2724":["We can prove something to ourselves or to others. The latter is possible only where the grounds of proof and their connection can be communicated. That something is experience we must often first prove to ourselves; [we must make sure that it is] not, say, a *vitium subreptionis* [fallacy of subreption] regarding cause and effect, or imagination in place of perception (were fanatics to do this, they would not be fanatics). Or [we must prove] to others that something is actual experience, although they have not had it themselves; that is, the proof of historical credibility. Proofs of reason *a priori* can be communicated.",[]],"refl:2725":["We are accustomed to say: that is as certain as that twice 2 is 4. But in philosophy no proposition is ever so certain (in respect of its form). For it is to be apodictically, not empirically, certain, and therefore [a pr] [certain] from grounds *a priori*. Philosophical knowledge, however, can have this certainty only [from] through concepts, mathematics only through their construction; the former merely discursively, the latter intuitively. In the former an error can lie hidden; in the latter it cannot.",[]],"refl:2726":["e.g. *theorema pythagoricum* [the Pythagorean theorem].\nOn L §. 193 no. 1:\n[*Baculus stat in angulo, ergo pluit.* The stick stands in the corner, therefore it is raining.]",[]],"refl:2727":["No errors are small *in sensu absoluto* [in the absolute sense].",[]],"refl:2728":["Many proofs are good.",[]],"refl:2729":["The world is the best.",[]],"refl:2730":["To refute someone.\nTo refute the knowledge.",[]],"refl:2731":["(s This holds only where the propositions really are *contradictorie opposita* [contradictory opposites]. (s A and non-A, but not A and B, where B is not-A. For there there can be a middle, namely that both are impossible.))\nWhen of two (g contradicting) [disputants] each can refute the other's proposition, but neither can directly prove his own, both have made their case well: each has convicted the other of error, but neither has thereby proved his own proposition. For a false idea lies at the basis, which, if it is assumed, necessarily |\ngenerates contradiction. E.g. The world of sense (as an absolute whole) is either infinite or finite. Both are false, because there is no absolute whole as object of the senses, and no appearance that exists absolutely and in itself.",[]],"refl:2732":["(s The given multitude of all the parts (taken together) is either finite or infinite. Neither: the multitude of all the parts is not given (together).)\nA body consists either of infinitely many parts or of a (g in itself) determinate finite number. Neither; it is divisible to infinity, that is, we determine the parts only through our *progressus*, and the concept of the parts lies only in this *progressus*. In itself the *phaenomenon* [sind] is nothing, together with its parts.\nThat everything has natural necessity (according to laws of appearance), and consequently also man's inner way of thinking, [which] of which the appearances perhaps give a very incomplete sign, is just as false as that actions, as appearances, are free.\nFor example: all flowers must have either one of these seven colours or a mixture of them. Neither: they can quite well be represented without any colours at all.\nThe world either has a beginning or it is from eternity. Neither, in itself: it has neither in itself a beginning nor in itself an infinite duration; it is to be met with only in our possible experience, which is [never] nowhere bounded, nor yet infinite, but goes on to infinity.\nWhether God is in motion or at rest in space?\n(g To assert: God is at rest because he is not in motion. Neither holds. He is in no place at all.)",[]],"refl:2733":["When the condition (g unnoticed) attached to a concept contradicts it, [two] both opposed judgments can be refuted.",[]],"refl:2734":["In every case where a mere idea, which we cannot give in intuition, lies at the basis, e.g. the absolute whole of the world in respect of space, we cannot prove apagogically. For we do not at once see that the ideas are empty or even self-contradictory, since this first requires [the A] other investigations.",[]],"refl:2735":["empirical proof,\nanalogical,\napodictic.",[]],"refl:2736":["Errors may have been introduced into the proof. Example of [the proof of] the existence of God from the many purposes of things. e.g. Mystery. We can arrive at truth through sheer error.\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\nIf experience contradicts the proof, the proof is false.",[]],"refl:2737":["The harm done by false and weak proofs.",[]],"refl:2738":["Most are mediate. Man has much experience.\nFurther, L 57':\nMuch that we take to be immediate experience is mediate experience. Hence the alleged error of the senses. Inferences from experiences.",[]],"refl:2739":["All knowledge comes first from the senses. The senses give |\nsingle sensations; the understanding notes which of them are usually together and in what order they are together.",[]],"refl:2740":["Appearance, (g intuition,) perception (sensation with consciousness), and experience (perception according to rules).",[]],"refl:2741":["Experience is the agreement of perceptions (empirical representations) for the knowledge of an object.",[]],"refl:2742":["That something is experience we must often first prove to ourselves: *illusio phantasiae vel vitium subreptionis* [illusion of the imagination or fallacy of subreption].",[]],"refl:2743":["I can well be immediately certain of my [experience] perception, but not of experience, that is, of the objective validity of judgments from perception. For this, repeated comparison is required, in order to distinguish what |\nthe understanding does from what is sensible; and often also the agreement of the judgment of others.",[]],"refl:2744":["Inferences drawn from experience must not be confused with experiences.\nFurther, L 57':\nIt was believed to be experienced that the piston in the pump draws the water.\nWe believe that we sense that the sourness lies in the vinegar itself, whereas we ought to believe that it lies only in the tongue, and the cause of this affection in the vinegar.\nL 57. On L §. 203 no. 3:\nfor the sake of the philosophical.\nL 58'. On the conclusion of L §. 203:\nWhich certainty is greater: mathematical certainty of the first rank or [of] another.",[]],"refl:2745":["If it is to be proved from reason that the world is not eternal, I must not appeal to the testimony of Scripture.",[]],"refl:2746":["If we are to prove that a pot, when the air has been driven out of it by fire, will suck water into itself, we must not appeal to experience.",[]],"refl:2747":["Experiences are either our own or those of other people.\nOn L §. 206, sentence 2:\nFor example, I wish to prove that it does not rain in the lower part of Peru.\nOn L §. 206, sentence 3:\nHistorical belief. Living [belief], on account of the inner testimony.\nOn the concluding sentence of L §. 206:\nWe must believe only *res facti** [matters of fact], not *veritates universales* [universal truths], except on the testimony of supernaturally enlightened witnesses.\n*(s We can also know historically.)",[]],"refl:2748":["On L §. 204:\n*A priori* knowledge is that through reason.",[]],"refl:2749":["To believe something or to believe someone, morally or historically.",[]],"refl:2750":["Reason, experience, testimony.",[]],"refl:2751":["The inference from consequences to grounds ought to be entitled *a posteriori*; [the inference] from experiences to universal judgments, *ab inferiori ad superius* [from the lower to the higher].",[]],"refl:2752":["Moral belief: confidence in the uprightness of another. Unbelieving in respect of virtue and providence.\nTo do good on belief is [to do it] without certainty of reward, being content, if need be, to have none at all for it.",[]],"refl:2753":["From above downwards and from below upwards. The former alone proves universal propositions; the latter singular ones, or also the possibility of things. A demonstration can indeed acquire illustration from experience, but not confirmation; otherwise it lacks evidence. Reason and experience must be subordinated to one another if each is to supply the other with the *complementum* to a proof. If they are coordinated, they do not increase each other's certainty, just as a *cavens* [surety] who has nothing is not strengthened by another who also has nothing.",[]],"refl:2754":["Historical belief is directed to what is; practical belief to what will happen. The latter, when it is a belief in |\nvirtue and uprightness, is moral. Without it no society is possible. Good faith in promising, and belief in expecting. Unbelief in dealing: *graeca fide* [Greek faith, i.e. cash only]. We also believe that God will do something that he has not promised, because it accords [zu] with the dignity of virtue.\nIn historical belief we must know, first, the relation to the object (s the possibility of the thing) (which must be an experience of the other), then to the subject (his logical quality; [experience] empirical: memory and capacity of speech; moral; and his situation of advantage or disadvantage).",[]],"refl:2755":["We must never believe evil of others; if we are to act upon it, we must know it. The good we must believe, if we have merely a logical ground for opining it.",[]],"refl:2756":["A theoretical ground of holding-to-be-true may also be entitled belief, because one might well wager on it; for example, that there are inhabitants on the moon.",[]],"refl:2757":["Testimony to objective modes of rational knowledge; testimony to one's own conviction.",[]],"refl:2758":["Belief is twofold: [as] either in respect of matter (g historical),\nas [princ] the ground for holding something to be true, or\nin respect of form (g intellectual),\nas the manner of holding something to be true.",[]],"refl:2759":["Division into rational knowledge and empirical knowledge. The latter into one's own experience and knowledge from reports.",[]],"refl:2760":["Experiments in regard to what has been judged *a priori*. Confirmation. Test.",[]],"refl:2761":["only of what one has experienced.",[]],"refl:2762":["historical. There is also a rational belief in regard to the practical.\nFurther ω? (φ?):\nTo believe something or to believe someone.",[]],"refl:2763":["Historical belief is rational when it is critical.",[]],"refl:2764":["He who is incredulous cannot supplement his deficient experience through reports. He takes everything for lies.",[]],"refl:2765":["Believing on testimony does not differ at all from holding-to-be-true through one's own experience, either in degree or in kind. But believing on (g logically) insufficient grounds of reason does so differ. The former we can know; the latter is not a knowing.\n(g Belief is either historical or rational belief. The latter is practical.)\n(s Matters of opinion, matters of belief, matters of fact.)",[]],"refl:2766":["Matters of belief (*a priori*) are (s true propositions which nevertheless lie in no possible experience.) those in regard to which we not only |\ncan know nothing, but can also opine nothing, and in regard to which we cannot even plead probability, but only that it is not contradictory to think an object so. The rest is a free (g logical) holding-to-be-true, which is necessary only for a practical purpose given *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2767":["Unbelief can be imputed, but not the lack of historical belief.",[]],"refl:2768":["The greater the moral disposition, the more it proceeds from mere belief in all that strengthens it; and in order that actions too may proceed from that [disposition], it is necessary that these incentives be only believed.",[]],"refl:2769":["Only 2 sources of knowledge: reason and experience. For belief is mediate experience, through the experience attested by others. *Formaliter*, believing is opposed to knowing; *materialiter*, [it belongs] to the communication of another's experience.",[]],"refl:2770":["He is morally unbelieving who does not accept what it is indeed impossible to know, but morally necessary to presuppose.\nRational belief is the knowledge of the necessity of a hypothesis [of the theoret] of reason, without which the (g absolutely) necessary practical laws would be wholly null. It is therefore a necessary hypothesis of practical reason.",[]],"refl:2771":["There is a rational belief and a historical one. The first rests on subjective grounds of morality (g the objective ground is the possibility of the thing: absolute and conditioned), that is, on the moral disposition, and on morality's being an incentive for him [who believes]. The unbeliever disowns this incentive; his lack of conviction rests on his own fault and can be imputed to him. Historical unbelief, as a way of thinking, likewise; but not as a lack of the [Über] requisite grounds in particular cases.",[]],"refl:2772":["(s *Citatio autorum* [the citing of authors] is not *testimonium*.)\nA witness is authentic who can himself know something (g unimpeachable: when he not only can but will tell the truth), *testis exceptione maior* [a witness beyond exception]. A witness who needs another witness (s *testis subsidiarius*) is unreliable; in so far as there are objections against his sincerity, suspect. A series of witnesses one after another [ver] does not increase credibility but diminishes it. *Testes coordinati sibi dant complementum ad sufficientiam* [coordinated witnesses supply one another the complement to sufficiency]. The testimony that something comes from an eyewitness is often difficult. Levity; a timorous readiness to believe.",[]],"refl:2773":["Believing*: he who acts on belief *a priori***; he is opposed to the slave of the senses.\n*(g theoretically: he who is capable of a moral rational belief where no knowing is possible.)\n**(g Morality is of such a kind that whoever is not capable of acting merely on the belief that virtue and good conduct will one day receive their good consequences, but needs knowledge of this, does not act from moral principles. For a moral belief, mere possibility suffices as objective ground. For a theoretical [belief] more is required, and there the measure cannot be determined.)",[]],"refl:2774":["[This] is the credibility of the testimony, so far as it can be known from the moral (g and physical) character of the witness.\nL 59'. On L §. 207 no. 1:\nIf someone claims to have seen something [that almost no] and has poor eyes.\nL 59'. On L §. 207 no. 2:\nHe must expect no advantage from what he testifies.\nHe must have an abhorrence of lies.",[]],"refl:2775":["He who claims to have seen everything is not credible.\nFurther, L 59, from ρ3−θ:\n*subordinatio testium* [subordination of witnesses].",[]],"refl:2776":["If someone relates something from former times and can cite no eyewitness, he is not to be believed.\nOn L §. 209 no. 2:\nMuch is required for an experience: 1. that one does not mix one's judgment with the experience; 2. that he has the gift of attention. E.g. ghosts.",[]],"refl:2777":["of a particular occurrence.\nFurther, from ρ3−θ:\nwhether something is such, or tested testimony.\nL 60. β1. Beside L §. 210 »fehlt — genung« (ll. 27f.):\nExample in Bouguer.",[]],"refl:2778":["He who is wholly disinterested speaks the truth; he who promises himself no good but from the truth, e.g. youth, also speaks the truth.\nIt does not follow that, because I cannot discern the good he promises himself, he has no purpose.\nOpposite L §. 211 »Und — Gute« (50635, 50721):\nHe may speak sincerely by his own lights; but he mixes his judgment with the senses.",[]],"refl:2779":["For example, a squadron of horsemen on a needle's point.\nAfter L §. 212 no. 2 Kant wishes to have inserted:\n3. When all the other presumptive eyewitnesses are silent.",[]],"refl:2780":["[It] is sustained by the mania for doubting. The habit of not believing a (g historically probable) testimony for the sake of rational probab|ility\nis entitled circumspection. The habit of according [with] to [a slight] every historical probability an assent proportioned to it is also thinking.\nOn L §. 213 »credulitas«:\n[It] is sustained by prejudices, by laziness.\nBoth are equally harmful.",[]],"refl:2781":["Unbelief is practical (g in regard to its consequences) in so far as one is not inclined to do good in hope of the other world that one hopes for. In regard to its grounds it is moral when one does not recognise the agreement with moral grounds.",[]],"refl:2782":["Practical, and especially moral, unbelief is that [in which the] which has a lack of moral interest as its ground; for otherwise [the unbeliever] would have to accept something. Morally certain. A believer is one who readily believes what contributes to moral interest; e.g. belief in virtue.",[]],"refl:2783":["He is incredulous who will accept nothing on testimonies [except so] that is not confirmed sufficiently to amount to knowing. (Here we must |\nsupply much by [Hypo] arbitrary assumption, in order to be able to make the attempt to arrive at certain modes of knowledge.) He is usually distrustful and suspicious.\nHe is unbelieving who will not grant something on morally (s for the practical observance of duty) sufficient grounds, merely because [it] he cannot become theoretically certain of it. [This] shows a lack of moral interest. The unbeliever does nothing on belief (in another world); he demands seal and letters, and cash.",[]],"refl:2784":["He is unbelieving who will accept nothing as true except on theoretically sufficient grounds. For there are also practically necessary hypotheses, and indeed ones that extend the practical use [of reason].",[]],"refl:2785":["Blind belief.",[]],"refl:2786":["Spiritual experience.\nOn L §. 215 no. 2:\nTheoretical belief.\nOn L §. 215 no. 3, from ψ−θ:\nOf rational belief, which always concerns the moral. [It] is distinct from empirical belief.",[]],"refl:2787":["Many insufficient proofs never make up a sufficient one.\nOnly many grounds of a single proof, as *compartes* [joint parts], can make up the sufficient ground of holding-to-be-true.",[]],"refl:2788":["*Fides est vel asserti,\nvel promissi,\nvel speculativa; posterior si sit [moralis est] absque promisso, est moralis.* [Belief is either of what is asserted, or of what is promised, or speculative; the latter, if it is without a promise, is moral.]\nBelief is properly a practical hypothesis [for the moving |\nof our will in regard to that] (s which is necessarily bound up with) that which is known in and for itself as certainly good.\nWe do not say: I know that the human race had a beginning, but: I believe it. It is therefore a hypothesis under which alone I can understand its existence through reason, although I cannot understand that [beginning] itself through reason. The beginning of the world and its infinite duration are equally incomprehensible; but the former makes my rational knowledge complete, the latter does not. (s That hypothesis is a necessary hypothesis of reason.)\nThus I believe every necessary hypothesis. This means: to accept something as if it were an arbitrary *position*; mathematical propositions are not accepted [in this way].\nOf propositions of reason that are believed. [They] are more than opinions. We cannot dispense with them.",[]],"refl:2789":["(s In knowing and believing, the subjectively unchangeable holding-to-be-true is belief, not opinion.)\nProperly there are not 3 but only 2 sources of knowledge*: experience and reason. 1. The former either our own or another's communicated experience [historical belief]. 2. Reason: either a knowing or a believing, that is, either from [the] speculative or from practical reason. Rational belief.\n(s NB. Holding-to-be-true is distinguished either by its sources, or by its kind and degree. In the latter case it is knowing or believing. Historical belief is no special principle of knowledge, but belongs to experience. Knowing belongs to theoretical, believing to practical rational knowledge.)\n*(s Both can be a knowing. If they are entitled belief, they are recognised as uncertain; and in that case it is not permissible to venture on such a belief anything that would be wrong if this knowledge erred; e.g. forcible conversion.)",[]],"refl:2790":["(s Rational) belief (as not a source of knowledge) is a hypothesis, in so far as it is held to be necessary for a practical purpose. There is therefore a pragmatic and a moral [Ver] belief. The latter is pure rational belief; it contributes nothing to knowledge.",[]],"refl:2791":["Belief [in] in the truth of a proposition or the actuality of a thing is what takes the place of a mode of knowledge, without itself being knowledge. Reason too is capable of a belief.",[]],"refl:2792":["*Fides* is properly good faith in a *pactum*, or (g subjectively, confidence) in one another that one will keep his promise to the other: *Treu und Glauben* [good faith and trust]. The 1st when the *pactum* has been made; the 2nd when it is to be concluded. By analogy, practical reason is as it were the *promittens*, man the *promissarius*, and the good expected from the deed the *promissum*.",[]],"refl:2793":["Belief is no special source of knowledge. It is a kind of holding-to-be-true that is consciously incomplete. When it is considered as [restrin] restricted to (g as to) special (g kinds of) objects (which belong only to belief), it is distinguished [from opinion] from opinion not by degree but by the relation that it, as knowledge, has to action. Thus, for example, a merchant, in order to enter upon a transaction, needs not merely to opine that there will be something to gain by it, but to believe it, that is, his opinion must be sufficient for an undertaking at a venture. Now we have modes of theoretical knowledge (of the sensible) in which we can attain certainty; and in regard to everything that we can call human knowledge, the latter must be possible. Just such certain knowledge, and indeed (g wholly) *a priori*, we have in practical laws; but these are grounded on a supersensible principle (of freedom), and indeed in ourselves, as a principle of practical reason. This practical reason, however, is a causality in regard to [the] a likewise supersensible object, the highest good, which is not possible in the world of sense (g through our power). Nevertheless nature, as object of our theoretical reason, must harmonise with it, for the consequence (g effect) of this idea is to be met with in |\nthe world of sense. We ought therefore to act so as to make this end actual. We find in the world of sense traces of an artistic wisdom too, and so we believe that the cause of the world works also with moral wisdom towards the highest good. This is a holding-to-be-true that suffices for action, that is, a belief. Now we do not need this [belief] for acting according to moral laws, for these are given by practical reason alone. But we do need the assumption of a highest wisdom for the object of our moral will, towards which, beyond the mere rightfulness of our actions, we cannot avoid directing our ends. Even though this would not objectively be a necessary relation of our will [Willkühr], the highest good is [unavoidably] subjectively necessarily the object of a good (even human) will, and belief in its attainability is necessarily presupposed for it.\nBetween the acquisition of a mode of knowledge through experience (*a posteriori*) and through reason (*a priori*) there is no middle. But between the knowledge of an object and the mere presupposition of its possibility there is a middle, namely an empirical or rational ground for assuming the latter, in relation to a necessary extension [of our] of the field of possible objects beyond those of which knowledge is possible for us. This necessity holds only in regard to that in which the object is known (g as) practical and (g practically) necessary through reason; for to assume something merely for the sake of extending theoretical knowledge is always contingent. This practically necessary presupposition of an object is that of the possibility of the highest good as object of the will [Willkühr], and therefore also of the condition of this possibility (God, [and] freedom and immortality). This is a subjective [Mogli] necessity of assuming the reality of the object for the sake of the necessary determination of the will. This is the *casus extraordinarius* without which practical reason cannot maintain itself in regard to its necessary end; and here *favor necessitatis* [the favour of necessity] comes to its aid in its own judgment. It cannot acquire any object logically, but can only resist everything that hinders it in the use of this idea, which belongs to it practically.\nThis [Nothwend] belief is the necessity of assuming the objective reality of a concept (g of the highest good), that is, the possibility of its object as an (g *a priori* necessary) object of the will [Willkühr]. If we look merely to actions, we have no need of this belief. But if through actions we would [ourselves] extend ourselves to the possession of the end thereby possible [acquire], we must assume [it] that this [Mogli] is thoroughly possible. I can therefore only say: I see myself constrained by my end, according to laws of freedom, to assume a highest good in the world as possible; but I cannot constrain anyone else by grounds (belief is free).",[]],"refl:2794":["Rational belief can never be directed to theoretical knowledge, for there the objectively insufficient holding-to-be-true is mere opinion. It is merely a presupposition of reason for a subjective but absolutely necessary practical purpose. The disposition [of] according to moral laws leads to an object of the will [Willkühr] determinable through pure reason. The assumption of the feasibility of this object, and therefore also of the actuality of the cause of it, is a moral belief, which is a free holding-to-be-true, necessary for the moral purpose of the completion of one's ends.",[]],"refl:2795":["Speculative knowledge might be opposed to pragmatic, and theoretical to practical.\nThat is pragmatic which moves the will (g and so is directly an incentive) in conformity with a rule, and so produces action.\n(s The speculative is objectively practical.\nThat opposed to it is subjectively practical.)\n(s Admonitions teach only what the other already knows, namely that vice is evil. What is lacking is the incentive that actually drives him to abstain from it. Mere eulogies and sarcasms do not supply it.)",[]],"refl:2796":["A mode of knowledge is theoretical in so far as it relates [immediately] to the object through the understanding (represents it as it is).\nA mode of knowledge is practical in so far as it relates to the object through the use of freedom (contains the action whereby an object becomes actual).\nIdle speculation is that which contains in itself no ground of conduct. Its opposite is *potentialiter* practical. E.g. God is just: [this] is theoretical, but *potentialiter* practical.\n(s A mode of knowledge is practical 1. in regard to the means,\n2. in regard to the ends,\nand to the incentives.\nDistinction of tautological solutions from real ones.)",[]],"refl:2797":["Theoretical modes of knowledge are those that determine the understanding to judgment;\npractical modes of knowledge, those that determine the will to action.\nNature and freedom.",[]],"refl:2798":["Practical propositions (s commands) either are 1) imperatives, and then they are opposed to theoretical ones; or they contain 2) the grounds of possible imperatives, and then they are opposed to speculative ones.\nEverything in the end comes down to the practical. This, and not utility, is the final purpose.",[]],"refl:2799":["In logic we may well speak of practical knowledge in so far as it is distinguished from theoretical, but not in so far as it is distinguished from merely speculative knowledge. It is, however, a precarious matter to determine what is mere speculation.",[]],"refl:2800":["Logical, aesthetic or practical perfection of knowledge; the two latter do not belong to logic.",[]],"refl:2801":["theoretical: what is;\npractical: what ought to happen;\npragmatic: what contains the incentives to actions.\nTheoretical propositions are opposed to practical,\nspeculative propositions to pragmatic.\nMany theoretical propositions (e.g. the world is supremely perfect) are [objectively] pragmatic;\nmany practical propositions are nevertheless not pragmatic, but belong to speculation.",[]],"refl:2802":["Knowledge is (g propositions are) either practical or theoretical.\nThe use of knowledge either [theoretical] practical or speculative.",[]],"refl:2803":["Philosophy (g namely the part that teaches the material use of reason) has also [in] a merely speculative part (s *discursiva*). This is the metaphysics of nature and of morals. The second, namely physics, might be entitled the exhibiting part: *philosophia exhibitiva*.",[]],"refl:2804":["merely practical sermons.",[]],"refl:2805":["Utility lies either in the understanding or in the governing of the will.",[]],"refl:2806":["He who practises religion must know that there is a God.\nFurther, L 63', on »in — liegt« (ll. 25f.):\nThe doctrine of monads not.\nFurther, L 63, beside »erhalten — Menschen« (ll. 26f.):\npractical geometry.",[]],"refl:2807":["Nature of freedom in regard to duties.",[]],"refl:2808":["Of man.\nBeside L §. 226 »cognitio — practica«:\nComparative.",[]],"refl:2809":["how a mathematical line arises.\nL 64'. On L §. 227 no. 2:\nWhether *Virgilius* or *Vergilius* should be read.",[]],"refl:2810":["To a philosopher the knowledge of monads (g human souls) is practical, but not to a *medicus* [physician].\nOn L §. 228, sentence 2:\nA theologian may, from ignorance, take metaphysics to be speculative.",[]],"refl:2811":["At least in regard to the understanding.\nOn L §. 229 no. 2:\nFor one to whom it does not belong within the compass of his science; e.g. philosophy for many a preacher.\nOn L §. 229 no. 3:\nHence it is good that all men pursue all things. Even if something is not useful to the state, it can still be of use to men.",[]],"refl:2812":["This is to be granted of the science of duties, of logic, of religion.",[]],"refl:2813":["In his principal science; e.g. mathematics for a philosopher, philosophy and history for a theologian.\nOn L §. 231 no. 2 »that part of learning«:\nWhich is more practical in view of his abilities, in which he can accomplish more.",[]],"refl:2814":["An object is sometimes practical, but the *modus cognoscendi* [mode of knowing] speculative.\nThe former can produce good consequences; the latter produces them.\nThe former is an abundance of water at a mill, or a treasure in a miser's keeping.\nLiving knowledge carries with it motives to action;\nrational [knowledge], rational [motives]; aesthetic, sensible ones.\nWhen someone [has recognised the nothingness of the world and] abhors avarice from the ugly idea that the sight of other misers has caused in him.\nThe tumult of the desires is opposed to the motives of reason.",[]],"refl:2815":["The Pharisees spoke of holiness and lived unholily.\nThe judge knows what is right, and yet does wrong.\nWhen someone has weighed the purpose of his existence and the marks of true happiness, and acts accordingly, his knowledge has a rational life.",[]],"refl:2816":["The doctrine of the Jesuits.\nOn L §. 234 no. 3:\nWith many scholars.",[]],"refl:2817":["otherwise only in one possible way.",[]],"refl:2818":["Effect on the faculty of knowledge.\nL 66'. On L §. 236 »cognitio symbolica«:\nIf someone hears of equity (g unseemly) and inequity and is for that reason to avoid vices, his knowledge is merely symbolic.\nFurther:\nWe have merely [intuitive] symbolic knowledge of the motives to piety that are derived from happiness.",[]],"refl:2819":["Everything that is an object of our faculty of desire gives pleasure.\nOn §. 237 »taedium« (? or on »indifferentia«?):\nE.g. when one [ge] hears a frosty sermon.\nL 66. On »rationally move — arouse displeasure« (l. 26):\nOn the faculty of desire.\nOn L §. 237 no. 2a:\nMere pleasure in the subjective perfection of knowledge is the reason why it does not become living.\nFurther, L 66':\nThe (g formal) perfection of knowledge always gives pleasure.\ne.g. of the doctrine of virtue.\nIf the manner of the knowledge contains no motive for knowing it (g in the most perfect way), it is dead.",[]],"refl:2820":["The material perfection of the object must give pleasure.\nOpposite L §. 238 »beides — entweder« (l. 18):\nIf we say: God commands this to be done, and we do not discern the pleasure that arises from obeying these commands, it is dead.\nOn the concluding sentence of L §. 238:\nSometimes the object is indifferent, but the knowledge (g form) gives pleasure. [e.g. To discern the cause of the *phaenomena naturae*] So it is with the moralists.",[]],"refl:2821":["As actual and as ours.\nThe perfection of the world does not move us enough.",[]],"refl:2822":["This produces confidence in oneself.\nTaming of the affects.\nHappiness in a freethinker.",[]],"refl:2823":["Otherwise it does not drive to action.\nL 67'. On L §. 241 no. 2:\nIf the passions still resist, the knowledge is not living enough.\nTo win over the passions.",[]],"refl:2824":["So it is in moral philosophy [*Sittenlehre*], and in the knowledge of the utility of the sciences.\nFurther, L 67' from β1−ε2:\nWhence is it that the knowledge of our duties towards God is not (g living? because it is not) sufficiently intuitive?",[]],"refl:2825":["Both the formal and the material perfection of the same.\nL 68'. On L § 243 »*vita cognitionis eruditae spuria*« [spurious life of learned knowledge]:\nFor example, a moral teacher who defends vices merely by sensuous grounds.",[]],"refl:2826":["Many a one judges in this way in respect of learning. Small motives [*bewegende Gründe*] where there is equilibrium.\nOn L § 245 No. 2:\nAccording to the character of the capacity, of the insights and of the affects. An ambitious man. A voluptuary. A miser.",[]],"refl:2827":["Only that [knowledge] must be living, and then only when it is meant to move [us].",[]],"refl:2828":["For example, the loss of the Romans at Cannae.",[]],"refl:2829":["*repraesentatio cum conscientia* [representation with consciousness].\nOn L § 249, end:\n*tres operationes mentis* [the three operations of the mind].\nFurther, L 69 from β1−ψ:\nnot all.\nβ1. L 69 in the right margin beside L § 249:\nPrincipal concept. Subsidiary concept.\n*complexus et incomplexus* [complex and incomplex].",[]],"refl:2830":["distinct, complete, exhaustive, determinate.\nOn L § 250 »beautiful concept« (l. 25):\nof eternity.\nL 69. On L § 250 »*conceptus pulcher*« (l. 26):\n*Complexus*.",[]],"refl:2831":["God almighty.\nL 69. On L § 251 »magnitude«:\nImportant, fruitful.\nL 69'. Opposite L § 251 »der Gewissheit — sein«, doubtless referring to the underlined word »practical«:\ngenetic.",[]],"refl:2832":["*abstrahendo* [by abstracting].",[]],"refl:2833":["Seeks useless methods.",[]],"refl:2834":["A concept is a reflected representation.\nThe matter of all concepts is the object;\nthe form of all concepts is universality.\nIn the latter there is abstraction from that whereby the manifold is distinguished.\nThe particular logical form is clearness, distinctness.\nSensation Intuition\nConcept\n}\nsubjective.\nobjective.\nForm is either intuition\nor reflection.",[]],"refl:2835":["*repraesentationes*\n{ s\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\n(g with consciousness) *perceptiones* of that which is given, [*perceptio*] *repraesentatio a posteriori*\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n(s *intuitus*) *conceptus** (s *forma logica*)\n{s\n{\n{\n*repraesentatio a priori: vel notio vel idea*\n}\n}\n}\n*notiones ideae* (s *communis*) (s *singularis*)\n〈\n(s *vel* [*per*] *intuitivus*)\n(s *vel* [*discursivus*] *cogitativus*)\n*(s *cognitio per conceptus est cogitatio, praesertim per conceptus a priori* [knowledge through concepts is thought, especially through concepts *a priori*].)\nThe idea is an archetypal concept [*conceptus archetypus*]; it contains the ground of the possibility of the object. It is the [deriv] representation of the whole through whose limitation others arise. It is a single [idea] (of the object); everything diverse is merely the limitation of it: e.g. the *ens realissimum* is the transcendental idea. It can never be thought *in concreto*, but precedes all judging *in concreto*. E.g. the idea of justice is the model idea for judging an Aristides. Order, unity and perfection [can be] are possible only in accordance with an idea, and therefore so also is the world which is to contain them. [The question arises] whether contingent things do not always presuppose an idea as their *principium*, which determines them among all that is possible. God, as *ens realissimum*, is the subject in which all ideas inhere, and is also the first cause of everything contingent.\nMany sciences are expounded without first establishing the objective idea: morals, metaphysics.\nTo draw the parts from the whole.\nThe division of species is with us merely nominal, and arises from comparison; but it would be real if the idea were known to us. For example, of a horse, and then of what is altered in it according to the diversity of circumstances. There must, however, be unity in the idea, namely in that which contains everything in a certain kind of relations or things.\nIn all sciences, especially those of reason, the idea of the science — its general sketch, its outline within the whole extent of all modes of knowledge, and so the whole of it — is the first thing to be sought. This is architectonic. The idea of humanity, of the |\nrepublic. The idea of jurisprudence. That of a happy life is lacking in most men.\nThe idea is opposed to empirical concepts. A perfect republic is a mere idea. Many men have no idea of what they want; they therefore proceed according to instincts and appearance [*Anschein*].\nAn idea cannot be obtained through composition. Here the whole is prior to the part. So with the idea of the best world, in which things are determined. This idea is a whole which, through limitation, yields the parts; how that is possible is not to be comprehended.\nGenius has the idea before the partial concepts. An imitative mind never attains to ideas. In philosophy everything depends on the idea.",[]],"refl:2836":["69.\nL 69':\n1. *repraesentatio*.\n2. *perceptio* (with consciousness).\n3. *cognitio** (relation, with consciousness, to an object) (s *perceptio obiective spectata* [perception regarded objectively]).\n(* s A perception which is related merely to the subject, as a state of it, is entitled sensation; one which is related to the object: knowledge.)\n4. *cognitio est vel intuitus vel conceptus* (g *repraesentatio discursiva*). In the former I am passive (receptivity), in the second active (spontaneity). The *intuitus* is single; the *conceptus* is *repraesentatio per notam communem* [representation through a common mark]. The understanding is here the formal cause of concepts.\n5. *Notio* (*conceptus*** *intellectualis*): when the concept arises from the understanding in respect of its content as well.\n6. *Idea*: a concept of reason, (g *cui nullus respondet intuitus* [to which no intuition corresponds],) (s which can have no object in experience,) when the concept of the understanding can have *in concreto* no object of experience, and contains the archetype of the employment of the understanding, e.g. the world-whole. The idea (s can therefore serve only for regulative (theoretical or practical), not for constitutive employment) must be (g subjectively) necessary (g to reason), not for the empirical employment of the understanding, but as the *principium* of the thoroughgoing connection of our empirical employment of the understanding.\nAn idea is a necessary fundamental concept, [required] either to complete objectively the act of subordination performed by the understanding, or to regard it as unlimited.\n**(g *conceptus est vel empiricus vel intellectualis*.)\n( s *Conceptus* (s *intuitus etiam*) *est vel empiricus* (s empirical knowledge is entitled experience) *vel purus, hic vel intellectualis* (g *notio*) *vel rationalis* (g *idea*). L 69: (s is a concept of reason which is subjectively necessary to reason for its completion, but only in the intellectual sphere. For in the sensible there is no completion.) L 69': a. *Conceptus est cogitatio*. b. *Exhibitio cogitati est relatio conceptus ad intuitum* [the exhibition of what is thought is the relation of the concept to intuition]. c. *Exhibitio a priori: constructio* (s objective reality of the concept through its presentation). )",[]],"refl:2837":["A notion is a concept which is not abstracted from experience; an idea\nis a notion whose object can nowhere be met with in experience. Ideas, therefore, can never lead to actual objects, since these must all be contained in a possible experience. But they serve [to guide the understanding], [the] through reason, in respect of experience and of the employment of its rules, towards the greatest perfection; or else even to show that [experience] not all possible things are objects of experience, and that the principles of the possibility of experience hold neither of things in themselves nor of the objects of experience regarded as things in themselves.",[]],"refl:2838":["Previously knowledge in general was treated, as a propaedeutic to logic; now logic itself follows.",[]],"refl:2839":["The concept is here considered only subjectively*: not as it determines an object through a mark, but [as it] can be related to several objects.\n*(g Not how they arise as representations, but how given representations become concepts in thinking. Only the form of a concept.)",[]],"refl:2840":["There are ideas to which an approximation is possible, namely [those] of the mathematical generation of a whole; and ideas which are wholly heterogeneous [from] with all concrete concepts, because the whole differs from these not in |\nmagnitude but in kind. Such are the dynamical ideas. They are nevertheless limiting concepts [*Grenzbegriffe*] of the series of concrete concepts: e.g. freedom, [the power] of beginning a series absolutely.",[]],"refl:2841":["Thinking is *repraesentare per conceptus* [to represent through concepts]: *cognitio discursiva*.",[]],"refl:2842":["No theoretical idea can [immediately] be given objective reality, nor can such reality be proved, save only that of freedom; and this because freedom is the condition of the moral law, whose reality is an axiom. The reality of the idea of God can be proved only through this [idea of freedom], and therefore only with a practical aim, that is, [as warranting us] to act as if there were a God; it is thus proved for this aim only.",[]],"refl:2843":["The concept of things present is entitled sensation. The faculty is: the senses,\nouter or inner. Outer: through different senses.\nOn L § 255, final sentence:\nImmediate: a clap of thunder. Mediate: that it takes place in the clouds. Of the faculty of thinking.",[]],"refl:2844":["We do not experience an event that is narrated to us.\nBefore »only affirmative« (l. 36) Kant places a 2 and remarks on it on L 70':\nWe sense only what is present. For example, that the wine is not sweet is no immediate experience.\nAll finite things exist in a changeable manner, e.g. thinking. Hence the faculty of thinking is not immediately experienced, nor in general are any necessary properties.\nNot the negative [experiences]. For example, the wine is bitter, therefore it is not sweet.",[]],"refl:2845":["*analysis*: thereby it becomes distinct.\nL 70'. On L § 257 No. 1 »weapons of the senses«:\nThese have disclosed to us a new world in the small and in the great.\nOn L § 257 No. 2:\nWhen man is dissected by anatomy, plants by chemistry.\nOn L § 257 No. 3:\nWhen, in a thing that is coming into being, the inner and outer connections are observed.\nWhen one imitates an earthquake.\nL 71. On L § 257 No. 4 »best marks«:\nconstant marks. For example, when I observe a man in the motives of ambition, of voluptuousness.",[]],"refl:2846":["Objects are the causes of representations; the latter, therefore, are in conformity with the former.",[]],"refl:2847":["All concepts are universal, [being concepts] either of abstraction [*Absonderung*] or of fiction.\n[The] The former have arisen either *a priori* or *a posteriori*.",[]],"refl:2848":["A [abstract] universal concept *in concreto*, in the case of an object of the senses.\nThat *in abstracto*: a concept of reason.",[]],"refl:2849":["All given modes of knowledge *a posteriori* are given as sensations and appearances, or as concepts (g *conceptus*), that is, universal representations which have arisen through abstraction; these are entitled *conceptus*, and are copies, *ectypae*. The given modes of knowledge *a priori*: *notiones*.",[]],"refl:2850":["All concepts are either empirical, or self-subsistent concepts of reason, or inferred concepts. The last are the moral concepts, which are neither abstracted from experience nor contain the mere laws of pure reason, but [contain] something which is thought in relation to the universal law of the will.",[]],"refl:2851":["The origin of concepts is considered in metaphysics, and is either empirical, or arbitrary, or intellectual. But the form of the concept is logical, and consists in the reflection whereby a *conceptus communis* comes about, this being the form required for the faculty of judgment.\nIn concepts, therefore, only the difference of reflection is considered.",[]],"refl:2852":["*Conceptus vel dati vel factitii*;\n*dati vel a priori vel a posteriori*\nintellectual\nempirical concepts.",[]],"refl:2853":["Concepts are either given or made; the former *vel a posteriori vel a priori*. The latter either [arbitrary or natural] (s concepts inferred by reason: ideas; or arbitrarily invented).",[]],"refl:2854":["Logical acts in the concept:\nfirst, the representation [of] of a *nota* as *communis*: *comparatio*;\nsecondly, this [mark] as ground of knowledge of a thing: *reflexio*;\nthirdly, abstraction from that in which the thing differs from other things.",[]],"refl:2855":["All concepts are, in respect of their matter, *dati* or *factitii* (g e.g. ®). The first are given either *a priori* or empirically.\nThe form of a concept, as a discursive representation, is always made.\n*Dati, a priori*, are *notiones*.",[]],"refl:2856":["The logical question is not how we arrive at concepts, but what acts of the understanding constitute a concept, whether it contain something derived from experience, or something invented, or something borrowed from the nature of the understanding.",[]],"refl:2857":["The (g transcendental) notions represent not things, but the acts [*Actus*] of the understanding whereby it makes for itself synthetic concepts of things.",[]],"refl:2858":["Empirical concepts are not, on that account, concepts drawn from experience; they are concepts which in part first make experience possible. A concept of experience [*Erfahrungsbegrif*] already presupposes empirical concepts; and [ih] »to abstract it« means: to separate out what one has previously thought.",[]],"refl:2859":["The origin of concepts, in respect of their mere form, is logical, and rests on abstraction from the difference of the things designated by a certain representation; in respect of their matter they are *dati* or *factitii*.",[]],"refl:2860":["*conceptus qvoad formam orti reflexione, comparatione, abstractione* [concepts, as regards their form, arise through reflection, comparison, abstraction].",[]],"refl:2861":["Empirical concept: 1. of sensation; 2. of knowledge. The latter is entitled a concept of experience [*Erfahrungsbegrif*], for experience already presupposes concepts of the laws (of nature).",[]],"refl:2862":["Common abstraction. Dog.\nOn »machen« (549 23) from γ−ρ:\n*est conceptus datus, non factitius* [it is a given, not a made, concept].\nFurther, from ρ2−ψ:\nThrough comparison and abstraction.\nOn L § 259, beginning, from λ−ρ:\n*cognitio discursiva per conceptus*.\n*in Mathesi simul est intuitiva* [in mathematics it is at the same time intuitive].",[]],"refl:2863":["What is entirely and wholly diverse cannot be known through abstraction.\nLikewise man and man.\nOn L § 259 Nos. 2-4:\n2. In order to arrive at distinct concepts. 3. Through attention. 4. So separated, they exist only in thought.",[]],"refl:2864":["Where many things agree in a common concept.\nOn L § 260 »single concepts«:\nGod is first an abstracted concept, finally a single one.\nNot through experience.\nVirtue.\nFurther, from ω? (φ?):\nare already concepts which are regarded not in themselves, but as in someone.\nβ1. On L § 260 »contained in it« (551 30):\nVirtue in chastity.\nL 72'. β1. On the beginning of the last sentence of L § 260:\nBecause many agree in it, as: machine. Ocean.",[]],"refl:2865":["Concepts do not arise through abstraction, but through reflection: either, when the concept is given, only the form [or through reflex], and it is then entitled reflected; or the concept itself [arises through reflection], and it is then entitled reflecting.\nComparison and abstraction do not produce concepts, but only their logical form.",[]],"refl:2866":["*Conceptus communis* (a tautology).",[]],"refl:2867":["To think something *in abstracto* or *in concreto* through a concept (g universal representation). No concept is produced through abstraction. All concepts are either given or made, invented (constructed). |\nThe given are given either *a priori* (pure concepts of the understanding) or *a posteriori* (empirical).",[]],"refl:2868":["*Perceptio communis est reflexa*.\n*Conceptus communis est abstractus, si non spectatur in suo inferiori; est concretus, si spectatur* [a common concept is abstract if it is not regarded in what falls under it; concrete if it is].",[]],"refl:2869":["not *aliqvid abstrahere* [to abstract something], but *ab aliqvo abstrahere* [to abstract from something]; [I] it is negative.",[]],"refl:2870":["The most abstract concept is that which has nothing in common with anything different from it, that is, the concept of something; for what is different from it is nothing, and has nothing in common with the something.",[]],"refl:2871":["The (g logical) form of the understanding consists in the logical subordination of the *conceptus communes*; abstraction is the condition under which *conceptus communes* can come about. *abstrahe ab aliis* [abstract from the rest].",[]],"refl:2872":["All concepts have an extent of application and limits of content: the former in respect of what many have in agreement; the latter in respect of what is different in them.",[]],"refl:2873":["*Vsus conceptuum est vel in abstracto vel concreto, et hic vel in pluribus communi vel singulari* [the use of concepts is either *in abstracto* or *in concreto*, and the latter either as common to several or as singular].\n(s Through the concept I think an object *in abstracto* or *in concreto*.)",[]],"refl:2874":["To represent something through a concept either *in abstracto* or *in concreto*.",[]],"refl:2875":["With every *conceptus communis* comparisons must indeed be made, otherwise it would not be a *conceptus communis*; but it |\nneed not first be formed from these compared representations.",[]],"refl:2876":["71.\nL 71':\n(g 1. attention: relation to consciousness.)\nLogical origin of concepts: 1. through comparison: [how in one consciousness] how they stand to one another in one consciousness.\nL 71:\n(g Comparison with one another.)\nL 71':\n2. through reflection (g with the same consciousness): [how they stand towards one another in one consciousness as identical or not] how diverse [representations] can be comprehended in one consciousness.\nL 71:\n(g whether one can have a concept as *repraesentatio communis* without comparison with others, and even prior to such comparison?)\nL 71':\n3. through abstraction: [through] in that one leaves out that in which they differ.\n    ( s 1. *apprehensio variorum* (g apprehension [*Auffassung*]). (g *comparatio* with the object, for knowledge.) 2. *reflexio*: consideration of the connection, for the unity of the concept. 3. *abstractio* from the rest. — Comparison with something else is not always necessary in order to obtain a universal concept; what is needed is consciousness of the possibility of the representation in a variety of ways. )",[]],"refl:2877":["(g *repraesentatio communis*)\nThe *conceptus* is opposed to the *intuitus*: [it is] the representation of what is common to several objects, and therefore [a representation] in so far as it can be contained in [many] different [objects].",[]],"refl:2878":["To reflect is to become conscious of representations successively, that is, to hold them together with one consciousness. To compare is to compare them with one another, that is, to hold them together with the unity of consciousness. Question: whether we can separate out something from a single intuition, without comparison, so as to subordinate under it more things, should such be found*.\n*(g We can become conscious solely of the act of imagination, that is, of the combination either of representations with one another or with our |\nsense, without regard to what is combined and its peculiarity: e.g. house. But the concept becomes clear only through application in comparison.)",[]],"refl:2879":["We do not abstract the mark of agreement; we abstract from the difference.",[]],"refl:2880":["We compare only the universal [element] in the rule of our apprehension. For example, one sees a shrub, and can thereupon represent to oneself a tree; an oblong gives occasion for the square. The unicorn is a horse to which the horn has been added, taken from other animals.",[]],"refl:2881":["In a concept there is:\n1. the universal validity of a representation as a mark;\n2. abstraction from the diversity of the objects in which this mark is found.\nUniversality does not rest on the concept's being a partial concept, but on its being a ground of knowledge — by this shall I know thee.",[]],"refl:2882":["*Abstractio*. We begin, indeed, to abstract by way of comparison; but when we are practised we become conscious that [when] we can think a representation as undetermined in respect of several [things].",[]],"refl:2883":["71.\nL 71':\n1. *abstractio* (g *in*) *data nota ab aliis* [abstraction, in a given mark, from the rest].\n2. *Communio plurium in hac nota sive communicatio obiectiva* [the community of several in this mark, or objective communication]. This common validity does indeed presuppose a comparison, not however of perceptions, but of our apprehension, |\nL 71: |\nin so far as it already contains the presentation of a still undetermined concept, and is in itself universal.",[]],"refl:2884":["Hence what we prove of a [triangle] right-angled triangle, and that from the property of being right-angled, we prove of all, although in the single instance. The partial concept (g can) be represented as a ground of knowledge *a priori*, prior to all comparison, by reason of the faculty of productive imagination.",[]],"refl:2885":["Not for the sake of importance, but of the extent which it comprehends under itself.\nOn L § 261 »lower concept«:\nA higher concept can *diverso respectu* [in a different respect] also be entitled lower.\nOn L § 261 sentence 2:\ne.g. the organic body of man in respect of spirits.\nChristian virtue.\nOn L § 261 »An abstracted concept« (l. 33):\nAbstraction is subtraction.\nOn L § 261, final sentence:\nHow I arrive at the concept of virtue.",[]],"refl:2886":["When they comprehend *heterogenea* under themselves.\nOn L § 262 »*conceptus latior*« (561 26):\nVirtue. Liberality.\nFurther φ? ω?:\nA concept is not wider than another because it contains more under itself, e.g. [the concepts] of man and of metal, for that cannot be known; but when it contains the other concept and, besides that, still more under itself.\nβ1. On L § 262 »reciprocal concepts«:\ncontingent, finite. Extension, body.\nOn L § 262 »*conceptus universalis*«.\nAll men are rational beings.\nOn L § 262 »*conceptus particularis*«:\nNot all men are learned.\nL 73'. On L § 262, third sentence from the end:\nMan.\nOn L § 262 »universal in a certain respect« (l. 34f.):\nin respect of those to whom it belongs. E.g. all Moors are men.",[]],"refl:2887":["*Qvicqvid repugnat Generi, illud et speciei et individuo* [whatever conflicts with the genus conflicts also with the species and the individual].\nOn L § 263 No. 2:\nWhat does not belong to men does not belong to angels either.\nI can indeed say: men are not omniscient, and angels are not either.\nFurther, L 73:\nbecause it does not contradict what they have in common with one another.\nL 73. On L § 263 No. 3:\nThrough all the *inferiora* one arrives at the *superius*. Thereby the *universalia* arise.",[]],"refl:2888":["Abstraction is an aid to attention\nSymbolic.\nOn L § 264 No. 2:\nextensive magnitude.\nL 73'. On L § 264 No. 3 (? No. 4??):\nBecause that in which things agree flows from their universal properties, and that in which they differ from their particular properties.\nL 73. On L § 264 No. 4:\nIt can be applied to all particular cases; but not the particular.",[]],"refl:2889":["A universal concept, if it is abstracted from nothing but true concepts, is true.",[]],"refl:2890":["The [universality] subordination of concepts arises either analytically through abstraction or synthetically through fiction.",[]],"refl:2891":["*Conceptus communis*.",[]],"refl:2892":["(g *repraesentatio*) *Intuitiva et discursiva*.\n*Prior singularis. posterior communis*.\n(*abstracta et sphaera*.)\n*tanqvam nota continetur in aliis et haec sub se continet* [as a mark it is contained in others, and contains these under itself].",[]],"refl:2893":["Genus and species are distinguished not by their nature, but in respect of the *terminus a qvo* or *ad qvem* in logical subordination: *conceptuum communium superior respectu sui inferioris* (s *conceptus*) *est genus, respectu superioris species. hinc species infima sub se continet individua* (g [*nihil*] *non* [*est*] *concipi potest ut genus*); [*sum*] *genus summum non est species. Terminus a priori* (g *conceptus summus*) *est conceptus, in qvo ab omnibus entis discriminibus abstraxi, ideoqve in qvo qvam minimum continetur. terminus a posteriori: sub qvo qvam minimum continetur, nempe conceptus unicus. Primus paucissimum in se et plurimum sub se continet. Quantum cognitio ab una parte lucri facit, tantum ab altera mulctatur.* [Of common concepts, the higher in respect of its lower is the genus, in respect of its higher the species. Hence the lowest species contains individuals under itself (cannot be conceived as a genus); the highest genus is not a species. The first term (the highest concept) is the concept in which I have abstracted from all the differences of being, and in which, therefore, as little as possible is contained. The last term: that under which as little as possible is contained, namely a unique concept. The first contains very little in itself and very much under itself. As much as knowledge gains on the one side, so much is it mulcted on the other.]\n(s *species qvae non est genus, est infima non datur species infima propter legem continui,* [*nec*] *nec datur species proxima sive immediate subordinata.* [A species which is not a genus is the lowest; there is no lowest species, on account of the law of continuity, nor is there a nearest or immediately subordinate species.])",[]],"refl:2894":["*Sphaera notionum* [*max*] *infinita et evanescens*.\n*Conceptus superior continetur in inferiori et hic sub superiori;* [*ergo*] *hinc dictum de omni et nullo.*\n*Conceptus summus paucissima in se continet, infimus plurima, nempe omnimodam determinationem. Qvo superior conceptus: ab eo pluribus abstraxi; qvo inferior: eo magis determinavi. igitur summus est maxime abstractus, infimus sive singularis maxime concretus.* (g *notio* from νουσ: *mens*.)\n[The sphere of notions is infinite and vanishing. The higher concept is contained in the lower, and the lower under the higher; hence the *dictum de omni et nullo*. The highest concept contains in itself the fewest [marks], the lowest the most, namely complete determination. The higher the concept, the more I have abstracted from; the lower, the more I have determined. The highest, therefore, is the most abstract, the lowest or singular the most concrete.]",[]],"refl:2895":["On the fourth sentence from the end of L § 262 (561 29-32):\nEvery *conceptus* can be used universally and particularly. The *inferior* is used universally in respect of its *superior*, the *superior* particularly in respect of its *inferior*.",[]],"refl:2896":["The lower concept is not contained in the higher, for it contains more in itself; but it is nevertheless contained under the higher, because the higher contains the ground of knowledge of the lower.\nThe *nota* contains the things under itself, and the things contain the *nota* in themselves.",[]],"refl:2897":["Every representation is either *superior* or *inferior*. A con|cept\nis always a *repraesentatio superior*. But concepts are entitled *superiores* only in so far as they have other concepts under them.",[]],"refl:2898":["A mark of a mark — a remote mark — is a higher concept; the concept in relation to a remote mark is a lower one.",[]],"refl:2899":["Parallel between the *ens summum*, which contains all things under itself as real ground, and the *conceptus summus*, which contains them all logically under itself.",[]],"refl:2900":["Popularity is [the art] of hitting the proportion, within the same knowledge, between representation *in abstracto* and *in concreto*, that is, between [thought] concepts and their presentation, whereby the maximum of knowledge is attained [both] in respect of extent as well as of content.",[]],"refl:2901":["*differentia numerica* (s Caius, Titius) and *specifica* (s — *generica* |\n(differing *toto genere*, a *materia peccans sui generis*, μεταβασισ εἰσ αλλο γενοσ [a passing over into another kind]).)\n*numero diversa possunt esse specie eadem* [things numerically diverse may be the same in species].",[]],"refl:2902":["Extent and content.\nEvery concept, as a partial concept (though not every mark need be such), is contained in the representation of things.\nAs ground of knowledge, that is, as marks, these things are contained under it.",[]],"refl:2903":["Carthage's thongs.",[]],"refl:2904":["The *conceptus inferior* is always the *conceptus communis* considered *in concreto*; the *conceptus infimus in individuo* has the smallest *sphaera*.",[]],"refl:2905":["1. A thing; a composite thing that can move. 2. This motion is either according to universal laws or according to particular ones. Here according to particular ones.\nL 74. On L § 266 »Dieser — nicht« (l. 29f.):\nOpaque body.\nAir.\nFurther, L 74' on the same words:\nIf I abstract the concept of virtue from Christian, civil and natural virtue, and afterwards add these again, I have no new concept. But another is new, e.g. unchangeable virtue.",[]],"refl:2906":["Universal medicine is an arbitrarily combined concept, i.e. the powder of immortality.\nOn L § 267 No. 1:\nImpregnable fortress. Model.\nL 74. Beside L § 267 »fahrung — entweder« (l. 26):\na wind-carriage.",[]],"refl:2907":["All arbitrary concepts are synthetic, and conversely.\nTo this synthesis belongs also separation [*Trennung*], which is distinct from abstraction [*Absonderung*]. The former is a negative synthesis: in it I deny a part of the whole; in the latter I merely do not think a part in the whole.\nAll concepts are either rational, or empirical, or feigned.\nThe last are either *arbitrariae* or *subrepticiae*.",[]],"refl:2908":["(g arise from reason)\nAll concepts are either given or made.\nThe first are given either through experience (g *empirice*) or through pure reason (g *rationales*, self-subsistent). In the first, what reason does is only [to confer] universality.\nInvented concepts are (g so) never in respect of their matter, but of their form.\nThe form is 1. the sensible, 2. the rational form. The latter cannot possibly be invented; but the given form can indeed be applied otherwise.\nThe sensible form consists in relations of space and time, some of which are given and cannot be invented.\nAll fiction is either voluntary or involuntary. The latter is either sensible or rational. To the former belong hypochondriac [fictions]. The second are either aesthetic or heuristic. The aesthetic are so either in respect of quality or of quantity. The ideal concerns quality, that is, the proportion of the determinations in the thing which is to please above all else. Fiction in respect of quantity can please only in description, not in sensation; that is, magnitude pleases in appearance, but not in the concept.\nFiction takes place either *combinando* or *separando*.\nSeparation is removal, not abstraction. He who abstracts invents nothing. In a teacher one abstracts from his language, in Socrates from his figure. Happy the man in whom, when one abstracts from his dress, rank and wealth, honour and respect still remain!",[]],"refl:2909":["Sensations. Intuitions and concepts.\nIn the first there is mere matter; in the second, the form of sensibility; in the third, the form of the understanding through abstraction (universality).\n*repraesentatio. intuitus. conceptus. notio. Idea.*\nThe pure concept of reason is that which represents what can in no way be contained in sensibility, and therefore [contains] no matter, and so nothing but relations.",[]],"refl:2910":["Concepts of experience are also made, since they determine and put together the object through perceptions which I arbitrarily pick up. But because the object itself is given, it can be analysed.",[]],"refl:2911":["One must begin with explanations [*Erklärungen*], even of the most general things.\nOn L § 268 »determinate concept«:\nIt must be distinct, exhaustive, determinate. E.g. »Man is a being that has reason« is not exhaustive; »[Man] is a being endowed with reason and an organic body (g that has affects and sin)« is not determinate.\nA triangle has 3 sides and 3 angles.\nFurther, L 74 from ψ−χ:\n*Conceptus rei complete determinatus*.\nFurther, from ψ−χ:\nA sufficiently distinct, measured concept.\nFurther, from φ−χ (? ρ−σ?):\n*conceptus rei adaeqvatus in minimis terminis* [an adequate concept of the thing in the fewest terms].\nFurther, from φ−χ:\n*Cognitio distincta conceptum adaeqvans*.\nFurther, from ψ−θ:\n[*Cognitio*] (g *Conceptus complete*) *distinctus* [*com*] [*plane*] [*complete*] (g *et praecise*) *determinatus*.",[]],"refl:2912":["or is over-complete. E.g. God is an infinite, almighty, etc.:\nOn L § 269 »non-exhaustive«:\nnon-exhaustive. God is that being which governs the world.",[]],"refl:2913":["The inner sufficiency of the marks is the ground of the inwardly sufficient distinctness, and is attained by the concept's being adequate to the |\nthing. Through this alone is outer sufficiency possible, and consequently *completudo*.\n(s Since the pure concept of reason and the arbitrary concept give the thing, and are not given by the thing, such a concept can be defined only analytically; it is capable of logical as well as of real *completudo*.\nDiscoveries *per analysin* can be made equally in respect of logical and of real *completudo*.)",[]],"refl:2914":["All empirical concepts, e.g. water, tree, etc., are representations of the understanding and can be treated only synthetically: I am not to analyse what [to them] is in them, but to learn through experience what belongs to them. Rational concepts, e.g. virtue, fate, etc., can be treated only analytically.",[]],"refl:2915":["Distinctness requires that the marks of the definition be co-ordinated with one another and not subordinated to one another; the latter, when it occurs, is contrary to precision.\nThere is in that case, namely, an implicit tautology [*tautologia implicita*] of the marks among themselves, just as in the *circulus definiendi* there is a tautology of the marks with the *definitum*.",[]],"refl:2916":["All definition is preceded by the materials for the definition; and the judgments about them are made, in empirical concepts, *per synthesin*, and in rational ones *per analysin*. These judgments are the *principia materialia definitionis*, and in them complete certainty can prevail; only |\none must attend to the form of distinctness, that is, [proceed] without tautology; likewise to the true synthesis, namely that one mark be not subordinated to another and yet regarded as co-ordinated (precision). As regards the form of the definition, it consists in exhaustiveness. This can never be proved, but only presumed through frequent use; and from this it is already evident that the definition is not what comes first.\nA real definition is that which contains the [in itself] ground (g *rationem essendi*) of what belongs to the thing; the logical: that which contains the ground (g *rationem cognoscendi*) of that which breaks off.\nLines 8-10 were for the most part struck out in phase ξ−φ (hardly not until ω), and by longer s-additions the following text was produced:\n(s A nominal definition is that which suffices for knowledge in comparison; a real definition: that which suffices for derivation. E.g. »The circle is a curved line all of whose equal parts coincide with one another« is nominal, for few properties can be derived from it. It is not sufficient for giving the object accordingly. Merely negative definitions (e.g. concepts of the understanding are those which are not sensible) contain. The explanation »God is the absolutely necessary being« is nominal in so far as I can know no properties from it save negative ones.)",[]],"refl:2917":["*in conceptibus empiricis non valent nisi descriptiones, quoniam semper augescunt notae* [in empirical concepts only descriptions are valid, since the marks are always increasing].\n*definitio realis et genetica differunt* [real and genetic definition differ].",[]],"refl:2918":["(g Logical) nominal definitions of given concepts of the understanding are taken from an attribute (g a consequence of the essence); real definitions from the essence, the first ground of possibility.\n(g Logical) nominal definitions of given empirical concepts contain what one always thinks in the concept upon a word.\nReal definitions: what always belongs to the thing. The former [concern] the logical, the latter the real essence.",[]],"refl:2919":["2917 and 2918:\nIn empirical modes of knowledge one derives nothing from the concept by which one designates a thing, but [everything] from experiences; in rational ones, from concepts. In the former, the exposition of the nominal concept serves only to mark out the [sign] object by a word.\nNominal explanations are arbitrary.\nTo expound the appearance or the concept which we have; the former is done *per* [*analysin*] *synthesin*. etc. etc.",[]],"refl:2920":["declaration.\nexposition (analytic*: either of the concept *a priori* or of its use).\ndefinition.**\nDefinition is either a precise declaration or an adequate exposition; the former takes place *in conceptibus factitiis*, the latter *in datis*. Exposition is either analytic, [being the exposition] of what (g I) already think in a universal concept (in the concept of the regular hexagon |\nI do not think the equality of its sides with the radii of the circle); or it is synthetic, when I add to it what belongs to it; the latter is empirical.\n*(g the sense of a given representation made distinct)\n**(g The determinate [interpretation of a] setting forth of a — — —)",[]],"refl:2921":["The *expositio* is either *completa* or not. The former *vel praecisa* or not. The latter is the description — — —",[]],"refl:2922":["*designatio* [a nominal explanation. e.g. of natural things]: the determination of an object for a word (g according to usage), e.g. water.\n(s *Explicatio* (s *conceptus incogniti sive termini*): interpretation [*Auslegung*], not translation [*Dollmetschung*]: *translatio*.)",[]],"refl:2923":["The distinct determination of a word (g of a given expression) is explication. This *explicatio est gratuita* when the word upon the properties the breaks off.",[]],"refl:2924":["declaration* (s announcement) is the meaning of a sign made clear.\nor the sense of an arbitrary representation made clear.\n*(g the arbitrary concept distinctly [made] set forth)",[]],"refl:2925":["Through declaration a distinct concept is made.\nThrough exposition a given concept is made distinct.\nThrough definition a distinct concept is made complete and precise.",[]],"refl:2926":["Definition of the understanding, or definition of reason. The former is nominal [diese] and presupposes the given concept. The second is real, and through it the concept is given *a priori*. The latter is either breaks off.",[]],"refl:2927":["A definition is to be regarded as an equation, or somewhat like ad/bc = 1. I resolve a confused concept into the clear marks which, taken together, are equal to it. Let the *definitum* be adbc: then the definition is a×d×b×c. Care is taken that there be neither too much nor too little in the numerator, so that the definition does not become *angustior* [narrower] or *latior* [wider]. Precision is *reduction ad minimos terminos* [reduction to the lowest terms].",[]],"refl:2928":["A definition is either of a given or of a made [*factitii*] concept. The former is either of a given thing or of a given notion. In the first case it is the definition of an empirical concept through *synthesis zetetica* [investigative synthesis]; in the second, through analysis of the given concept.",[]],"refl:2929":["The definition of empirical concepts must arise through empirical [Begriffe] synthesis.\nThat of given concepts of reason, through analysis.\nThat of arbitrary ones, through synthesis *a priori*.\nTo make a distinct concept, and to make a concept distinct.",[]],"refl:2930":["The mathematician says in his definition: *sic volo, sic iubeo* [thus I will, thus I command].",[]],"refl:2931":["Either I give to a word its concept (g determination of the word) in accordance with usage, or I give a word to the arbitrary concept and establish a usage. The first is *declaratio nominalis*, the second *declaratio realis*.\nI determine the meaning of a given expression (*per explicationem*), or I give a word to a determinate concept *per declarationem*.\nThe explication of an expression.\nThe exposition of a concept.\nExposition becomes definition through [prae] *completudo* [completeness] and precision.",[]],"refl:2932":["In the case of concepts of reason, exposition is analytic: it is of what is already actually (g *implicite*) thought in them, not of what merely can be thought. In the case of empirical concepts it is only synthetic; otherwise it is explication.",[]],"refl:2933":["In explication the meaning [oder der S] is determined, that is, the concept attached to a word, or the sense of a concept.",[]],"refl:2934":["The determination of concepts comprises: 1. For arbitrary concepts: denotation, where a universal concept comes first, under which something is ordered along with other things for division into classes, e.g. earths, or the blossoms of plants. 2. For given concepts: explication, e.g. water. Both are nominal: the former for identity, the latter for diversity; both serve to attach a signification to the expression.\nDeclaration and exposition are real.\nDescription is [deutliche Vorstellung] empirical distinctness.",[]],"refl:2935":["There are 1. empirically given concepts (body), 2. empirically made concepts (water).",[]],"refl:2936":["The definition of the empirical concept (s what I think through the concept of experience) arises *per analysin* and is always nominal. The definition of the object [kan], which is therefore meant to be real, must always arise *per synthesin*.\nThe definition of things of art is also arbitrary.\n(s Instead of the definition of empirical concepts, their exposition, and indeed empirical exposition, is required.)\nDefinitions of empirical concepts are never used to draw consequences from the marks enumerated in them; their use is synthetic. The use of rational or arbitrary definitions, on the other hand, is always analytic. — Definitions of empirical concepts are only for the understanding, that is, for understanding the word one uses. And since the word contains much or little of the object, they are rather an arbitrary determination. On the other hand, when the concept is given through reason [Be], accompanied by a word, its meaning is not alterable, since no synthesis can produce or alter it. I shall not define water, but describe it for the sake of the word, and then cite the experiences relating to it.",[]],"refl:2937":["74'.\nL 74:\nDefinition of a given or of a made concept. The former is given either *a priori* or *a posteriori*: *a priori* from the nature of the understanding, *a posteriori* empirically. |\nL 74': |\nGiven concepts through exposition, |\nmade ones through construction of the concepts; the former either through logical or through empirical exposition.\n(s *conceptus vel dati vel factitii* [concepts either given or made]. The latter either *a priori factitii* or through *synthesis empirica*.\nSynthetic, analytic definitions.)",[]],"refl:2938":["Nominal definition is that through ..., real definition ....",[]],"refl:2939":["*Definitio empirica* is often also a lack of precision.",[]],"refl:2940":["*Descriptio* is synthetic distinctness through extraneous marks.",[]],"refl:2941":["A nominal definition contains marks sufficient for the use of the word. Through it the concept does not become more distinct; the concept is merely determined for the word.",[]],"refl:2942":["All definitions are either analytic (of given concepts) or synthetic (of made concepts). The former are *a priori*, or of empirical concepts. The latter are likewise *a priori*, namely of mathematical concepts, or of objects of experience. For in this case we do not define the concept of experience, but make a concept for ourselves out of experiences.",[]],"refl:2943":["Every adequate concept of a thing is definition, not mere exposition. Definition is either synthetic or analytic; the former either *a priori* through construction or *a posteriori* through exposition of appearances.",[]],"refl:2944":["The greatest logical perfection of a concept is its definition.",[]],"refl:2945":["Right margin\nDefinition of an object of experience is possible only synthetically, although the concept is given.\nThe definition of the [Begrifs] given concept of this object can, however, be found analytically, but is of no use. It is a logical (nominal), not a real definition.",[]],"refl:2946":["Nominal definition for distinguishing, real for deriving.",[]],"refl:2947":["All definitions arise either from the analysis of a given concept or from the synthesis of a made concept. This synthesis is either that of exposition or that of construction. The made concept is made either arbitrarily or from given appearances. In the latter case the concept is not given, but only the matter for it.\nAll concepts are either given or made: the former given either *a priori* or *a posteriori* (g empirically), the latter made either *a priori* or empirically. The former *per analysin*, the second *per synthesin*. In the former the concept is only made distinct; in the latter it is itself brought forth.\nWith given concepts everything is done through exposition, which is not always complete; with made concepts, through [decla] aggregation. When the concept is made from what is in the [Gegenstande] object given *a posteriori*, it is entitled a synthetic concept of experience.",[]],"refl:2948":["All definitions are either analytic or synthetic. — The latter are produced either empirically or *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2949":["Definitions both of empirical and of mathematical concepts are |\nsynthetic, the former through empirical exposition, the latter through construction. The former give no definition at all.",[]],"refl:2950":["All making-distinct of a concept is *explicatio*. All distinct representation of made* concepts is *declaratio*; of given concepts it is *expositio*, whether they are given empirically or *a priori*. The former is synthetic, the latter analytic.\n*(g synthetic. With these the declaration must [vorher] precede the concept.)",[]],"refl:2951":["*Arbitrary concepts must (s become distinct through declaration, given ones through exposition); concepts *a priori* need not always be defined; empirical concepts can [nicht] never be defined (g only described). *Definitio. Expositio. Descriptio.*\n*(g The *completudo* of the analysis is never certain (s that of an empirical concept is impossible). (s Thus only in mathematics and in hypotheses can one begin with definitions.) Elementary propositions towards definitions.)",[]],"refl:2952":["Description is the recension of those marks of a thing which can be used for definition.",[]],"refl:2953":["Exposition is analytic in what is given *a priori*, synthetic in what is given empirically.",[]],"refl:2954":["A concept of experience is only a sign of experience.",[]],"refl:2955":["The exposition of the appearance is not the analysis of my concept into the marks which I think in it (or also think along with it). It is the extension of my concept beyond what I think through it, and consequently the synthesis of what belongs to it.",[]],"refl:2956":["Description is the exposition of a concept that is not precise; it can take place only with empirically given concepts.",[]],"refl:2957":["No concepts [zu] remain as capable of definition except those *a priori*, namely those given through the understanding (analytic), and [synth] made, arbitrary ones (synthetic): the former are philosophical, the latter mathematical definitions.\nAll others are declarations in the case of arbitrary concepts, descriptions in the case of empirically given ones. Exposition precedes (analytic) definition. Empirically [gegeb] made concepts are technical, *per fabricationem*; those made *a priori* are mathematical, *per constructionem*. Hence the fabrication of projects and hypotheses.",[]],"refl:2958":["All definitions must be made *a priori*; otherwise they do not define from concepts or through construction.\nEmpirical ones are not definitions.",[]],"refl:2959":["Empirical concepts, except those which indicate nothing but an object of experience in general (outer or inner), must all be regarded as made concepts. Their synthesis, however, is not arbitrary but empirical, and therefore can never be complete. They cannot, therefore, be defined.",[]],"refl:2960":["I need not always define: not when a part of my concept suffices for the truth of my proposition.",[]],"refl:2961":["The definition of an empirical concept is the (g complete) exposition of the appearances, which is impossible.",[]],"refl:2962":["I need a definition only when I wish to predicate the *definitum* of a certain other concept. E.g. if the question is, what is virtue?, I need only exposition. But if the question is whether holiness is virtue, I must know this concept completely. I do not wish |\nto know what virtue is, but which properties belong to the concept of virtue; and for that I must know everything.",[]],"refl:2963":["Definitions of arbitrary concepts which others have devised can be found from the use made of them, and so as definitions of given concepts.",[]],"refl:2964":["Empirical concepts can, however, be defined (g explicated) when one can make the object, e.g. gypsum, cinnabar, sulphur: complete exposition of the appearance.",[]],"refl:2965":["Elementary propositions before definitions. Synthetic definitions come first, analytic ones not.",[]],"refl:2966":["Approximation to definition is either exposition or description.",[]],"refl:2967":["There is a difference between making something intelligible to someone and defining [erklären] it for him. Red, for example, is intelligible to everyone, and no one can define it. But we understand something in two ways: 1. when we can think the same thought; 2. when we can think its application. If the object was never given in experience, or if one cannot give it in experience, one does not understand it *in concreto*. |\nE.g. whoever has never seen a shoe does not know what a shoemaker is. When one understands the mere thought, for which one can at will provide an object of application, one can also define it, so long as it is composite. Space and time, however, although composite objects enough can be thought in them, are simple concepts which make composition possible, and they cannot be defined; and so likewise in discursive thought. There must in the end be simple concepts which can indeed be understood but not defined. But these are only logical acts of defining, that is, of a rule of representations in general in so far as they are applied to objects in general. These logical acts of determining the representation of an object in general, that is, of distinguishing it from others [ohne Erf] prior to all experience, are entitled categories.",[]],"refl:2968":["Definitions of mathematical concepts and of concepts of experience must be made synthetically, those of concepts given *a priori* analytically — because the former have intuition as their ground. The latter require prior exposition.",[]],"refl:2969":["A circle is a figure that has no corners.\nFurther, L 75, from ψ−θ:\nThe exactness of definition.\nβ1. L 75'. On L §. 270b:\nA circle is a figure all of whose points are at equal distances from the centre, and whose [all] enclosed space is the greatest.\nOn L §. 270c:\nHas not marks and determinations enough.\nA man is a rational being.\nA spirit is what is not flesh and bone.\nFurther, L 75:\nRests on the first. For that by which I cannot distinguish things belongs to them all.\nOn L §. 270d:\n[hat nicht genug merkmale] Has too many marks.\nFurther, L 75':\nIf I say: a triangle is a figure whose angles taken together amount to 180°, this does not hold of curvilinear triangles.",[]],"refl:2970":["The lower [concept] consists of the *genus* and the *differentia specifica*.\nL 75'. Opposite L §. 271, sentence 1:\nIf I wish to define the distinct concept, I must say: it is that through which I distinguish from one another those [marks] through which I can distinguish one thing from others.",[]],"refl:2971":["Because they extend equally far.\nOn L §. 272 no. 1:\nWhoever is a man must have reason.\nOn L §. 272 no. 2:\nWhoever has reason and an earthly body is a man.",[]],"refl:2972":["I cannot say: man is that which thinks.\nFurther, L 76, from ψ−θ:\n*nec plus, nec minus* [neither more nor less].\nβ1. L 76'. On L §. 273b:\nEither king or subject.\nOn L §. 273c:\nWhen the negative marks occur in opposition to the affirmative ones which have been defined.\nFurther, L 76, from γ−ξ:\nNegative marks may well serve to distinguish a thing from others just as well as affirmative ones, but not for inner knowledge of the thing.",[]],"refl:2973":["Objective or material truth, e.g. the fortuitous [Ungefähre], chance.\nIt must be a concept that corresponds to a possible thing.\ncorresponds to that thing\nwhich one always thinks when one utters the word.\nSubjective or formal truth.\nOpposite L §. 274 »bei — der« (ll. 31-33):\nIf I call a spirit that which is not a body, the concept is objectively true, but formally, tested by logical rules, false.\nOne can also define concepts of which one can afterwards demonstrate a contradiction.\nβ1−ε2. L 76. On L §. 274, sentence 3:\nA definition can be a true proposition and yet be no definition.",[]],"refl:2974":["This is the chief rule.\nThe rule of determinateness must give way to it.\nFurther, L 76':\nWhat is incapable of distinct concepts is also incapable of definition: colours, taste.\nOn §. 275, sentence 2:\nLight is that which makes outer objects visible to us.\nOn L §. 275 »aus — voraus setzen« (l. 35):\nIn this way one can presuppose logical concepts in other sciences.\nOn L §. 275 »erst erklären« (ll. 35f.):\nHence one defines in advance all concepts that are to occur in other definitions.\nFurther, L 75, from ψ−θ. Below the end of L §. 275:\n*obscurum per aeque obscurum* [the obscure through the equally obscure].",[]],"refl:2975":["A body is the shrinking-together of the ethereal essence.\nOn L §. 276, sentence 2:\nThe soul is the monad that rules in an animal.\nOriginal sin is the preponderance of the lower powers over the higher.",[]],"refl:2976":["The definition of ground;\nof magnitude: what can be increased or diminished.\nTherefore these concepts must first be defined.\n(g A horse is an animal that neighs.)\nWhat dost thou believe? etc.\nL 77. γ−φ. Beside »Wenn — diesen« (ll. 35f.):\n*imperspicuitas* [lack of perspicuity].",[]],"refl:2977":["Opinions of some, that they are absolutely indemonstrable.\nOn L §. 278, sentence 2:\na. That it agree with the common concept.\nb. That it contain what is first in the thing.",[]],"refl:2978":["A man is an animal that laughs.\nGod is the almighty being.\nA triangle is that one among all rectilinear figures which has the fewest sides.",[]],"refl:2979":["The mathematician cannot make a false definition; he must therefore be precise: *reductio ad minimos terminos*.\nThat one should not possess (keep) more than one needs is a practical rule; that one should not think more than one needs (*praecisio*) is a logical one.\nIncorrect definitions can be correctly used as propositions: when they are *latiores suo definito* [wider than what they define], by supplementing them; when they are *angustiores* [narrower], by cutting them down [*praescindiren*].\nThe mathematician expresses [im subiect d] in the definition the synthesis of the marks, and in the *definitum* the whole idea, following the successive addition.",[]],"refl:2980":["There are four acts which I have to perform in so far as I examine definitions.\n1. Whether, as a proposition, it is true.\n2. Whether, as a concept, it is distinct, that is, whether the predicate is also a mark and not identical with the subject.\n3. Whether, as a distinct concept, it is exhaustive, that is, adequate to the thing itself.\n4. Whether, as an exhaustive concept, it is determinate.\nThese same acts are to be performed in framing definitions:\n1. Seek true propositions.\n2. Such [die nicht schon] as have a predicate that does not already presuppose the concept of the thing.\n3. Gather several of them and compare them with the [Sache] concept of the thing itself, as to whether it is adequate to the consequences.\n4. See whether one mark does not lie in another (s are subordinated to one another).\nVerbal definitions are not makings-distinct of concepts, but exchanges of words, so that one who does not understand one word may learn its meaning through the others, or, if it has been lost, may retain it.",[]],"refl:2981":["Let a definition not be tautological (*obscurum per aeqve obscurum*), nor wider nor narrower than its *definitum*.",[]],"refl:2982":["Let a definition not loosely but strictly [definiat] determine the *definitum*. *Conceptus vagus*: one that has no determinate meaning. Whether the *notae* [marks] must also be defined; otherwise it would be called explaining *obscurum per aeqve obscurum*.",[]],"refl:2983":["Whether definitions can be proved. Yes, as regards the elementary propositions, but not as regards *completudo*.",[]],"refl:2984":["(s Whether definitions ought to be proved.)\nThe sciences are full of tautological answers to my questions, especially in so far as they are meant to show how something can be done.",[]],"refl:2985":["(s 1. As regards the *sphaera*.) (s *quantitas*.) *Definitio* and *definitum* are *conceptus reciproci* (s *nec latior nec angustior*): *cui competit definitio* etc etc.\n( s 2. Quality: *obscurum per aeqve obscurum* [contra circulum]. 3. Relation to marks that are not identical or tautological (g but are distinct from the *definitum* as grounds of knowledge of it). *Contra circulum*. 4. Modality [nicht ohne noth negative Merkmale oder nominaldefinition]: against the definition of concepts of experience, because these are not capable of necessity in their marks, and do not [keine] sufficiently contain *ad essentiam pertinentia* [what pertains to the essence]. (g 4. Concepts cannot be defined through predicates which are added through experience.) )",[]],"refl:2986":["God as the infinite being, distinct from the world.\nThe real definition of the circle — —.\nFurther, L 77, from γ−ρ, on »Sacherklärung«:\nfor insight;\nOn »Worterklärung«:\nfor understanding.\nFurther, from κ−λ (? ν?? π−ρ??), on »Sacherklärung«:\nobjectively sufficient;\nOn »Worterklärung«:\nsubjectively sufficient;\nin arbitrary concepts there is no difference,\nnor in intellectual ones.\nFurther, from ρ−φ (? ν??), on Sacherklärung«.\nin the generation (inwardly sufficient, and sufficient for deriving the whole concept from it);\nOn »Worterklärung«:\nin the comparison.\nFurther, lower margin, from ζ (? η? κ??):\n*descriptiones* are comparative definitions.\nFurther, lower margin, from η−ρ:\nDefinition of the word;\nDefinition of the concept.\nFurther, lower margin, from κ−σ:\nThe one of these definitions knows no more in the object than the other. [Aber]",[]],"refl:2987":["if I [take] God as the almighty being,\nOn »definitio accidentalis«:\n[wenn ich ihn als den obersten Regierer der Welt ansehe]\nthat being to which everything owes its existence.",[]],"refl:2988":["One cannot always find it, but in brass it can be found.\nThe circle.\nL 78. β1−ε2. On L §. 281 »mittelbare Erfahrung«.\nWhat makes objects visible.\nL 78'. β1. On L §. 281 »Absonderung«:\nBy always abstracting the most universal from all particular properties.\nOn L §. 281 »willkürliche Verbindung«:\nWhen the concept which I make is new, e.g. of a medicament.\nOn L §. 281 no. 2:\nReal definitions which still stand in need of a proof must [alsdenn] not be given, e.g. of the circle from the *angulus ad peripheriam*; of God, that he is an immaterial being; *item* of the soul.",[]],"refl:2989":["*essentialia reciproca*.\nE.g. of the circle. Mirror.\nFurther, L 78, from β1−ε2, beside »schaften — entweder« (60335f.):\nOf fire.\nL 78'. β1. On »durch einen Beweis aus der Vernunft« (60424f.):\nDefinition of a clear concept.\nβ1−ε2. On L §. 282 no. 2:\nAnimal: that which has a body which displays desires and aversions.",[]],"refl:2990":["They are not wholly arbitrary, e.g. sin is an action displeasing to God.\nFurther, L 78, beside L §. 283, sentence 1, from β1−ε2:\nVice is an action done contrary to the will of God,\nor: by which unhappiness is promoted.\nL 78'. β1. On L §. 283 »nach Wohlgefallen — ausmachen«:\nE.g. a triangle is a rectilinear figure with the fewest sides.\n*item*: a triangle is a figure whose angles taken together are equal to two right angles.",[]],"refl:2991":["Some we cannot [define], e.g. representation, reality, (g position).\nβ1−ε2. On L §. 284 »Horizont unserer gelehrten Erkenntniss«:\nE.g. in morals one must define what duty is.\nBut one need not define what eating, drinking, clothing are.\nβ1. On L §. 284, end:\n1. When one defines logically where one does not want learned knowledge.\n2. When one makes far too many definitions.",[]],"refl:2992":["Descriptions too are either nominal or real: either sufficient for distinguishing a thing (but not from all others), or for deriving its other properties from them. All definitions made merely through comparison with other things are descriptions.\nA nominal definition has subjectively sufficient marks, a real definition objectively sufficient ones. Empirical concepts cannot even be defined nominally, because one does not always think precisely the same thing under the name of the *definitum*. Since, moreover, they are always growing, they are descriptions.",[]],"refl:2993":["Logical definitions are all synthetic; real definitions of abstract concepts are all analytic. In philosophy all real definitions are impossible, but their grounds are empirical. We seek [bey] *analytice* only the subjective sufficiency, but [objective d] *synthetice* the objective; for in the latter we seek out not what lies in the concept, but what must be joined to it.",[]],"refl:2994":["All definitions are either of concepts (logical) or of things (real). (g Things are defined when they are given only through experience, and the distinct, complete concept is sought out from experience through observation.)\nThe definitions of concepts are either [gemachter Willkührlicher oder gegebener Begriffe] of concepts (g which) *a priori* or *a posteriori* (g are given). Of the former a complete definition can be given. Of the latter [nur eine] the logical definition is only nominal; the real definition cannot be given at all. Concepts *a priori* can be defined synthetically |\nwhen they have been [sind] given arbitrarily, or analytically when they have been given *a priori* but not arbitrarily, e.g. virtue, substance. *Definitiones* are either diagnostic or genetic.\n(s All definitions are either synthetic or analytic propositions. The former either of empirical or of pure intuitions. The latter arbitrary.)",[]],"refl:2995":["The concepts which arise from the understanding can all be defined, whether they arise through choice or through its nature. Nominal definitions are possible of all objects (something of *positio arbitraria* often enters in). Real definitions, which contain the possibility of the thing itself, are to be [geben] found only of concepts that are given through the understanding. [Sie werden per anal sind sie willk] And here nominal and real definitions coincide. With arbitrary concepts, however, they are produced synthetically; with natural concepts of the understanding, analytically. Empirical concepts can be defined only nominally; fundamental concepts of the senses not at all. (It is lexicographical when I make a word of one language distinct in another, not through one equivalent word but through many.) Various concepts seem to be empirical, but have arisen through the understanding, e.g. earth, of which it is nevertheless said that none is found pure. Or is it to be only the abstraction of a concept. There are real definitions of what is given, or even already thought, but not under the proper name.\n(g e.g. metaphysics)\nPedantry in defining: when one [einen nothigt] determines the meaning of a word where it would not in any case be confused, or compels the other person to grasp all the marks where, given the purpose, some suffice.",[]],"refl:2996":["We expound either a concept or experience. The first is done *per analysin*, the second *per synthesin*. Expounding is the [klare Vorst] connected representation of the marks found *per analysin*. Even if we cannot define, we can still expound. Where the exposition is incomplete, we can use the concept *directe* (from the concept to the properties), but cannot use it to infer *inverse*, that is, from the properties to the concept.",[]],"refl:2997":["A complete concept, either for the full distinguishing or for the complete (g derivation of the properties) comprehension of the thing itself. In their objective completeness both are the same.",[]],"refl:2998":["The nominal definition is sufficient in the comparison (g of the *definitum*) with other things.\nThe real definition is sufficient inwardly.",[]],"refl:2999":["Nominal definition: for distinguishing.\nReal definition: for knowledge of the object according to its inner determinations.",[]],"refl:3000":["All mathematical definitions are real definitions, except only those in which it is said how something is made.",[]],"refl:3001":["A genetic definition is one that gives a concept through which the concept can be exhibited *a priori* *in concreto*. All others are dianoetic, and suffice merely for comparison with [s] the known ones.",[]],"refl:3002":["Real definitions are not always genetic. They do not concern the way in which the object appears through its consequences, but what it is according to its sources. They are not always delineating [verzeichnend]. E.g. the [Gefühl ist] agreeable is [was] the feeling of the furtherance of life. Here one must already know the agreeable.\nAll mathematical [Begriffe] definitions are genetic, the empirical ones dianoetic.",[]],"refl:3003":["Nominal definitions are those which contain the meaning that one has chosen arbitrarily to give to a certain name.\nThere are, further, certain real definitions, which are either diagnostic or genetic. The former arise from the similarities or dissimilarities of things in comparison, and so through outer marks; the latter through inner possibility. The former bring about a scholastic division, the latter a canonical one. The former is a [compendium] method of bringing an order of coordination, that is, of places, into our concepts.\nThere are definitions of concepts which we already have, but which are not yet properly designated by the name, e.g. metaphysics. And here it is not so much the meaning of the word that is analysed as a concept that resides in us; and it must here be shown in particular which name expresses it, and how far.\n(g Space is defined by pointing out, because it is not a universal concept.)",[]],"refl:3004":["Given things of nature, when they are brought under a common mark, [geben] stand through this mark under a determinate concept, which is their nominal definition. For names are meant to be distinguishing marks, to serve for classes, and to indicate sameness in certain respects. They are not meant to indicate properties and what is inner, and so they are not grounds of explanation but grounds of division.",[]],"refl:3005":["Nominal definition: that which designates the object (if need be also through description).\nReal definition: that which sets forth its possibility from inner marks. Objects of experience permit merely nominal definitions.\nThe definition of right as a coercive law is nominal.",[]],"refl:3006":["A distinct concept which is inwardly sufficient is entitled a real definition. One which is merely outwardly sufficient in a certain relation is a nominal definition (s conventional meaning).\nUnlimited outer sufficiency is impossible without inner sufficiency. (s For only through all the necessary marks together, not merely through some, can a thing be distinguished from all others.)",[]],"refl:3007":["Every verbal definition must be regarded as the definition of an arbitrary concept, and is synthetic *a priori*.",[]],"refl:3008":["Whether *genus* and *differentia specifica* constitute the definition. Yes, as a nominal definition in comparison; not as a real definition in derivation.",[]],"refl:3009":["Lower concepts have something in which they agree and something in which they differ from one another. E.g. men and angels, in so far as they have something in common, form a higher concept, e.g. rational (g finite) beings; and in what they have that differs, they contradict one another: man has an earthly body, the angel not. So say: rational finite beings, either those which have an earthly body or those which do not.\nRepresenting beings: either distinct or confused or obscure; distinct: either those endowed with earthly bodies or those endowed with other bodies; confused: breaks off.",[]],"refl:3010":["So far from its being the dividing-up of the concept, the members of the division contain, on the contrary, more in themselves than the divided concept.",[]],"refl:3011":["An abstracted concept together with the difference.",[]],"refl:3012":["E.g. men in the condition in which they are merely by birth: *qvoad connatos habitus*, or *qvoad acqvisitos*: in the natural state or in the civil state.\nOn L §. 286 »codivisiones«:\nLearned or unlearned, rules or obeys.\nL 79. β1−ε2. On L §. 286 »subdivisum«:\na member of the division.\nL 79'. β1−ε2. Opposite L §. 286, sentences 1, 2; probably on »codivisiones«:\nConcepts are divided either *ratione* of their origin into *connatas aut acquisitas*, or *ratione* of clarity into *claras* and *obscuras*, or *ratione* of their [practischen] consequences into practical and theoretical.",[]],"refl:3013":["E.g. I cannot say: all men are either Jews or Christians.\nThe ancients divided into bodies and spirits.\nFurther, L 79, from φ (? ω?):\nToo few members of the division. E.g. all men are either virtuous or vicious.",[]],"refl:3014":["If I divide the learned into those who know much, those who know little, and those who know nothing at all.\nFurther, L 79, from φ (? ω?):\nToo many members of the division. E.g. surface of the earth: land, sea and air.\nFurther, from γ−τ:\nVirtue or vice (indifferent), holiness.",[]],"refl:3015":["I cannot say: all men are either virtuous or vicious or philosophers. (s For the one does not belong under the concept of the other.)\n*figurae rectilineae. curvilineae. mixtilineae.* (s Not: ideas are clear or distinct.)",[]],"refl:3016":["They must be contained under it, and so belong to it.\nFurther, L 80', from β1:\nSpirits that have understanding and those that have none.",[]],"refl:3017":["Because in that case the members of the subdivision belong to the higher member and are not opposed to it. E.g. concepts into clear, obscure and distinct; for what is distinct is clear. Or triangles into isosceles, equilateral, scalene; for equilateral triangles belong under isosceles. One must divide into triangles which have either [lauter] some sides equal, or all, or none at all.",[]],"refl:3018":["E.g. of the kinds of creatures. Posit two: simple and composite; divide the simple into those which [have] obscure, (g confused,) clear or distinct breaks off.",[]],"refl:3019":["All figures are either rectilinear or curvilinear or mixtilinear; therefore so too is a circle.\nAll men are either virtuous or vicious; therefore so too is Socrates.\nFurther, L 80, from β1−ε2:\nwhoever is entitled to the title of a virtuous man is entitled to the title of man.\nβ1. L 80'. On L §. 289 »wem der — Eintheilung zu« (ll. 26f.):\nAll goods are either chiefly of the soul or of the body or of both together. Sin is no good.",[]],"refl:3020":["When one has divided representations into clear and obscure, confused and distinct, one can thereby form learned concepts. (g What is an animal — — — —.)\nOne knows to which higher concepts one is to assign them, and what their difference is.\n[Alle Eigenschaften der Korper sind entweder die Figur oder die Große. Alle si]",[]],"refl:3021":["*divisio in opposita vel disparata*.\n*prior est infinita* [s. in], *posterior* [determi] *definita* [the former is infinite, the latter definite].\n**We either divide [theilen] the concept or divide it logically [eintheilen]. In the former case we seek what is contained in it: *divisio metaphysica*; in the second, what is contained under it (*divisio logica sphaerae*). In this case |\n[enthalten die] there arise *membra*, not *partes*, and they contain more in themselves than the *conceptus divisus*.\nHence there is a difference between the *judicium logicum* and the *judicium metaphysicum*.\n*(s The latter, when it takes place *a priori* through concepts, is always trichotomy.)\n**(s The division of a tree (s properly a partition of the consideration of it) into root, trunk and crown, or of the earth into zones, etc. (s *Fleischern*, nightcap, etc etc.): these are divisions of an object (g of intuition), not of a concept according to how many things it belongs to; this is entitled partition [Abtheilung]. Separation [Scheidung] concerns the [unte] removal of what is heterogeneous, and is the resolution of a mixture. Division [theilung] concerns a composite merely as a magnitude, and the division is never complete until it reaches the simple. In partition regard is had to distinguishing the heterogeneous pieces; in division, to distinguishing parts of the same kind. Composition, in the transcendental sense, signifies the conjugation of the homogeneous, and proceeds to the abolition of all conjugation; this is possible even when the parts are substances in the intellectual meaning.)\n(g Division according to the diversity of concepts of the same object (points of view).\nDivision of the points of view themselves.)",[]],"refl:3022":["/\nSubdivision is a decomposition; it must properly begin with dichotomy.",[]],"refl:3023":["We ascend from lower concepts to higher, and afterwards we can descend again from these to the lower through division.",[]],"refl:3024":["The determination of a concept in respect of all that is possibly contained under it, in so far as these are opposed [ist] to one another, that is, distinguished from one another.",[]],"refl:3025":["Division is the representation of the manifold which, through opposition, is in the community of a single sphere.\nAll dividing takes place first into two parts.",[]],"refl:3026":["/\nAbove no. 3025:\nAll polytomy is empirical.\nDichotomy is the only division from principles *a priori*.",[]],"refl:3027":["The first division is dichotomy.",[]],"refl:3028":["The division of concepts is twofold: either (g 1. logical [*logica*] or formal [*Formalis*]) [a division] of them in themselves, [namely] dichotomy; [or] 2. [a division] in respect of the possible (g or actual objects of appearance.) objects in intuition (g intuition *a priori*): *divisio realis* [real division].\n*partitio* [partition]. (s e.g. land and sea.) (s Partition [*Abtheilung*] as distinguished from division [*Eintheilung*].)",[]],"refl:3029":["*Divisio sphaerae, non conceptus* [a division of the sphere, not of the concept] (g — is distinct from *analysis*).",[]],"refl:3030":["All division proceeds either according to [a] the principle of analytic judgments or according to that of synthetic judgments. The former proceeds according to the *principium exclusi medii* [principle of excluded middle]; the latter according to the principle of ground in the combination of the manifold [*des Verschiedenen*]. The first is dichotomy, the second polytomy. [In synthetic propositions *a priori*] In the division of concepts [of a] which contain or prescribe a syn|thesis\n*a priori*, either the synthesis is divided or a synthetic concept *a priori* is divided; both belong to transcendental division. The first is tetrachotomy, the second trichotomy. Logical division is *partitio* [partition], because all the members of the division come in pairs. The second is *decompositio logica* [logical decomposition]: in it, first, something is posited; secondly, something else is in addition posited or removed [*aufgehoben*]; thirdly, the composition of both is represented. The synthetic unity of consciousness is the transcendental ground of the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*. For I combine A with consciousness. Then B (represented either merely as non-A or also as something which is added to it). Thirdly, the [combination of bo] unity of both kinds of distributive consciousness in a collective one, that is, in the concept of a thing. Thus first the analytic unity of the consciousness of A and non-A (=B), and then the synthetic unity of both. But there is also this copula breaks off.",[]],"refl:3031":["Polytomy can [not] not be taught in logic; for it requires knowledge (g of the object) on the part of the understanding as regards content. Dichotomy, on the other hand, needs only the principle of contradiction, without our having to know as regards its content the concept which we wish to divide. Polytomy requires intuition, either *a priori*, as in mathematics (conic sections), or empirical, as in the description of nature. Division [*Eintheilung*] [of] from the principle of synthesis *a priori* [a synthetic], however, has a trichotomy: 1. the concept [as] of the condition; 2. [the concept] of the conditioned; 3. [the concept] of the derivation of the latter from the former.",[]],"refl:3032":["/\nWhen something can be regarded as a mark of a thing.\nHence through abstraction and comparison.\nTwo mutually conflicting concepts make up a single impossible concept.\nFurther, L 81. Beside L § 292 »der — logischen« (62421f.):\nA body is divisible.\nThe sun is spherical.",[]],"refl:3033":["in every case not.\nFurther, L 81 beside L § 293 »im — er dem« (l. 35):\nThe soul is of a simple nature.\nOn L § 293 »copula«:\n*est*.\nOn L § 293 »Verneinung des Verbindungsbegriffs« [negation of the concept of connection]:\nThe sign *non*.",[]],"refl:3034":["*omnes propositiones vel sunt positionis aut remotionis absolutae* [all propositions are either of absolute positing or removal] (There is a God; there is not) *vel relativae: praedicati ad subiectum (alias logicae)* [or relative: of the predicate to the subject (otherwise called logical)].\n*iudicia vel sunt noetica vel dianoetica (ratiocinia) discursus* [judgments are either noetic or dianoetic (inferences), discursive].\n*demonstrationes vel ostensivae vel apogogicae* [demonstrations are either ostensive or apagogic].",[]],"refl:3035":["Logic looks not to content but to form (relation) in respect of quality. Hence negations in the predicate [are] do not yield a negative judgment.\n*Quaeitas est relatio [praed] praedicati ad subiectum* [quaeity is the relation of the predicate to the subject].",[]],"refl:3036":["/\nRegarding particular judgments it is to be noted that, if they are to be capable of being comprehended through reason, and so have a rational and not merely intellectual (abstracted) form, the subject must be a *conceptus latior* [wider concept] than the [subject] predicate. Let the predicate be always =  and the subject  ; then  is a particular judgment, for something that falls under a is b, something not b. That follows from reason. But let it be ; then [it is] all a can at least be contained under b, if it is the smaller, but not if it is the larger; it is therefore only contingently particular.",[]],"refl:3037":["A judgment is the relation (g knowledge) of the connection or of the conflict of concepts.",[]],"refl:3038":["/\nA concept is the relation of one representation to others as a mark (g *communis* [common]) of them or breaks off.",[]],"refl:3039":["The matter of the judgment: the *termini* [terms].\nThe form, or the relation, is exhibited [*exponirt*] by the copula.\nIf\neither or —\nin the *iudicia [limitata] extraordinaria* [extraordinary judgments].",[]],"refl:3040":["*Forma iudicii in logicis consideratur (materiam non attendit)* [in logic the form of judgment is considered (it does not attend to the matter)].\n*In illa est relatio, qvalitas, qvantitas, modalitas* [in it there is relation, quality, quantity, modality].\n*categorica*\n*hypothetica*\n*disiunctiva*\n*relatio est vel subiecti vel principii vel oppositi* [relation is either of subject, or of ground, or of opposite].\nRelation constitutes the content.\nThe agreement of the judgment with the rules of relation breaks off?",[]],"refl:3041":["/\nJudgments are the means of distinct modes of knowledge, and the (g real) *functiones* [functions] for judgments, as distinct knowledge of appearances, breaks off.",[]],"refl:3042":["A judgment is a knowledge of the unity of given concepts: namely, that [that which] B, together with various other things x, y, z, belongs under the same concept A; or again, that the manifold which is under B also belongs under A; and likewise that the concepts A and B can be represented through one concept B.",[]],"refl:3043":["All judgments are analytic or synthetic. The affirmative judgments of the former kind rest on identity, in that the predicate is contained in the subject; the negative, in that it contradicts the subject.\nThe synthetic [rest on the fact] that the subject is or is not contained under the predicate.",[]],"refl:3044":["A judgment is the relation of the subordination of concepts to one another.\nA judgment is the representation of unity in the relation of modes of knowledge.*\nWhen [what accordi] several modes of knowledge are regarded as one, [so that] through one of them the other is also posited.\n*\n(s The [subordination] connection of a mode of knowledge [under] with its universal condition according to concepts. The connection of different knowledge [according to] through concepts.)",[]],"refl:3045":["A concept, in virtue of its universal validity [*Gemeingültigkeit*], has the function of a judgment. It relates to other concepts *potentialiter* [potentially]. The actual relation of a concept to others, as a means of knowing them, is the judgment.\nThrough it our knowledge becomes distinct.\nMatter of judgments.\nForm of judgments.\nQuantity.\nAll relation (g of concepts) is either of comparison,\nor of association,\nor of inclusion or connection.\nA judgment is the unity of a concept arising from the relation (connection) of different concepts.\nIn ω Kant replaced l. 17, retaining Ein — Einheit, by lines 63021-6312, but forgot to strike out l. 18.\nA judgment is the representation of the unity of the relation of the ground of knowledge to the [kn] possible knowledge of an object. Accordingly it is |\nthe (g [distinct] clear representation of the) unity of the consciousness of different representations.\nFrom phase ω there also derives the following remark (beginning under 6266f. and 63314f.), introduced by an NB to which no second one corresponds:\nTo judge is to represent one concept as contained under another (s or excluded from it): 1. subject under predicate; 2. consequence under ground; 3. part of the sphere under the whole.",[]],"refl:3046":["Categorical judgments constitute the matter of the rest.\nMatter of all judgments: either concepts or other judgments.\nAll distinct knowledge is knowledge made clear through a judgment. Understanding.\nWhat is distinct through an inference of reason is an intensively distinct knowledge (*adaeqvat* [adequate]).",[]],"refl:3047":["A judgment is the mediate knowledge of a representation through other representations. The relation of the mediate [knowledge] representation to the immediate is (the relation in the judgment, or) the form. The subject is the immediate representation, the predicate the mediate.",[]],"refl:3048":["81.\nL 81':\nIf universal propositions cannot be comprehended in their universality without [the pa|rticular\ncases of the appli us] knowing them *in concreto*, they cannot serve as a guiding rule, and so cannot hold heuristically in application. They are only problems [*Aufgaben*] for the investigation of the universal grounds of what first became known in particular cases. For example: he who has no interest in lying and knows the truth speaks the truth. This proposition is not comprehended in its universality, because we know the restriction to the condition of being disinterested only through experience, namely, that men can lie from interest. This comes from their not adhering firmly to morality, which is an observation of the weakness of human nature. Mathematicians err here |\nL 81: |\nespecially. They give the semblance of knowledge *a priori* which is in fact only *a posteriori*. The exposition thereby becomes abstract to no purpose.",[]],"refl:3049":["A judgment is the representation of the relation of concepts to one another which is required for the distinctness of knowledge.\n[A judgment is the (g composition) [distinct representation of the c combination] of the manifold of representations [in one] into one]",[]],"refl:3050":["A judgment is* the representation of the unity of different representations, in so far as one belongs to the concept of the other.\n*(s the consciousness of the relation of representations in so far as they constitute a concept.)",[]],"refl:3051":["/\nThe representation of the manner in which different concepts (g as such) *belong to one consciousness** (g in general (not merely my own)) is the judgment. They belong to one consciousness either according to laws of the imagination, and so [conti] subjectively, or according to laws of the understanding, that is, objectively valid for every being that has understanding. The subjective connection depends on the particular situation of the subject in experience.\n*(g universally necessary (empirically or *a priori*))\n**(s Concepts belong to one consciousness only through being thought under one another, not alongside one another (as sensations are).)",[]],"refl:3052":["*Iudicium est repraesentatio unitatis obiectivae* [a judgment is the representation of the objective unity] (s in the knowledge of an object) *[plurium consci] in conscientia variorum conceptuum* [in the consciousness of various concepts]. The objective unity (g of consciousness) is universally valid and necessary.",[]],"refl:3053":["A judgment is the consciousness that one concept is contained under another: either as its predicate, or as its ground, or as a member of its division. This is the matter of judgments in general. The form is that of quantity, quality, relation, modality.",[]],"refl:3054":["The categories represent that objective unity of consciousness as |\nconcepts of things in general, because it is in fact through them alone that things are thought as objects corresponding to our representations.",[]],"refl:3055":["Judgment: the representation of the manner in which different concepts [representations] belong objectively* (for everyone) to one consciousness.\n*(g that is, so as to constitute a knowledge of the object.)",[]],"refl:3056":["The objective unity of representations is that of representations which are represented as combined not subjectively, that is, not according to the laws of association.",[]],"refl:3057":["A representation which becomes universal through consciousness [of it] as a mark is entitled a (g clear) concept.",[]],"refl:3058":["The logical relation is the relation of concepts* to the unity of consciousness in general (of agreement and of conflict).\n*(g Under the condition of the unity of the consciousness of the manifold in general.)",[]],"refl:3059":["/\nA concept which is clear through a judgment is a consequence of it.",[]],"refl:3060":["A judgment is the representation of the unity [of the conc] of given concepts in so far as one is subordinated to the other: 1. as under the sphere of the other; 2. as consequence to ground; 3. as member of the division to the divided concept.",[]],"refl:3061":["Division into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive judgments.",[]],"refl:3062":["/\ne.g. The soul is immortal.\nMan is imperfect.\nOn L § 294 »qualitas judicii«:\n*huic opponitur qvantitas* [to this quantity is opposed].",[]],"refl:3063":["(g Quality of judgments: affirmative or negative.)\nThe relation of concepts. (exponent):\nThe [pred]\nground —\nsubject to predicate the\nconsequence — whole — part\n} form of judgments.\n}\ncategorical,\nhypothetical,\ndisjunctive.\n[The moment] Quantity. Universal, particular, singular.\nThe mode. Problematic, assertoric, apodeictic.\nIt is of great importance to know which propositions, expressed problematically, I can use sufficiently. For example: there can be a future life. For problematic propositions are often indisputable, although assertoric ones are exposed to objections.\nLikewise [which] assertoric ones [I can use], although they are not apodeictic; for example, empirical propositions.\nProblematic propositions [taken] as grounds of other truths are entitled hypotheses.\nThe proposition *qvidam homines non sunt eruditi* [some men are not learned] can be expressed by fig. 1 (*non omnes* [not all]).\nThe infinite proposition *qvidam sunt non-eruditi* [some are non-learned] by fig. 2 (*qvidam non* [some not]). The concept of the learned is restricted in respect of men, that is, narrower than the [same] latter.\nThat the learned and the non-learned [all] together make up all men, and that men are therefore thought through the [non] learned, but with a restriction.\nThe learned are regarded either as all standing under the concept of men, but only as one part of its sphere (g the other part is not learned); [The c] or only a part [the m] of men is regarded as making up one sphere [under] with the concept of the learned. The latter restricts the concept of men. [the former]\n(g Through negative predicates I place my understanding outside a determinate sphere in an infinite space.)\nThe negative proposition indicates that something is not contained under the sphere of a given concept. The infinite proposition indicates that something is contained under the sphere which lies outside the given concept. It therefore [that some] presupposes that outside its sphere there is another in which it [the subject] is contained, and consequently that it belongs to a sphere which restricts the former. Fig. 2. The first takes place according to the *principium exclusi medii* (g between a and non-a there is no third) etc. etc.; the second according to that of thoroughgoing determination, which is infinite. The first is the principle of determination: of two opposed judgments one is true. It says only that the proposition »anima non est mortalis« [the soul is not mortal] is opposed to the proposition »anima est mortalis« [the soul is mortal]. The second takes place according to the principle of thoroughgoing determination. This [determination], which is to be made in respect of a thing in general, determines only in respect of thinghood [*Sachheit*] in general, that is, of reality; and outside the sphere of a concept it brings in an infinite sphere of the determination of all things, namely of thinghood, that is, reality. Outside the *sphaera* of a concept there is room for an infinity of *sphaerae*.\nThe proposition »anima [non] est non-mortalis« [the soul is non-mortal] is a judgment of determination, which says that of two opposed predicates a and non-a the latter belongs to the soul. Judgments of determination are all infinite [and are of], [their purpose being] to determine a thing throughout, not merely to indicate the relation of connection or of conflict. Logic looks not to the content [thereby], that is, the determination of the concept, but only to the form of the relation: agreement or conflict.",[]],"refl:3064":["»The soul is something other than the mortal«: is infinite, but affirmative.",[]],"refl:3065":["/\nThe infinite judgment indicates not merely that a subject is not contained under the sphere of a predicate, but that it is somewhere outside the sphere of the predicate, in the infinite. Consequently it represents the sphere of the predicate as limited.",[]],"refl:3066":["Every negative proposition has been preceded by a division of concepts: *eruditi* (g *homines*) [learned (men)] — *non eruditi* [not learned]. Now the proposition »qvidam homines (g non) sunt eruditi« [some men are (not) learned] says that the one [divided concept] member of the division does not belong to a subject. But in the *iudicium infinitum* [infinite judgment] the subject is contained under the infinity of things outside the *eruditi*. Thus the determination of the concept of men is [effected] through the limits of the concept of the learned.",[]],"refl:3067":["All true division is dichotomy. Why, then, divide judgments into three kinds, e.g. in respect of quantity? Since many, which is all, constitutes one, or conversely one, in respect of its predicate, is all?",[]],"refl:3068":["82'.\nL 82:\nIn the *iudicium affirmativum* [affirmative judgment] the subject is thought under the *sphaera* |\nof a predicate; in the *negativum* it is placed outside the *sphaera* of the latter; in the *infinitum* it is placed in the *sphaera* of a concept which lies outside the sphere of another.\nL 82':\nIn the universal judgment [a concept] the *sphaera* of one concept is enclosed wholly within the *sphaera* of another; in the particular, a part of the former is enclosed under the sphere of the other; in the singular, a concept which has no *sphaera* at all, and so is enclosed merely as part, under the *sphaera* of another. Singular judgments [*iudicia singularia*] are therefore to be valued as equivalent to universal ones [*universalibus*]; and conversely a *iudicium universale* is to be regarded, in respect of the *sphaera*, as a singular judgment. Many, in so far as it is in itself only one.",[]],"refl:3069":["Although exclusion [restriction] is a negative [act], the limitation of a concept is nevertheless a positive act. Hence limits are positive concepts [of the] of limited objects.\nEverything possible is a or non-A. If, therefore, I say that something is non-A, it is a *iudicium indefinitum* [indefinite judgment]. For beyond the *sphaera definita* [determinate sphere] A it is not determined under which concept the object belongs, but only that it belongs to the sphere outside A. This is properly no sphere at all, but only the bordering of a sphere on the infinite, or the limitation itself. The remainder is infinite when a determinate part is taken away from the infinite.",[]],"refl:3070":["/\nIf (g merely) the logical form is to be altered, the matter of the concepts, that is, their content, must not be altered (*nonmortalis* must not be made out of *mortalis*). Now since logic has to do merely with the form of the judgment, and not with concepts as regards their content, the distinction of infinite from negative judgments does not belong to logic. This name, moreover, serves only to prevent the semblance of a negation of the minor premiss in a syllogism, when its predicate is merely negative. For example: every substance which is not divisible is not corruptible. *Atqui* [but] the soul is [a] not a divisible substance etc. etc. Here the minor is affirmative, that is, the predicate is: *anima est nondiuisibilis* [the soul is non-divisible].",[]],"refl:3071":["Above and in the concluding sentence of L § 294:\na negative judgment is distinguished from a judgment which affirms a negative concept of the subject. The difference consists in the relation.",[]],"refl:3072":["According to the *principium exclusi medii* the sphere of one concept, relatively to another, is either exclusive or inclusive.",[]],"refl:3073":["The assertion is either merely conditioned (hypothetical judgment), or [the restriction falls on] the subject, in which case it is a *iudicium restrictum* [restricted judgment]: all scholars, as men, are to be esteemed equal to one another. If the limitation lies in the copula, it is a modal [judgment].",[]],"refl:3074":["1. The former give a demonstration, e.g. of the fallibility of men. Finite nature.\ninsufficient is of learning the capacity\n2. The first are inner marks of the thing, the others e.g. testimony.",[]],"refl:3075":["If God wills to annihilate the world (g wills always to preserve the world), then it can perish (the world endures eternally).\nL 83'. On L § 298 no. 3:\nBodies are divisible.\nBodies all have heat.",[]],"refl:3076":["Man can draw inferences.\nThe sun illuminates the [northern] poles for half a year.\nWhat is taken from the logical definition is a necessary [judgment].",[]],"refl:3077":["It is not necessary to say: man, because he has reason, can draw inferences.\nIf a man is virtuous, he loves his enemy.",[]],"refl:3078":["/\nA triangle which has three sides has three angles.\nA man, if he is finite, can err.\nOn L § 299 no. 2:\nA wise man, if he is of finite nature, deceives himself.\nThe sun is eclipsed when the moon comes before it.\nL 83'. On L § 299 »iudicium determinatum«:\nIs changed from a higher into a lower.\nFurther, L 83, from β1−ε2:\nAll men must naturally die.",[]],"refl:3079":["The character of the (g subject) predicate. Of the condition.\nOn L § 300, beginning of sentence 2:\nA body is divisible.\nOn L § 300, end:\nThe world is not God.",[]],"refl:3080":["Adam was fallible.\nChrist is the redeemer from sin.\nOn L § 301 sentence 3:\nAll men are mortal;\nsome are learned.\nψ−θ. L 84. On »iudicium particulare«:\nplural.\nβ1. L 84'. Opposite L § 301 sentence 5 (ll. 29-31):\nsome men [are] err.\nall err.\nOn the *termini* A, E, I, O in L § 301 (ll. 33f.):\nAll men are mortal. No man is blameless.\nSome are possessed by prejudices. Some are not possessed by prejudices.\nOn L § 301 »Das Prädicat — werden« (64734, 64820f.):\nAll men are mortal. No man is blameless. All are blameworthy.\nOn L § 301, penultimate sentence:\nGod is without fault. Everything that is God is without fault.\nOn L § 301 »so — rechnen« (ll. 23f.):\nOnly not the merely particular.",[]],"refl:3081":["85.\nIn L § 302 Kant adds a 1 before »wenn« (l. 29) and a 2 before »oder einigen« (l. 30), and on L 85, in the right margin, makes the following remark, which probably refers to »oder einigen«, but possibly also to »oder einigen — zukommt« (l. 30):\noften the latter can be made universal by means of the condition.",[]],"refl:3082":["Such a judgment becomes universal. E.g. all spirits are immortal.\nOn L § 303 no. 2:\nAll works of God, if God wills, are indestructible.\nNo man can naturally be immortal.\nEvery particular judgment can be changed into a universal one by the condition added to it.",[]],"refl:3083":["All analytic judgments are universal;\nsynthetic [judgments] are empirical and particular.\nIn the former the predicate is contained both objectively and subjectively in the *conceptus* of the subject; in the latter only objectively.",[]],"refl:3084":["[In respect of quality: affirmative, negative and p]\nIn respect of position.\nproblematic and dogmatic or assertoric.\nQuantity concerns not concepts but their relation.\nIn respect of quantity there are only [a and affirmative and negative] universal and particular [judgments]; for the subject is either wholly in- or excluded from the notion of the predicate,\nor partly included, partly excluded.\n(s whether the proposition is categorical (or conditional or disjunctive).)\n*qvaeitas. qvalitas. qvantitas.*\nQuaeity concerns the copula, as the relation of a ground of knowledge to the subject, and the energy, that is, the degree of this ground and of the relation. E.g. God is necessarily just.",[]],"refl:3085":["The general [*das generale*] is that which takes into consideration only something, namely that which is common to most.\nGeneral [judgments] propositions (s are those which do not contain sufficient conditions of subsumption) are those which contain something merely [about the] concerning what is universal in certain objects. E.g. one must make one's proofs thorough. Universal [propositions]: those which assert something universally of an object.\n(s The *generalia* of the art of government are only what concerns the *genus*, e.g. of the education of children as children in general; *specialia*: according to their differences of age, health, circumstances.)",[]],"refl:3086":["Universal rules [(or also] are either analytically universal, in that they abstract from differences [and the d], or synthetically universal; the latter attend to the differences and yet also determine in respect of them. The more simply an object is thought, the more readily is analytic universality possible in accordance with a concept. Trigonometry already requires many rules.",[]],"refl:3087":["Quantity, quality, relation and modality.",[]],"refl:3088":["God and all finite spirits are immortal.\nOn L § 304 »subiectum et praedicatum principale«:\ne.g. Almighty God has created the best world.\n[God and one's neighbour s]\nL 86'. On L § 304 »iudicium copulativum«:\nGod and one's neighbour are to be loved.\nGod has created all things and governs them.",[]],"refl:3089":["[Extraord] Compound judgments:\n1. of copulation: two predicates through one subject, or conversely;\n2. of consequence: conditional;\n3. of opposition [disjunction] and conjunction together: disjunctive.",[]],"refl:3090":["If the things of the world are absolutely necessary, then the world is God.\nIf the souls of fathers have no influence on the souls of their children, then there is no original sin.\n»This world, which was created according to the highest wisdom, is the best«: is not a conditional judgment.",[]],"refl:3091":["The condition can be false and the conditional judgment nevertheless true. If there is a fate, no actions are free.",[]],"refl:3092":["/\nThe soul is either a composite or a simple being.\nBird, eat or die.",[]],"refl:3093":["One must become either a philosopher or a pious man.\nOn L § 308 no. 2:\nOne must either conquer or die.\nThe souls of animals are either spirits or bodies.\nOn L § 308 no. 3:\nAll things are either bodies or spirits.\nAll machines are moved either by pressure or by impact or by themselves.",[]],"refl:3094":["[The relation] Judgments of relation consist of merely problematic [judgments]. *Iudicia extraordinaria*.\n*omne iudicium est vel problematicum vel assertorium* [every judgment is either problematic or assertoric]; the latter are those in which the predicate is regarded [as] as in actual connection with the subject. An example of the former is: God is corporeal.\nThe relation between two problematic judgments is either of connection or of conflict. From the latter arise disjunctive judgments, which can contain only two contradictorily opposed [judgments]. [They ex] Logical division which in its subdivisions omits the negative *membra* [members] is, like [the procedure] which in the chain of subordinate concepts omits the subject (in the *sorites*), only an abbreviated *series iudiciorum disiunctivorum* [series of disjunctive judgments]. For the [either] or, once posited, yields a *iudicium disiunctivum*.",[]],"refl:3095":["/\nAll [true] pure disjunction takes place only between *contradictorie opposita* [contradictory opposites].\nDisjunction between *disparata* [disparates] is a logical division whose [univ] correctness, [namely] that no member of the division has been omitted, can be known only *per inductionem* [by induction].\nIn a categorical judgment the thing [which as a] whose representation is regarded as a part of the *sphaera* of another, subordinated, representation [the representation which] is regarded as contained under this its higher concept. Here, therefore, in the subordination of the *sphaerae*, part of a part is compared with the whole. But in |\n[cat] disjunctive judgments I go from the whole to all the parts taken together: what is contained under the sphere of a concept is also contained under one of the parts of this [spha] *sphaera*. Accordingly the *sphaera* must first be divided. E.g. a scholar is either an historical scholar or a scholar of reason; by which I mean that these concepts are, as regards their *sphaera*, parts of the *sphaera* of scholars, but not parts of one another, and that all together they are complete.\nIf we regard a universal concept as a surface, in which the *conceptus communes* [common concepts] contained in it are again surfaces and the *singulares* [singular concepts] are points, then the question is whether the surface consists of surfaces or of points, that is, whether under a *notio communis* there is or is not contained a series of possible *subordinatio notionum communium* [subordination of common notions] to infinity. In the first case the *lex continui* [law of continuity] holds of forms, that is, of the *species sub aliqvo genere contentae* [species contained under some genus]. If a thing passes naturally from one *species* into another, it runs through all the *species intermediae* [intermediate species]. Further, if the world has a common cause, each *species* is possible only through the ground of the other *species* taken together. [in so far as] Hence the transitions of the understanding from one actual *species* to another are also in a flux; that is, there is a *continuum formarum* [continuum of forms] among actual things.",[]],"refl:3096":["In the disjunctive judgment it is not the *sphaera* of the divided |\nconcept that is regarded as contained in the *sphaera* of the divisions, but what is contained under the divided concept that is regarded as contained under one of the members of the divisions. E.g. a categorical judgment is: [all] x which is contained under b is also under a:  in the disjunctive: (g x) which is contained under a is contained either under b or under c, etc. etc.:\nThus division indicates the coordination not of the parts of the whole concept but of all the parts of its sphere. Here I think many things through one concept, but there [many] one thing through many concepts, e.g. the *definitum* through all the marks of coordination etc. etc. The question is: through how many higher concepts can a thing be thought, so that [their *sphaerae*] it is no longer contained under any *sphaera*?",[]],"refl:3097":["/\nEvery disjunction is a division of the *sphaera* [of a concept] of knowledge:\neither of a determinate concept\nor of truth in general.",[]],"refl:3098":["Whatever is contained under a part of a concept is also contained under the whole. Universal affirmative.\nWhatever is contained under a whole of a concept is also contained under one of the parts. Disjunction of the division of a concept; otherwise there is disjunction of mere opposition.",[]],"refl:3099":["Problematic judgments are those whose matter is given together with the possible relation between predicate and subject. In these the subject must always have a smaller *sphaera* than the predicate.",[]],"refl:3100":["Judgment and proposition [*Urtheil und Satz*] are distinct from one another. The judgment is the sense; the proposition is how the concepts are placed, whether in categorical |\nor hypothetical or disjunctive relation. One and the same judgment can be expressed in different propositions.\nLogical division of a concept or of knowledge in general.",[]],"refl:3101":["Disjunction of *opposita* or of *disparata*.",[]],"refl:3102":["The members of the disjunction are problematic judgments of which nothing else is thought than that, as parts of the sphere of a knowledge (each the *complementum ad totum* [complement to the whole] of the other), they are taken together equal to the sphere of the first; that is, that the truth must be contained in one of them, that is, that one of them must hold assertorically. [This is] because outside them the sphere of knowledge, under the given conditions, comprises nothing further, and each is opposed to the other; consequently there can be nothing else true outside them, nor more than one true among them.",[]],"refl:3103":["Thus both kinds of judgment are an assertion [from mere pr] of the relation of problematic judgments.",[]],"refl:3104":["In every disjunctive judgment each member determines every other, but |\nonly in so far as they all stand in community as parts of a whole sphere of knowledge, outside which, in a certain respect, nothing can be thought.",[]],"refl:3105":["A judgment is disjunctive when the parts of the sphere of a given concept determine one another as *complementa* in the whole or to a whole. So likewise in the real whole of substances, through the *commercium* [reciprocal interaction] in coexistence.",[]],"refl:3106":["All disjunctive judgments represent different judgments as in the community of one sphere, and produce each judgment only through the limitation of the others in respect of the whole sphere. They therefore determine the relation of each judgment to the whole sphere, and thereby their relation to one another.",[]],"refl:3107":["86'.\nL 86:\nAll division through mere concepts can only be dichotomy: either, or. The members determine one another reciprocally as *complementa* to a whole. If this determination is not logical, and the whole is not logical but real, they are substances *in commercio*.\nL 86'.\nThe reciprocal exclusion of the members of the disjunction, like that of the parts in a space, is no contradiction, except [it would be] if one were in the other.",[]],"refl:3108":["All dark heavenly bodies are either planets or comets or satellites. All the matters of the earth are animals or plants or minerals; the others are *vehicula* [vehicles].",[]],"refl:3109":["Polytomy is a subordinated dichotomy.",[]],"refl:3110":["All men are naturally mortal.\nFurther, L 87, on the concluding sentence of L § 309, from β1−ε2:\nThe opposite of all events in the world is in and for itself possible.",[]],"refl:3111":["Without modality no judgment at all is possible; hence the [judgment] modal judgment is not impure.\nAssertoric judgments alone are propositions.",[]],"refl:3112":["The affirmation takes place explicitly [*deutlich*], the negation covertly.\nOnly men are redeemed by Christ.\n1. Men are;\n2. Those who are not men: not.\nTo the left of the preceding three lines:\nFew men are pious:\nsome are;\nmany — — not.",[]],"refl:3113":["Is the vanity of the world indeed worth much trouble.",[]],"refl:3114":["/\nOpposite the beginning of L § 312:\nMathematical division aims at complete certainty.",[]],"refl:3115":["Practical judgments are either thought problematically (that is, practical in the logical sense), or categorically (without condition): moral [judgments]; or hypothetically: *imperativi* of skill.\nThe practical *imperativi* of mathematics are properly possible necessitations to actions: *imperativi possibiles* [possible imperatives].\nThe rules of prudence are *imperativi actuales* [actual imperatives], namely those in which [pure] inclination commands.\nThe rules of morality are *imperativi actuales*, namely those in which reason commands.",[]],"refl:3116":["/\nTheoretical propositions are those which relate to the object;\npractical propositions are those which* relate to the action whereby an object is brought about.\n*(s which determine (g state) the action whereby an object becomes possible, and which is therefore the necessary condition of the latter.)\nObjectively practical propositions contain the idea of the action whereby the object becomes possible;\nsubjectively practical propositions contain the conditions in the subject whereby it [the action] becomes possible. If there are in the subject conditions of the opposite, or if the idea is not sufficient for execution, the study of the subject, and so of the hindrance, must come first.\nSubjectively practical [propositions] are not needed where the idea at the same time contains the construction.",[]],"refl:3117":["Practical propositions (g modes of knowledge), as opposed to speculative ones, do not belong to logic.",[]],"refl:3118":["Practical propositions as regards their form: these are treated by logic, which distinguishes them from theoretical propositions.*\nPractical propositions as regards their content: these are treated by morals, which distinguishes them from speculative propositions.\n(g [They] are sources of practical propositions.)\n*(g These do not state what belongs to the *obiect*, but through what action a certain *obiect* is possible. Thus they express not the relation of modes of knowledge, but the relation of freedom to an object of knowledge which is possible through that [freedom]. Practical propositions are either rules (conditioned) or commands (unconditioned *imperativi*).)",[]],"refl:3119":["/\nMan is a rational being. Bodies are divisible.",[]],"refl:3120":["/\nMen are men (g wholly empty). Men are rational creatures with earthly organic bodies.\nOn L §. 314 »*iudicium ex parte identicum*« [judgment partly identical]:\nMen are rational beings.\nOn the penultimate sentence of L §. 314:\nWhat is possible has a ground.",[]],"refl:3121":["Virtue is an aptitude [*Fertigkeit*]. Whoever would become virtuous must acquire an aptitude.",[]],"refl:3122":["Virtue:\n3. Whoever is to practise virtue must be free.",[]],"refl:3123":["Whoever would practise virtue must acquire a freedom in his actions,\nthe rightfulness of these [actions],\naptitude.\nFurther, L 89, beside L §. 317 Nos. 1-3:\nWhoever would make a circle must move a straight line about a fixed point.",[]],"refl:3124":["Little brooks are the beginnings of great rivers.\nMathematical method.",[]],"refl:3125":["*identitas implicita vel explicita; posterior tavtologia* [identity is implicit or explicit; the latter is tautology].",[]],"refl:3126":["All *principia* are either formal or material. The latter are either analytic or synthetic; [nicht a] only in the case of empirical concepts, as in mathematics, can synthetic principles occur. Analytic principles arise in that the *praedicat* is always thought in the concept of the *subiect*, but [ver] *implicite*; synthetic principles, in that it [merely] belongs to it.",[]],"refl:3127":["/\nAnalytic propositions contain *praedicata logica* [logical predicates]; synthetic propositions contain *determinationes* [determinations]. The subjects of the former are concepts (*conceptus reflexi*), of the latter *conceptus abstracti*. For example: 1. A body is extended. 2. A body has attraction.\n(s Example of an analytic proposition.\nEvery body is extended: (s of a synthetic one — every body is heavy.)\nEvery x to which the concept of body (a+b) belongs, to it extension (b) also belongs.\nOf a synthetic one:\nEvery x to which the concept of body (a+b) belongs, to it attraction (c) also belongs.\nConsequently both propositions represent the identity of the predicate with the subject x. The first, however, [represents it] at the same time with the mark a+b of that subject; the second does not. Synthetic propositions increase knowledge *materialiter*, analytic propositions *formaliter*.)",[]],"refl:3128":["Every judgment means as much as this: everything to which the notion of the subject belongs, to it the predicate also belongs. This can come about in that the predicate is identical with the [subi] notion of the subject, as in analytic propositions, or also not, as in synthetic propositions. The |\nsubject is something x. Its notion is S, the predicate P; therefore [the text] breaks off.",[]],"refl:3129":["Judgment is a means to the distinctness of concepts. If this distinctness is not attained, for example, man is man, the judgment is empty.",[]],"refl:3130":["There are empty propositions of [Geschiklichkeit] intelligibility, of skill, of prudence and of morality. Those of skill: when what is given as the means is what was really the question of the problem, merely paraphrased; those of prudence: when what is commanded is what everyone wills in any case; those of morality: when exhortations are taken for moral instruction, without showing how their practice is to be furthered.\n(s Empty judgments are either void of sense or void of fruit. The latter are tautological.)",[]],"refl:3131":["The limit of *analysis* is tautology (battology).",[]],"refl:3132":["(s Synthetic (g intuitive) judgments *a priori*.)\nAxioms are *iudicia intuitiva a priori* [intuitive judgments *a priori*]. (g The principle of contra|diction\nis an *iudicium discursivum*.) *Principia formalia sunt axiomata, materialia sunt propositiones elementares* [formal principles are axioms, material principles are elementary propositions].\n(s Analytic *principia* are not axioms, because they are discursive.\nSynthetic [principles] too are axioms only if they are intuitive. Thus philosophy has no axioms, but [only] acroamatic principles.)",[]],"refl:3133":["A *postulat* is, properly, a practical, immediately [noth] certain proposition. But one can also have theoretical postulates (g on behalf of practical reason), namely a theoretical hypothesis necessary for the purposes of practical reason, such as that of the existence of God, of freedom and of another world. Practical propositions are objectively certain; subjectively they can become practical only in so far as that hypothesis lies at their basis.",[]],"refl:3134":["Practical postulates and discursive knowledge. For example: that what it is a duty to do can also be done.",[]],"refl:3135":["An axiom is an immediately certain intuitive judgment *a priori*.",[]],"refl:3136":["/\nAnalytic judgments all rest on identity.\n*Iudicium inane* [empty judgment].",[]],"refl:3137":["Void of sense, or void of understanding (s[Gedanken] void of consequence); the first are [judgments] of *qvalitatibus occultis* [occult qualities], the second are tautological judgments.",[]],"refl:3138":["Intuitive judgments, when they contain abstracted concepts, are not sensations. Only individual things are sensed.\nFurther, L 89:\nI am cold (g warm), iron is heavy. [Das Licht macht die Körper sichtbar] is a judgment of subsequent reflection [*Nachurtheil*].\nFurther, from ψ−θ:\n2×2=4.\nFurther, likewise from ψ−θ:\neither of empirical or of pure intuition.",[]],"refl:3139":["On the penultimate sentence of L §. 319:\nThere are spots in the sun.\nFurther, L 89:\nMediate experiences are demonstrable. For example: iron is heavy, gold the heaviest.",[]],"refl:3140":["*iudicia intuitiva sunt synthetica, e.g. intuitus puri.\nanalytica sunt discursiva, vel demonstrabilia vel indemonstrabilia* [intuitive judgments are synthetic, for example those of pure intuition; analytic judgments are discursive, either demonstrable or indemonstrable].",[]],"refl:3141":["In order that it may become distinct. For example, we do not see mountain and valley in the moon; we infer them.\n*caveatur vitium subreptionis* [let the fault of subreption be guarded against].\nFurther, L 90, from β1−ε2, beside L §. 320 Nos. 2, 3:\nHeat expands bodies.",[]],"refl:3142":["I do not experience that gold is denser than iron, but that it is heavier.\nFurther, L 90, from β1−ε2:\nNot divisibility.\nL 90', from β1, on L §. 321 sentence 2:\nI do not sense that the moon has no light of its own.",[]],"refl:3143":["First, of some.\nAll bodies become denser through cold.\nOn L §. 322 Nos. 2a, b, c:\nBodies.\nThe cohesion of the parts, fire.\nNecessary.\nFurther, L 90, from β1−ε2, beside L §. 322 Nos. 2a, b, c:\nA triangle has 3 angles which amount to 360°; therefore all [triangles do].",[]],"refl:3144":["All things (g of the world) are mutable.\nFurther, L 90, from β1−ε2:\nFor example, that man is mortal.\nβ1. L 90'. On the final sentence of L §. 323:\n»Air is elastic«: is a judgment of subsequent reflection [*Nachurtheil*].",[]],"refl:3145":["A judgment from mere perceptions is hardly possible, save in that I declare my representation to be a perception. I who perceive a tower perceive in it the red colour. But I cannot say: it is red; for that would be not merely an empirical judgment but also a judgment of experience, that is, an empirical judgment through which I obtain a concept of the *Object*. For example, »On touching the stone I sense warmth« is the former; but »the stone is warm« is the latter. — It belongs to the latter that I do not count as belonging to the *Object* what is merely in my subject; for a judgment of experience is the (g perception) from which a concept of the *Object* |\narises. For example, [the question] whether points of light are moving in the moon, or in the air, or in my eye.",[]],"refl:3146":["A judgment of perception* is merely subjective; an (g objective) judgment from perceptions is a judgment of experience.\n*(g »I sense warmth on touching the stove« is perception; »the stove is warm« is a judgment of experience.)",[]],"refl:3147":["Virtue is attained through practice.\nFurther, from β1−ε2:\nOf the triangle, the lesser Pythagorean theorem [*theorema Pythagoricum minus*].\n*Corollarium* I. Therefore no triangle can have more than 1 right angle.\nII. Therefore if two angles are given, the third is given.",[]],"refl:3148":["The method of philosophy is zetetic, that of mathematics heuristic. Hence the form of the edifice with its divisions, for example *Definitio*, *Axioma* etc. etc., in mathematics is not suited to philosophy.",[]],"refl:3149":["or to base something old on a new proof.\nβ1−ε2. L 91'. Opposite L §. 326 No. 2a:\nGod is not material.",[]],"refl:3150":["to frame an explanation.",[]],"refl:3151":["How learning may be drummed in.\nOn L §. 329 No. 1, from β1−ε2:\nTo raise water so that it may [nütz] drive mills.\nβ1. L 92. On L §. 329 No. 3:\nTo burn with ice.\nFurther, L 92':\nFor example, the shooting star.\nOn L §. 329 No. 4:\nThunder.\nThe cheerful hour.",[]],"refl:3152":["How to make an airship.\nHow one is to practise philosophical self-denial: when one forgoes a lesser good in order to obtain a greater.\nFurther, from β1−ε2:\nTo make water spout.\nOn L §. 330 No. 2, from β1:\nmedicine. Gunpowder.\nAir-gun.",[]],"refl:3153":["For example, to explain how the human soul comes into being.\nOn the second half of L §. 331:\nIf I ask how a sensuous, [or] a spiritual pleasure arises, I can also ask how pleasure in general arises.\nIf I know how to make a crane, [or] a windlass, I also know how to make a machine in general that lifts loads.\nFurther, L 93, beside the end of L §. 331, from γ−λ (? ν? ρ??):\nTo make a burning-mirror.",[]],"refl:3154":["To make a clock.\nFurther, L 93':\nIn the case of the northern lights: the light. The position. The time. The motion.",[]],"refl:3155":["When it is composite.\nβ1. L 93'. On L §. 333 No. 1:\nFor example, how one is to love one's [Nächsten] enemy. 1. the man. Then the enemy.\nOn L §. 333 No. 2:\nIf one knows how pleasure arises, one also knows how rational [pleasure] arises.",[]],"refl:3156":["To prevent earthquakes.\nOn L §. 334 No. 2:\nFor example, to examine the parts of the blood.\nFurther, L 93', from β1−ε2:\nTo make water spout.\nβ1. L 93. On L §. 334 No. 3:\nDifferent ways make many solutions.\nFurther, L 93', from β1−ε2:\nTo lift a load, even if it [the machine] also moves it rapidly.",[]],"refl:3157":["For example, happiness through the extirpation of the affects.\nL 93'. On L §. 335 No. 2:\nFor example, the Platonic republic; the proposals of the Abbé St. Pierre.\nL 94'. On L §. 335 No. 3:\nFor example, to drive away fever with little slips of paper.\nOn L §. 335 No. 4:\nEpicureans. *ede, bibe, lude* [eat, drink, play].",[]],"refl:3158":["Pray. Not sufficient for a happy temporal life.\nOn L §. 336 No. 2:\nWhen perfect wisdom is prescribed for the attainment of happiness.\nOn L §. 336 No. 3:\nNot magnifying glasses in the whole of physics.\nFurther, L 94, from β1−ε2:\nNot water-mills everywhere.",[]],"refl:3159":["The rabbinical [applied] to philosophy.\nFurther, L 94, from β1−ε2:\nFor example, the old [methods of] attacking fortresses.",[]],"refl:3160":["When the thing to be explained is taken as the cause.\n*vis plastica* [formative power]. (s instinct, *horror vacui*, sense of truth. (g idiosyncrasy.) The manifold feelings of Home: feeling of right, feeling of honour, the sympathetic [feeling]. The *consensus* of the nerves [assumed by] physicians. The *miasmata*. — Sympathy. Antipathy. Oil and water.)\nβ1−ε2. L 94. On L §. 338 sentence 2:\nAlchemistic.",[]],"refl:3161":["What is the cause of moral satisfaction, as distinct from all self-interest? The moral feeling.",[]],"refl:3162":["*qvalitates occultae** are tautological explanations of appearances, just as empty solutions of problems are tautological imperatives.\nMorals [der Ausüb] applied.\n*(s is tautology in the (citing) determination of the cause.)",[]],"refl:3163":["In logic, *principium rationis* [the principle of sufficient reason].\nFurther, L 94, from ψ−θ:\nEpisodic part.",[]],"refl:3164":["One must borrow [propositions]; but only what has previously been proved, and not what is borrowed from that very thing which one seeks to infer from it. For example, I wish to prove the immaterial nature of the soul and assume immortality as a borrowed proposition [*Lehnsatz*].",[]],"refl:3165":["Use. Elucidation. History. Objections.\nFurther, L 94, from ψ−θ:\nbut always a non-essential part.",[]],"refl:3166":["In *consequentiis immediatis* [immediate inferences] there is no middle concept.",[]],"refl:3167":["are not consequences.\nFurther, ψ−θ:\n*Conseqventiae immediatae* [immediate inferences].",[]],"refl:3168":["*Conseqventiae immediatae*. They show merely the form of the inference, or the derivation of one judgment from another (g without subsumption). |\nWhen the subsumption of a concept [da] through judgment is added, the knowledge of another thing is inferred from a given knowledge. For example, »All men are mortal, consequently some [sind] mortals are men« shows only that subsumption under the predicate yields a particularity, and therefore no syllogism *ab universali* [from the universal].",[]],"refl:3169":["Inferences of the understanding can be transformed into syllogisms only through tautological subsumptions.\nThe derivation of one judgment from another from which it differs merely in form, but is the same in content or matter.\n(s Difference: 1. Merely in quantity. 2. [blos] in quality (g and quantity (perhaps)). 3. in the relation of the subject to the predicate, quality being retained. 4. in this same relation, with change of quality but retention of quantity.)",[]],"refl:3170":["95'.\nL 95:\nThe *conseqventiae immediatae*, as immediate, that is, inferences of the understanding (g they proceed only from categorical judgments), run through all the classes of the categories.\nThose *per iudicia subalternata*\nbelong to quantity.\nThose *per iudicia opposita*\nbelong to quality.\nThose *per conversionem iudicii*\nbelong to relation.\nThose *per contrapositionem iudicii*\nbelong to modality: to make an apodeictic judgment out of an assertoric one.\nL 95':\nAll bodies are divisible (assertoric). Therefore what is not divisible is not a body (apodeictic judgment, because it is self-contradictory to be a body and not divisible, and a proposition whose opposite contradicts itself is apodeictic.)\n(s One can also use an empirical proposition as an example. For example, all men are mortal. For, this being presupposed, the proposition 'what is not mortal is not a man' is apodeictically expressed.)",[]],"refl:3171":["Under the universal [judgment] all the particular and singular ones are contained,\nbut not all the singular ones under the particular.\nTo the end of L §. 342 Kant adds:\n3. One cannot infer: if the universal judgment is false, then the particular and singular judgments are also false; for the predicate may be merely a particular concept in respect of the subject. For example, it is false that all scholars are pious, but true that some are.\n4. Not from particular judgments and their truth to universal ones.",[]],"refl:3172":["*particulariter negans* [particular negative].\nOn »E«:\n*vniversaliter negans* [universal negative].\nOn »I«:\n*particulariter affirmans* [particular affirmative].\nBeside L §. 343 sentence 2, from µ−φ:\nAccording to the *principium exclusi Medii* [principle of excluded middle].",[]],"refl:3173":["In singular contradictory judgments the subject must be the very same: this world is the best.\nFurther, L 95':\nIf *judicia communia* [common judgments] are to contradict one another, then one must affirm universally and the other deny particularly, or [one] deny universally and the other affirm particularly.\nAll the virtues of the heathen are true virtues: some are not.",[]],"refl:3174":["One judgment affirms of a concept what the other denies, but not in the degree in which the former affirms it and [the latter] denies it, or in a greater [degree].",[]],"refl:3175":["Some men are pious, some not pious.\nFurther, L 96, from κ−θ:\nnot enough for opposition.",[]],"refl:3176":["Scoffers at religion: some clergymen are hypocrites, therefore it is false that many were not hypocrites.\nOn L §. 344 No. 2:\nIf it is false that some are pious, then all men must |\nbe godless; for then the limitation is removed from the predicate, and then it runs: all men are (g not) pious, and: some are pious.",[]],"refl:3177":["All men are learned, no man is learned.\nsome —— —\nsome not.\nOn L §. 345 No. 2:\nFor what is denied of all is also denied of some.\nFor example, all men can err,\nno man can err.\nsome men cannot err.\nκ−θ. L 96. Beside L §. 345 »allgemeine — sein« (ll. 30f.):\ntoo much for opposition.\nµ−φ? (η?) L 96. On L §. 345 No. 2:\ntoo much for opposition.",[]],"refl:3178":["Where I cannot say *Aut Aut* [either–or], that is, where both can be true or [both] false, there is no true *opposition*.",[]],"refl:3179":["We have to do only with the conversion of categorical judgments.",[]],"refl:3180":["The *metathesis terminorum*, or transposition [of the terms], is entitled conversion only when the quality remains the same.\nIt is *contrapositio* when merely the quantity remains the same.",[]],"refl:3181":["but, regarded merely as affirmative judgments, only the particular ones can [be so converted].\nFor the predicate is the *conceptus latior* [wider concept]; therefore only some of those contained under it can belong to the subject.",[]],"refl:3182":["When I say: »some men are learned«, this means as much as: »they occupy a part in the *sphaera* of the learned«; therefore a part of these learned is the same as men. This conversion is an identical proposition.",[]],"refl:3183":["One can infer thus:\nTo all those to which the [subiect] predicate of the negative judgment belongs, the subject is repugnant. For example, no man is infallible, and no one who is infallible is a man.",[]],"refl:3184":["in that they are changed into particular [judgments]. All triangles are figures; some figures are triangles.",[]],"refl:3185":["[Alle] In all affirmative judgments the subject is particular in respect of the predicate; for since the latter is the *notio latior* [wider notion], the [ist das sub] subject signifies only some of the things contained under the universal concept of the predicate. Hence men are some of the mortals, that is, some mortals are men; whereas the mortals are in everything that is entitled man.",[]],"refl:3186":["The predicate is [bet] the *conceptus latior*; therefore only something of it is contained under the notion of the subject.",[]],"refl:3187":["Some men are not learned. Some are unlearned. Therefore some unlearned [beings] are men.\nOtherwise not: some men are not learned men, and some learned men are not men.\nOne can always infer thus: to some to which the predicate does not belong, [sind] the subject belongs.",[]],"refl:3188":["All men are rational beings. [Einige die] What is not a rational being is not a man.",[]],"refl:3189":["Contraposition [*Entgegenstellung*].\nFor if the predicate, as that which contains the subject under it, and therefore the whole sphere, is denied, a part of that [sphere] is also denied, that is, the subject also.",[]],"refl:3190":["Just as concepts are combined, so also are judgments.\nThe grounds of proof with the propositions proved.\nL 99'. On L §. 353 »*nexus veritatum*« [connexion of truths]:\nIs entitled an inference. All animals infer. When my master looks for the stick, he means to beat [me]; *atqvi* [but]: *ergo*.\nA man who keeps to the twelfth hour as mealtime.",[]],"refl:3191":["Distinction from the aesthetic.",[]],"refl:3192":["Why is the world the best? Answer: because God has chosen it.\nOn L §. 355 sentence 2:\n1. The condition. 2. The derivation from this condition.",[]],"refl:3193":["Only the *Maior*; for the *Minor* is *Subsumtio*.",[]],"refl:3194":["Opposite 704, line 19:\n*Conseqventia immediata est [transitus a iudicio derivatio] deductio iudicii ab alio absqve iudicio intermedio* [an immediate inference is the deduction of one judgment from another without an intermediate judgment].",[]],"refl:3195":["/\nAll syllogisms contain the consequence of one judgment from another by means of a universal *principium*, which is the major premiss. They are entitled inferences of reason [*Vernunftschlüsse*] for this very reason, that the derivation takes place *a priori*, that is, according to a universal principle.\nThe syllogism premises a universal rule and a subsumption under its condition. Through it the *conclusion* is known *a priori*, not in the singular, but as contained in the universal and as necessary under a certain condition.\nThat everything stands under the universal and is determinable in universal rules is the *principium rationalitatis sive necessitatis* [principle of rationality or of necessity].",[]],"refl:3196":["The syllogism is the consciousness of the necessity of a mode of knowledge through the subsumption (g of the condition) of it under a universal rule.\nThe *Maior* is the universal rule, which says something under a condition. The *Minor* is the subsumption under the condition of the rule. The *conclusio* says of the subsumed concept just what the rule said of the condition.",[]],"refl:3197":["A syllogism is the mediate inference of a proposition from a universal rule.\nThe mediating proposition is (g subsumption) that which subsumes something under the condition of the rule. Matter and form —\nThey are categorical, hypothetical or disjunctive. The former alone have *maiorem*, *minorem* and *medium terminum*, and are the matter of the others.",[]],"refl:3198":["A syllogism is a [Erkenntnis] judgment *a priori* through subsumption of [ihrer] its condition under (g the condition of) a universal rule.",[]],"refl:3199":["Division of syllogisms into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive.\nFor all rules (judgments) contain (g objective) unity of consciousness of the manifold of knowledge, and therefore a condition under which one mode of knowledge belongs with another to one consciousness.\nThe rule is the *Major*. The subsumption of a mode of knowledge under its condition is the *minor*, etc. etc. There are only three conditions of this unity:\nSubject of the inherence of the marks.\nGround of the dependence of one mode of knowledge on another.\nCombination of the parts in a whole.\nLogical division.\nHence there are as many *maiores propositiones*.\n(s Categorical syllogisms alone infer *per medium terminum* (g in them alone is the *maior propositio* assertoric, in the |\nothers problematic); the others [infer] only through the subsumption of a proposition represented problematically in the *maior* and assertorically in the *minor*.)",[]],"refl:3200":["1) Inferences of the understanding (s are immediate inferences) proceed from the universal to the particular, or from the particular to the particular (s but immediately), never, however, from the particular to the universal, since they are meant to yield determining judgments.\n2) Inferences of judgment proceed from the particular to the (s empirically) universal (s they are ways of advancing from *individuis* to *generibus*): from some things that belong to a certain species to all, or from some properties in which things of the same species agree to the remaining ones, in so far as these belong to the same principle. They are nothing but ways of arriving at universal concepts from particular ones. They thus belong to reflective (s not determining) judgment, and so serve not to determine the *obiect*, but only the mode [über] of reflection upon it, in order to attain to knowledge of it. (s They are inferences for arriving at provisional, not determining, judgments. — Analogy and induction.)\nAre inferences of judgment immediate inferences? No; a principle of judgment lies at their basis, namely that many [things] will not agree in one [respect] without a common ground, and that therefore what belongs to them in this manner will be necessary from a common ground. (s — Analogy, induction.)\n(s 3. Syllogisms are (g mediate) inferences from the universal to the particular *per iudicium intermedium*.)",[]],"refl:3201":["A syllogism is the knowledge of the necessity of a proposition through the subsumption of its condition under a [allge] given universal rule. — What stands under the condition of a rule (g the subject of the conclusion) stands also under the rule itself (g the predicate of the conclusion).",[]],"refl:3202":["A rule is an *assertion* under a universal condition. The relation of the condition to the *assertion*, namely how the latter stands under the former, is the *exponent* of the rule.\nThe knowledge that the condition [sta] (somewhere) obtains is the subsumption.\nThe combination of what has been subsumed (g under the condition) with the *assertion* of the rule is the conclusion.\nSyllogisms are as manifold (in respect of their *conclusionen*) as judgments (affirmative, universal, etc.).",[]],"refl:3203":["Because the predicate is for the most part more universal, and consequently greater. For example, all vice makes men unhappy.\nOn L §. 357 »*terminus medius*«:\nFor the lesser or greater principal concept cannot be the ground of proof.",[]],"refl:3204":["Demonstrable judgments are never convincingly true without the condition.",[]],"refl:3205":["I proceed from the universal to the particular, and consequently from P to the *subiect*, *per notam intermediam*, thus: P. M. S.",[]],"refl:3206":["The *Major propositio* is not so entitled because the *terminus major* is in it, but because it is the rule. For the distinction of the *termini* obtains only in categorical syllogisms.",[]],"refl:3207":["Both together make up the whole syllogism.",[]],"refl:3208":["Either when a premiss is false, or 2.\nor [when] the conclusion does not follow from the premisses. *Homo habet pedes, Asinus* — — — [a man has feet, an ass — — —].\nWhen both are false.",[]],"refl:3209":["From false judgments nothing true can ever be correctly inferred, but it can well be from their partial truth. For example: All souls of men are immortal;\n— Angels are souls of men;\n*Ergo* ...\n*Conditio veritatis* [condition of truth]: they are spirits.",[]],"refl:3210":["The argumentation may be correct (form), although from incorrect grounds (matter).",[]],"refl:3211":["/\n*ratiocinium est vel purum vel hybridum (in qvo conclusio immediata immixta est)* [a syllogism is either pure or hybrid (one in which an immediate inference is intermixed)].",[]],"refl:3212":["Uncertain syllogisms.\nOn L §. 361 sentence 2:\nWhy it is believed that logical order is not necessary.",[]],"refl:3213":["is a *principium formale* of propositions, not of inferences.",[]],"refl:3214":["What holds of all men holds of some and of one.\nψ−θ. L 101. On L §. 363 »der Schluss — Besondere« (l. 23):\nHolds in all inferences.",[]],"refl:3215":["{S16_716.SHG}\nIn this figure  lie two kinds of inference:\nAll C are B; all A are C; consequently all A are B. 1st figure.\nAll A are C; all C are B; consequently some B are A. 4th figure.\n Herein lie the following:\nAll C are B; some A are C. *Ergo*: some A are B.",[]],"refl:3216":["The logical relation of all [Urt] concepts is that the one is contained under the *sphaera notionis* of the [subiects] other:  The |\nmetaphysical relation consists in whether the one is combined with the other synthetically or analytically:\nsynthetically\nanalytically",[]],"refl:3217":["*Nota notae est nota rei ipsius* [a mark of a mark is a mark of the thing itself] (g is the *principium* of categorical inferences): this is the *dictum* and at the same time the ground of the *prima figura*.",[]],"refl:3218":["The principle of categorical syllogisms is the *dictum de omni et nullo*: *Nota notae est nota rei ipsius: repugnans notae* etc. etc.\nThat of hypothetical [syllogisms] is: *a ratione ad rationatum valet conseqventia, a negatione rationati ad negationem rationis itidem* [the inference from ground to consequence is valid, likewise from the denial of the consequence to the denial of the ground].\nThat of disjunctive [syllogisms] is: *a contradictorie oppositorum negatione vnius* (g *ad affirmationem alterius*) *valet conseqventia et [viceversa] a positione vnius* etc. [from the denial of one of contradictory opposites to the affirmation of the other the inference is valid, and from the positing of one etc.] (not as in hypothetical [syllogisms], where [nur (g der) umgek] only one mode of inference held in the conversion).",[]],"refl:3219":["Three principles for the 3 kinds of inference: 1. *Principium contradictionis*. 2. *rationis* 3. *exclusi medii* — —",[]],"refl:3220":["The three sides of a triangle are the sufficient ground of the angles.\nTherefore I can say: where the sufficient ground is met with ....\nOn L §. 364 sentence 2 »so — falsch«:\nfor otherwise, were it true, two mutually opposed consequences would be true at the same time.\nBut where the sufficient ground is not, one cannot always say that the consequence is not. For the sufficient ground may be twofold. All men have sensations; here, therefore, I cannot infer: what is not a man has no sensations.",[]],"refl:3221":["All the members of the disjunction save one, taken together, make up the *contradictorie oppositum* of this one; thus [there is] a dichotomy, in which it holds that if one of the two is true, the other is false, and conversely.",[]],"refl:3222":["All men are sinners; some sinners are men; no one who is not a sinner is a mere man.",[]],"refl:3223":["/\nAll men can err;\nLeibniz was a man;\nTherefore Leibniz could err.",[]],"refl:3224":["Take the *medium terminum* as subject, and affirm of it the predicate of the conclusion. Take the subject of the conclusion, and affirm of it the *medium terminum*. |\n|\n|\n|\n|\n| that in which the ground is met with contains also what is grounded. On L 102, after »gemein haben« (l. 26), the following words, deriving from σ−φ (? ω?), are to be inserted:\nThe extraordinary [inferences] have one or more judgments in common in their premisses.\nβ1. L 102'. Opposite L §. 368 »der — pro« (ll. 27f.):\nThis must be so in all *ratiociniis*.",[]],"refl:3225":["1. There are only 2 *praemissae* in which the *medius terminus* is contained.\n2. That *praemissa* in which the *terminus medius est loco subiecti* [the middle term is in the place of the subject] is the *maior*, and the predicate is affirmed of it.",[]],"refl:3226":["No thinking substance is composite.\nAll bodies are composite.\nTherefore no body is a thinking [wesen] substance.",[]],"refl:3227":["All [gefallene] natural men sin.\nSome natural men are [gelehrt vernünft] prudent.\nTherefore [sündigen] some prudent [men] sin.\nFurther, L 103':\nNo triangle has [4] more than 3 angles.\nsome triangles are [rechtwinklichte Figuren] equilateral figures.\nTherefore some equilateral figures have not more than 3 angles.\nFurther, L 102, from ψ−χ (? µ??):\nAll men are [fehlerhaft] imperfect.\nSome men are virtuous.\nSome virtuous [men] are imperfect.",[]],"refl:3228":["*Omnis spiritus est substantia.\nOmnis substantia habet vim agendi.\nQvoddam, qvod habet vim agendi, est spiritus.* [Every spirit is a substance. Every substance has a power of acting. Something which has a power of acting is a spirit.]\nFurther, ψ−χ (? µ??):\n*Reductio*.\n*Omnis substantia habet vim agendi.\nOmnis spiritus est substantia.\nErgo: Omnis spiritus habet vim agendi.\nErgo: Qvoddam, qvod habet vim agendi, est spiritus.*\nFurther, ψ−χ:\nIf the reduction were to take place through the [invers] conversion of the premisses:\n*Qvaedam substantia est spiritus.\n[Omnis substantia habet] Omne, qvod vim agendi habet, est substantia.\nErgo*: then nothing would follow.\nFurther, ψ−θ:\n*Qvicqvid non est substantia, non est spiritus.\nQvicqvid non habet vim agendi, est non substantia.\nErgo qvicqvid non habet vim etc etc., non est spiritus.* [Whatever is not a substance is not a spirit. Whatever has no power of acting is a non-substance. Therefore whatever has no power etc. etc. is not a spirit.]",[]],"refl:3229":["In the final sentence of L §. 372 Kant adds the following letters: over »dummer«: P, over »gelehrt«: M, over »Gelehrte«: M, over »fromm«: S, over »Fromme«: S, over »dummen«: P.\nBeside the final sentence of L §. 372 and the beginning of L §. 373 stand the following schemata (probably also deriving from ι-φ):\nPM\nMP /\nMS\nSM /\nSP\nSP",[]],"refl:3230":["When, as here, the *maior* is *negans* and the *minor particulariter affirmans*, the premisses can be converted. If it [the *maior*] is affirmative, a *metathesis praemissarum* must take place.",[]],"refl:3231":["In the 4th figure the reasoning runs: the predicate depends on the *medio termino*, the *medius terminus* depends on the subject, therefore the subject on the predicate, which is false. It must rather run: the predicate [depends] on the subject, and this proposition must afterwards be converted.",[]],"refl:3232":["In the fourth figure the inference is: the predicate depends on the |\n*medio termino*, the *medius terminus* on the subject (of the conclusion), consequently the subject on the predicate. This does not follow at all; at most its converse does. [Es] To make this possible, the *maior* must be made the *minor* and *vice versa*, and the conclusion must be converted, since by the first alteration the *minor terminus* is changed into the *major*.",[]],"refl:3233":["(s Reduction takes place either *per metathesin terminorum* or [*per metathesin*] *praemissarum*.)",[]],"refl:3234":["It is necessary to the *synthesis* [nicht blos] that the predicate of the conclusion be in the first premiss; and in this *synthesis* lies the sole ground [of the order of the figures], without which it would be arbitrary, and there would then be more than four figures (g namely 8 *per metathesin*). But this *synthesis* absolutely requires that the predicate of the conclusion[d] be predicated first of the *medio termino*, and this in turn of the subject in the *minor*.",[]],"refl:3235":["/\n└",[]],"refl:3236":["The subject is always particular or *inferior* in respect of the predicate. Therefore the predicate must be taken universally in the *maior*.\nIn the second figure P is particular. It must therefore be capable of being raised to universality, of becoming *superior*.\nIn the third, S is universal; it must be capable of being lowered to particularity.",[]],"refl:3237":["»*Maior universalis, Minor affirmans*« means that the predicate of the conclusion must be a *conceptus [maior] superior* to the *medius terminus*. (s The subject depends on the predicate by means of the *medii termini*.)",[]],"refl:3238":["The proposition »*ars non habet osorem*« [art has no hater] etc. etc. has the effect that one cannot reject this *artificium characteristicum* [characteristic device].",[]],"refl:3239":["The *medius terminus* must take the place of a *nota intermedia* [intermediate mark], and therefore stand first *loco subiecti*, secondly *loco praedicati*. Hence the 1st figure tacitly underlies all *syllogismen*.\n(g NB. *Nota notae est nota rei ipsius*: is the principle of the first figure, and yet at the same time of all syllogisms.)",[]],"refl:3240":["/\n*ratiocinium vel purum vel hybridum. [Conclusio] Conseqventia immediata per metathesin terminorum vel conversionem vel contrapositionem* [a syllogism is either pure or hybrid. An immediate inference [takes place] through transposition of the terms, either by conversion or by contraposition].",[]],"refl:3241":["A fox is a four-footed animal.\nHerod was a fox.\n*Ergo* ...\n*In prima propositione proprie, in secunda improprie* [in the first proposition [the term is taken] literally, in the second figuratively].\n[Der Glaube rechtfertigt ohne Werke.\nDer Glaube]\nThe place in which I am contains no one else.\n(g This room is) The place in which I am. [ist die Stube.]\nTherefore there is no one else in this room.\nσ−φ? (ω?) L 103. On L §. 374 »In — drei« (729, ll. 22-24):\nOtherwise the syllogism goes on 4 feet.",[]],"refl:3242":["Because otherwise there would be 4 *termini*.\nAll affects good in themselves become [verderbt sündi] faulty through the weakness of men.\nParental love is an affect good in itself.\nTherefore parental love in itself becomes faulty through the weakness of men.",[]],"refl:3243":["No man is an [Vieh] irrational animal.\nNo ox is a man.\nTherefore no ox is an irrational animal.\nFurther, L 104, from β1−ε2:\n*ex puris negantibus nihil seqvitur* [from purely negative premisses nothing follows].",[]],"refl:3244":["(s Example of the *iudicium infinitum* [infinite judgment]:)\nWhat is not composite is not corporeal.\nThe soul is not composite.\nTherefore it is not body.",[]],"refl:3245":["For according to the rule of regular inferences, one must infer from a universal to a universal or to a particular.\nFor example: Some sentient beings are men.\nSome irrational animals are sentient beings.\nTherefore some irrational animals are men.\nFurther, L 104, from ε2−ψ (? β1??):\n*ex puris particularibus nihil seqvitur* [from purely particular premisses nothing follows].",[]],"refl:3246":["According to the rule: what is universally denied of a concept is also denied of what belongs under it.\nNow this [Begriff] thing belongs under the concept; therefore it is denied of it.\nFurther, L 104, from µ−φ:\n*Conclusio seqvitur partem debiliorem* [the conclusion follows the weaker part].",[]],"refl:3247":["To all things that belong under [diesen] a concept, this [predicate] or its opposite pertains.\nNow some things belong under the concept; therefore ...\ne.g. All who persevere in faith will be saved.\nSome men are — — —\n*Ergo* ...",[]],"refl:3248":["All quadrilateral rectangular figures are parallelograms.\nA figure that has four equal angles is a quadrilateral rectangular figure.\nConsequently a figure that has four angles is a parallelogram.",[]],"refl:3249":["All virtues make me happy.\nThe philosophical virtues are virtues.\nConsequently they make me happy.",[]],"refl:3250":["If I say that it does not belong under it, the denial of the predicate does not thereby follow. E.g. all angels are [Geister] rational; men are not angels; therefore [they are] not rational.\nL 105'. On L § 383, end:\nWhoever is not virtuous will not be happy.\nNow no vicious person is virtuous.",[]],"refl:3251":["For if this were particular, the conclusion would also have to be so.\nOn L § 384, ‘therefore must — be’ (l. 29):\nfor were it also a particular [proposition], then *ex puris particularibus* [from purely particular premisses] ...\nFurther, L 105', opposite L § 384, sentence 2, ‘is it — be’ (ll. 28 f.):\nSome men are godless.\nAll believers are men;\nconsequently all [are] godless.\nL 105. On L § 384, end:\nGod is almighty.\nChrist is God.",[]],"refl:3252":["*qvia conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem* [because the conclusion follows the weaker part].\nβ1−ε2. Beside L § 386, sentence 2:\nUniversal\naffirmative.\n— — —\nnegative.\nParticular\naffirmative.\n— — —\nnegative.",[]],"refl:3253":["No composite being is imperishable.\nAll matter is a composite being.\nTherefore\nno matter [ist] is imperishable.",[]],"refl:3254":["All beings that think are spirits.\nSome substances are thinking beings.\nSome substances are spirits.",[]],"refl:3255":["(s In the first [figure]. (g *Medius terminus* [middle term]) The position of a *nota intermedia* [intermediate mark].)\nIn the second figure: *major* universal, conclusion negative.\nWhat justifies is pleasing to God.\nAll good works [rechtfertigen] are pleasing to God.\n*ergo* — —\nÙ Some silver is money.\nÖ\nÜ No cup is money.\nÜ\nÛ Therefore no cups are\nØ\nIn the third: *minor* affirmative, conclusion particular.\nEvery spirit is substance.\nNo spirit is a body.\nTherefore some bodies are not substances.\nEvery spirit is simple.\nEvery spirit is substance.\nTherefore every substance is simple.\nIn the fourth: [maj] if the *major* is affirmative, the *minor* is universal.\n(s If the *major* is universal, the conclusion (g will be) negative.)\nIf the *minor* is affirmative, the conclusion is particular.\nIf the conclusion is negative, the *major* is universal (s drops out).\nEvery spirit is substance.\nSome substance is body.\nSome body is [subst] spirit.\n(s The conclusion either negative or particular.*)\n*(s This follows from the foregoing, because the *major* must be converted as in the second figure, and the *minor* must also be converted as in the third. The former, however, is possible only if the *major* is negative (s since a universal proposition is to follow). If it is affirmative, a *metathesis praemissarum* [transposition of the premisses] must take place. (For otherwise the *major* would become particular.) The latter is possible only if the *minor* is affirmative; for by conversion it yields an affirmative proposition.)\n(s Therefore in none of the three remaining figures [is there] a universal affirmative conclusion.)",[]],"refl:3256":["/\n(s Of 64, 28 drop out; 36 remain.)\n*modi possibiles* [possible moods]: 64.*\n│\nA a a\n│\ne, a a\n│\ni a\n│\no a\n│\n│\n— a e\n│\na e\n│\n│\n│\n│\na i\n│\na i\n│\n│\n│\n│\na o\n│\na o\n│\n│\n│\n│\ne a\n│\ne e a\n│\n│\n│\n│\ne e\n│\ne e e\n│\n│\n│\n│\ne i\n│\ne e i\n│\n│\n│\n│\ne o\n│\ne e o\n│\n│\n│\n│\ni a\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\ni e\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\ni i\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\ni o\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\no a\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\no e\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\no i\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\no o\n│\n│\n│\n│\n*modi* 10: *utiles* [valid].\nthereafter [16].\nI.**\nBarbara, Celarent, Darii, Ferio.\nII. cesare, camestres, festino, barocco.\nIII. darapti, felapton, disamis, datisi, bocardo, ferison.\nIV. Calemes, bamalip, dimatis, fesapo, fresisom.\nBy the rule ‘*conclusio seqvitur partem debiliorem*’ [the conclusion follows the weaker part], 24 are excluded;\n[by the rule] ‘*ex mere negativis nihil seqvitur*’ [from merely negative premisses nothing follows], 16 are excluded.\n(s 19 possible kinds of syllogism.)\n*(s Of these, [54 weg, nemlich] 28 (g drop out), because the conclusion would have to follow *e puris negativis* [from purely negative premisses] or *ex puris particularibus* [from purely particular premisses] — [18] 36 remain. Of these, 18 drop out in which the conclusion does not follow the weaker part, [and] 8 in which a negative conclusion would follow from purely affirmative premisses; thus 10 remain.)\n**(s Here only the three vowels are to be attended to; hence *disamis* and *dimatis* are equally correct, and [so too] *camestres* and *Calemes*. The initial consonant shows to which mood of the first figure the syllogism is to be reduced: *barocco* to *barbara*. — As to the rest: S requires simple conversion, etc. etc.)\nS requires simple conversion,\nP conversion *per accidens*,\nM requires transposition,\nC reduction *per impossibile*.\nCam\n│\n│\nEveryone devoted to virtue shuns sensuous appetites.\nEstr:\n│\n│\n│\n│\nNo one who leads a soft and voluptuous life shuns sensuous appetites.\nes\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\nNo one who leads a soft and voluptuous life is devoted to virtue.\nCel\n│\n│\n│\n│\nNo one who shuns sensuous appetites leads a soft and voluptuous life.\nar\n│\n│\n│\nEveryone devoted to virtue shuns sensuous appetites.\nent.\n│\n│\n│\nNo one devoted to virtue leads a soft and voluptuous life.\nBar.\n│\nEvery wise man is happy.\noc\n│\n│\nSome learned man is not happy.\nco\n│\n│\nSome learned man is not wise.\nBar[ba]\n│\nEvery wise man is happy.\nbar\n│\nEvery learned man is wise.\na\n│\nEvery learned man is happy.\n(s On the manufacture of syllogisms. Men always seek in the end to make reason technical, so that, by subjecting it to treatment by the senses, they may be sure about errors.)",[]],"refl:3257":["The rule of the second figure is: whatever a mark of a thing contradicts, that contradicts the thing itself. Here I must first convert and say: whatever contradicts a mark, that is contradicted by this mark; or I must convert the conclusion: whatever a mark of a thing contradicts, that the thing itself contradicts; consequently it contradicts the thing.\nWhat is (not true) does not stand in the Bible.\nEverything absurd is (not true).\nNothing absurd stands in the Bible.\n*Consequentia* [inference]: Nothing that stands in the Bible is absurd.\nWhat does everything that stands in the Bible contradict? The absurd.",[]],"refl:3258":["The rule of the third figure is: what pertains to, or contradicts, a mark [wiede] also pertains etc. etc. to some of the things under which this mark is contained. Here I must first say: it pertains to all that are contained under this mark.",[]],"refl:3259":["105.\nL 105':\n(g The *major* is the rule; the *minor* can be thought either through subsumption under the condition of the rule, or through the negation of the conditioned, that is, of the predicate of the rule. From this latter, however, an inference can be drawn only if I convert the negative proposition.)\nIt must first be proved that in all syllogisms the *major* must be universal and the *minor* affirmative; that consequently the first figure underlies all the others, and that the others must be brought to it through *conseqventias immediatas* [immediate inferences] or *metathesis praemissarum* [transposition of the premisses].\nFor in all other syllogisms the *medius terminus* [middle term] does not serve as *nota intermedia* [intermediate mark].\nThe remaining figures infer only because the *medius terminus* receives such a position in the propositions that its position according to the first figure can arise from it through immediate inferences. They are *ratiocinia impura, mixta cum conseqventiis immediatis* [impure syllogisms, mixed with immediate inferences].\n(s Rules of all figures: *ex puris* [affirm:] *negativis et particularibus nihil seqvitur* [from purely negative and from purely particular premisses nothing follows]. *Conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem* [the conclusion follows the weaker part].\n(s *Non sunt plures quam 3 termini* [there are no more than three terms].)\nRule of the first figure: *major* universal, *minor* affirmative.)\nIn the second figure the *minor* stands rightly; therefore the *major* must be converted, and in such a way that it remains universal. This is possible only if it is universal negative (s by conversion), or, if it is affirmative, if it is contraposed; in both cases the conclusion becomes negative (it follows the weaker part).\nIn the third figure the *major* stands rightly; the *minor* must be converted, yet so that an affirmative proposition arises from it. This, however, is possible only in that the affirmative proposition is particular; consequently the conclusion is particular.\n(s In the fourth, if the *major* is (g universal) negative, it can be converted simply; likewise the *minor*, as particular; therefore the conclusion is negative. If the *major* is universal affirmative, it can be either converted only *per accidens* or contraposed; therefore the conclusion is either particular or negative; |\nL 105: |\nin the fourth figure, if the conclusion is not to be converted, PS into SP, then a *metathesis praemissarum* or a conversion of both [premisses] must take place.)\nL 105':\nThe *ratiocinia* in the remaining figures are not merely unnecessary but spurious (*ratiocinia spuria, genuinis opposita* [spurious syllogisms, as opposed to genuine]); for they are mixed.",[]],"refl:3260":["Compound syllogisms consist of compound premisses.\nThey are either conditional or disjunctive or copulative.\nIf the world has a beginning, it is not God.\n*atqvi* [but] — — —",[]],"refl:3261":["*a positione antecedentis ad positionem consequentis* [from the positing of the antecedent to the positing of the consequent].\nOn L § 393, no. 2:\n*a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis* [from the removal of the consequent to the removal of the antecedent].\nFurther, L 107':\nÙ If God has created the world, it is [ohne fehler] beautiful.\nÖ\nÜ Now it is not without faults.\nÜ\nÛ Therefore — —\nØ\nIf animals think, they have reason.\nNow they have no reason; therefore they do not think.",[]],"refl:3262":["not *a remotione antecedentis ad remotionem consequentis* [from the removal of the antecedent to the removal of the consequent],\nnor *a positione consequentis ad positionem antecedentis* [from the positing of the consequent to the positing of the antecedent].\nIf the world is God, it is the best world; *atqvi non: ergo* [but it is not; therefore].\nIf the earth is an animal, it has motion; now it has motion ....",[]],"refl:3263":["In hypothetical syllogisms the *major* expresses the consequence of two propositions from one another [in categorischen werden bey und in minori], of which the first is a premiss and the second a conclusion. The *minor* is a transformation of the problematic condition into a categorical proposition. It is evident from this that it is not a *ratiocinium purum* [pure syllogism] but *exponibile* [requiring exposition], namely through a special *ratiocinium*.",[]],"refl:3264":["/\nA hypothetical syllogism consists of two propositions; consequently it is not a *ratiocinium*. For whoever says, ‘God is just — consequently he punishes the persistently wicked’, says by the word ‘consequently’ that if the first proposition is true, [also] then so is the second; and I can speak in just the same way in immediate inferences. Hypothetical syllogisms are therefore only *conclusiones immediatae demonstrabiles (ex antecedente et conseqvente)* [immediate inferences, demonstrable (from antecedent and consequent)], either *qvoad materiam* or *[qvoad] formam* [as to matter or as to form].\nAs when I say: God is just, therefore he is not unjust, etc. etc.\n(s Hypothetical syllogisms have no *medius terminus*; the consequence of one (g categorical) proposition from the other is merely indicated.)",[]],"refl:3265":["Every syllogism ought to be a proof;\nnow the hypothetical [syllogism] carries with it only the ground of proof;\nconsequently it is not a syllogism.\nIn the hypothetical proposition we think only that one proposition follows from the other; in a hypothetical inference, that [einer] the one (the *antecedens*) is also true. When the *antecedens* [Geleugnet wird] is negative, e.g. ‘The world is not necessary (consequently it [hat] is also not eternal)’, the proposition does not thereby become negative; this, therefore, is not a special *modus*. For the premiss, ‘If the world is eternal, it is also necessary’, is already contained in the consequence.",[]],"refl:3266":["/\nOn the logical principle\nthat in hypothetical syllogisms\nwe cannot infer from the consequence to a determinate\nground,\nsince there can be more than one ground of the same consequence.\nWhence do we obtain this proposition? The proposition *unius ejusdemqve causati plures possunt esse causae* [of one and the same effect there can be several causes] is indeed correct; but it can be known only through experience, or, as in mathematics (where we are speaking only of ground and consequence), in pure intuition, and not from the mere concept of this relation. — The answer is this. In the hypothetical syllogism the major premiss is the universal rule; the minor premiss [da] contains the particular case under that rule; the conclusion is the [Folge der] application of the universal proposition to the case standing under it.\nThat I cannot, conversely, infer from the consequence to a determinate ground rests, therefore, not on the proposition *vnius eiusdemque caussati* etc. etc., but on this, that I cannot infer from the particular case [auf] to the universality of the rule.",[]],"refl:3267":["The human soul is either simple or composite. Now it is not composite; therefore [it is] simple.\n(g false:)\nThe souls of animals are either spirits or bodies; now they are not spirits; therefore — —",[]],"refl:3268":["Holy Scripture is either a divine book, or there are untruths in it.\n*Atqvi* [but] it is a divine book.\nOn L § 396, no. 2:\nEither God made the world, or it is eternal.\nNow it is not eternal; therefore he made it.\nFurther, opposite L § 396, ‘are false’ (l. 33) etc.:\nTo this the categorical [syllogism] is opposed, if [sumitur vel] the member that is posited or removed is taken as the *medius terminus*.\nThe *major* of a copulative syllogism is always negative. E.g. No one serves God and the devil at the same time; *atqvi* the Americans serve the devil; therefore [they do] not [serve] God.",[]],"refl:3269":["All disjunctive syllogisms with more than two *membra disiunctionis* [members of the disjunction] (s dichotomy of a concept) are, strictly, polysyllogistic. For every true disjunction can be only *bimembris* [two-membered], and logical division too is *bimembris*; but for the sake of brevity the *membra subdividentia* [subdividing members] are set among the *membra dividentia* [dividing members].",[]],"refl:3270":["If God does not punish the (g persistently) wicked, he must either approve their wickedness or not have the power to punish.\n*atqvi* the latter is false; therefore the former also.\n(g If Christ has not risen, then) the witnesses of the resurrection [haben] either could have told the truth and would not, or would have and could not.",[]],"refl:3271":["They infer from one judgment and a logical rule.",[]],"refl:3272":["*Crypticus per transpositionem praemissarum* [cryptic [syllogism] through transposition of the premisses].\n*Crypticus per omissionem vel enthymema* [cryptic through omission, or enthymeme]\n— *contractus* [contracted],\nif the conclusion [is joined] with the *medius terminus*.",[]],"refl:3273":["Or one merely adds the *medius terminus*. This world is the best, because it has been created by the highest being.",[]],"refl:3274":["I am a man; what befalls others can befall me also.\nFurther, from ω (? φ??):\n*Circulus in probando* [circle in proof] — and *petitio principii*.",[]],"refl:3275":["Sight represents to me things present Hearing likewise Smell — — — — — — — }\n}\n} therefore all the senses represent to me things present.\nE.g. all men die.\nThe planets attract one another; therefore all heavenly bodies [do so].\nOn L § 401, no. 3:\nLeibniz can err; consequently all philosophers can err.\nIn the higher concept there must lie the sufficient ground why something is ascribed to the lower.\n(g Other concealed inferences:)\nIn place of a principal concept we can set its logical transformation. Whoever loves his enemy is virtuous. Now Caius does good to his slanderer; therefore he is virtuous.",[]],"refl:3276":["/\nEvery syllogism must yield necessity. Induction and analogy are therefore not syllogisms [Vernunftschlüsse] but presumptions (s inferences of the [faculty of] judgment, namely for it) or else [Erfa] empirical inferences.",[]],"refl:3277":["In the empirical sciences, in which experiences are to become *principia*, we infer from the particular to the universal; for unless they yield universal propositions they cannot be used for inferring. Empirical principles are therefore always true only *per inductionem* [by induction], and state that what holds of as many of a certain genus as I know will hold also of the rest.\nFrom part of a whole concept we cannot infer to [Ganze] the remainder. But if everything that I perceive in things [die] of one genus [als zur Gattung gehörig] agrees, they will agree also in respect of the rest, which was always perceived as combined [zu] with those determinations. Inference from analogy.",[]],"refl:3278":["Extraordinary inferences proceed from the particular to the universal: |\ninduction and analogy. But in that case they are not true inferences of reason [Vernunftschlüsse], but presumptions of reason.",[]],"refl:3279":["Analogy proceeds according to the rule: *similium eadem* (g *par*) *est ratio* [of similar things the ground is the same (equal)]. (s The inference would be strict if the ground of their similarity lay in the concept of their [ganz] essence.)",[]],"refl:3280":["Induction (s extends what is empirically given from the particular to the universal in respect of many objects; analogy [extends] the given properties of one thing to more [properties] of that same thing) makes universal what one knows of things of a genus, and extends it to things of the same genus which one does not know (synthetically).\nAnalogy ascribes [das was] [was er an einem Dinge kennt, andern, daran er es nicht kennt] to things of one genus, in which [er] we know something in agreement, also the rest, which we know in one of them but not in the others. E.g. Earth and Moon.",[]],"refl:3281":["On the necessity (g [of empirical universality]) of general propositions (s through induction), that is, properly, of particular propositions which in use count *instar vniversalium* [as if universal]. They are rules which admit of exceptions, though rarely [oder], like grammatical rules, because they are empirical. |\nIf no exception has yet occurred, it is rightly presumed that the ground of the truth lies in the constitution of the thing, and not merely in the circumstances of its perception.\nWe cannot dispense with such general propositions.",[]],"refl:3282":["110'.\nL 110:\nInduction infers *a particulari ad universale* [from the particular to the universal] (g through induction we obtain general, not universal, propositions) according to the principle of generalisation.\nAnalogy [infers] from partial* similarity of two things to total [similarity], according to the principle of specification.\n(g Through analogy: things similar on the whole, but not wholly similar.)\nL 110':\n(* g One in many, therefore in all: induction. Many in one (which are also in others), therefore all the rest also in that same one: analogy.)",[]],"refl:3283":["L 110:\nInduction: one mark in many — *ergo* in all of the same kind; yet here attention must be paid to the condition.\nAnalogy: many marks in one, therefore also all the rest that are connected with the same condition.",[]],"refl:3284":["/\n1. What pertains to many things of a genus pertains also to the rest (g all).\n2. If (s so) much pertains (s to) the things of a genus (s as we can perceive) [so kommt] and they agree in it, then there pertains to them also the rest which we know in some of this genus and do not perceive in those given.\nThe latter inference proceeds from something which [in] one thing has in common with others to the rest, which it will also have in common with them.\n(s If many (g all of a genus, so far as I know) agree in something, then all [do].\nIf two things agree in much (g in everything, so far as I know), then [they agree] in everything.)",[]],"refl:3285":["According to analogy we bring things empirically under a genus.\nAccording to analogy we cannot infer *ultra tertium comparationis* [beyond the third term of comparison].\nEverything depends on how universal judgments are to be drawn from experience, and so not *a priori*: either from many to all things of a kind, or from many determinations of a thing to the rest of its determinations. That the Moon has inhabitants, not that they look as we do. That they have eyes.",[]],"refl:3286":["Logical universality states something of a genus or of species, and |\nnot of all individuals in an aggregate of them; for the latter yields only particular propositions. As: all planets of our system are dark bodies.\nEmpirical universality is only [gen] an *analogon* of logical universality.",[]],"refl:3287":["The [faculty of] judgment is twofold: determinant or reflective judgment. The former proceeds from the universal to the particular, the latter from the particular to the universal. The latter has only subjective validity. — (Inference by analogy (g and induction) is logical presumption.)\n(s They are principles of the [empirischen] procedure in the extension of our knowledge through experience.\nWe can indeed think a being (which is incomprehensible to us) according to analogy, but we cannot infer [nach] from a relation of given things to the same relation in unknown beings.)",[]],"refl:3288":["these can be either divisions *a priori* [eines Begriffs] or empirical division.",[]],"refl:3289":["an empirical universality.",[]],"refl:3290":["All men are mortal (g all planets [are] dark bodies): an inference from induction.\nOn account of the identity of the *medius terminus*: analogy.",[]],"refl:3291":["[Aus particula] From a part of the *sphaera* of a concept to the whole *sphaera*. — From a part of the concept to the identity of the whole concept, that is, [what holds of the part] holds also of the rest. E.g. gravitation; suns do not move about dark bodies.",[]],"refl:3292":["According to analogy we infer only to rational inhabitants of the Moon, not to men; therefore analogy requires only identity of the ground, *par ratio*.",[]],"refl:3293":["/\nImmortality, according to the analogy [der Angemessenheit] of the complete development of the natural predispositions [zu] of every creature.",[]],"refl:3294":["If what we become aware of in things [mit als zu ihrem Gattungsbegriffe gehorig zur Einheit der] must be thought as belonging to one and the same genus, then the rest also which is required for that same genus may be presumed of them, although we do not become aware of it in them. For example, fungi agree with plants in respect of growth; therefore, according to the analogy with [Pflanzen] them, they will also agree with them in propagating themselves by seed. But I cannot infer that, because animals [nach], so far as we know them, have circulation of their fluids, plants are likewise so constituted. For they differ in [Species] genus in respect of what concerns the ground of the given determination, since the former [animals] take in nourishment voluntarily.\nWe can therefore indeed think (g the causality) of a cause of the world according to the analogy [mit] (g of the constitution) of a cause in the world, namely, as to the manner of being a cause, as men are (namely through understanding) the cause of a watch; [aber] but we cannot infer from the future constitution of the things of the world to that causality. For in the first case we have only similar relations, in the second a similar thing — but God and causes in the world are wholly heterogeneous things.\nTo represent something to oneself according to an analogy — to infer [according to it].",[]],"refl:3295":["Sophistries.",[]],"refl:3296":["The right eye is not necessary for seeing; nor is the left; therefore neither.",[]],"refl:3297":["No artist is born. Some men are artists ....\nIf you say, ‘you are lying’, and in saying this, etc.\nWhoever can get through the whole world with Latin can come to every place in the world.\nNow the bog is a place in the world.\n— — — —",[]],"refl:3298":["He who drinks well sleeps well; he who sleeps well does not sin (g while he sleeps); he who does not sin is saved; therefore he who drinks well is saved.\nL 111. On the example in L § 404, no. 3:\nin the major premiss without, in the minor premiss with, a more precise determination.",[]],"refl:3299":["Because Paul warns against philosophy.",[]],"refl:3300":["The Liar.",[]],"refl:3301":["if it is not true, then either the dogs as well as the others have no reason; 2. [or] that besides the dogs other four-footed animals also have reason.",[]],"refl:3302":["The sophist who had a lawsuit with his pupil.\nEither I win the suit: then I need give you nothing. Or I lose it: then also I need give you nothing.",[]],"refl:3303":["Dialectic: art of illusion.\n— — mere form is deceptive, not merely [the form] of syllogisms, but of the — — [presentation]. E.g. many grounds heaped together, of which none is any good. Similarities with — —, instead of using analogies. Agreement of others instead of proofs.",[]],"refl:3304":["/\nAbelard. There flies an ox, etc. etc.",[]],"refl:3305":["The logic of the Megarian sect (of Euclid, not the geometer).\nSophistical modes of reasoning:\n1. *Mentiens* [the Liar]. If you say that you are lying, and you say what is true (s objectively), you are lying (s subjectively); therefore you lie in saying that you lie, i.e. you are not lying.\nEpimenides says that all Cretans are liars; but he himself is a Cretan; therefore he is lying, and the Cretans are truthful; but he himself is a Cretan; therefore (s and since he is truthful) he is to be believed, and the Cretans are liars.\nSeneca: My whole life lies to me: refute it, direct it to the truth, if you are acute.\nPhiletas became so thin and slight from these subtleties that, lest he be carried off by the wind, he was obliged to put leaden balls under [his feet].\n2. *Electra*. (The daughter of Agamemnon did not recognise her brother Orestes standing beside her.)\n3. *Obvelatus* [the Veiled Man]. Do you know your father? Yes. But if I set a veiled man near you? Do you know him? Not at all. But he is your father himself; therefore you know and at the same time do not know.\n4. *Acervus* (*Sorites*) [the Heap]. This dream is probable; why not also (g very) likely? Why not so much so that it is hard to distinguish from the true? Finally: so that nothing lies between this and that?\nDoes one grain make a heap? Do two, do three? Therefore, that a heap at last comes to be, a single grain brings about.\n5. *Cornutus* [the Horned Man]. But you have not lost horns; therefore you have them.\n6. *Calvus* [the Bald Man]. With the loss of which hair does someone begin to be counted bald?\nIf something moves, it moves either in the place in which it is, or in that in which it is not.\nThe sophism *acervus* always arises where we seek to make a specific difference by mere magnitude. There must be a *punctum flexus contrarii* [point of contrary flexure] in the progression, where the one direction ceases and the other begins. Thus avarice cannot be explained by excessive thrift, etc. etc. Rich and poor differ in this, that the former exceeds competence, or has more than he needs, while the latter falls below it.\n*Summum ius summa iniuria* [the extreme of right is the extreme of wrong] — against the shallow principles of equity — or the principle that what is useful to the greatest number is to be preferred to what is right.\n*Fiat iustitia, pereat mundus* [let justice be done, though the world perish] — it will not perish thereby.\nTo be too kind (not too clever).",[]],"refl:3306":["/\nthrough subordination.\nBeside L § 406, ‘not — immortal’ (ll. 22–24):\nthrough co-ordination: when the *major* is a copulative proposition.",[]],"refl:3307":["Ù All triangles have three angles.\nÖ\nÜ All right-angled triangles are triangles.\nÜ\nÛ — — — —\nØ\n[Alle Figuren, die nicht mehr als drey winkel haben.]\nEverything that is divisible occupies a space.\nThe soul occupies no space.\nTherefore it is not divisible.\nWhat is not divisible is imperishable.",[]],"refl:3308":["to which a proof is added.\nFurther:\n4. I am obligated to whatever makes me more perfect; for that is [die] (g the rule of the) nature of the free will. Now virtue makes me more perfect, because it is in conformity with the laws of nature. Therefore I am obligated to virtue.",[]],"refl:3309":["The soul is a simple thing.\nEverything simple is indivisible.\nEverything indivisible [is] imperishable.\nEverything imperishable endures for ever.\nTherefore the soul endures for ever.",[]],"refl:3310":["(s let the *major* be I.\n*major*\nII.\nA spirit that has no obscure representations at all has also no confused ones. Whoever has no confused ones has only distinct ones. Whoever has only distinct — — —, has the most distinct. Whoever has the most distinct knowledge — knows all possible things. Whoever [knows] these is omniscient. Whoever is omniscient is infinite. Whoever is infinite is God. *ergo*.)\nIt is constructed thus:\n1.\nWe take the judgment that we wish to prove, and prove it in the first figure.\n2.\n[Then] the *minor*, and so on.\nThe last is the first prosyllogism.\n3.\nAll judgments that occur twice are omitted,\nexcept in the first prosyllogism.\n4.\nWe begin with the *minor* of the first premisses.\n5.\nThe *majores* follow one upon another.\n6.\nThe conclusion has the subject of the first premiss and the predicate of the last.\nFurther, L 113, from σ−χ? (ξ?) (ω?):\nChain-syllogism.\n*acervus*.",[]],"refl:3311":["all premisses universal affirmative and universal.",[]],"refl:3312":["The chain-syllogism through categorical propositions, i.e. predicate. The chain-syllogism through hypothetical propositions, i.e. consequence. {\n{\n{\n{\n{just like the chain rule [in arithmetic]. *praedicatum praedicatorum sibi subordinatorum est etiam praedicatum subiecti* [the predicate of predicates subordinated to one another is also the predicate of the subject]; or *rationatum rationatorum sibi subordinatorum est etiam rationatum principii* [the consequence of consequences subordinated to one another is also the consequence of the ground].",[]],"refl:3313":["E.g. the principle of sufficient reason.\nOn L § 410, ‘*saltus illegitimus*’ [illegitimate leap]:\nThere is order in the world; there must therefore be an author existing outside the world.",[]],"refl:3314":["/\nA freethinker is not to be refuted from Holy Scripture,\nnor a Catholic from the apocryphal books.\nOn L § 411, ‘*circulus in probando*’:\nThe reality of God from Scripture.\nFurther, L 113', from ψ−θ:\n(s *Circulus vitiosus* [vicious circle]:) e.g. the proof that duty rests on happiness, and afterwards that one cannot find oneself happy without being conscious of virtue.\n(s There one must presuppose duty before the [Pflicht] desire for happiness.\nOr when one proves the Deity from the ends in the world, and afterwards infers that everything in the world is wise because it belongs to the divine ends.)",[]],"refl:3315":["When one proves the immortality of the soul and wishes [thereby] to prove its imperishability.\nL 113. On L § 412, ‘*probatio plus probans*’ [a proof that proves too much]:\nE.g. that we ought not to take our own lives, because we have not given them to ourselves.",[]],"refl:3316":["Without order there is no perfection.\nFurther:\nWhoever does not have insight into the rules of order does not have insight into order itself.",[]],"refl:3317":["E.g. how a speech is to be delivered.\nHow the catechism is to be taught.\nσ−χ? ω? L 114. On L § 415, ‘common method’:\npopular.\nOn L § 415, ‘rational method’:\nscholastically correct.\nψ−θ. Between L § 415 and 416:\n*Methodus naturalis, artificialis* [natural, artificial method].",[]],"refl:3318":["Wit, imagination, reason.",[]],"refl:3319":["Scientific or free method.",[]],"refl:3320":["/\n(s *Locus communis*: commonplace.)\n*De locis* or topics. *Loci* are the general heads of arguments, or the names of the classes under which they are found. Grammatical *loci*: from etymology. 2. Logical: from definition, from genus and species, from opposites. 3. Metaphysical: from the whole to the parts; axioms concerning efficient causes. 4. Ethical: ultimate ends, the becoming and the honourable; likewise in questions of fact. 5. Physical: from the ends of nature.",[]],"refl:3321":["Method [Lehrart] is only the form of a whole of modes of knowledge in accordance with logical perfection.",[]],"refl:3322":["Regularity of procedure is either —\nMethod: a [bestimmte] rule (g *a priori* for) the exercise of modes of knowledge; a constitutive rule or prescription; or manner [Manier]: a kind of uniform conduct without [Regel] prescription.\nIn everything that must relate to an idea there is method. Consequently in every doctrine, but not in conversation.",[]],"refl:3323":["/\nOn the last sentence of L § 421:\nAll knowledge, and every whole of it, must conform [nach] to a rule. Absence of rule is at the same time unreason. But this rule is either that of manner (free) or that of method (constraint). Knowledge as science [steht] must be arranged according to a method. For science is a whole of knowledge as system, and not merely as aggregate. In this the scholastically correct has its ground.\n(s For science requires a systematic knowledge, and therefore one drawn up according to deliberate rules.)",[]],"refl:3324":["The grounds of every method relate:\n1. to the object;\n2. to the subject;\n3. to the cognitive power with which the object is compared;\n4. [vor die Persohn und deren Stand, die da lehrt] to the science or special kind of modes of knowledge to which something belongs. E.g. we must distinguish the objective from the subjective, what is true in the idea from what happens, the object of mere reason from the empirical, and in general every part of our modes of knowledge from what is of another kind. *Philosophia pura* [pure philosophy].",[]],"refl:3325":["Method is unity of a whole of knowledge from principles. It is therefore always scientific; it makes an aggregate into a system, and common knowledge into science.\nPopularity requires only manner, i.e. unity according to empirical laws of social intercourse.\nPractical logic is a guide to method and its varieties in general.",[]],"refl:3326":["Theory (g [Lehre] doctrine): that which contains the conditions of the perfection of a mode of knowledge.\nMethod: that which sets forth the way of attaining it. The latter abstracts also from the content.\n*Modus*, manner, differs from *methodus* in that the latter is a *modus* [proceeding] from principles, the former only from empirical grounds. E.g. *svaviter in modo, fortiter in re*. Investigations into method are the last and the most difficult [of all].",[]],"refl:3327":["Method is properly the rule of *praxis* (practice), in so far as [sie] one is conscious of it. At first we think tumultuarily, then regularly, and finally methodically. Method is the last and the most important [element] in thinking.\n(s The mode of teaching [Lehrart] is either methodical or tumultuary; the former is either systematic or rhapsodic (s — aphoristic).)",[]],"refl:3328":["The learned [sind] must teach not only pupils but also the learned. For a learned man must learn from others. This takes place |\nthrough books. A learned man therefore has only two courses open to him. Either he is a teacher in respect of other learned men also (or at least can be one); or he is indeed a teacher in respect of the unlearned [idioten], but a pupil in respect of other learned men.\nA teacher who is himself only a pupil is not, properly speaking, a learned man. For if he cannot himself extend the science, he is not in possession of its methods; he does not lack information [Kenntnis], but he does lack insight [Einsicht].\nA teacher who writes no books, and in particular none for the learned, is no public person in the field of learning. To write for the public, and not at all for the learned, is therefore — since this public has no coherence — much the same as keeping a tavern as against him who serves the police.",[]],"refl:3329":["Method is not always necessary, and the manner is then rhapsodic; e.g. anecdotes, similes, etc. The parts of a system without elaboration yield the aphoristic method.\nPopularity consists in accommodation and condescension to common knowledge and common (g interest) attention. It is the greatest perfection of a fine mind [eines schönen Kopfs], but only when it is combined with thoroughness.\nThe rules of popularity are still a philosophers’ stone for logicians. (s It has also a method of its own, which is properly the end of popularity and from which its knowledge begins.)",[]],"refl:3330":["*Mentis suae sensa sibimet ipsi exponendi et apte expromendi ratio est modus proponendi.* [The manner of setting out the thoughts of one’s mind to oneself, and of fittingly expressing them, is the mode of presentation.]",[]],"refl:3331":["On »Der andere Haupttheil« (77617):\nGeneral doctrine of method. Not practical logic.",[]],"refl:3332":["114'.\nL 114:\nLogic has two parts: 1. a theoretical part, the doctrine of the elements of the understanding in general; 2. (g practical) the doctrine of method. Both [treat of knowledge] without regard to the object, and also to the origin and the principles of the knowledge.\nThe doctrine of elements is (a) Analytic, (b) Dialectic. The doctrine of method treats only of the [Mannigfaltigen] way of combining the manifold of knowledge into a science. It is either doctrinal or polemical. This logic is *logica pura*. Anthropological logic is *applicata*. |\nL 114': |\nOf prejudices. Of persuasion. Of the interest in knowledge for its own sake: the logical interest of the understanding, the aesthetic interest of sensibility.",[]],"refl:3333":["/\n*Modus*, mode of teaching [Lehrart], is either *modus aestheticus*: manner, or [scholasticus] *logicus*: method (s *Forma systematis*). The former is (s with a view to) popularity, the latter (s with a view to) scholastic method (s perfection).\nPractical logic treats solely of the form of a science in general and of its presentation; it treats, therefore, of method.\nMethod may also be entitled ‘way of thinking’ [Denkart] (g in respect of form, not of principles), to distinguish it from cast of mind [DenkungsArt].",[]],"refl:3334":["Popularity concerns either the method or the presentation.",[]],"refl:3335":["To think without method is tumultuary.\nTo think with an [nur verstekten] empirical method (g but not to present [one’s thoughts] by it): rhapsodic.\nWith an order according to method, but without [Verbindung d.i.] transitions, so that it constitutes not a *quantum continuum* but a *quantum discretum*: aphoristic.\nWith method of thinking and of presentation, and at the same time of connection: systematic.",[]],"refl:3336":["/\n(g The mode of expression) Presentation or style is distinct from method (g the mode of connecting thoughts). Both [proceed] according to principles.",[]],"refl:3337":["The word ‘mode of teaching’ [Lehrart] is unsuitable. It should signify simply the systematic way of thinking. In thinking for ourselves we pass over much that belongs to the coherence and totality of a system; in teaching, on the other hand, we are compelled to take account of it. [so] For this reason the systematic form is entitled ‘mode of teaching’.",[]],"refl:3338":["Systematic presentation is opposed to fragmentary; methodical presentation is opposed to tumultuary.\n}\n}\n}\nThe fragmentary [Metho] mode of teaching which is nevertheless (g at the same time) methodical is aphoristic.\n(Fragmentary and rhapsodic are one and the same.)",[]],"refl:3339":["Everything contingent is mutable; the happiness of men is contingent, and therefore mutable.",[]],"refl:3340":["One must not think through a large doctrinal structure [zeichn] in this way. A beginner must not learn it after this fashion.\nOn L §. 423 No. 1:\n*inventio*.\nThere I know what premisses will be needed for the consequences.\nOn L §. 423 No. 3:\nImpress the propositions upon oneself, and then the grounds.\nOn L §. 423 No. 4:\nFirst the conclusion, and from it the proof.",[]],"refl:3341":["E.g. clear [representations] before distinct.\nOn L §. 425 No. 2:\n*Axiomata, postulata, lemmata*.",[]],"refl:3342":["Synthesis and analysis are either of co-ordination — when the whole is thought before the parts, or conversely — or of sub|ordination:\nwhen the ground is thought before the consequence, the universal before the particular, the *abstractum* before the *concretum*.",[]],"refl:3343":["All knowledge is, as regards its content, either historical or rational.\nThe method of the latter is synthetic or analytic. The analytic is the method of discovery, the synthetic that of comprehension [des Fassens]. Even when analysis has come first (especially in religion) — and this must always be done with concepts — synthesis must nevertheless follow last, in order that a system may result. (s Astronomy first through appearances, then according to a system.)",[]],"refl:3344":["Empirical natural science: analytic; rational: synthetic.\nMorals, [the proof] that there is such a thing: analytic.\nIn history, to begin from chronology is synthetic. — In morals, from freedom and natural necessity. — In the exposition of texts [Sprucherklärung].",[]],"refl:3345":["It is still a question whether so much learning is really needed in order to know how men ought to live so as to be well-pleasing to God |\n(g so that, were all Greek to cease, no one would know how he was to be saved), or in order that men’s rights among one another may be apportioned (g and) administered. In both cases no rules need be given other than those which each man’s natural understanding teaches him when he acts, or when he represents to himself a right against others arising from his actions. Deep and hidden grounds of right, [an] which no one ever thinks of in his civil transactions, cannot avail him, even if they do in fact lie in them. If the current rules of reason cannot [zu] determine what is to be done, a man may be in the wrong merely because he is not learned or has not consulted a learned man. Positive laws ought to form an entirely separate collection. In a lawsuit the parties must not be regarded as learned men; consequently the advocate (g who merely puts their claim in order) must not represent them as a learned man would, that is, he must not reason more subtly than they themselves can. [Jeder] The one party must adduce the ground of right on which he has based his claim, the other must justify his title. Neither is to reason about right in general; each is only to set forth what his intention was in the transaction and his opinion as to the right, while the judge sets forth the ground of right of the decision. What is useless in all this philosophy must determine. Everything here depends on actions being brought under rules, and indeed under universal practical rules. A distinction must always be drawn between universal civil right and the right of an arbitrary constitution. A distinction must also be drawn between making use of the learning which sharpens the judgment of the judge, and the ground of right on which the parties can rely.\nNo one can complain that his right is not administered to him if, without delay and without partiality, he obtains satisfaction in respect of the concepts which he forms of his right (and which his opponent likewise forms). The public, however, employs no learning in contriving its concepts of right; and he who contrives a ground of right not contained in common concepts [der] is already resorting to intrigue. Legislation therefore requires no learning, and the administration of justice still less. Where a particular constitution of the state, or the locality*, requires positive laws (of which, however, there must be few), there the principles must serve as precepts. Otherwise unlearned peoples would be unable to administer justice.\n*(g or where cunning and the uncertainty of the proofs of the *pacta* are to be guarded against.)\nThere are two kinds of concept of right: the one requires learning and reflection, the other none at all, but only an understanding practised in affairs. The latter concept is inferred from the conditions of the unity of the will of the participants; the former from the conditions of the distribution of right, or of the determination of the difference of the manifold (g which stands) under a rule. (When anyone demands something by right, he must name it, and must also be able to suppose that his opponent must have thought the same thing in the matter. Consequently he does not require that something be settled for him according to concepts unknown to him, but only that his ground of right be compared with that of the other. The law must therefore refer to concrete, yet universal, concepts of right.)\n*Iustitia distributiva* presupposes that every right must be capable of proof which one has not possessed prior to any *laesio*. *Commutativa*, however, etc. etc. E.g. when someone lends something and damage arises from it. [The question is] whether the advocate is an intermediary of this kind, to whom the party can hand over his cause so that he may seek a right in it, and how the law is to forestall this assaying art [probirkunst] of the advocate. In commutative [justice] the question is what right follows solely from the relation of the parties |\nto one another. In distributive: what follows from the relation to the whole.",[]],"refl:3346":["A law is either necessary (g law *stricte*) or contingent; the latter is entitled a statute and has the general advantage as its aim. Laws (*leges stricte tales*) must be derived not [w] from principles of the unity of the system, but from principles of the unity of the will.",[]],"refl:3347":["Whether legislation ought not to conform to the principle of *iustitia commutativa*, or to determine it?\nWhat accords with the common judgment of right is the unity of the *principium*, but not the multiplicity of restricting conditions derived from the kinds of right (personal or real). Sale breaks hire. *Vindicatio ablati*. He who without fault on his part has acquired what belongs to another cannot indeed gain thereby, but neither must he suffer loss.\nOne *principium* is: one can acquire from another *facto iusto* only so much as the other declares. Thus [the buyer does not acquire from] the seller, before *traditio*, the security of what he sells, unless he has stipulated for it; nor the *commodatarius*, beyond the use, exemption from all risk in respect of the thing itself.\nThe representation which each man forms of right in his juridical actions is the consent of the other under known conditions. The common understanding does indeed know principles of right, but not the sources of right. *Principia constitutiva* (g or rather *principia regulativa*, not *constitutiva*), which prescribe what is right, but not *genetica*, which set forth the ground of rightfulness. E.g. *casum sentit dominus* in the case of what has been lent. The latter knowledge is also doubtful.",[]],"refl:3348":["What has arisen from a contract can therefore easily be brought under common *principia*.\nA system of popular knowledge of right, distinct from the speculative. The popular concepts of right which contradict the speculative must first be sought out.",[]],"refl:3349":["*Jus reale* is an ideal obligation of a thing towards the *dominus*.",[]],"refl:3350":["To every right there corresponds an obligation on the part of others not to hinder him in the exercise of it. But since all men have a right |\nto acquire *per occupationem* or *translationem* (g just as the owner himself has), there corresponds to this right, on the side of the owner, an obligation to distinguish his property recognisably and securely; and there is no obligation on others to investigate this distinction or the *titulus* of the *possessio* of a third party.\n[Gesetzt] The *dominus* therefore has no right except against the *primus laedens*; the others rightly ignore the *titulus possessionis* of the latter. It is either the fault or the misfortune of the *dominus* if, after possession has been (g unlawfully) interrupted, the thing is transferred in due form.\nIt has to be proved that in the *permutatio iurium* everyone has a natural right to ignore the *titulus possessionis* of the other — indeed, that he is not entitled to inquire into it before he is agreed [with him]. Der *iniustus possessor*. Otherwise it would follow that everyone who is to transfer his right must first prove that he has one.",[]],"refl:3351":["The right which anyone acquires from another depends solely on the *modus translationis* of the thing, and not on the right which the latter has in the thing. Otherwise we could acquire no thing *originarie*, since in that case there is as yet no right in the thing. Thus a man cannot indeed give another more (thing) than he has, but he can nevertheless constitute a right which he does not have. For the other acquires through his acceptance and does no one wrong, although the former does someone wrong.\nHe has [ein] the right to transfer a thing as to which it remains undetermined |\nhow he came by it. I can sell, as my own, something which another has given me to sell.",[]],"refl:3352":["Right may be taken negatively; it then signifies merely: not wrong. Such is the case with *ius reale*. But it is also [taken] affirmatively; it then refers to obligation and is *ius personale*.",[]],"refl:3353":["We must always distinguish what belongs in itself to perfection from what is required for carrying it out. The latter must be left to the last. In fairness the judge ought to judge from the *factum* and not from the grounds of right alleged by the advocates; for these may be false while the case is nevertheless just. Although this way of judging is burdensome, it is perhaps not impossible.",[]],"refl:3354":["All laws may be divided into those which determine right and those which are provisionary [vorsorgende]. The latter must be kept entirely separate from the former. Through them he for whom the government makes provision acquires no right, and must not profit. Further, [there are] laws which serve to facilitate the determination of right; these must be simple.",[]],"refl:3355":["In the sale of a thing there are three elements: 1. the seller, |\n2. the thing, 3. the buyer. In the contract no wrong is done between them. The seller does the thing no wrong: it has no obligation, contrary to which he would compel it to pass over to another. Nor does he wrong the buyer, nor the buyer, *vice versa*, him. This is an *actus originarius* and not *derivativus* (except when the buyer knows that the seller is *non dominus*). To say that the buyer must derive his right from the right of the seller makes the difficulty infinite, if the right of the seller is taken to mean that by the sale he does not act contrary to an obligation towards anyone else and injures no one else thereby. But as regards the *actus translatorius*, he has the thing in his hands, and no one may take it from him except the owner. The owner, however, is not party to the transaction. Among these three, therefore, the seller really does transfer his right, which is visible. But when the thing has its existence only from the (g determinate) soil, from which it cannot be moved without perishing, the buyer must first inquire into the right. For since the soil can never actually be possessed, and so has no proper *dominus* but can only be used, [hat] it can belong only to him who by his possession (g and original *occupatio*) can do no wrong, that is, to the society which has the power to administer right among its members.\nIn respect of things one is never in the wrong, to whomsoever they may belong; but one may well be so towards the owner. The owner, too, has only just such a right to the thing, but [he has] the priority which binds all others to do no prejudice to his right.\nIn respect of me even the thief has a right to the possession of the thing which he offers me for sale. I should do him wrong were I to take it from him by force. He relinquishes this right in my favour, and I acquire. Whatever wrong he may have done to another is not my affair but that other’s. My *actus* is rightful. I have dealt with this man and know of no other.",[]],"refl:3356":["/\nNatural right is that which concerns merely the rule of the community of men among one another, whereby one confers a right upon another; artificial and transcendental right is that which seeks to derive all rights from their original genesis. The latter contradicts itself. For in that case no one can communicate a secure right to another, since he can never prove originally how he himself came by it.",[]],"refl:3357":["The judge ought 1. as *inquirens*, to proceed analytically, in order [zuerst] to obtain from all the *momenta in facto* an idea of the *factum*. But since much in it may be indifferent in respect of the disputed right — juridically at least — he must compare all the *varia facti* with the *ius* (*cuiusvis partis*) *controversum*, in order to select the *momenta facti* from among them. [Denn muß] He must therefore first weigh the *ius praetensum*, in order [zu wissen] to determine *a priori* what is requisite for it. E.g. what belongs to a partnership contract [Mascopeycontract] and to its continuance on the part of one of the partners. This must first be settled in general by the judge; and the parties must either appeal on this point to the legislative commission, or else consent to bring forward the *momenta in facto* according to all these *momenta in iure*.\n2. As judge he must proceed synthetically: according to *iustitia distributiva* he must declare [dasje] those *praestanda* which each of the disputants, according to the right of the other, [hatten] must perform or for which he must render satisfaction.",[]],"refl:3358":["Rational science is either authentic or doctrinal. The |\nformer is itself law-giving [die] (legislative), the latter merely versed in the law (*legisperitia*).",[]],"refl:3359":["He who acts naturally acts according to rules, but rules of which he is not conscious.",[]],"refl:3360":["Natural or artificial.\nNatural or contrary to nature.",[]],"refl:3361":["/\nThe natural method must be opposed not to the artificial, but to the artificed [gekünstelten]. For the greatest art is required to be truly adequate to nature.\nThe natural method is the popular.",[]],"refl:3362":["All method is art and not nature.\n(g Purposeless) dispensable art is affectation.\nPopularity requires merely manner.",[]],"refl:3363":["School method.",[]],"refl:3364":["*Methodus scientifica vel popularis*.\nThe former removes everything heterogeneous; the latter takes it along.\nThe former begins from elements; the latter from the familiar.\nThe former aims at thoroughness; the latter at entertainment.",[]],"refl:3365":["Art and science (being able and knowing).\nArt and handicraft: as science and common knowledge.",[]],"refl:3366":["/\nOne pursues an art either as a liberal art or as a trade (art for a livelihood).",[]],"refl:3367":["*methodus vel scholastica* (g *scientifica*) *vel popularis*;\n*prior vel naturalis vel popularis*.",[]],"refl:3368":["The scientific mode of teaching.\nOn L §. 428 No. 2 »methodus tabellaris«:\nDisposition.\nFurther L 117':\nDichotomy.",[]],"refl:3369":["/\n*Methodus erotematica*.",[]],"refl:3370":["[See image above.]\nClear\n[Deutlich] Obscure Confused | distinct.\n│\n│\n│ Marks\n│\n│\n│\n│\nSufficient:\nclear\nInsufficient\nThe marks\nare clear, distinct\nobscure: indistinct\nMarks distinct: complete indistinct: incomplete\nPropositions.\ntheoretical\npractical\nindemonstrable: demonstrable indemonstrable: demonstrable through\nPrinciples through one inference: corollaries through several: theorems Postulates several inferences: problems",[]],"refl:3371":["Mathematical method.",[]],"refl:3372":["E.g. If you come upon a statue that is very beautiful, do you not say that it was a good artist who made it?\nYou therefore presuppose that an artist must have made it.\nAre not statues the more perfect, the more they resemble men?\nAre men [breaks off].\nFurther L 118 from ψ−θ beside »einander folgen — veranlassen« (ll. 22f.):\nsystematic or aphoristic.",[]],"refl:3373":["dogmatic or critical (to that end sceptical).\nFurther L 119 from β1− ρ3 beside »einander — Gedanken« (ll. 25f.):\nLeibniz’s *Theodicy*.",[]],"refl:3374":["We sketch rhapsodically and arrange methodically.",[]],"refl:3375":["/\nIn *theologia naturalis*, the concept of God.\nIn logic: that of learned knowledge which is not merely learned.",[]],"refl:3376":["The historical mode of teaching (s of history) is pragmatic when it has yet another aim than the scholastic, [that is,] when it is not merely for the school but also for the world or for morality.",[]],"refl:3377":["History of states.",[]],"refl:3378":["Just as in the manner of living.\nThose who have accustomed themselves to the mathematical method.",[]],"refl:3379":["*A colloquium is\n1, catechetical, [either] mechanical (s learning by heart) or judicious: teacher towards learner. (g epistolary mode of teaching.)\n2, dialogical, where the thoughts are elicited from the other and each submits to mutual examination.\n(s Systematic or aphoristic. erotematic.)\n(* s is either merely one-sided (g in teaching (otherwise it is examination, interrogation)) erotematic: [(doctrinal) oder] catechetical, [oder Exam] or reciprocal: dialogue. methodical dialogue.) )",[]],"refl:3380":["(g playful.) whimsical. gallant. courtly. ponderous. erudite. notes. brain-racking etc. etc.",[]],"refl:3381":["*Methodus acroamatica*: where [andere nur dem Vortrage zuhören] one\n(s /\\ / \\ / \\ Historical or rational; the latter: mathematical or philosophical; synthesis — analysis. The mixed must be separated. speaks alone; erotematic: where two converse together. The latter either dialogical or catechetical. The dialogical in respect of the | )\nlearner is Socratic; dialogue is either mechanical or judicious; the former: *catechesis*.",[]],"refl:3382":["The dialogical (g and erotematic method), the Socratic and the catechetical.\nThe thoughts which the pupil recites catechetically are not his own; it is a work of memory.\nIt is Socratic when the pupil questions the teacher (who is nevertheless properly the pupil); it is applicable only in rational knowledge from mere concepts, since these we develop.\nWhen the teacher questions the pupil, it must be matters of memory; for the pupil cannot open up insights to him. (g catechetical.)\nLastly, it is dialogue when they converse together and reason [wie] (and so not in question and answer), as in the Dialogues in the Realm of the Dead: Fassmann, Lyttelton.",[]],"refl:3383":["The Socratic dialogue is no conversation, since one party is always regarded as teacher. In a conversation no one is teacher or pupil; they are *in commercio* of thoughts.",[]],"refl:3384":["The erotematic method is either examinatory (g where one does not seek at the same time to instruct someone through questions, but questions him on what he has learned elsewhere), or catechetical (g where the teacher himself [durch] in his questions seeks to instruct the learner), or dialogical.\n(s The catechetical holds only for empirical modes of knowledge, the dialogical for rational; both only for philosophical.)",[]],"refl:3385":["One cannot teach erotematically otherwise than through the Socratic dialogue. Through ordinary *catechesis* one cannot teach, but only question [the pupil] on what one has taught acroamatically.\nThe Socratic dialogue teaches through questions, in that it acquaints the learner with his own principles of reason and sharpens his attention to them.",[]],"refl:3386":["In dialogue (g by which I teach) I address questions to the understanding; the other must therefore be able in turn to ask questions.\nIn *catechesis* I address them merely to the memory, in order to hear again what |\nhas been taught. *Catechesis* is either mechanical or judicious.\nWhether, in the catechism of religious teaching, the historical [Lehren] or merely historically known propositions should be presented as matters of faith, or as mere history or tradition, does not belong here.",[]],"refl:3387":["or critique.",[]],"refl:3388":["Instruction [institution] is either doctrine (positive) or discipline (negative). The *organon* of the latter is critique.",[]],"refl:3389":["In so far as it is regarded as an object of knowledge; otherwise it is a skill in proving known truths.\nAll sciences were [first] doctrines; e.g. theology. Then came methodical minds. Finally sciences.",[]],"refl:3390":["E.g. Beaumelle.",[]],"refl:3391":["He who thinks methodically may present [his thoughts] systematically or fragmentarily.\nPresentation which is outwardly fragmentary but in itself methodical is aphoristic.",[]],"refl:3392":["False meditating. Borrowing from postils.",[]],"refl:3393":["/\nSynthetic, analytic.",[]],"refl:3394":["Otherwise one thinks nothing.",[]],"refl:3395":["To think along with an author.* To think after [him]. To think ahead [of him].\nThinking along with [an author] does not proceed well without thinking over and thinking after — namely, of the whole before the parts. For in dogmatic modes of knowledge the parts are determined only in the whole, and\ncan be made distinct, even in themselves, only from the whole. Expositions of texts are therefore faulty when they are micrological.\n*(s Hence the obscuring of a witty notion, so that we may have something to unriddle. One takes pleasure in conversation only in so far as one gets the opportunity to speak oneself.)",[]],"refl:3396":["One must sketch according to an idea, seek out the manifold tumultuarily, and combine it according to a method",[]],"refl:3397":["not rhapsodically.\nTo think methodically *a priori*.",[]],"refl:3398":["On L 436:\n1. To understand oneself, what one wants.\n2. Not only to analyse one’s thoughts but also to hold them together: system.",[]],"refl:3399":["/\nGrammatical correctness is to be distinguished from elegance of speech or style.",[]],"refl:3400":[") η?? L 122.\nSpeaking [Sprechen] and discoursing [reden].",[]],"refl:3401":["[Vortrag] An aggregate has manner and style. A system: method.",[]],"refl:3402":["Discourse which refers everything to logical perfection, or which refers everything to the entertaining play of sensibility.",[]],"refl:3403":["Wealth of ideas through wealth of languages.\nFurther L 123' from ψ−θ:\nThe [gelehrte] scholastic and the popular presentation. The latter is the work of a genius.",[]],"refl:3404":["Solemn speeches have no technical terms.\nLearned language.\nGreek [aus] technical terms.",[]],"refl:3405":["Hunters.\nFurther L 123 from ρ2−ψ (? ι2−λ?):\nProper and technical expressions befit an expert: mining. Machines. Handicraft.",[]],"refl:3406":["*Geschwind, hurtig, behend* and *schnell* [quick, nimble, agile, swift].\n*Muthig, rüstig, waker* [courageous, vigorous, valiant].\nFurther from ψ−θ:\n*synonyma*: when many words have one meaning,\n*homonymon*: when one word has many meanings. (s as *acies*.) (s Poverty of language.)\nFurther from ω (φ??):\nFitting expression.\nEconomy of words in a language in general is when composite concepts are expressed [aus] through words for the partial concepts; the false wealth of the Arabic language. — But economy of words in a discourse makes it partly obscure, partly forceful.",[]],"refl:3407":["The three Persons of the Godhead have one essence.",[]],"refl:3408":["Eye.",[]],"refl:3409":["[bekante] Customary usage determines the meaning of words. One must not introduce meanings of one’s own for old words, nor new |\nwords in place of old. *Verba valent sicut numi*; from *nomos*: law.",[]],"refl:3410":["One must not introduce a new language, [but] make use of the expressions of the ancients.",[]],"refl:3411":["Not to fetter oneself to terminologies and formulae.",[]],"refl:3412":["Crab [Krebs].",[]],"refl:3413":["Obedient servant.\nIt is worth knowing how [to do].",[]],"refl:3414":["transs:\nL 124'. On L §. 449 No. 2:\nSubstance. Instinct.\nψ−θ. Opposite L §. 449 »druck — Be« (l. 33):\nEpicurus’s *Canonica*.\nω? (φ?) ξ?? On L §. 449 »conceptus deceptor« (l. 19):\nE.g. the smallest time, the greatest velocity. — Entelechy.\nβ1. On L §. 449 No. 3:\nE.g. calling the representations of animals thoughts.",[]],"refl:3415":["Nature. Force.\nFurther opposite »Ein — fa« (l. 22) from γ−φ:\nInstinct.",[]],"refl:3416":["Of blind chance.\nOn L §. 451 No. 1:\nThink; then speak.",[]],"refl:3417":["The germ of groundlessness [Der Ungrundskeim].\nFurther from ω (? ξ−φ?):\nLogomachy, often pretended.\nβ1. On L §. 452 »Wortkrämerei«:\nAttractive force.\nFurther from ψ−θ:\nLogodaedaly.\nFurther beside L §. 452 sentence 3 from ρ−θ:\nAesthetic or logical verbiage [Wortkrämerey].\nFurther beside L §. 452 sentences 3 and 4 from ρ−θ:\nWord-picking in interpretation.\nWord-forging.\nWhittling.",[]],"refl:3418":["Heaviness [Schwere]. Weight.\nBeside L §. 453 »Bedeutung — dunkele« (l. 29), probably on the beginning of sentence 3:\nThe word may be clear; e.g. conversion.\nOn L §. 453 sentence 4, first half:\nE.g. mystery.\nOn L §. 453 sentence 4, second half:\nMonads. Harmony.",[]],"refl:3419":["Nature of man. Colour.",[]],"refl:3420":["Otherwise he sets his words upon screws [i.e. speaks equivocally].",[]],"refl:3421":["Nature.",[]],"refl:3422":["Logic is a science of the rules of learned knowledge and of learned presentation.",[]],"refl:3423":["Tropical use of words.\nL 128'. β1. Opposite L §. 460 »von — könnte« (ll. 36f.):\nClearness of knowledge. The light of knowledge.\nOpposite L §. 460 »nen — Er« (l. 31):\nobscure knowledge.",[]],"refl:3424":["It is open to me whether to use the word ‘understanding’ for the whole faculty of knowledge, or merely for the faculty of knowing distinctly.",[]],"refl:3425":["*iudicium assertorium*.",[]],"refl:3426":["It is not necessary that every concept should have a particular expression; e.g. ‘There is a God.’\nOn the penultimate sentence of L §. 462:\nThe Germans have no wit.\nOn the closing sentence of L §. 462:\nOr understand the judgments signified by the expressions.",[]],"refl:3427":["*Syllogismus est ratiocinium formale.*",[]],"refl:3428":["The crab [Krebs] is an animal; now a certain disease is a cancer [Krebs] ....\nFurther L 129' from ξ−χ:\namphiboly\n( s Instead of *malum*: *pravum*. The [Gute] evil [Böse] — ill [Übel].\nAppearance [Erscheinung] — illusion [Schein]. )",[]],"refl:3429":["Method and style.\n*stilus vel eruditus vel popularis*.",[]],"refl:3430":["What method is in thinking, style is in the signifying of thought for others. For myself I need no style.\n(s Whether purity and elegance in a dead language are so necessary in philosophical works? *purismus*.)",[]],"refl:3431":["*modus aestheticus in* [communicandis perceptio] *declaratione mentis suae*.\nThe way of making one’s thoughts conform to the subjective conditions of their communication. — Style in paintings.\nStyle in building.\nTo stylise is to be affected.",[]],"refl:3432":["Not affected.",[]],"refl:3433":["Learned distinctness: [its] difference from the common.\nLearned style must not be precious (*pretieux*).",[]],"refl:3434":["Intervals and resting-points.\nOn L §. 468 »punctum«:\n*Interpunctio*.",[]],"refl:3435":["In learning, purity and wealth not of synonymous but of proper words.",[]],"refl:3436":["Logikalisch. Physikalisch. Vernunftlehrerisch. (s longimetry.)\nOn L §. 471 No. 2:\nIchheit [I-hood].\nOn L §. 471 No. 4:\ncomplicirt. Fenster.\nOn L §. 471 No. 5:\nendelich, eilfertig.\nOn L §. 471 No. 7:\nDependentz. Substantz.",[]],"refl:3437":["Purists.",[]],"refl:3438":["God said: Let there be light!\nWhere one thinks in a merely learned manner, one must not think in periods.",[]],"refl:3439":["Figurative words, unusual constructions.",[]],"refl:3440":["When we present something, we seek to produce learned knowledge.\nHe who produces it is the teacher; he in whom it is produced: the pupil.\nOral or written presentation. He who learns from an oral presentation: the hearer; he who learns from a written one: the reader.",[]],"refl:3441":["On »von — Rede« (83821):\nTo speak like a book, or to write like chatter.",[]],"refl:3442":["The mode of teaching is distinct from the presentation; the presentation from the style.\nDidactic, polemical mode of teaching.",[]],"refl:3443":["The understanding (g of the learner) must not be burdened by the presentation, but strengthened — that is, exercised in thinking for itself.",[]],"refl:3444":["We need words in order to make ourselves intelligible not only to others but to ourselves. This faculty of using words is language, and children learn to speak. On the other hand, the skill of speaking [zu] coherently about an object is the faculty of discourse, which children do not attain at all, and women scarcely.\nThis faculty is either fluency [Beredtheit], or elegance of speech [Wohlredenheit], or eloquence [Beredsamkeit]. The first is wealth of speech (g without concern for the interest): the ability to say much, and in varied ways, about a thing. Loquacity is the inclination to talk much — whether about one single thing or about many things without connection makes no difference.\nFluency is the foundation; one must have a store in order to [reden] speak well. Elegance of speech is speaking in a manner suited both to the object and to taste. This is the condition under which alone fluency has its place, and it is style. It concerns quality [und ist] more than extent. Eloquence, on the other hand, *eloquentia*, is an art of persuading; it is dialectical, and therefore harmful. Rhetoric has also always brought ruin, alike in the state and in the sciences [auf], in religion and in philosophy: Demosthenes, Carneades, Cicero. On the other hand, the decline of elegance of speech (barbarous style) either indicates the decline of the sciences as its effect, or brings it about as its cause; e.g. the Scholastics, the later Romans.",[]],"refl:3445":["Prolixity of matter or of words.",[]],"refl:3446":["*Laus Asini*. *De sandaliis Hebraeorum*.\nTo deliver no great *panegyrici* upon any monarch of the schools.",[]],"refl:3447":["In a funeral oration one must not overstep the truth.",[]],"refl:3448":["He is understood when one connects with his discourse the very same series of thoughts that he has connected with it. He must either explain what concepts he connects with the words, or use the common expressions.",[]],"refl:3449":["The confused. Not as to one who has seen and felt it.",[]],"refl:3450":["He appeals to authority.\nOn L §. 488 No. 3:\nThrough prejudices; e.g. Papists.",[]],"refl:3451":["The use of each thing.",[]],"refl:3452":["Jesting. *Pathema*.",[]],"refl:3453":["With a varied audience, *condescendentz* [condescension] is needed in edifying (g practical) discourses; with [unt] scholars, *coascendentz* [co-ascension].",[]],"refl:3454":["Refutation. Before it, the dissolution of the illusion [Schein]. The cause of the dispute is removed.",[]],"refl:3455":["First it must be shown how far the true still extends in the [Ged] assertions of the opponent; secondly, in what the illusion consists which misleads him into error, and [woh] whence it could have arisen for him, or indeed for anyone else.\nWe refute the man, or a proposition, not because he puts it forward, but because it contains illusion.\nRefutation [ist] should be a step towards agreement, and indeed by means of the correction of the knowledge [Erkentnisse] on both sides.",[]],"refl:3456":["Exposition [Vortrag] is dogmatic or polemical. The [proper] means is critical, whereby one forestalls the opponent.",[]],"refl:3457":["Gegenüber von L 138 Z. 1:\nThat the earth moves. Joshua.",[]],"refl:3458":["*discussio quaestionis dubiae* [discussion of a doubtful question]. [Unt] Dispute in order to come to agreement.",[]],"refl:3459":["This world is the best. Two meanings.\nFerner L 138 aus ρ−θ:\nThere are none that last long.",[]],"refl:3460":["The author gives a reading [*lection*] and a counter-reading [*contralection*].",[]],"refl:3461":["Leibniz in [his treatment of] the *Mysterium SS. Trinitatis* [mystery of the Most Holy Trinity].",[]],"refl:3462":["Is not always permissible.",[]],"refl:3463":["Let us grant it to him and show its absurd consequences.\nGegenüber von L §. 504 »wir — Feh« (84930-85016):\nSceptic [Zweifler]. — — —\nHe who denies the principle of sufficient reason.",[]],"refl:3464":["*argumentatio ex concessis* [argument from what has been conceded].\nZuL §. 504 Satz 2, speciell zu »argumentatio ad hominem«:\nκαθ ανθροπον [according to the man].\nκαθ' αλητειαν [according to the truth]\nFerner aus ψ−θ:\n*ad crumenam* [to the purse], a wager.\n*ad verecundiam* [to modesty], appeal to great men.",[]],"refl:3465":["*fallacia ignorationis elenchi* [fallacy of ignorance of the point to be refuted].\nZuL §. 505 Schluss:\n*methodus Socratica* [the Socratic method].",[]],"refl:3466":["or not so unconditionally: restriction.",[]],"refl:3467":["God chooses the best that is possible.\n[Das Beste] Everything that is the best possible is such that there is no evil in it.\nTherefore in what God chooses there is no evil.\nThat world in which the most perfections are to be found is the best. Now in this [world] the most are to be found.\n}Restriction: and the greatest.\n} another restriction: no\n}imperfections at all.",[]],"refl:3468":["When I assert that everything composite consists of monads, the other [party] breaks off [the argument].\nI assert: some good does not move the will. The other objects: what we represent to ourselves as good, that we desire. I then answer with the distinction that living and dead representation must be distinguished.",[]],"refl:3469":["When it is said that learning improves the will, the counter-instance [*instantz*] of wicked scholars is brought against it.\nVilliers's answer to a priest.\nGegenüber von L §. 508 Satz 2:\nWhen it is said that religion promotes the welfare of the commonwealth.",[]],"refl:3470":["/\nIm letzten Satz von L §. 508 hat Kant vor »wenn« (Z. 33) eine 1 hinzugefügt, vor »dass« in Z. 34 eine 2, vor dem zweiten »oder« in Z. 34 eine 3, vor »oder« in Z. 35 eine 4. Auf diesen Satz bezieht sich auf L 140' folgende Bemerkung:\n1. If someone brings against my proposition that all finite spirits have bodies the counter-instance [*instantz*] that in that case the souls of the departed must also have bodies, I accept it.\n2. If someone were to say that in that case God too has a body.\n3. If I had said that all finite spirits have bodies, and the other objected that in that case [wen] God could not have created a world of spirits entirely by itself, I say: all finite spirits in this world.\n4. For brevity's sake one does not enter into the counter-instance.",[]],"refl:3471":["It is the right of retaliation.\n*e.g.* Those who attack the system of pre-established harmony because it would overthrow freedom.",[]],"refl:3472":["he brands them with the most odious names.\nHe reproaches them with a craving for innovation.\nFerner aus ξ−φ:\n*argumentum ab odio* [argument from hatred].\nFerner aus ρ−θ:\n*argumentum a tuto* [argument from safety].",[]],"refl:3473":["To controvert for the sake of truth; to dispute for the sake of being in the right, that is, of gaining the upper hand in the dispute.\n(s eristic. Wrangling.)",[]],"refl:3474":["/\nAdvocate's proof: that which draws advantage from the other's poor defence and from what he hastily concedes (Pompadour).",[]],"refl:3475":["Hennigs.\nNeben L §. 518 »dogmatica — systematische« (Z. 19-22):\ndoctrinal (s historical or dogmatic)\npolemical\ncritical (s Bayle)\nsceptical",[]],"refl:3476":["*Mega biblion: mega kakon* [a great book, a great evil].\nA great charnel-house: a library.\nTo interpret the author to his advantage, since he cannot speak for himself.\nThe end is reason and its growth.",[]],"refl:3477":["With regard to the content.\nWith regard to the exposition [Vortrag] (method).\nWith regard to the clothing (style).\nScholastic or popular; urbanity.\nNot to speak like a book; not to write as one chatters.\n1. System or rhapsody.\n2. Elegance of the system.\nLiterary (g learned) [scholastisch] or pragmatic.",[]],"refl:3478":["/\nEvery book [ist] should be: 1. thorough; 2. complete; 3. attractive through novelty, importance, agreeableness; 4. eloquent; 5. contain something lasting.\nIn ω (? φ?) wurden Wichtigkeit, 4., 5 — enthalten durchstrichen und links von der Rfl. folgende Bemerkungen hinzugefügt:\n1. Scholastic perfection. [2. Interessant] 2. Aesthetically interesting. 3. Practically interesting.\nAus ω (φ?) stammt auch der folgende, unter Z. 4 stehende Satz:\nTo prohibit and destroy books is to destroy the soul of mankind from the earth.",[]],"refl:3479":["Popularity does not consist in setting scholastic perfection aside, but only in this, that the clothing does not let it be seen as the scaffolding. (So we rule lines in pencil on which we write, and afterwards rub them out.) Everything scientific must be in accordance with the rules of the school; but its technical side must not be visible. Instead there must be condescension [*condescendenz*] to the power of comprehension and to customary expressions.",[]],"refl:3480":["/\nGegenüber von der Überschrift des 4. Abschnitts, über Nr. 3477, wohl \nThe scepticism of (g ancient learned) history (g or humanities) in Hardouin's opinion.",[]],"refl:3481":["Instrument of prudence.",[]],"refl:3482":["Rhapsody without method.",[]],"refl:3483":["The *literatus* is at least refined; not [so] the *eruditus*.\nCulture civilises.\nHume's judgment that the true scholar is at least an honest man.",[]],"refl:3484":["In learning not to forget sound understanding, and in the school not to forget the good manners of the world.\n*doctor umbraticus* [the scholar of the shade] (s *Graeculus* [little Greek]).",[]],"refl:3485":["One must display good sense [*bon sens*] more than learning.",[]],"refl:3486":["Naturalist* (s in fencing) in comparison with the school-trained scholar.\n*(s is: he who has not gone through the school.)\nNaturalists in metaphysics and morals are chatterers.\n(s The school consists in discipline, to grind away crudeness, and doctrine, to furnish with knowledge.)",[]],"refl:3487":["and of popular writings.\nOne must read many kinds of things and choose few as models for oneself. (s Mere reading is enjoying without digesting; it is gluttony. Hence *helluo librorum* [a glutton of books].)",[]],"refl:3488":["One must read some works of genius often.",[]]};
